/* Generated from the approved PHY 101 Study Pack. */
window.__PHY101_PACK__ = {"notes":{"title":"PHY 101","source":"PHY 101 Study Pack","topics":[{"title":"Space and Time","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Everyday thinking treats space as empty room and time as a clock that simply ticks. Physicists find that <b>space and time</b> are a surprisingly complex system, and they mean different things in different branches of physics. This is one reason physical theories are hard to unify.</p><p>The module's objectives: identify the types of space and time in science; explain the classical view; explain the relativity view; distinguish classical, relativity and quantum views; and solve problems on the classical view (position vectors, displacement, velocity).</p><p><b>Exam tip:</b> this topic is mostly recall. Learn each name together with its one-line identity (for example: sidereal = relative to the stars, coordinate = fixed reference frame, Planck = smallest time).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul><li><b>Absolute time</b>: Newton's classical idea. Time flows uniformly regardless of external factors, the same for all observers whatever their motion or position.</li><li><b>Relative time</b>: Einstein. Time depends on the observer's speed and on position in a gravitational field. Example: time dilation for fast astronauts; clocks near strong gravity tick more slowly.</li><li><b>Proper time</b>: the time interval measured by an observer along their own trajectory, in their own reference frame. To the astronaut, time seems to pass normally.</li><li><b>Coordinate time</b>: the time measured by an observer using a <i>fixed reference frame</i>; it describes events at different spatial locations. Example: time recorded by distant observers in the International Celestial Reference System (ICRS) rather than by a local clock.</li><li><b>Planck time</b>: the smallest meaningful unit of time in quantum mechanics, the time light takes to travel one Planck length in vacuum. About 10⁻⁴³ s (more precisely 5.39 × 10⁻⁴⁴ s). Used in discussions of the Big Bang and quantum gravity.</li></ul><p><b>Trap:</b> proper time (own clock, moving with you) is not the same as coordinate time (fixed frame, distant observer).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Psychological</td><td>Subjective experience of time</td><td>Fast when excited, slow when bored</td></tr><tr><td>Biological</td><td>Natural cycles of living organisms (circadian rhythms)</td><td>24-hour sleep-wake cycle</td></tr><tr><td>Universal (UT)</td><td>Standard based on Earth's rotation, essentially GMT</td><td>Astronomy, navigation, broadcasting</td></tr><tr><td>Atomic</td><td>Vibrations of caesium atoms; defines the SI second</td><td>TAI, GPS</td></tr><tr><td>Sidereal</td><td>Earth's rotation relative to distant stars; sidereal day is slightly shorter than a solar day</td><td>Aligning telescopes</td></tr><tr><td>Solar</td><td>Earth's rotation relative to the Sun; apparent (true Sun) versus mean (averaged)</td><td>Sundials = apparent; clocks = mean</td></tr><tr><td>Historical</td><td>Human or geological eras, epochs, centuries</td><td>Anthropocene Epoch</td></tr><tr><td>Cosmological</td><td>Largest scales; age and evolution of the universe</td><td>13.8 billion years</td></tr><tr><td>Clock</td><td>Everyday time in hours, minutes, seconds</td><td>24-hour clock</td></tr><tr><td>Dynamical</td><td>Precise time for planetary motion (TDB, TDT)</td><td>Predicting planet positions</td></tr><tr><td>Event</td><td>Duration an event takes; depends on the time system adopted</td><td>Length of an event in h, min, s</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The SI second is 9 192 631 770 periods of the caesium-133 hyperfine radiation. A sidereal day is about 23 h 56 min 4 s, so it is roughly 3 min 56 s shorter than the 24 h solar day.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The module lists many kinds of space. The main ones:</p><ul><li><b>Physical (real) space</b>: the 3D continuum (height, width, depth) where objects exist and events occur.</li><li><b>Euclidean space</b> (flat space): the geometry of Euclid; parallel lines never meet and triangle angles sum to 180°. A sheet of paper or a box.</li><li><b>Non-Euclidean space</b>: Euclid's rules fail; includes spherical geometry (triangle angle sum above 180°, e.g. the globe) and hyperbolic geometry (negative curvature).</li><li><b>Curved (Riemannian) space</b>: non-Euclidean space of general relativity, warped by mass and energy; gravitational lensing.</li><li><b>Minkowski space</b>: the four-dimensional spacetime of special relativity (three space dimensions plus time); the speed of light is constant.</li><li><b>Vector space</b>: a mathematical structure of vectors that can be added and scaled; the 3D space of displacement, velocity and force vectors.</li><li><b>Outer space</b>: the expanse beyond Earth's atmosphere containing stars, planets and galaxies.</li><li><b>Metric space</b>: a set in which distance between points is defined. <b>Topological space</b>: concerned with closeness and continuity, not exact distance.</li><li><b>Hilbert space</b>: the infinite-dimensional vector space with an inner product used for quantum states. <i>Slide slip:</i> the module's Hilbert space slide repeats the curved-space (general relativity) text; the correct physics is the quantum meaning.</li><li><b>Phase space</b>: each point is a complete state (positions and momenta). <b>Configuration space</b>: all possible arrangements of a system.</li></ul><p>Other listed spaces (personal construct, personal, cognitive, social, imaginary, digital/virtual, hyperspace) are psychological, social or virtual uses of the word and are not physics spaces.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Classical (Newton).</b> Absolute space is a fixed, unchanging stage independent of what is in it. Absolute time flows uniformly for all observers. Space and time are separate entities.</p><p><b>Relativity (Einstein).</b> Space and time are interwoven into one four-dimensional <b>spacetime</b>. An event has four coordinates (x, y, z, t). Observers in relative motion disagree on time and space, but all agree on spacetime intervals. In general relativity, gravity is not a force in the traditional sense but the <b>curvature of spacetime</b> by mass and energy; objects follow the curved paths, which we see as attraction.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Classical</th><th>Relativity</th><th>Quantum mechanics</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Space</td><td>Fixed, absolute framework</td><td>Part of spacetime; curved by mass/energy</td><td>Background for quantum fields; uncertain at small scales</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>Absolute, flows uniformly for all</td><td>Relative; changes with velocity and gravity</td><td>Uncertain at small scales; may be linked to space in novel ways</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Trap:</b> the PES question 'who introduced spacetime' expects Einstein, although Minkowski gave the geometric form in 1908.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul><li><b>Constancy of the speed of light:</b> c (about 3 × 10⁸ m/s) is the same for all inertial observers, regardless of their motion or the source's motion. This is the foundation; the next three effects follow from it.</li><li><b>Time dilation:</b> moving clocks run slower than clocks at rest (as seen by the observer). Only the idea is needed: Δt = γΔt₀ with γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²) is above 1.</li><li><b>Length contraction:</b> an object moving relative to an observer appears shorter along its direction of motion only.</li><li><b>Relativity of simultaneity:</b> events simultaneous for one observer may not be for another moving relative to the first.</li><li><b>Gravitational time dilation (general relativity):</b> clocks near a massive body tick more slowly.</li></ul><p><b>Common mistake:</b> using classical addition of speeds for light. A light beam fired from a speeding train still moves at c for the platform observer.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>For the course, space and time are treated as a fixed framework for measuring moving bodies. Each point P is labelled by a distance and direction from an arbitrary origin O.</p><p><b>Position vector:</b> r = x i + y j + z k = (x, y, z), where i, j, k are unit vectors (magnitude 1) along +x, +y, +z. Velocity and acceleration are written the same way: v = (vx, vy, vz), a = (ax, ay, az). Newton's law in vector form: F = m a.</p><p><b>Magnitude:</b> |r| = √(x² + y² + z²); in 2D, |r| = √(x² + y²). <b>Direction:</b> θ = tan⁻¹(y/x) from the +x axis.</p><p><b>Unit vector</b> along A: Â = A/|A| (magnitude 1). Multiplying a vector by a number multiplies each component.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A displacement vector from an initial point (x₁, y₁) to a final point (x₂, y₂) is <b>final minus initial</b>:</p><p><code>d = (x₂ − x₁) i + (y₂ − y₁) j</code></p><p>The order matters: reversing it gives the negative vector (the return journey, same size, opposite direction). Successive displacements add component by component. Distance (path length, scalar) differs from displacement (straight line start to end, vector).</p><p><b>Worked example 1 (classwork A, B, C).</b></p><ul><li>A from (6, 1) to (13, 4): (13 − 6) i + (4 − 1) j = <b>7i + 3j</b>.</li><li>B from (3, 7) to (3, 1): (3 − 3) i + (1 − 7) j = <b>−6j</b>.</li><li>C from (−14, 4) to (−6, 6): (−6 + 14) i + (6 − 4) j = <b>8i + 2j</b>.</li></ul><p>Slide slip: the module's solution labels C's result as 'A'; the vector is still C = 8i + 2j.</p><p><b>Worked example 2 (magnitude and angle).</b> |A| = √(7² + 3²) = √58 = 7.62; θ = tan⁻¹(3/7) = 23.2° above +x. |C| = √68 = 8.25.</p><p><b>Common mistake:</b> subtracting a negative incorrectly: −6 − (−14) = +8, not −20.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Exercise 2 adds time. <b>Average velocity = displacement ÷ time taken</b>, so v = (Δx/t) i + (Δy/t) j. It is a vector pointing along the displacement; <b>average speed</b> = path length ÷ time is a scalar. For a straight line, speed = |displacement|/t.</p><p><b>Worked example 3.</b> Classwork vectors with exercise times (units of length per second):</p><ul><li>A = 7i + 3j in 2 s: v = 3.5i + 1.5j; speed = 7.62/2 = 3.81.</li><li>B = −6j in 3 s: v = −2j; speed 2.</li><li>C = 8i + 2j in 5 s: v = 1.6i + 0.4j; speed = 8.25/5 = 1.65.</li></ul><p>The exercise times for D to I (D = 3 s, E = 4 s, F = 2 s, G = 5 s, H = 4 s, I = 7 s) are applied the same way, using the displacement read from the diagram (the diagram is not reproducible as text).</p><p><b>Worked example 4 (time of light travel).</b> Sun to Earth, 1.5 × 10¹¹ m at c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s: t = d/c = 500 s = 8.3 min. Planck time = Planck length/c = 1.6 × 10⁻³⁵ / 3.0 × 10⁸ ≈ 5.4 × 10⁻⁴⁴ s.</p><p><b>Exam tips:</b> (1) write final minus initial; (2) divide every component by t; (3) check units; (4) magnitudes need the square root.</p>"}]},{"title":"Units and Dimensions","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Physics is built on measurement. Before the 17th century, units of length, land area and weight varied from region to region, which made trade and science confusing. The internationally accepted answer is the <b>SI</b> (Système International d'Unités), developed in 1960 by an international committee and recommended by the General Conference on Weights and Measures.</p><p>A <b>physical quantity</b> is anything that can be measured and is needed to describe a physical phenomenon. Every measurement has two parts: a number and a unit (for example 20 m/s). <b>Dimensions</b> describe the physical nature of a quantity (length, mass, time) regardless of which units we use. A distance is always a length [L], whether quoted in metres, feet or light years.</p><p><b>Exam tip:</b> this topic is a favourite for quick MCQs. Learn the dimensional formulas by heart and practise the three uses of dimensional analysis: checking, deriving and converting.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>SI lists <b>7 base (fundamental) units</b>. Every other unit is derived from them.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>SI unit</th><th>Symbol</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Length</td><td>metre</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td>Mass</td><td>kilogram</td><td>kg</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>second</td><td>s</td></tr><tr><td>Electric current</td><td>ampere</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>Thermodynamic temperature</td><td>kelvin</td><td>K</td></tr><tr><td>Luminous intensity</td><td>candela</td><td>cd</td></tr><tr><td>Amount of substance</td><td>mole</td><td>mol</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Definitions as given in the module:</p><ul><li><b>Metre:</b> the length of the path travelled by light in vacuum during a time interval of 1/299 792 458 of a second.</li><li><b>Kilogram:</b> the mass of the international prototype kept at the BIPM (a platinum-iridium artifact, sanctioned by the 1st CGPM in 1889). Modern SI now defines it through Planck's constant, but the module uses the prototype.</li><li><b>Second:</b> the duration of 9 192 631 770 periods of the radiation from the transition between the two hyperfine levels of the ground state of the caesium-133 atom.</li><li><b>Ampere:</b> the constant current which, in two straight parallel conductors of infinite length and negligible cross section, 1 m apart in vacuum, produces a force of 2×10⁻⁷ newton per metre of length.</li><li><b>Kelvin:</b> the fraction 1/273.16 of the thermodynamic temperature of the triple point of water.</li><li><b>Candela:</b> luminous intensity in a given direction of a source emitting monochromatic radiation of frequency 540×10¹² Hz with radiant intensity 1/683 watt per steradian.</li><li><b>Mole:</b> the amount of substance containing as many entities as there are atoms in 0.012 kg of carbon-12.</li></ul><p>Besides the 7 base units there are two <b>supplementary units</b>: the <b>radian (rad)</b> for plane angle and the <b>steradian (sr)</b> for solid angle. Both are dimensionless.</p><p><b>Common mistakes:</b> the kilogram (not the gram) is the SI base unit of mass; the henry, newton, joule, watt and pascal are derived units, not base units; the mole measures amount of substance, not mass.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A system of units is defined by the base units chosen for length, mass and time.</p><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Length</th><th>Mass</th><th>Time</th><th>Force</th><th>Work/energy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CGS</td><td>centimetre</td><td>gram</td><td>second</td><td>dyne</td><td>erg</td></tr><tr><td>FPS</td><td>foot</td><td>pound</td><td>second</td><td>poundal</td><td>foot-poundal</td></tr><tr><td>MKS (SI)</td><td>metre</td><td>kilogram</td><td>second</td><td>newton</td><td>joule</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Conversion factors to learn:</p><ul><li>1 ft = 30.48 cm (= 0.3048 m); 1 in = 2.54 cm; 1 m = 3.28 ft</li><li>1 lb = 454 g</li><li>1 mile = 5280 ft = 1.61 km; 1 hour = 3600 s</li><li>1 N = 10⁵ dyne (1 kg m s⁻² = 10³ g × 10² cm s⁻²)</li><li>1 J = 10⁷ erg</li><li>9.8 m/s² = 980 cm/s²</li></ul><p><b>Method:</b> multiply by conversion fractions equal to 1 so that unwanted units cancel. The conversion factor for a combined unit is the product of the factors.</p><p><b>Worked example 1.</b> Convert 60 miles per hour to m/s.<br>60 mi/h × (5280 ft/1 mi) × (0.3048 m/1 ft) × (1 h/3600 s) = 26.8 m/s. (So 1 mph = 0.447 m/s, as in the module.)</p><p><b>Worked example 2.</b> Convert 15.0 in to cm: 15.0 × 2.54 = 38.1 cm.</p><p><b>Common mistake:</b> converting the wrong way. If the new unit is bigger (feet instead of metres) the number gets smaller: 9.80 m/s² = 9.80/0.3048 = 32.2 ft/s².</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul><li><b>Coherent:</b> all derived units are obtained from the base units by multiplication or division, without introducing numerical factors (1 N = 1 kg m s⁻², exactly).</li><li><b>Rational:</b> only one unit is assigned to a particular physical quantity. The joule is the unit of all kinds of energy; in other systems the same quantity can have several units (erg, calorie, foot-pound).</li><li><b>Absolute:</b> there are no gravitational units, so the factor g is eliminated (mass in kg, force in N, not kgf).</li><li><b>Metric:</b> multiples and sub-multiples are powers of ten.</li></ul><p><b>Exam tip:</b> questions often give one of these descriptions and ask which property it is. Match the keyword: no numerical factors = coherent; one unit per quantity = rational; no g = absolute; powers of 10 = metric.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Fundamental (base) quantities</b> are independent of each other: in mechanics they are <b>length, mass and time</b>. The seven base quantities have dimensional symbols [L], [M], [T], [A] (current), [K] (temperature), [cd] and [mol]. <b>Derived quantities</b> are expressed in terms of the fundamental ones: area, volume, force, work, pressure and so on.</p><p>Volume, pressure, energy, force and speed are all derived, not fundamental.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Factor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>tera</td><td>T</td><td>10¹²</td></tr><tr><td>giga</td><td>G</td><td>10⁹</td></tr><tr><td>mega</td><td>M</td><td>10⁶</td></tr><tr><td>kilo</td><td>k</td><td>10³</td></tr><tr><td>centi</td><td>c</td><td>10⁻²</td></tr><tr><td>milli</td><td>m</td><td>10⁻³</td></tr><tr><td>micro</td><td>μ</td><td>10⁻⁶</td></tr><tr><td>nano</td><td>n</td><td>10⁻⁹</td></tr><tr><td>pico</td><td>p</td><td>10⁻¹²</td></tr><tr><td>femto</td><td>f</td><td>10⁻¹⁵</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Useful small lengths:</b> 1 Angstrom (Å) = 10⁻¹⁰ m (size of an atom; hydrogen diameter about 1×10⁻¹⁰ m); 1 fermi (fm) = 10⁻¹⁵ m (size of a nucleus or proton). So 1 Å = 10⁵ fermi.</p><p>Typical sizes from the module: radius of the Earth 6.4×10⁶ m; Earth to Sun 1.5×10¹¹ m; wavelength of blue light 4×10⁻⁷ m; diameter of a proton 1×10⁻¹⁵ m. Times: one year 3.2×10⁷ s; one hour 3.6×10³ s. Masses: Earth 6×10²⁴ kg; electron 9×10⁻³¹ kg; proton 2×10⁻²⁷ kg.</p><p><b>Worked example 3.</b> A density of 8 g/cm³ in SI: 8×10⁻³ kg / 10⁻⁶ m³ = 8×10³ kg/m³. Rule of thumb: g/cm³ to kg/m³ multiply by 1000.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The <b>dimensions</b> of a quantity are the powers to which the fundamental quantities are raised to represent it. The expression showing this is its <b>dimensional formula</b>; setting the quantity equal to its formula gives the <b>dimensional equation</b>. Square brackets denote 'dimension of'. A quantity with powers a, b, c of mass, length and time has [A] = [MᵃLᵇTᶜ].</p><p>Examples from the module: [volume] = [M⁰L³T⁰], [speed] = [LT⁻¹], [force] = [MLT⁻²], [density] = [ML⁻³].</p><p>To find a dimensional formula, start from a defining equation and substitute dimensions. Dimensions behave like algebra: multiply, divide and raise to powers, but you never add them.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Defining relation</th><th>Dimensions</th><th>SI unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Area</td><td>L²</td><td>[L²]</td><td>m²</td></tr><tr><td>Volume</td><td>L³</td><td>[L³]</td><td>m³</td></tr><tr><td>Speed/velocity</td><td>length/time</td><td>[LT⁻¹]</td><td>m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Acceleration (g)</td><td>velocity/time</td><td>[LT⁻²]</td><td>m/s²</td></tr><tr><td>Force</td><td>ma</td><td>[MLT⁻²]</td><td>N</td></tr><tr><td>Momentum</td><td>mv</td><td>[MLT⁻¹]</td><td>kg m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Impulse</td><td>F × t</td><td>[MLT⁻¹]</td><td>N s</td></tr><tr><td>Work, energy</td><td>F × d</td><td>[ML²T⁻²]</td><td>J</td></tr><tr><td>Torque</td><td>F × r</td><td>[ML²T⁻²]</td><td>N m</td></tr><tr><td>Power</td><td>energy/time</td><td>[ML²T⁻³]</td><td>W</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure, stress, elastic modulus</td><td>F/A</td><td>[ML⁻¹T⁻²]</td><td>Pa</td></tr><tr><td>Density</td><td>m/V</td><td>[ML⁻³]</td><td>kg/m³</td></tr><tr><td>Coefficient of viscosity</td><td>F/(A · dv/dx)</td><td>[ML⁻¹T⁻¹]</td><td>Pa s</td></tr><tr><td>Surface tension</td><td>F/length</td><td>[MT⁻²]</td><td>N/m</td></tr><tr><td>Angular momentum</td><td>r × p</td><td>[ML²T⁻¹]</td><td>kg m²/s</td></tr><tr><td>Planck's constant</td><td>E = hf</td><td>[ML²T⁻¹]</td><td>J s</td></tr><tr><td>Gravitational constant G</td><td>F r²/(m₁m₂)</td><td>[M⁻¹L³T⁻²]</td><td>N m² kg⁻²</td></tr><tr><td>Gravitational potential</td><td>energy/mass</td><td>[L²T⁻²]</td><td>J/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Angular velocity</td><td>angle/time</td><td>[T⁻¹]</td><td>rad/s</td></tr><tr><td>Frequency</td><td>1/period</td><td>[T⁻¹]</td><td>Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Moment of inertia</td><td>m r²</td><td>[ML²]</td><td>kg m²</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Worked derivation (viscosity).</b> η = F/(A · dv/dx). [dv/dx] = [LT⁻¹]/[L] = [T⁻¹], [A] = [L²], so [η] = [MLT⁻²]/([L²][T⁻¹]) = [ML⁻¹T⁻¹].</p><p><b>Same-dimension groups to remember:</b> work = energy = torque [ML²T⁻²]; pressure = stress = elastic modulus = energy per volume [ML⁻¹T⁻²]; momentum = impulse [MLT⁻¹]; angular momentum = Planck's constant [ML²T⁻¹]; surface tension = spring constant [MT⁻²]. <b>Different:</b> work and power; force and surface tension (tension in a rope is a force, [MLT⁻²]); energy and momentum.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul><li>A <b>pure number</b> is dimensionless (2, π, ½, 6π).</li><li>The arguments of trigonometric, logarithmic and exponential functions are dimensionless (sin θ, ln x, eˣ, and so x in eˣ cannot carry units).</li><li>A <b>ratio of like quantities</b> is dimensionless: angle = arc/radius (length/length); refractive index = c in vacuum / speed in medium; strain = change in length / length; relative density.</li><li>Plane angle (radian) and solid angle (steradian) are dimensionless, although they have units.</li></ul><p><b>Dimensionless constants:</b> refractive index, relative density. <b>Dimensional constants</b> have dimensions: universal gravitational constant G, electric permittivity, elastic coefficients, Planck's constant, the speed of light.</p><p><b>Slide slip:</b> the module lists the 'velocity of light in vacuum' under dimensionless constants. That is wrong: c = 3×10⁸ m/s has dimension [LT⁻¹]. Only the refractive index (a ratio of speeds) is dimensionless.</p><p><b>Trap:</b> a dimensionless quantity can still have a unit (radian) and a quantity with a unit like the degree has no dimension.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Only quantities with the <b>same dimensions</b> can be added or subtracted. This is the <b>principle of homogeneity of dimensions</b>: a physical equation is dimensionally correct only if every term on both sides has the same dimensions. Dimensional analysis is the technique that uses this to check equations or help derive them. A dimensionally consistent equation is not guaranteed to be physically right; a dimensionally inconsistent one is definitely wrong.</p><p><b>Worked example 4 (module).</b> Check ½mv² = mgh (the equation printed in the module, m = mass, v = velocity, g = gravity, h = height). LHS: [M][LT⁻¹]² = [ML²T⁻²]. RHS: [M][LT⁻²][L] = [ML²T⁻²]. Same, so consistent.</p><p><b>Check V = √(E/d)</b> (speed of sound, elasticity E, density d): [E/d] = [ML⁻¹T⁻²]/[ML⁻³] = [L²T⁻²]; square root gives [LT⁻¹] = [V]. Correct.</p><p><b>Check h = 2T cosθ/(rρg)</b> (capillary rise): [T] = [MT⁻²]; [rρg] = L·ML⁻³·LT⁻² = [ML⁻¹T⁻²]; ratio = [L], same as h. Correct. (The slide prints d in the numerator and cosθ in the denominator because of garbled text; the real formula has ρ in the denominator.)</p><p><b>Check the pendulum options (module exercise).</b> P must be [T]. (a) P = 2(dg)²: (L·LT⁻²)² = L⁴T⁻⁴, wrong. (b) P = 2π(d/g): L/(LT⁻²) = T², wrong. (c) P = 2π(d/g)^0.5 = T. Correct.</p><p><b>Check s = ut + ½at.</b> ut is [L] but at is [LT⁻¹]; they cannot be added, so it is wrong (the right form is ½at²).</p><p><b>Finding unknown dimensions:</b> in F = at + bt², each term must be a force, so [a] = [F]/[T] = [MLT⁻³] and [b] = [F]/[T²] = [MLT⁻⁴].</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Assume the quantity is a product of powers of the variables it depends on, with a dimensionless constant k. Equate dimensions on both sides and solve for the exponents by matching powers of M, L and T.</p><p><b>Worked example 5 (ocean wave, Class Tutorial Part A Q1).</b> c = k gˣ λʸ ρᶻ. Then LT⁻¹ = (LT⁻²)ˣ (L)ʸ (ML⁻³)ᶻ.<br>Mass: z = 0.<br>Time: −2x = −1, so x = ½.<br>Length: x + y − 3z = 1, so y = ½.<br>Therefore c = k√(gλ). The density drops out.</p><p><b>Worked example 6 (pendulum).</b> P = k dᵃ gᵇ: T = Lᵃ (LT⁻²)ᵇ. Time: −2b = 1, b = −½. Length: a + b = 0, a = ½. So P = k√(d/g). Experiment or theory gives k = 2π.</p><p><b>Other results by this method:</b> T = C√(m/k) for a spring (k has dimension MT⁻²); drag on a sphere F ∝ ηrv (Stokes: 6πηrv); string frequency f ∝ (1/L)√(F/μ); v = √(2gh) for free fall.</p><p><b>Changing the unit of a derived quantity.</b> If the units of length and force are both made 4 times bigger, the unit of energy (force × length) becomes 4 × 4 = 16 times bigger. If M, L, T units all double, the unit of force (M L T⁻²) changes by 2 × 2 × 2⁻² = 1.</p><p><b>New unit systems:</b> if force F, acceleration A and time T are the base quantities, then L = AT² and energy = F·L = [FAT²].</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul><li>It gives <b>no information on dimensionless constants</b> (2π, ½, 6π). Those come from theory or experiment.</li><li>It cannot be used if the quantity depends on trigonometric or exponential functions.</li><li>It cannot handle an equation with <b>two or more variables of the same dimensions</b> in a way that fixes their powers uniquely, nor a quantity that is a <b>sum or difference of terms</b> (like s = ut + ½at²).</li><li>It is hard when a quantity depends on more than three unknown variables (only three base equations for M, L, T).</li><li>It cannot tell whether a quantity is a scalar or a vector, and it cannot show that an equation is truly correct, only that it is consistent.</li></ul><p><b>Trap:</b> an equation that passes the dimensional check can still be wrong (for example E = mv² passes but the correct kinetic energy is ½mv²).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Every measurement carries uncertainty from the apparatus, the experimenter and the number of readings. We approximate it using <b>significant figures</b> (s.f.).</p><ul><li>All non-zero digits are significant; zeros between non-zero digits are significant; trailing zeros after a decimal point are significant.</li><li>Leading zeros are not significant: 0.00450 has 3 s.f.</li><li><b>Multiplication/division:</b> the result has as many s.f. as the factor with the fewest. 4.56 × 1.4 = 6.384, quoted as 6.4.</li><li><b>Addition/subtraction:</b> keep as many decimal places as the least precise number.</li></ul><p><b>Percentage uncertainty:</b> relative errors add for products and quotients, and a power multiplies the relative error. For area A = πr², ΔA/A = 2 Δr/r. With r = 4.0 ± 0.1 cm, Δr/r = 2.5% so ΔA/A = 5%.</p><p><b>Exam tips:</b> (1) always convert to base units first; (2) write the dimensions of each term before judging an equation; (3) read options carefully, because ML²T⁻¹ (angular momentum, h) and ML²T⁻² (energy) differ by one power of T.</p>"}]},{"title":"Vectors and Scalars","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <b>scalar</b> has magnitude only (a number and a unit). A <b>vector</b> has both magnitude and direction. Vectors obey special addition rules; scalars add like ordinary numbers.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Scalar quantities</th><th>Vector quantities</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Mass (50 kg), temperature (25 °C), time (10 s)</td><td>Displacement (30 m east)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy (500 J), work (100 J), power (100 W)</td><td>Velocity (20 m/s west)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed (60 km/h), distance (200 m)</td><td>Force (15 N downward), weight (9.8 N downward)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Density (1.2 g/cm³), volume (2 m³)</td><td>Acceleration (9.8 m/s² downward), momentum (100 kg m/s north)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electric charge (5 C), pressure (101 325 Pa)</td><td>Electric field (50 N/C upward), magnetic field (0.1 T north)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Potential energy (50 J)</td><td>Torque (10 N m), angular velocity (3 rad/s), current (5 A, as listed on the slides)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><b>Distance vs displacement; speed vs velocity.</b> Distance is the total path length (scalar). Displacement is the straight line from start to finish, with direction (vector). A runner who completes a 400 m lap has covered 400 m but has displacement 0. Speed is distance/time (scalar); velocity is displacement/time (vector).</p>\n<p><b>Notes on the slide list.</b> Pressure is a scalar even though it comes from a force. Electric current is treated as a vector on the slides because it flows in a direction, but strictly it is a scalar (currents add arithmetically at a junction); in an exam answer follow the list in your own lecture notes if the question is taken from them. Energy, work and power are scalars even though they are built from vectors (the dot product of two vectors is a scalar).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Collinear vectors lie along the same line. Choose one direction as positive and add with signs.</p>\n<ul><li>Same direction: add magnitudes, direction unchanged.</li><li>Opposite directions: subtract magnitudes; the result points the way of the larger vector.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Worked example 1 (man).</b> A man walks 54.5 m east and then 30 m west. Displacement = 54.5 − 30 = <b>24.5 m east</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 2 (car).</b> A car goes 80 km east, 30 km west, then 50 km east. With east positive: 80 − 30 + 50 = <b>+100 km, i.e. 100 km east</b>. The distance travelled is 160 km, which is different.</p>\n<p>Subtraction of vectors is addition of the reversed vector: A − B = A + (−B). Reversing a vector keeps its length and flips its direction.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>When two vectors are at right angles, they form the two legs of a right triangle and the resultant is the hypotenuse.</p>\n<ul><li>Pythagoras: R = √(A² + B²)</li><li>Direction: tan θ = opposite/adjacent, so θ = tan⁻¹(opposite/adjacent)</li><li>sin θ = opp/hyp, cos θ = adj/hyp</li></ul>\n<p><b>Reading directions.</b> \"X of Y\" means: start pointing along Y and turn through the stated angle towards X. So 28.1° W of N is 28.1° from north towards west; it is the same direction as 61.9° N of W. Always draw the triangle and label which angle you are finding.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 3 (boat and river).</b> A boat heads north at 15 m/s in a river that flows west at 8.0 m/s.<br>\nR = √(8² + 15²) = √289 = 17 m/s.<br>\ntan θ = 8/15 = 0.5333, so θ = tan⁻¹(0.5333) = 28.1°.<br>\nResultant = <b>17 m/s at 28.1° W of N</b>. In unit-vector form: −8i + 15j m/s.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 4 (bear).</b> A bear walks 35 m E, 20 m N, 12 m W, 6 m S. Net east = 35 − 12 = 23 m; net north = 20 − 6 = 14 m.<br>\nR = √(23² + 14²) = <b>26.93 m</b>; θ = tan⁻¹(14/23) = 31.3° N of E. The distance walked is 73 m.</p>\n<p>When drawing motion triangles, place the vectors head to tail (tip to tail), and the resultant runs from the first tail to the last tip.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Resolving a vector means replacing it by two perpendicular components. For a vector A at angle θ above the +x axis:</p>\n<p>Aₓ = A cos θ &nbsp;&nbsp; A_y = A sin θ &nbsp;&nbsp; A = √(Aₓ² + A_y²) &nbsp;&nbsp; tan θ = A_y/Aₓ</p>\n<p>The cos goes with the side <i>adjacent</i> to the angle. If the angle is measured from the y axis, swap sin and cos.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 5 (plane).</b> A plane flies at 63.5 m/s at 32° south of east.<br>\nHorizontal: 63.5 cos 32° = <b>53.85 m/s east</b>.<br>\nVertical: 63.5 sin 32° = <b>33.65 m/s south</b> (the slide shows 33.64 from truncation).</p>\n<p><b>Adding by components (rectangular method).</b> Resolve every vector, add all x-components to get Rₓ, add all y-components to get R_y, then R = √(Rₓ² + R_y²) and θ = tan⁻¹(|R_y/Rₓ|). Check the quadrant from the signs of Rₓ and R_y. For 2.0 m at 40° and 4.0 m at 127°: Rₓ = 1.532 − 2.407 = −0.875 m, R_y = 1.286 + 3.195 = 4.480 m, so R = 4.56 ≈ 4.6 m, pointing at 101° from +x (second quadrant).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A unit vector has magnitude 1 and shows direction only. î is the unit vector along +x, ĵ along +y and k̂ along +z (read \"i-hat, j-hat, k-hat\"). Any vector is written A = Aₓ î + A_y ĵ + A_z k̂, or as an ordered triplet (Aₓ, A_y, A_z).</p>\n<p><b>Magnitude (3D Pythagoras):</b> |A| = √(Aₓ² + A_y² + A_z²).</p>\n<p><b>Unit vector along A:</b> Â = A/|A|.</p>\n<p><b>Sum and difference in component form</b> are done component by component. If A = Aₓ î + A_y ĵ + A_z k̂ and B = Bₓ î + B_y ĵ + B_z k̂:</p>\n<p>A ± B = (Aₓ ± Bₓ) î + (A_y ± B_y) ĵ + (A_z ± B_z) k̂</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 6 (Class Tutorial Q18 and Q20).</b> A = 2i + j + 3k, B = 3i + 2j + 6k.<br>\n|A| = √14 = 3.742, |B| = √49 = 7, so |A| + |B| = 10.74.<br>\nA + B = 5i + 3j + 9k, so |A + B| = √115 = 10.72.<br>\nThe magnitude of a sum is less than the sum of magnitudes unless the vectors are parallel.</p>\n<p><b>Position and displacement vectors.</b> A point P(x, y, z) has position vector r = x î + y ĵ + z k̂. The displacement from P to Q is Q − P (final minus initial): PQ = (x₂ − x₁) î + (y₂ − y₁) ĵ + (z₂ − z₁) k̂. For P(1,2,3) and Q(4,6,15): PQ = 3i + 4j + 12k, with length 13. Classwork: the arrow from (6,1) to (13,4) is 7i + 3j; from (3,7) to (3,1) is −6j.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Tail-to-tip (triangle) method.</b> Draw A, then draw B starting at the tip of A. The resultant R = A + B goes from the tail of A to the tip of B. The <b>parallelogram method</b> draws A and B from a common point (tail to tail), completes the parallelogram, and takes the diagonal from that point; it gives the same result.</p>\n<p><b>Rules for any triangle</b> with sides a, b, c opposite angles A, B, C:</p>\n<ul><li>Sine rule: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C</li><li>Cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A</li></ul>\n<p>Use the cosine rule when you know two sides and the included angle (or all three sides). Use the sine rule to find a direction afterwards.</p>\n<p><b>Resultant formula.</b> For vectors A and B with angle θ between their directions (tail to tail):</p>\n<p>R² = A² + B² + 2AB cos θ</p>\n<p>The plus sign appears because the interior angle of the tip-to-tail triangle is 180° − θ, and cos(180° − θ) = −cos θ. The direction of R from A: tan α = B sin θ / (A + B cos θ).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 7.</b> 2.0 m at 40° and 4.0 m at 127°. The angle between them is 127° − 40° = 87°.<br>\nR² = 2² + 4² + 2(2)(4)cos 87° = 4 + 16 + 0.84 = 20.84, so R = <b>4.6 m</b>.<br>\n(The slide writes R² = 4² + 2² − 2(4)(2)cos 93°, using the interior triangle angle; both give the same value.)</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Take A = 4.0 N and B = 5.0 N and vary the angle θ between them. R = √(41 + 40 cos θ).</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Angle θ</th><th>Resultant R</th><th>Comment</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>0°</td><td>9.0 N</td><td>maximum, R = A + B</td></tr>\n<tr><td>45°</td><td>8.3 N</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>90°</td><td>6.4 N</td><td>Pythagoras, √41</td></tr>\n<tr><td>135°</td><td>3.6 N</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>180°</td><td>1.0 N</td><td>minimum, R = |A − B|</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The resultant falls steadily as the angle opens up. In general |A − B| ≤ R ≤ A + B. (On the lecture slide the diagrams are arranged out of order, so read the values by their angle label, not their position.)</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Definition:</b> A·B = AB cos θ, where θ is the angle between the vectors (0° to 180°). The result is a <b>scalar</b>: magnitude only, no direction. It can be negative (θ above 90°).</p>\n<p><b>In components:</b> A·B = AₓBₓ + A_yB_y + A_zB_z.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Commutative: A·B = B·A. Distributive: A·(B + C) = A·B + A·C.</li>\n<li><b>Perpendicular</b> (θ = 90°): A·B = 0, because cos 90° = 0.</li>\n<li><b>Parallel</b> (θ = 0°): A·B = AB. Anti-parallel: A·B = −AB.</li>\n<li>A·A = A². Unit vectors: î·î = ĵ·ĵ = k̂·k̂ = 1 and î·ĵ = ĵ·k̂ = k̂·î = 0.</li>\n<li>Angle between vectors: cos θ = A·B/(AB).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Uses:</b> work W = F·d (a scalar), power P = F·v, and projection of one vector on another. <b>Worked example 8.</b> F = (10i + 5j) N and d = (4i + 2j) m give W = 40 + 10 = 50 J. <b>Test for perpendicular:</b> A = 2i + 3j and B = 3i − 2j give 6 − 6 = 0, so they are perpendicular.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Definition:</b> A × B = (AB sin θ) n̂, where n̂ is the unit vector perpendicular to both A and B. The result is a <b>vector</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Magnitude:</b> |A × B| = AB sin θ (equals the area of the parallelogram on A and B).</li>\n<li><b>Direction (right-hand rule):</b> curl the fingers of your right hand from A towards B through the small angle; the thumb points along A × B.</li>\n<li><b>Parallel</b> (θ = 0° or 180°): A × B = 0, since sin 0° = sin 180° = 0.</li>\n<li><b>Perpendicular</b> (θ = 90°): magnitude is the maximum, AB.</li>\n<li><b>Not commutative:</b> A × B = −(B × A). A × A = 0. Distributive over addition.</li>\n<li>Unit vectors: î × ĵ = k̂, ĵ × k̂ = î, k̂ × î = ĵ; reversing the order gives a minus sign.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Component form (determinant):</b> A × B = (A_yB_z − A_zB_y) î + (A_zBₓ − AₓB_z) ĵ + (AₓB_y − A_yBₓ) k̂.</p>\n<p><b>Uses:</b> torque τ = r × F, angular momentum L = r × p, magnetic force F = qv × B. <b>Worked example 9.</b> A = i + 2j, B = 3i + j: z-component = (1)(1) − (2)(3) = −5, so A × B = −5k. A 20 N force at 30° to a 0.5 m lever arm gives τ = 0.5 × 20 × sin 30° = 5 N m.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Dot product</th><th>Cross product</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Result</td><td>scalar</td><td>vector</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>AB cos θ</td><td>AB sin θ</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Zero when</td><td>perpendicular</td><td>parallel</td></tr><tr><td>Order</td><td>commutative</td><td>anti-commutative</td></tr></tbody></table>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Adding magnitudes of non-collinear vectors. Only collinear vectors in the same direction add arithmetically.</li>\n<li>Confusing |A + B| with |A| + |B| (Tutorial Q18 vs Q20: 10.74 vs 10.72).</li>\n<li>Mixing sin and cos for components: the component adjacent to the angle uses cos.</li>\n<li>Reading \"28.1° W of N\" backwards. Start from N (the last letter) and turn 28.1° towards W.</li>\n<li>Using the wrong sign in the cosine rule. With θ between the vectors (tail to tail) use +2AB cos θ; with the interior triangle angle use −2AB cos.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that distance (73 m) is not displacement (26.93 m) and that displacement can be zero.</li>\n<li>Calling the dot product a vector, or saying the cross product of parallel vectors is AB (it is 0).</li>\n<li>Subtracting vectors without reversing the second one; or using initial − final for a displacement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Quick checks.</b> Perpendicular: dot = 0. Parallel: cross = 0. Resultant of two vectors lies between |A − B| and A + B. Unit vector: divide by the magnitude.</p>\n<p><b>Past-paper reminders.</b> Energy is the scalar (Resit Q7); acceleration is the vector rate of change of velocity; the electric field has magnitude and direction; the x unit vector is î; work is a scalar; the dot product is a scalar; the dot product of perpendicular vectors contains cos 90° = 0.</p>"}]},{"title":"Differentiation of Vectors","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Motion is described by how position changes with time. Calculus gives exact meaning to \"how fast\" and \"how quickly the speed changes\" at a single instant, which a simple <i>distance ÷ time</i> only gives on average.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Differentiation</b> turns position into velocity and velocity into acceleration.</li>\n<li><b>Integration</b> is the reverse: acceleration gives velocity, velocity gives position.</li>\n<li>For vectors, the position r(t) = x(t) i + y(t) j + z(t) k is differentiated <b>component by component</b>, because the unit vectors i, j, k are fixed in direction and magnitude.</li></ul>\n<p>The derivative of y = f(x) is dy/dx = lim (Δx→0) of Δy/Δx. Geometrically it is the <b>slope of the tangent</b> to the graph. Physically, with x as position and t as time, the slope of an x–t graph is the velocity, and the slope of a v–t graph is the acceleration.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Let u and v be functions of x and a, n be constants.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Constant</td><td>d(a)/dx = 0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Power</td><td>d(xⁿ)/dx = n xⁿ⁻¹ (n can be negative or a fraction)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Constant multiple</td><td>d(a u)/dx = a du/dx</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sum / difference</td><td>d(u ± v)/dx = du/dx ± dv/dx</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Product</td><td>d(uv)/dx = u dv/dx + v du/dx</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quotient</td><td>d(u/v)/dx = (v du/dx − u dv/dx) / v²</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chain</td><td>if y = f(u) and u = g(x): dy/dx = (dy/du)(du/dx)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><b>Examples.</b> d(5x³ − 4x + 7)/dx = 15x² − 4. For x²sin x use the product rule: 2x sin x + x² cos x. For x/(x²+1) use the quotient rule: [(x²+1)(1) − x(2x)]/(x²+1)² = (1 − x²)/(x²+1)². For (3x+1)⁵ use the chain rule: 5(3x+1)⁴ × 3 = 15(3x+1)⁴.</p>\n<p><b>Common slip:</b> in the quotient rule the order in the numerator matters (denominator × derivative of top, <i>minus</i> top × derivative of bottom). Swapping gives the wrong sign.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>With the angle x in <b>radians</b> (the formulas fail in degrees unless you convert):</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>f(x)</th><th>df/dx</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>sin x</td><td>cos x</td></tr>\n<tr><td>cos x</td><td>−sin x</td></tr>\n<tr><td>tan x</td><td>sec² x</td></tr>\n<tr><td>cot x</td><td>−csc² x</td></tr>\n<tr><td>sec x</td><td>sec x tan x</td></tr>\n<tr><td>csc x</td><td>−csc x cot x</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>With the chain rule: d[sin(ωt)]/dt = ω cos(ωt), and d[cos(ωt)]/dt = −ω sin(ωt). The extra factor ω is the derivative of the inside function and is the most commonly forgotten piece. Differentiating sin twice returns −sin, which is why oscillations satisfy d²x/dt² = −ω²x.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>f(x)</th><th>df/dx</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>eˣ</td><td>eˣ</td></tr>\n<tr><td>aˣ</td><td>aˣ ln a</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ln x</td><td>1/x</td></tr>\n<tr><td>log<sub>a</sub> x</td><td>1/(x ln a)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>sin⁻¹ x</td><td>1/√(1 − x²)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>cos⁻¹ x</td><td>−1/√(1 − x²)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>tan⁻¹ x</td><td>1/(1 + x²)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Chain rule versions: d(e^(kt))/dt = k e^(kt); d[ln(g(x))]/dx = g′(x)/g(x), so d[ln(5x)]/dx = 5/(5x) = 1/x (not 1/(5x)); d[tan⁻¹(2x)]/dx = 2/(1 + 4x²).</p>\n<p><b>Medical link.</b> Drug concentration in blood often decays as C = C₀e^(−kt). The rate of change is dC/dt = −kC, so the elimination rate is proportional to the concentration (first-order elimination).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>If r(t) = x(t) i + y(t) j + z(t) k then\n<br><b>dr/dt = (dx/dt) i + (dy/dt) j + (dz/dt) k.</b></p>\n<ul><li><b>Velocity:</b> v = dr/dt = vₓ i + v<sub>y</sub> j + v<sub>z</sub> k. It is a vector, always <b>tangent to the path</b> and pointing the way the particle moves.</li>\n<li><b>Acceleration:</b> a = dv/dt = d²r/dt².</li>\n<li><b>Speed:</b> |v| = √(vₓ² + v<sub>y</sub>² + v<sub>z</sub>²). Speed is a scalar, the magnitude of velocity.</li>\n<li>Magnitude of acceleration: |a| = √(aₓ² + a<sub>y</sub>² + a<sub>z</sub>²).</li></ul>\n<p><b>Trap:</b> |dr/dt| (speed) is not the same as d|r|/dt (rate at which the distance from the origin changes). For circular motion about the origin |r| is constant so d|r|/dt = 0, but the speed is not zero. Also |dv/dt| (size of acceleration) differs from d|v|/dt (rate of change of speed).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Example 1.</b> r = (3t² + 2) i + (t³ − t) j (metres, seconds). Find v, a, and the speed and acceleration at t = 2 s.</p>\n<p>v = dr/dt = 6t i + (3t² − 1) j. a = dv/dt = 6 i + 6t j.<br>\nAt t = 2: v = 12 i + 11 j, speed = √(12² + 11²) = √265 = 16.3 m/s. a = 6 i + 12 j, |a| = √(36 + 144) = √180 = 13.4 m/s². Position: r = 14 i + 6 j.</p>\n<p><b>Example 2 (lecture class exercise).</b> A particle has x = ct² and y = bt². Find its speed.</p>\n<p>vₓ = dx/dt = 2ct, v<sub>y</sub> = dy/dt = 2bt. Speed = √(4c²t² + 4b²t²) = <b>2t√(c² + b²)</b>. The acceleration is a = 2c i + 2b j, constant, with magnitude 2√(c² + b²). Eliminating t gives y = (b/c)x, a straight line, so the particle accelerates uniformly along a straight path.</p>\n<p><b>Example 3.</b> x = Ct + Dt² with C = 2 m/s and D = 0.1 m/s². Displacement from t = 2 s to 5 s, and average velocity.</p>\n<p>x(5) = 10 + 2.5 = 12.5 m; x(2) = 4 + 0.4 = 4.4 m. Δx = 8.1 m. Average velocity = Δx/Δt = 8.1/3 = 2.7 m/s. The instantaneous velocity is v = C + 2Dt = 2 + 0.2t, so at t = 2 it is 2.4 m/s and at t = 5 it is 3.0 m/s; the average 2.7 lies between them (it is the mean because a is constant).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The product rule carries over to both vector products:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Dot product:</b> d(A·B)/dt = A·(dB/dt) + (dA/dt)·B. The result is a scalar.</li>\n<li><b>Cross product:</b> d(A×B)/dt = A×(dB/dt) + (dA/dt)×B. <b>Keep the order</b> of A and B in each term, because A×B = −B×A.</li>\n<li><b>Scalar times vector:</b> d(f A)/dt = f dA/dt + (df/dt) A.</li></ul>\n<p>Useful consequences. d(A·A)/dt = 2A·dA/dt. So if |A| is constant (A·A constant) then A·dA/dt = 0: <b>a vector of constant magnitude has a derivative perpendicular to it.</b> This is why in uniform circular motion v ⟂ r and a ⟂ v. Also d(r·r)/dt = 2 r·v. And v·a = d(v²/2)/dt, so the particle speeds up when v·a > 0, slows when v·a < 0, and has constant speed when v·a = 0.</p>\n<p><b>Unit vectors.</b> A fixed unit vector (i, j, k) has derivative zero. A unit vector that rotates, n̂ = cosθ i + sinθ j, has dn̂/dt = (dθ/dt)(−sinθ i + cosθ j). This is a unit vector perpendicular to n̂ multiplied by the angular rate, so its magnitude is |dθ/dt|. A vector A = A Â with changing magnitude and direction differentiates as dA/dt = (dA/dt)Â + A (dÂ/dt).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Integration reverses differentiation. For vectors, integrate each component and fix the constants with <b>initial conditions</b>.</p>\n<p>v = ∫a dt + v₀ and r = ∫v dt + r₀, where v₀ = v(0) and r₀ = r(0). Useful: ∫tⁿ dt = tⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) (n ≠ −1), ∫cos ωt dt = (sin ωt)/ω, ∫e^(kt) dt = e^(kt)/k. Never forget the constant: it carries the initial condition.</p>\n<p><b>Constant acceleration a (one dimension).</b><br>\nv = ∫a dt = at + u (u = velocity at t = 0, the constant of integration).<br>\ns = ∫(u + at) dt = ut + ½at² (taking s = 0 at t = 0).<br>\nFor v² = u² + 2as, write a = dv/dt = v dv/ds, so v dv = a ds. Integrating from u to v and from 0 to s gives ½v² − ½u² = as, i.e. <b>v² = u² + 2as</b>. Also s = ½(u + v)t.</p>\n<p>These hold only when a is constant. For vectors they apply to each component separately: v = u + at, r = r₀ + ut + ½at².</p>\n<p><b>Example 4.</b> a = 6t i − 2 j, v(0) = 4 i, r(0) = i + j. Then v = (3t² + 4) i − 2t j and r = (t³ + 4t + 1) i + (1 − t²) j. At t = 2: v = 16 i − 4 j and r = 17 i − 3 j.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Direction of motion.</b> The tangent to the path at time t is along v. The unit tangent is T̂ = v/|v|. The angle the tangent makes with the x-axis satisfies tanθ = v<sub>y</sub>/vₓ. For r = t² i + t³ j, v = 2t i + 3t² j; at t = 1 v = 2 i + 3 j, |v| = √13, T̂ = 0.555 i + 0.832 j, and θ = tan⁻¹(3/2) = 56.3°.</p>\n<p><b>Related rates.</b> If a quantity depends on another that changes in time, use the chain rule. Sphere: V = (4/3)πr³ gives dV/dt = 4πr² dr/dt. Circle: A = πr² gives dA/dt = 2πr dr/dt. At r = 2 cm and dr/dt = 0.5 cm/s, dV/dt = 4π(4)(0.5) = 8π ≈ 25.1 cm³/s.</p>\n<p><b>Turning points.</b> A particle is momentarily at rest when v = 0. For x = 2t³ − 9t² + 12t, v = 6t² − 18t + 12 = 6(t − 1)(t − 2), so it stops at t = 1 s and 2 s (the motion reverses there).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul><li>Forgetting the chain-rule factor: d(sin 3t)/dt = 3cos 3t, not cos 3t.</li>\n<li>Differentiating a power wrongly: d(t³)/dt = 3t², and the derivative of a constant is 0 (so constants disappear from v and a).</li>\n<li>Giving speed as a vector or velocity as a scalar. Speed = √(vₓ² + v<sub>y</sub>²), never vₓ + v<sub>y</sub>.</li>\n<li>Squaring only part of the expression: 2t√(c² + b²) keeps the t outside the root once t² is taken out.</li>\n<li>Using v = u + at, s = ut + ½at² when a depends on t. If a = 6t, integrate instead.</li>\n<li>Leaving out the constant of integration or the initial condition.</li>\n<li>Using degrees inside a derivative or an integral of trig functions.</li>\n<li>Average velocity is Δx/Δt, not the mean of the two end velocities unless a is constant.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Exam tips.</b> Write v and a component by component before substituting the time. Substitute only at the end. Check units: v in m/s, a in m/s². For a given path, speed constant does not mean acceleration is zero (circular motion). If a question gives x = Ct + Dt² with D in m/s², then C is in m/s.</p>"}]},{"title":"Kinematics","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Mechanics</b> is the study of bodies at rest or in motion. It splits into <b>statics</b> (objects in equilibrium) and <b>dynamics</b> (objects in motion). Dynamics splits again into <b>kinematics</b> (the geometric aspects of motion: how things move, with no interest in the cause) and <b>kinetics</b> (the analysis of the forces that cause motion).</p>\n<p>Kinematics describes motion with five quantities: distance, displacement, speed, velocity and acceleration. A <b>particle</b> is an idealised body whose mass is concentrated at a point: it has location but no size, shape or rotation. All the formulas below treat the moving object as a particle.</p>\n<p><b>Exam tip:</b> if a question says \"ignore the cause of motion\" or \"geometry of motion\", the word wanted is kinematics; if it says \"forces causing motion\", the word is kinetics.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><b>Distance</b>: scalar, the total ground covered along the path. Unit m.</li>\n<li><b>Displacement</b>: vector, the straight-line change in position from start to finish, with direction. Unit m. Δs = s' − s. Its magnitude is the straight-line distance between start and end.</li>\n<li><b>Speed</b>: scalar, how fast. Average speed = total distance / total time.</li>\n<li><b>Velocity</b>: vector, rate of change of position. Average velocity = displacement / time. Unit m s⁻¹.</li>\n<li><b>Acceleration</b>: rate of change of velocity (or speed). Unit m s⁻². The module notes it can be treated as scalar or vector. Negative acceleration is often called <b>deceleration</b> or <b>retardation</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Average vs instantaneous.</b> Average velocity = Δs/Δt over an interval. Instantaneous velocity is the limit as Δt → 0, that is v = ds/dt, the slope of the displacement-time graph at one instant. Instantaneous acceleration is a = dv/dt.</p>\n<p><b>Classic trap:</b> a runner finishing one lap of a track has covered a large distance but has zero displacement, so average velocity is zero while average speed is not. A negative acceleration does not always mean slowing down: it slows the body only when velocity is positive. If velocity and acceleration are both negative, the body speeds up in the negative direction.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>For straight-line motion with <b>constant acceleration</b>:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Equation</th><th>Missing variable</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>v = u + at</td><td>s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>s = ½(u + v)t</td><td>a</td></tr>\n<tr><td>s = ut + ½at²</td><td>v</td></tr>\n<tr><td>s = vt − ½at²</td><td>u</td></tr>\n<tr><td>v² = u² + 2as</td><td>t</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Here s = displacement (m), u = initial velocity (m s⁻¹), v = final velocity (m s⁻¹), a = acceleration (m s⁻²), t = time (s).</p>\n<p><b>Strategy:</b> list the three known quantities, identify the unknown, and pick the equation that contains those four and omits the fifth. Choose a positive direction first and give every vector (s, u, v, a) its sign.</p>\n<p><b>Validity.</b> These equations hold <b>only when acceleration is constant</b>. If a depends on time or position, for example a = 6t, you must integrate instead (see the calculus section).</p>\n<p><b>Derivation of v² = u² + 2as.</b> Start with (1) v = u + at and (2) s = ½(u + v)t. From (1), t = (v − u)/a. Substitute into (2): s = ½(u + v)(v − u)/a, so 2as = (u + v)(v − u) = v² − u², giving v² = u² + 2as.</p>\n<p>The same result follows by calculus: a = v dv/ds, so ∫v dv = ∫a ds, giving v² = v₀² + 2a(s − s₀).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>For vertical motion the acceleration is g ≈ 9.8 m s⁻² <b>downward</b> (use 10 if the question says so). Take up as positive, so a = −g, and use the suvat equations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>At the top</b> of the flight, v = 0 (but a is still −g, not zero).</li>\n<li><b>Maximum height</b>: 0 = u² − 2gH, so H = u²/(2g).</li>\n<li><b>Time to the top</b>: 0 = u − gt, so t = u/g.</li>\n<li><b>Time of flight</b> (back to the launch level): T = 2u/g. Up and down times are equal.</li>\n<li><b>Return speed</b> equals the launch speed (air resistance neglected), but is directed downward.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Free fall</b> means falling under gravity alone, starting from rest: v = gt, s = ½gt², v² = 2gs. The result does not depend on mass: in a vacuum a feather and a hammer fall together.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 1 (module).</b> A stone is thrown up at 10 m s⁻¹ from 15 m above the ground. Find its maximum height and the time to reach the ground (g = 9.8).</p>\n<p>Maximum: 0 = 10² + 2(−9.8)s gives s = 100/19.6 = 5.10 m above the throw point, so the maximum height above ground is 15 + 5.10 = 20.1 m. Ground: s = −15, so −15 = 10t − 4.9t², i.e. 4.9t² − 10t − 15 = 0, giving t = 3.05 s (the other root, −1.01 s, is rejected).</p>\n<p><b>Common mistake:</b> forgetting to add the 15 m, or keeping the negative root for time. Time cannot be negative.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><b>Displacement-time (s-t) graph</b>: slope = velocity. A straight line means constant velocity; a curve bending upward means increasing speed; a flat line means at rest.</li>\n<li><b>Velocity-time (v-t) graph</b>: slope = acceleration; <b>area under the graph = displacement</b>. A horizontal line is constant velocity (zero acceleration); a straight sloping line is constant acceleration. A v-t graph therefore gives velocity, acceleration and displacement.</li>\n<li><b>Acceleration-time (a-t) graph</b>: area under it = change in velocity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Areas below the time axis count as negative displacement. Total distance adds the magnitudes of all areas; displacement adds them with sign.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 2.</b> A body accelerates uniformly from rest to 10 m s⁻¹ in 5 s, then moves at constant velocity for 10 s. Displacement = triangle ½ × 5 × 10 = 25 m plus rectangle 10 × 10 = 100 m, total 125 m.</p>\n<p><b>Trap:</b> do not read the slope of an s-t graph as acceleration, and do not read the height of a v-t graph as displacement.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>For one-dimensional (rectilinear) motion with position s(t):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>v = ds/dt</li>\n<li>a = dv/dt = d²s/dt²</li>\n<li>a = v dv/ds (chain rule: dv/dt = (dv/ds)(ds/dt)), equivalent to a ds = v dv.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Constant acceleration</b> by integration (the module's route): ∫dv = ∫a dt gives v = v₀ + at; integrating v = ds/dt gives s = s₀ + v₀t + ½at²; integrating v dv = a ds gives v² = v₀² + 2a(s − s₀).</p>\n<p><b>Given a(t), find v(t):</b> integrate, v = v₀ + ∫a dt. <b>Given v(t), find s(t):</b> integrate again, s = s₀ + ∫v dt. Use the initial conditions to fix the constants.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 3.</b> A particle starts from rest with a = 6t m s⁻². Then v = ∫6t dt = 3t², and s = ∫3t² dt = t³. At t = 3 s, v = 27 m s⁻¹ and s = 27 m. The suvat equations would give wrong answers here because a is not constant.</p>\n<p><b>Module example:</b> x = ct², y = bt². Then vₓ = 2ct, v_y = 2bt, so speed = 2t√(b² + c²).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Launch at speed u and angle θ above the horizontal. Resolve the velocity: uₓ = u cos θ and u_y = u sin θ. The two directions are independent.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Horizontal</b>: no acceleration, so the horizontal component stays constant (unaffected by gravity): x = u cos θ · t.</li>\n<li><b>Vertical</b>: acceleration −g: y = u sin θ · t − ½gt².</li>\n<li><b>Time of flight</b> (landing at launch level): T = 2u sin θ / g.</li>\n<li><b>Maximum height</b>: H = u² sin²θ / (2g).</li>\n<li><b>Range</b>: R = u² sin 2θ / g.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Range is largest when sin 2θ = 1, that is θ = <b>45°</b>, giving R_max = u²/g. Angles that add to 90° (such as 30° and 60°) give the same range. At the top, vertical velocity is zero but the horizontal component u cos θ remains, so the speed there is u cos θ, not zero.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 4.</b> A ball is kicked at 20 m s⁻¹ at 30° (g = 10). T = 2(20)(0.5)/10 = 2 s. H = (20 × 0.5)²/20 = 5 m. R = 400 × sin 60°/10 = 34.6 m.</p>\n<p><b>Horizontal launch</b> from a height: the fall time depends only on the height (t = √(2h/g)), not on the horizontal speed. A dropped stone and a stone thrown horizontally from the same height land together.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Velocity depends on the observer. The velocity of A relative to B is v_AB = v_A − v_B, with all velocities measured in the same frame (for example the ground).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cars moving toward each other at 20 and 30 m s⁻¹ approach at 50 m s⁻¹. Moving the same way, the faster gains on the slower at the difference.</li>\n<li><b>Ball in a moving bus.</b> A bus moves at constant v₁. A man inside throws a ball upward at v₀. In the bus frame the ball goes straight up and down and returns to his hand. From the ground the ball also keeps the horizontal speed v₁, so its path is a parabola. The time of flight is 2v₀/g, so its displacement relative to the ground is v₁ × 2v₀/g = 2v₀v₁/g.</li>\n<li>A ball rolled forward at 3 m s⁻¹ relative to a bus moving at 10 m s⁻¹ moves at 13 m s⁻¹ relative to the ground.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Trap:</b> the vertical motion is the same in both frames; only the horizontal speed differs. Because the bus does not accelerate, the ball lands back in the thrower's hands.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A particle on a circle of radius r with speed v has two acceleration components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Tangential acceleration</b> aₜ = dv/dt, along the tangent. It changes the speed.</li>\n<li><b>Radial (centripetal) acceleration</b> aᵣ = v²/r, directed toward the centre. It changes the direction of the velocity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The two are perpendicular, so the total acceleration has magnitude a = √(aₜ² + aᵣ²). Its angle φ with the tangential direction satisfies tan φ = aᵣ/aₜ.</p>\n<p><b>Class exercise.</b> Starting from rest on a circle of radius 4 m with aₜ = 3 m s⁻²: at t = 2 s, v = aₜt = 6 m s⁻¹, so aᵣ = 36/4 = 9 m s⁻². Then tan φ = 9/3 = 3, giving φ = 71.6°.</p>\n<p><b>Trap:</b> at t = 0 the radial component is zero because v = 0, and uniform circular motion has aₜ = 0 (only radial acceleration).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Always fix a positive direction and keep signs consistent. For up-then-down problems, a = −g all the way.</li>\n<li>Use suvat only if acceleration is constant. For a(t) or v(t), integrate or differentiate.</li>\n<li>Distance is not displacement; speed is not velocity. Round trip: displacement = 0.</li>\n<li>v = 0 at the top does not mean a = 0.</li>\n<li>Convert units first: km/h to m s⁻¹ divide by 3.6 (72 km/h = 20 m s⁻¹).</li>\n<li>Quadratic in t gives two roots; reject the negative or physically impossible one.</li>\n<li>Use g = 9.8 unless told otherwise. If the options look like g = 10 was used (for example 20 m instead of 20.4 m), pick the nearest.</li>\n<li>Slide note: the module's text \"v² = u² + 2as\" and its other equations are correct, but its example gives the maximum as 5.10 m before adding the starting height of 15 m. Read the question for \"above the ground\" versus \"above the launch point\".</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"title":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Mechanics</b> is the study of the motion of objects and of the forces acting on them. <b>Newtonian mechanics</b> (classical mechanics) is the application of Newton's laws of motion (together with the law of gravitation) to bodies that are much larger than atoms and move much slower than light.</p>\n<p><b>Motion</b> is the change in position (or direction) of an object with time. The quantities commonly used to describe it are <b>distance, displacement, speed, velocity, acceleration and time</b>. Distance and displacement both describe a change in position: distance is the total path length (a scalar), displacement is the straight-line change in position from start to finish (a vector).</p>\n<p>Different objects move in different ways: some along straight lines, some along curves, some spinning, some vibrating. This leads to the classification of motion in the next section.</p>\n<p><b>Exam tip:</b> the three definitions the module wants word-for-word are those of mechanics (motion + forces), Newtonian mechanics (application of Newton's laws) and motion (change in position or direction with time).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The module lists three broad types: <b>linear, rotary and oscillatory</b>.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Rectilinear (linear, straight path)</td><td>Particles move from one point to another along a straight line</td><td>A car on a straight road, a train on a straight track, a stone falling freely</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Curvilinear (linear, curved path)</td><td>Particles move from one point to another along a curved path</td><td>A projectile, a car going round a bend, a planet in orbit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rotary</td><td>The body rotates about its own axis</td><td>The Earth spinning on its axis, the wheels of a moving vehicle, a ceiling fan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Oscillatory</td><td>To-and-fro motion about a mean (equilibrium) position</td><td>A pushed swing, a simple pendulum bob, guitar strings, a mass on a spring</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><b>Combined motion.</b> Real objects often do two things at once. A rolling wheel translates (linear motion of its centre) and rotates about its axis. The Earth rotates on its axis and also moves along a curved path round the Sun.</p>\n<p><b>Common mistakes.</b> (1) Calling a pendulum \"rotary\" because it swings: it is oscillatory, because it goes to and fro about a mean position. (2) Forgetting that \"linear motion\" in the module covers both straight (rectilinear) and curved (curvilinear) paths. (3) Confusing rotation (about an axis through the body) with revolution (going round an external point, e.g. Earth round the Sun).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>An object of ordinary size is called a <b>macroscopic system</b>: it contains a huge number of molecules (of order 10²³ or more). We cannot follow every molecule, so we describe such a body through collective quantities: total mass, centre of mass, total momentum, total energy.</p>\n<p>A <b>system of particles</b> is a collection of objects that have mass, charge and other properties and that may interact with one another. Examples named in the module: <b>atoms and molecules, galaxies, the solar system</b> (also a gas in a container, a spinning wheel, two colliding trolleys).</p>\n<p>Forces on a particle in a system are of two kinds:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Internal forces</b>: exerted by other particles of the system. They occur in equal and opposite pairs (Newton's third law).</li><li><b>External forces</b>: exerted by agents outside the system (gravity from the Earth on a two-ball system, a push by hand).</li></ul>"},{"type":"html","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Exchange with surroundings</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Isolated</b></td><td>No external interaction: no matter and no energy exchanged</td><td>Idealised: a sealed, perfectly insulated flask; the universe</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Closed</b></td><td>No exchange of matter, but energy can be exchanged</td><td>A sealed pot being heated; the Earth (receives sunlight, matter exchange negligible)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Open</b></td><td>Both matter and energy can be exchanged</td><td>An open pot of boiling water; a living cell or a human body</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><b>Slide-wording note.</b> The module uses the thermodynamic meaning of \"closed\". In some mechanics texts \"closed system\" means \"no net external force\", which is the condition used in momentum conservation. Follow the module definition in the exam.</p>\n<p><b>Laws governing systems</b> (module list): Newton's laws of motion; the law of conservation of momentum; the law of conservation of energy; the law of conservation of angular momentum. Ohm's law, Hooke's law and similar are specific force or circuit laws, not the general principles of systems.</p>\n<p>Conservation of linear momentum applies when the net external force on the system is zero; this is exactly the condition under which the centre of mass moves with constant velocity (section 7).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The <b>centre of mass (COM)</b> is the point at which the whole mass of a body or system may be considered concentrated for the purpose of analysing its translational motion. Equivalent wording in the module: the point about which the total mass is evenly distributed.</p>\n<p><b>Definition by formula.</b> For n particles with masses m₁, m₂, ..., mₙ at position vectors r₁, r₂, ..., rₙ:</p>\n<p><code>r_cm = (m₁r₁ + m₂r₂ + ... + mₙrₙ) / (m₁ + m₂ + ... + mₙ) = Σ mᵢrᵢ / M</code></p>\n<p>where M = Σ mᵢ is the total mass. The module's wording \"sum of weights of the position vectors\" means mass-weighted position vectors; the correct statement is that r_cm is the mass-weighted average of the positions, not that the sum equals the total mass.</p>\n<p><b>In components</b> (1D, 2D, 3D):</p>\n<ul><li>1D: x_cm = Σ mᵢxᵢ / M</li><li>2D: x_cm = Σ mᵢxᵢ / M, y_cm = Σ mᵢyᵢ / M</li><li>3D: add z_cm = Σ mᵢzᵢ / M</li></ul>\n<p><b>Continuous bodies:</b> x_cm = (1/M) ∫ x dm, with dm = λ dx for a rod of linear density λ.</p>\n<p><b>Key properties.</b></p>\n<ul><li>The COM lies closer to the heavier particle. For two particles, distances from the COM satisfy m₁d₁ = m₂d₂.</li><li>The COM position depends on the origin chosen, but its position relative to the particles does not.</li><li>The COM need not lie inside the body (a ring, a boomerang, a horseshoe).</li><li>Units of position: metre; dimension [L]. Equal masses: COM is the simple midpoint.</li></ul>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Symmetry rule.</b> A uniform body's COM lies on every axis or plane of symmetry. Hence: uniform rod, its midpoint; rectangle or square, the intersection of diagonals; circular disc, ring or sphere, the geometric centre; triangular lamina, the centroid, one third of the height above the base.</p>\n<p><b>Trick for composite bodies.</b> Replace each uniform part by a point mass at its own COM, then apply the formula. For uniform bodies of the same material and thickness, mass is proportional to length (rods) or area (laminas).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 1 (two particles).</b> A 2 kg mass is at x = 0 and a 6 kg mass at x = 4 m. x_cm = (2×0 + 6×4)/(2 + 6) = 24/8 = <b>3 m</b>, which is 1 m from the heavy mass and 3 m from the light one (6×1 = 2×3).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 2 (2D).</b> Masses 1 kg at (0, 0), 2 kg at (3, 0) and 3 kg at (0, 4), in metres. M = 6 kg. x_cm = (0 + 6 + 0)/6 = 1 m; y_cm = (0 + 0 + 12)/6 = 2 m. COM = <b>(1 m, 2 m)</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 3 (L-shape).</b> Two uniform rods of the same material, one 4 m along the x-axis and one 2 m along the y-axis, joined at the origin. Masses are proportional to length: 4 units and 2 units. Rod 1 has its COM at (2, 0), rod 2 at (0, 1). x_cm = (4×2 + 2×0)/6 = 1.33 m; y_cm = (4×0 + 2×1)/6 = 0.33 m. COM = <b>(1.33 m, 0.33 m)</b>. Note that the COM lies off the metal itself, in the corner of the L.</p>\n<p><b>Rod with a load.</b> A uniform 1 m rod of mass 0.4 kg carries a 0.1 kg lump at one end. Measuring from the lump: x_cm = (0.4×0.5 + 0.1×0)/0.5 = 0.4 m.</p>\n<p><b>Non-uniform rod (calculus).</b> If λ = kx on 0 ≤ x ≤ L: M = kL²/2 and ∫x dm = kL³/3, so x_cm = 2L/3, beyond the midpoint because the far end is heavier.</p>\n<p><b>Hole in a disc.</b> A disc of radius R with a circular hole of radius R/2 whose centre is R/2 from the disc centre: treat the hole as negative mass. Area fraction of hole = 1/4. Shift of COM = −(1/4)(R/2)/(3/4) = −R/6, i.e. R/6 away from the hole.</p>\n<p><b>Earth-Moon.</b> m_E = 5.97×10²⁴ kg, m_M = 7.35×10²² kg, separation 3.84×10⁸ m: the COM is 3.84×10⁸ × 7.35×10²² / (6.04×10²⁴) ≈ 4.7×10⁶ m from Earth's centre, inside the Earth (radius 6.4×10⁶ m).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Differentiate r_cm = Σ mᵢrᵢ / M with respect to time:</p>\n<ul><li>v_cm = Σ mᵢvᵢ / M, so total momentum <b>P = M v_cm</b></li><li>a_cm = Σ mᵢaᵢ / M, so <b>M a_cm = Σ Fᵢ = F_ext</b></li></ul>\n<p>The internal forces cancel in pairs (Newton's third law), so only external forces matter. This is the module's statement \"the COM moves as if all external forces act on it (and all the mass were concentrated there)\".</p>\n<p><b>Consequences.</b></p>\n<ul><li>If F_ext = 0, then a_cm = 0: the COM <b>remains at rest if initially at rest</b>, or <b>moves with constant velocity</b> if it was moving. Total momentum is conserved.</li><li>Internal forces (explosions, springs, a person walking, collisions between parts) can never change the motion of the COM.</li><li>A projectile that explodes in flight: the fragments fly apart, but the COM keeps following the original parabola until a fragment hits the ground (gravity is the only external force).</li></ul>\n<p><b>Worked example 4 (explosion).</b> A 6 kg shell at rest bursts into 4 kg and 2 kg parts. The 2 kg piece moves at 12 m/s. Momentum: 0 = 4v + 2×12, so v = −6 m/s: the 4 kg piece moves at 6 m/s in the opposite direction. The COM stays at rest.</p>\n<p><b>Recoil.</b> A 4 kg gun fires a 0.02 kg bullet at 400 m/s: recoil speed = 0.02×400/4 = 2 m/s, opposite to the bullet.</p>\n<p><b>Man in a canoe.</b> A 50 kg man walks 3 m towards the bow of a 100 kg canoe on still water. No external horizontal force, so the COM does not move. If the canoe moves x backwards, the man moves (3 − x) forwards relative to water: 50(3 − x) = 100x, so x = 1 m.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The <b>centre of gravity (CG)</b> is the point where the total weight of a body may be taken to act. It is defined using weights (mᵢg) instead of masses.</p>\n<ul><li>If g is the same at every part of the body (any body of ordinary size near Earth's surface), <b>CG coincides with COM</b>, because g cancels in the weighted average.</li><li>For a very large body in a non-uniform field (a tall mountain, a long structure in orbit), CG and COM differ slightly; the CG is nearer the stronger-field part.</li></ul>\n<p>COM is a property of the mass distribution only; CG depends on the gravitational field as well. In a free-fall or weightless environment the CG has no meaning, but the COM does.</p>\n<p><b>Why it matters:</b> a body topples when the vertical line through its CG falls outside its base. This is why low, wide objects are stable, and why a tightrope walker keeps the pole to lower the system's COM.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Scientists.</b> Aristotle (early ideas of motion); Galileo Galilei (inertia, falling bodies, kinematics); Isaac Newton (three laws of motion and universal gravitation, Principia 1687); Archimedes (centre of gravity and the lever); Johannes Kepler (planetary motion); Leonhard Euler and Joseph-Louis Lagrange (formal mechanics of rigid bodies and systems of particles); Christiaan Huygens (collisions and the pendulum).</p>\n<p><b>Applications of COM and systems of particles.</b></p>\n<ul><li>High jump (the Fosbury flop): the athlete's COM can pass under the bar while the body goes over it.</li><li>Vehicle and ship design: low COM gives stability against rollover.</li><li>Rocket and recoil problems: total momentum, hence COM velocity, is unchanged by the engine's internal action.</li><li>Astronomy: stars and planets orbit their common COM (barycentre); the Sun wobbles about the solar-system COM. Exoplanets are detected this way.</li><li>Molecules: rotation and vibration of diatomic molecules (CO, HCl) are analysed about the COM.</li><li>Biomechanics: gait analysis and balance in standing, walking and posture, relevant to clinical practice.</li></ul>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><b>Mass-weighted, not a simple average.</b> The COM of masses 3 kg and 1 kg at x = 0 and x = 4 m is at 1 m, not 2 m.</li>\n<li><b>Divide by the total mass</b> M, not by the number of particles.</li>\n<li><b>Treat x and y separately.</b> Each coordinate has its own sum.</li>\n<li><b>Use the right origin consistently.</b> Measure every position from the same point and quote the answer from that point.</li>\n<li><b>Signs.</b> Particles on the negative side contribute negative mᵢxᵢ.</li>\n<li><b>Closer to the heavier mass.</b> Check your answer: if it is nearer the lighter body, you slipped.</li>\n<li><b>COM velocity</b> is the mass-weighted average velocity, v_cm = P/M; signs matter for opposite motions.</li>\n<li><b>Only external forces</b> change the COM motion; internal forces (explosion, spring, walking) do not.</li>\n<li><b>Closed vs isolated:</b> isolated = no matter or energy exchange; closed = energy only; open = both.</li>\n<li><b>Fragments after an explosion in flight:</b> the COM, not any fragment, follows the original parabola.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Quick formula sheet.</b> r_cm = Σmᵢrᵢ/M; v_cm = Σmᵢvᵢ/M; P = Mv_cm; M a_cm = F_ext; m₁d₁ = m₂d₂ (two particles).</p>\n<p><b>Source note.</b> The lecture text supplied ended in the middle of the COM section (\"It remains at rest when NO extern...\"). The remainder (constant velocity, explosions, recoil, COM versus CG, scientists and applications beyond the first listed) is standard physics and has been completed here.</p>"}]},{"title":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Newton published the three laws of motion in the <i>Principia Mathematica</i> in <b>1687</b>. They relate the motion of a body to the forces acting on it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>First law (law of inertia):</b> a body stays at rest, or keeps moving with uniform velocity in a straight line, unless an external (impressed) force acts on it. Inertia is the reluctance of a body to change its state of motion; mass is the measure of inertia.</li>\n<li><b>Second law (law of acceleration / momentum):</b> the rate of change of momentum is proportional to the applied force and takes place in the direction of the force. In symbols F = dp/dt, with p = mv. For constant mass this becomes <b>F = ma</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Third law (action and reaction):</b> if body A exerts a force on body B, then B exerts an equal and opposite force on A: F(AB) = − F(BA). Forces always come in pairs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Key point about the third law:</b> the two forces of a pair act on <b>different bodies</b>, so they never cancel each other. A book resting on a table: its weight (Earth pulls the book) and the table's normal reaction (table pushes the book) both act on the book, but they are NOT an action-reaction pair. The pair of the weight is the book's pull on the Earth; the pair of the normal reaction is the book's push on the table.</p>\n<p><b>Inertia examples (first law):</b> a passenger thrown forward when a bus stops suddenly (the body tries to keep moving); passengers pushed back when a bus starts suddenly (the body tries to stay at rest); dust shaken out of a carpet; a coin dropping into a glass when the card beneath it is flicked away; seat belts.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Momentum</b> p = mv is a vector with the direction of the velocity. Unit: kg m s⁻¹ (same as N s). Dimensions [M L T⁻¹].</p>\n<p><b>Second law in full form:</b> F = dp/dt = d(mv)/dt. If mass is constant, F = m dv/dt = ma. The net (resultant) force is the vector sum of all forces on the body. If net force is zero, a = 0 and velocity is constant (this is the first law as a special case).</p>\n<p><b>Unit of force:</b> the newton, 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻². Dimensions of force [M L T⁻²]. In the CGS system the unit is the dyne (1 N = 10⁵ dyne).</p>\n<p><b>Mass from the second law:</b> m = F/a, the ratio of force to acceleration. Mass is a scalar and does not change with place.</p>\n<p><b>Weight vs mass.</b> Mass is the amount of matter (kg, scalar, same everywhere). Weight is the gravitational force on the body: W = mg (newton, vector, points down, depends on g). On the Moon, g is about 1.6 m/s², so your mass is unchanged but your weight is about one sixth.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>These describe straight-line motion. They are valid ONLY when acceleration is constant.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Equation</th><th>Missing variable</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>v = u + at</td><td>s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>s = ut + ½at²</td><td>v</td></tr>\n<tr><td>v² = u² + 2as</td><td>t</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>u = initial velocity, v = final velocity, a = acceleration, t = time, s = displacement. Choose a positive direction and give signs to u, v, a, s (a body slowing down has a negative a if u is positive).</p>\n<p><b>Link to the second law:</b> find a from a = F/m, then use these equations for v, s or t.</p>\n<p><b>Lecture question: displacement proportional to t².</b> If s = kt² then v = ds/dt = 2kt, which grows steadily with time, so the object moves with <b>increasing velocity</b>. The statement is TRUE (the acceleration a = 2k is constant).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> A 1000 kg car is pushed by a net force of 3000 N from rest. a = 3000/1000 = 3 m/s². After 10 s: v = 0 + 3×10 = 30 m/s and s = ½×3×10² = 150 m.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Definition:</b> impulse J of a force F acting for a time Δt is J = FΔt (for a constant force). For a varying force J = ∫F dt, which is the <b>area under the force-time graph</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Unit:</b> N s, equal to kg m s⁻¹. Dimensions [M L T⁻¹], same as momentum.</p>\n<p><b>Impulse-momentum theorem:</b> from F = dp/dt, integrating over the time of action gives J = FΔt = Δp = mv − mu. The impulse of the net force equals the change in momentum.</p>\n<p><b>Average force in a collision:</b> F(avg) = Δp/Δt. For the same change in momentum, a longer contact time gives a smaller force. This is why airbags, crumple zones, cricket-catching with the hands drawn back and landing with bent knees reduce injury.</p>\n<p><b>Food for thought: is force always an impulse?</b> No. They have different units and dimensions: force is [M L T⁻²], impulse is [M L T⁻¹]. A force becomes an impulse only when multiplied by the time it acts. If a single force acts, its impulse equals the change in momentum; if several forces act, it is the net force whose impulse equals Δp.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (lecture exercise).</b> A 200 g ball moving at 30 m/s hits a wall and returns along the same path at the same speed; contact time 0.2 s. Take the initial direction as positive: Δp = m(−30) − m(30) = 0.2 × (−60) = −12 kg m/s. Force on the ball = −12/0.2 = −60 N, so the ball exerts <b>60 N</b> on the wall (third law). Trap: the velocity <i>reverses</i>, so Δv = 60 m/s, not 0, and not 30 m/s.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Free-body diagram (FBD):</b> draw the body alone as a dot or box and show only the forces acting ON it, as arrows from the body. Choose axes, resolve forces into components, then apply ΣFx = max and ΣFy = may. Do not draw forces the body exerts on others.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Weight W = mg:</b> acts vertically downward through the body.</li>\n<li><b>Normal reaction N (R):</b> contact force exerted by a surface, always <b>perpendicular</b> to the surface. It is not always equal to mg (inclines, lifts, forces at an angle change it).</li>\n<li><b>Tension T:</b> pull transmitted along a rope or string, directed along the rope and away from the body. For a light (massless) string over a smooth pulley, the tension is the same throughout.</li>\n<li><b>Friction:</b> parallel to the surface, opposing relative motion (covered under friction in a later topic; these problems here are frictionless unless stated).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Force at an angle.</b> A force F at angle θ above the horizontal pulling a body on a flat surface has horizontal component F cosθ and vertical component F sinθ. Horizontal: F cosθ = ma (no friction). Vertical: N + F sinθ = mg, so N = mg − F sinθ (the pull lightens the contact).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (Bowen PES exam).</b> A 50 kg box is pulled by a rope at 25° above the horizontal and accelerates at 2 m/s². No friction is stated, so F cos25° = 50 × 2 = 100 N, giving F = 100/cos25° = 110.3 N (about 110.4 N). Note that 100 N is the horizontal component, not the rope force.</p>\n<p>Torque question in the same item: if the force is applied perpendicular to a 1.5 m rope, τ = F × r = 110.3 × 1.5 = 165.5 N m, which matches none of the options. The option with 70.1 N m is what you get from F sin25° × 1.5 (≈ 70.0 N m). The question is flawed; learn the method but expect to choose the nearest option (110.4 N / 70.1 N m).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A body of mass m on a smooth plane inclined at θ to the horizontal. Resolve weight mg into two parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>along the plane (down the slope): <b>mg sinθ</b></li>\n<li>perpendicular to the plane (into it): <b>mg cosθ</b></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Perpendicular to the plane there is no acceleration, so the normal reaction is <b>N = mg cosθ</b>. Along the plane, the only unbalanced force is mg sinθ, so ma = mg sinθ and</p>\n<p><b>a = g sinθ</b> (down the slope).</p>\n<p>The mass cancels: all bodies slide down a smooth slope with the same acceleration. For θ = 0 the acceleration is 0 (flat), for θ = 90° it is g (free fall).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (Tutorial Part A Q15).</b> A 4 kg block on a frictionless 30° incline: a = g sin30° = g/2. With g = 10 m/s², a = 5 m/s²; with g = 9.8 m/s², a = 4.9 m/s². The speed after sliding a distance s from rest is v = √(2as). Trap: using cosθ instead of sinθ gives 8.7 m/s² (g = 10), which is wrong.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A person of mass m stands on the floor of a lift. The scale reading is the normal reaction R. Take upward as positive for acceleration a of the lift:</p>\n<p>R − mg = ma, so <b>R = m(g + a)</b> for upward acceleration and <b>R = m(g − a)</b> for downward acceleration a.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Lift motion</th><th>Reaction R</th><th>Feels</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>At rest or constant velocity</td><td>mg</td><td>normal weight</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Accelerating up (or slowing while going down)</td><td>m(g + a) &gt; mg</td><td>heavier</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Accelerating down (or slowing while going up)</td><td>m(g − a) &lt; mg</td><td>lighter</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Free fall (cable broken, a = g)</td><td>0</td><td>weightless</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> A 60 kg person in a lift accelerating upward at 2 m/s² (g = 9.8): R = 60(9.8 + 2) = 708 N. Accelerating downward at 2 m/s²: R = 60(9.8 − 2) = 468 N. Constant speed: 588 N.</p>\n<p><b>Tutorial Part A Q16</b> (lift moving down with acceleration a): R = m(g − a), which is positive as long as a &lt; g. Only in free fall (a = g) is R = 0. Trap: speed direction does not matter, only the direction of acceleration.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Treat each body separately with its own FBD, write F = ma for each, and use the constraint that connected bodies share the same acceleration magnitude (taut, inextensible string).</p>\n<p><b>Two blocks pulled along a smooth table.</b> Masses m₁ and m₂ joined by a string, pulled by F on m₁: a = F/(m₁ + m₂) for the whole system; tension T = m₂a is the force that accelerates m₂ alone.</p>\n<p><b>Atwood machine</b> (masses m₁ &gt; m₂ over a light smooth pulley):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>m₁: m₁g − T = m₁a</li>\n<li>m₂: T − m₂g = m₂a</li>\n<li>Adding: <b>a = (m₁ − m₂)g/(m₁ + m₂)</b></li>\n<li>Substituting: <b>T = 2m₁m₂g/(m₁ + m₂)</b></li>\n</ul>\n<p>If m₁ = m₂ then a = 0 and T = mg. T always lies between m₂g and m₁g.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> m₁ = 5 kg, m₂ = 3 kg, g = 9.8: a = 2 × 9.8/8 = 2.45 m/s²; T = 2 × 5 × 3 × 9.8/8 = 36.75 N. Check: 5×9.8 − 36.75 = 12.25 = 5 × 2.45.</p>\n<p><b>Mass on a table joined to a hanging mass</b> (smooth table): hanging mass m₂ drives the system, a = m₂g/(m₁ + m₂), T = m₁a.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Forgetting that the net force, not a single force, equals ma. Subtract opposing forces first (Part A Q13: 20 − 5 = 15 N, a = 15/5 = 3 m/s²).</li>\n<li>Calling weight and normal reaction an action-reaction pair. They act on the same body.</li>\n<li>Mixing mass (kg) and weight (N). Convert grams to kg (200 g = 0.2 kg).</li>\n<li>Impulse sign: when a ball rebounds, Δp = m(v − u) with v and u opposite in sign, so Δv is the SUM of the speeds.</li>\n<li>Using the equations of motion when acceleration is not constant.</li>\n<li>Sine/cosine slip on inclines: along the slope is sinθ, perpendicular is cosθ.</li>\n<li>In lifts, choose R = m(g + a) if accelerating up, m(g − a) if accelerating down.</li>\n<li>F = ma at a given moment: Part A Q12 asks for the force that gives 10 m/s in 5 s from rest; a = 10/5 = 2 m/s², F = 5 × 2 = 10 N.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Quick recall:</b> Principia 1687; 1 N = kg m s⁻²; impulse unit N s = kg m s⁻¹; the area under an F-t graph is impulse; free fall means R = 0; incline a = g sinθ; Atwood a = (m₁ − m₂)g/(m₁ + m₂).</p>"}]},{"title":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Definition.</b> When a constant force F moves its point of application through a displacement d, the work done is the scalar (dot) product <b>W = F · d = F d cosθ</b>, where θ is the angle between the force and the displacement. Only the component of force along the displacement, F cosθ, does work.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>θ = 0°: W = Fd (maximum, positive). θ = 90°: W = 0. θ = 180°: W = −Fd (negative).</li>\n<li><b>Unit:</b> joule, 1 J = 1 N m = 1 kg m² s⁻². <b>Dimensions:</b> [M L² T⁻²]. Work is a scalar but can be positive or negative.</li>\n<li>A force perpendicular to the motion does no work: the centripetal force in circular motion, the normal reaction on a horizontal surface, a porter carrying a case at constant height.</li>\n<li>Friction opposes motion, so the work it does is negative: W = −f d.</li>\n<li>Variable force: W = ∫F dx, the area under the force-displacement graph. For a spring this gives ½kx².</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Worked example 1.</b> A 50 N force acts at 60° above the horizontal on a trolley that moves 4 m horizontally. W = 50 × 4 × cos 60° = 50 × 4 × 0.5 = <b>100 J</b>. Trap: using sin 60° or omitting cosθ.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Kinetic energy</b> is the energy of motion: <b>KE = ½mv²</b>. It is a scalar and never negative. Doubling the speed multiplies KE by 4; doubling the mass only doubles KE.</p>\n<p><b>Work-energy theorem (principle).</b> The work done by the resultant external force on a body equals the change in its kinetic energy:</p>\n<p><b>W_net = ΔKE = ½mv² − ½mu²</b></p>\n<p>Derivation for constant force: v² = u² + 2as, multiply by m/2 to get ½mv² − ½mu² = (ma)s = Fs. Positive net work raises KE (speeds up), negative net work lowers it (slows down). Note that KE depends on v², so ΔKE = ½m(v² − u²), never ½m(v − u)².</p>\n<p>The Bowen tutorials name this the <i>Work-Energy Theorem</i> or <i>Work-Energy Principle</i>.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 2 (skidding car).</b> A car at 20 m/s skids to rest on level road with μk = 0.5 (g = 10 m/s²). Friction does the work: −μk m g d = 0 − ½mv². So d = v²/(2μk g) = 400/(2 × 0.5 × 10) = <b>40 m</b>. The mass cancels.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Potential energy</b> is energy stored because of position or configuration.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Gravitational PE:</b> U = mgh (near the Earth's surface, h measured from a chosen reference level). Only the change ΔU = mgΔh matters, and it depends on vertical height difference alone, not on the path.</li>\n<li><b>Elastic PE of a spring:</b> U = ½kx², where k is the force constant (N/m) and x the extension or compression from natural length. The work to stretch a spring from x₁ to x₂ is ½k(x₂² − x₁²).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Mechanical energy</b> E = KE + PE. Kinetic and potential energy are the two types of mechanical energy (Tutorial Q45). When only conservative forces (gravity, spring force) do work, E is constant:</p>\n<p><b>½mu² + mgh₁ = ½mv² + mgh₂.</b> If friction or other non-conservative forces act, mechanical energy is converted into heat and E decreases by the work done against them.</p>\n<p>Examples: a stone dropped from height h reaches v = √(2gh); a pendulum bob released from height h has v = √(2gh) at the bottom; a spring of constant k compressed by x launches mass m at v = x√(k/m).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 3.</b> A spring (k = 400 N/m) is compressed 0.10 m and launches a 0.20 kg block on a frictionless surface. ½kx² = ½mv², so v = 0.10 × √(400/0.20) = 0.10 × 44.7 = <b>4.47 m/s</b>.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Power</b> is the rate of doing work: <b>P = W/t</b> (average power), or instantaneous P = dW/dt = <b>F · v = F v cosθ</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Unit:</b> watt, 1 W = 1 J s⁻¹. Dimensions [M L² T⁻³]. 1 kW = 1000 W. 1 horsepower ≈ 746 W.</li>\n<li>Energy can be quoted in kilowatt-hours: 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J.</li>\n<li>At constant power, P = Fv means force is inversely proportional to speed: doubling the speed halves the driving force.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Examples.</b> A motor lifting 60 kg through 10 m in 20 s at constant speed (g = 9.8): P = 60 × 9.8 × 10/20 = 294 W. A car with driving force 2000 N at 15 m/s: P = 2000 × 15 = 30 kW.</p>\n<p><b>Common slip:</b> dividing F by v instead of multiplying, or forgetting the time in the first formula.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Friction</b> is the force that opposes relative motion (or attempted motion) between two surfaces in contact. It acts along the surface, opposite to the (tendency of) relative motion.</p>\n<p><b>Two types</b> (Tutorial Q5): <b>static</b> friction, between surfaces at rest relative to each other, and <b>kinetic (dynamic)</b> friction, between sliding surfaces.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Static friction is self-adjusting.</b> It equals the applied force until a maximum is reached. That maximum is the <b>limiting friction</b> F. A block with limiting friction 20 N pushed with 12 N feels friction of 12 N, not 20 N.</li>\n<li><b>Coefficient of friction:</b> <b>μ = F/R</b>, the ratio of limiting friction force F to the normal reaction R (Tutorial Q41). It has no unit. So F = μR. On a flat floor R = mg, so F = μmg.</li>\n<li>Kinetic friction f = μk R, with μk usually a little smaller than the static coefficient μs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Laws of friction.</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Friction opposes the relative motion between the surfaces.</li>\n<li>Its limiting value is proportional to the normal reaction (F ∝ R).</li>\n<li>It depends on the nature (roughness, material) of the surfaces, through μ.</li>\n<li><b>It is independent of the area of contact</b> (for a given normal reaction, Tutorial Q40).</li>\n<li>Kinetic friction is, to a good approximation, independent of the relative speed. So \"friction depends on relative velocity\" is the false statement in Tutorial Q22.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> Take g = 9.8. A 10 kg block on a floor with μ = 0.4 has R = 98 N, so limiting friction F = 0.4 × 98 = <b>39.2 N</b>. Trap: dividing by μ, or forgetting R = mg.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Combine the normal reaction R and the friction force F into one <b>resultant (total) reaction</b> S. The angle S makes with the normal reaction is the <b>angle of friction λ</b> (Tutorial Q17). At limiting friction:</p>\n<p>tan λ = F/R = <b>μ</b>, so λ = tan⁻¹ μ.</p>\n<p><b>Rough inclined plane (angle θ).</b> Resolve the weight mg: component down the slope mg sinθ, component into the slope mg cosθ, so R = mg cosθ.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>At rest:</b> friction F = mg sinθ, and the body stays put while tanθ ≤ μ, i.e. θ ≤ λ.</li>\n<li><b>Limiting equilibrium:</b> θ = λ, so tanθ = μ. Measuring the tilt at which a block just slips gives μ = tanθ (not sinθ or cosθ).</li>\n<li><b>Sliding down with kinetic friction:</b> a = g(sinθ − μk cosθ).</li>\n<li><b>Pushing up at constant speed:</b> force needed = mg(sinθ + μ cosθ).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Worked example 4.</b> Take g = 10. A block slides down a 30° incline with μk = 0.2: a = 10(0.5 − 0.2 × 0.866) = 10 × 0.3268 = <b>3.27 m/s²</b>. A plank tilted to 25° just makes a block slip: μ = tan 25° = 0.47. If μ = 0.75, λ = tan⁻¹ 0.75 = 36.9°.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Moment of a force</b> about a point (Tutorial Q19, Q29) is the product of the force and the <b>perpendicular distance</b> from the point to the line of action of the force: <b>M = F × d⊥ = F r sinθ</b>, where θ is the angle between r and F. Unit: N m (do not confuse with joule, although the dimensions [M L² T⁻²] match). The moment is zero if the line of action passes through the point.</p>\n<p><b>Sign convention</b> (Tutorial Q11): <b>anticlockwise moments are positive, clockwise moments are negative</b>. There are two types of moment, clockwise and anticlockwise (Tutorial Q46, Part B Q6). For several forces, add the moments, each with its sign, to get the algebraic sum.</p>\n<p><b>Torque as a vector.</b> In three dimensions torque is τ = r × F, with magnitude rF sinθ, and direction perpendicular to the plane of r and F (right-hand rule). In planar problems the axis direction is fixed, so a signed scalar is enough. This is why Tutorial Q28 is ambiguous: statement (c) \"the moment is a vector\" and (d) \"the moment is a scalar\" are both defensible, depending on whether you treat the moment as a signed scalar about an axis or as a full torque. The choice made here follows the module's signed-scalar usage.</p>\n<p><b>Couple.</b> Two equal, opposite, parallel forces that do not act along the same line. Resultant force is <b>zero</b>, but the turning effect is not: <b>moment of couple = F × perpendicular separation</b>, and it is the same about every point. A couple produces pure rotation, no translation. Examples: turning a tap or steering wheel, a compass needle in a field.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> 15 N forces 0.4 m apart: moment = 15 × 0.4 = 6 N m. A 40 N force at 30° to a 0.25 m spanner: 40 × 0.25 × sin 30° = 5 N m.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A rigid body is in equilibrium when it is in both <b>translational</b> and <b>rotational</b> equilibrium (Tutorial Q3, Q37, answer \"Translational and Rotation\").</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Translational equilibrium</b> (zero linear acceleration): ΣF = 0, that is ΣFx = 0 and ΣFy = 0 (Tutorial Q31).</li>\n<li><b>Rotational equilibrium</b> (zero angular acceleration): Στ = 0 about any axis (Tutorial Q32).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Principle of moments.</b> For a body in rotational equilibrium, the sum of the clockwise moments about any point equals the sum of the anticlockwise moments about that point. Choose the pivot at an unknown force to eliminate it.</p>\n<p><b>Problem method:</b> (1) draw all forces; (2) write ΣFy = 0 and ΣFx = 0; (3) take moments about a convenient point; (4) solve.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example 5 (beam).</b> A uniform 6 m beam of weight 200 N rests on supports at its ends. A 300 N load sits 2 m from the left end. Moments about the left end: R_R × 6 = 200 × 3 + 300 × 2 = 1200, so R_R = <b>200 N</b>. Then R_L = 500 − 200 = 300 N. See-saw check: 40 kg at 2.0 m balances 50 kg at d = 40 × 2/50 = 1.6 m.</p>\n<p><b>Rope example:</b> a 100 N weight hung from two ropes each at 30° to the horizontal: 2T sin 30° = 100, so T = 100 N (each rope's tension equals the weight, a surprise to many).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Like parallel forces</b> (same direction) have a resultant equal to their sum, acting between them, closer to the larger force. <b>Anti-parallel forces</b> (opposite directions) behave differently:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Equal anti-parallel forces</b> (not collinear) form a couple: <b>ΣF = 0</b> but a non-zero turning effect. This is the reading chosen for Tutorial Q30 (\"The net force of anti-parallel body is ...\" with options ΣF &gt; 0, ΣF &lt; 0, ΣF = 0, ΣF ≠ 0). The stem is garbled in the source; for <i>unequal</i> anti-parallel forces ΣF ≠ 0, so the question is genuinely ambiguous.</li>\n<li><b>Unequal anti-parallel forces</b> F₁ &gt; F₂: resultant R = F₁ − F₂, in the direction of the larger force, acting <b>outside</b> the pair on the side of the larger force, at a distance from F₁ given by F₁ x = F₂ (x + L), where L is the separation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> 30 N and 10 N anti-parallel, 2 m apart. Resultant 20 N. Moments about its line: 30x = 10(x + 2), so x = 1 m from the 30 N force, on the side away from the 10 N force.</p>\n<p><b>Check:</b> as F₂ → F₁, the resultant → 0 and x → ∞, which is the couple limit.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Centre of gravity (CG)</b> is the point through which the whole weight of a body acts. For a uniform body it is at the geometric centre. For point masses on a line, x = Σmx/Σm (e.g. 2 kg at 0 and 6 kg at 4 m gives 24/8 = 3 m).</p>\n<p><b>Three kinds of equilibrium</b> (for a body displaced slightly):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Stable:</b> the body returns; CG rises on displacement, PE is a minimum (ball in a bowl, cone on its base).</li>\n<li><b>Unstable:</b> the body moves farther away; CG falls, PE is a maximum (cone on its tip, ball on top of a hill).</li>\n<li><b>Neutral:</b> the body stays in its new position; CG height unchanged (ball on a flat table, cone on its side).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Stability</b> is the ability of an object to return to its original position after being tilted slightly (Part B Q5). It is increased by a <b>low CG</b> and a <b>wide base</b>. A body topples when the vertical line through the CG falls outside the base.</p>\n<p><b>Exam tips and common mistakes</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Work-energy: square each speed separately. Free fall from h: v = √(2gh), not 2gh.</li>\n<li>μ = F/R, not R/F; tan λ = μ; slip angle: μ = tanθ.</li>\n<li>Friction is independent of contact area and (roughly) of speed, and static friction can be anything from 0 up to μR.</li>\n<li>Couple: ΣF = 0 but Στ ≠ 0, so it is NOT in equilibrium.</li>\n<li>Moments need the <i>perpendicular</i> distance; anticlockwise is positive.</li>\n<li>Check the unit prefix: kJ vs J, kW vs W.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"title":"Conservation Principles","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <b>system</b> is the part of the universe we choose to study; everything else is the <b>surroundings</b>. Conservation laws only make sense once you say exactly what the system is.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Open system:</b> exchanges both matter and energy with its surroundings (a boiling pot without a lid).</li>\n<li><b>Closed system:</b> no matter crosses the boundary, but energy may (a sealed flask being heated). This is the wording of the lecture slide.</li>\n<li><b>Isolated system:</b> neither matter nor energy crosses the boundary, and no net external force or torque acts on it.</li></ul>\n<p><i>Slide wording:</i> the module lists a closed system as \"may exchange energy\" and also as \"isolated from external influences\". Strictly these are two different things. For the conservation laws below, what you need is that <b>no net external force (or torque, or charge flow) acts on the system</b>, which is the isolated case. Use that meaning in exams.</p>\n<p>A <b>conservation law</b> says that a certain measurable quantity of an isolated system does not change as the system evolves in time. The laws in this module are: conservation of <b>linear momentum</b>, <b>mass-energy</b> (energy), <b>electric charge</b> and <b>angular momentum</b>.</p>\n<p><b>The big trap:</b> every conservation law is conditional. Momentum is conserved only if the net external force is zero, angular momentum only if the net external torque is zero, and so on. If something outside the system pushes, drags or twists it, the law does not apply to that system. The fix is often to enlarge the system (ball + Earth) until the outside influence becomes internal.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Linear momentum</b> is the product of mass and velocity: <b>p = mv</b>. It is a vector pointing along the velocity. SI unit: kg m/s (equivalently N s). Dimensions: [M L T⁻¹].</p>\n<p>Newton's second law in its general form is F = Δp/Δt. If the net external force on a system is zero, Δp = 0, so the total momentum is constant:</p>\n<p><b>Law:</b> The total linear momentum of a closed (isolated) system remains constant over time, unless acted on by external forces.</p>\n<p>For two bodies: m₁u₁ + m₂u₂ = m₁v₁ + m₂v₂ (momentum before = momentum after). Being a vector equation it holds separately along each axis. In one dimension, choose a positive direction and give velocities in the opposite direction a minus sign.</p>\n<p>Internal forces (the forces the parts exert on each other) are equal and opposite by Newton's third law, so they cannot change the total momentum. They only redistribute it. Examples: collisions, explosions, billiards, rocket propulsion, football and hockey, and in particle physics, astrophysics and aerospace engineering.</p>\n<p>Momentum is additive: the total is the vector sum of the momenta of all parts.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>In <b>every</b> collision in an isolated system, total momentum is conserved. Collisions differ in what happens to kinetic energy.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Momentum</th><th>Kinetic energy</th><th>After collision</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Elastic</td><td>conserved</td><td>conserved</td><td>bodies bounce apart (e.g. billiard balls, ideal gas molecules)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inelastic</td><td>conserved</td><td>some lost (heat, sound, deformation)</td><td>bodies separate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Perfectly inelastic</td><td>conserved</td><td>maximum possible loss</td><td>bodies stick and move together</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><b>Perfectly inelastic (one dimension):</b> m₁u₁ + m₂u₂ = (m₁ + m₂)v, so v = (m₁u₁ + m₂u₂)/(m₁ + m₂). The kinetic energy lost is ΔK = ½ μ (u₁ − u₂)² with μ = m₁m₂/(m₁ + m₂). Maximum loss does not mean total loss: the joined bodies still move, unless the total momentum was zero.</p>\n<p><b>Elastic (one dimension):</b> using both conservation of momentum and of kinetic energy,</p>\n<p>v₁ = [(m₁ − m₂)u₁ + 2m₂u₂]/(m₁ + m₂), v₂ = [(m₂ − m₁)u₂ + 2m₁u₁]/(m₁ + m₂).</p>\n<p>Handy special cases: equal masses exchange velocities; a body hitting a much heavier body at rest bounces back with nearly the same speed; a heavy body hitting a light one at rest barely slows down. A useful check: in an elastic collision the relative speed of approach equals the relative speed of separation.</p>\n<p><b>Trap:</b> kinetic energy is not conserved in an inelastic collision, but total energy always is (the missing KE became internal energy). Never say \"energy is lost\" without saying where it went.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>An explosion is a collision run backwards: one body splits into pieces by internal forces. Because the internal forces cannot change total momentum, the total momentum just after equals the total just before. If the body was at rest, the vector sum of the fragments' momenta is zero. Kinetic energy is not conserved: chemical or elastic potential energy is converted into KE.</p>\n<p><b>Recoil.</b> A gun (mass M) fires a bullet (mass m) at speed v. Starting from rest, 0 = mv + MV, so the gun recoils with V = −(m/M)v. The heavier body gets the smaller speed, but both have equal and opposite momentum. The same idea explains a boat drifting back when you jump off it, and an astronaut who throws a tool.</p>\n<p><b>Rocket propulsion.</b> The rocket plus its fuel is the system; in space there is no external force. As exhaust gas of mass Δm leaves backward at speed vₑ relative to the rocket, the rocket gains forward momentum. This gives:</p>\n<ul><li>Thrust F = vₑ (dm/dt), the exhaust speed times the mass burned per second.</li>\n<li>Speed gained (no gravity): Δv = vₑ ln(m₀/m), where m₀ is the initial mass and m the final mass.</li></ul>\n<p>A rocket does not push against the air or the ground; it works best in a vacuum. Higher exhaust speed or a larger fuel fraction gives a larger Δv.</p>\n<p><b>Sports and engineering.</b> A hockey puck striking another, a football collision, crash-test design (crumple zones lengthen the collision time to cut the force while the momentum change stays the same), and jet and rocket engines are all applications of momentum conservation.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Energy can be neither created nor destroyed, only converted from one form to another or transferred between bodies. For an isolated system the total energy is constant.</p>\n<p><b>Mechanical energy.</b> E = KE + PE, with KE = ½mv², gravitational PE = mgh, spring PE = ½kx². If only conservative forces do work, mechanical energy is constant: ΔKE + ΔPE = 0.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Falling body</b> (no air resistance): mgh = ½mv², so v = √(2gh), independent of mass. PE falls and KE rises by the same amount.</li>\n<li><b>Simple pendulum:</b> at the highest point the bob is momentarily at rest (PE maximum, KE zero); at the lowest point PE is minimum and KE maximum. If the bob is released from a height h above the lowest point, its speed there is v = √(2gh).</li>\n<li><b>Sliding down a frictionless curve:</b> the speed at the bottom depends only on the height dropped, not on the shape of the path.</li></ul>\n<p>With friction or drag the mechanical energy decreases, but the lost amount appears as thermal energy, so total energy is still conserved.</p>\n<p><b>Mass-energy.</b> The module states that mass and energy are conserved separately in isolated systems at everyday scales, that they can be interconverted, and that the total mass-energy of a closed system stays constant. The interconversion is Einstein's relation <b>E = mc²</b> with c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s. Precisely, only the total mass-energy is conserved: in nuclear reactions a small mass defect appears as a large amount of energy (1 g of mass is about 9 × 10¹³ J). In ordinary mechanics the mass changes are far too tiny to notice, so we treat mass and energy separately.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Electric charge can be neither created nor destroyed; it can only be transferred from one body to another. The total charge of an isolated system stays constant. (The slide says \"transferred or conserved\"; the intended meaning is that the net total is unchanged.)</p>\n<ul><li>Charge is a <b>scalar</b>: it has magnitude and sign but no direction.</li>\n<li>Charge is <b>additive</b>: the total charge is the algebraic sum, keeping signs.</li>\n<li>Three kinds of state: positive (+), negative (−) and neutral (0). A neutral body has equal positive and negative charge, not \"no charges\".</li>\n<li>SI unit: the coulomb (C).</li></ul>\n<p><b>Examples.</b> Rubbing a glass rod with silk moves electrons from the rod to the silk: the rod becomes +q, the silk −q, and the total is still zero. When two identical conducting spheres touch, the total charge is shared equally: +6 μC and −2 μC give a total of +4 μC, so each ends with +2 μC. In pair production or radioactive decay, the total charge before equals the total after.</p>\n<p><b>Trap:</b> do not confuse conservation of charge with the idea that a charged body keeps its charge. A body can gain or lose charge; the isolated system as a whole cannot.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>For a body rotating about a fixed axis, <b>angular momentum</b> is L = Iω, where I is the moment of inertia (kg m²) and ω the angular velocity (rad/s). Unit: kg m²/s. For a point mass in motion, L = r × p. Angular momentum is a <b>vector</b> and is <b>additive</b>.</p>\n<p>The rotational form of Newton's second law is τ = ΔL/Δt. If the net external torque is zero, ΔL = 0:</p>\n<p><b>Law:</b> The total angular momentum of a closed system remains constant unless acted on by an external torque. Therefore I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂.</p>\n<p><b>Examples.</b></p>\n<ul><li><b>Ice skater:</b> pulling the arms in reduces I, so ω must increase; extending them does the reverse.</li>\n<li><b>Diver or gymnast:</b> tucking cuts I and speeds up the somersault; opening out before entry slows it.</li>\n<li><b>Planets:</b> gravity from the Sun acts along the line to the Sun, so it exerts no torque about the Sun. L is constant, so a planet moves faster when closer to the Sun (Kepler's equal areas law).</li>\n<li><b>Turntable and weights:</b> moving the masses inward spins the platform faster.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Energy note.</b> When I drops, ω rises by the same factor, but KE = ½Iω² = L²/(2I) rises, because the skater does work pulling the arms in. Angular momentum is conserved; rotational kinetic energy is not.</p>\n<p><b>Trap:</b> zero net force does not guarantee zero net torque. Two equal and opposite forces at different points (a couple) give no net force, so momentum is conserved, but they do exert a torque, so angular momentum changes.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A force is <b>conservative</b> if:</p>\n<ul><li>the work it does between two points is <b>independent of the path</b> taken;</li>\n<li>the work it does around any <b>closed path</b> is <b>zero</b>;</li>\n<li>the work is <b>reversible</b>: energy given to the potential energy store can be fully recovered.</li></ul>\n<p>These three statements are equivalent. A conservative force has an associated potential energy, and the work it does equals the decrease in that potential energy: W = −ΔPE.</p>\n<p><b>Conservative examples:</b> gravity, the elastic (spring) force, the electrostatic force. The module also lists the electromagnetic force.</p>\n<p><b>Non-conservative examples:</b> kinetic friction, air resistance and drag, viscous forces, and in general any force that turns mechanical energy into heat. Friction's work depends on the path length: sliding a book around a long route on a table does more negative work than going straight. Around a closed loop the work is not zero; it is negative and the mechanical energy decreases.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Conservative</th><th>Non-conservative</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Work vs path</td><td>independent</td><td>depends on path</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Work over closed loop</td><td>zero</td><td>not zero</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mechanical energy</td><td>conserved</td><td>changes (usually falls)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Potential energy</td><td>can be defined</td><td>cannot be defined</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>A <b>work-energy extension</b>: W_nc = ΔKE + ΔPE, where W_nc is the work done by non-conservative forces (negative for friction).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Example 1: Perfectly inelastic collision.</b> A 2 kg trolley moving at 6 m/s hits a 4 kg trolley at rest and they couple. Momentum: 2 × 6 = (2 + 4)v, so v = 2 m/s. Initial KE = ½ × 2 × 6² = 36 J; final KE = ½ × 6 × 2² = 12 J; KE lost = 24 J. Momentum is conserved even though two thirds of the KE is lost.</p>\n<p><b>Example 2: Recoil.</b> A 4.0 kg rifle fires a 0.020 kg bullet at 400 m/s. 0 = 0.020 × 400 + 4.0 × V, so V = −2.0 m/s. The rifle recoils at 2.0 m/s opposite to the bullet.</p>\n<p><b>Example 3: Skater (exam style).</b> I₁ = 3.0 kg m², ω₁ = 2.0 rad/s, I₂ = 1.5 kg m². Then ω₂ = I₁ω₁/I₂ = (3.0 × 2.0)/1.5 = 4.0 rad/s. KE rises from ½(3.0)(2.0)² = 6 J to ½(1.5)(4.0)² = 12 J, so the skater did 6 J of work.</p>\n<p><b>Example 4: Pendulum.</b> A bob is pulled aside so it is 0.45 m above its lowest point and released. Take g = 10 m/s². mgh = ½mv², so v = √(2 × 10 × 0.45) = √9 = 3 m/s at the bottom. The mass cancels.</p>\n<p><b>Example 5: Elastic collision.</b> 1 kg at 6 m/s hits a 2 kg mass at rest, elastically. v₁ = (1 − 2)(6)/3 = −2 m/s; v₂ = 2(1)(6)/3 = 4 m/s. Check momentum: 6 = −2 + 8. Check KE: 18 J before; ½(1)(4) + ½(2)(16) = 18 J after.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul><li><b>Forgetting the condition.</b> If the question mentions friction, a push, gravity acting on part of the system, or a torque from outside, check whether the law applies before using it.</li>\n<li><b>Signs.</b> Momentum is a vector. Pick a positive direction and put minus signs on velocities in the opposite direction (the recoil velocity is negative).</li>\n<li><b>Mixing up the energy statements.</b> Momentum is conserved in all collisions; KE only in elastic ones; total energy always.</li>\n<li><b>Perfectly inelastic does not mean the objects stop.</b> They stick and move with the common velocity v = Σmu/Σm.</li>\n<li><b>Skater questions:</b> L = Iω stays constant, so ω₂ = I₁ω₁/I₂. Smaller I means bigger ω. Do not conserve KE.</li>\n<li><b>Charge is a scalar</b> and adds algebraically; angular momentum is a vector.</li>\n<li><b>Friction is the standard non-conservative force.</b> Gravity, spring and electrostatic forces are conservative.</li>\n<li><b>Units:</b> p in kg m/s, L in kg m²/s, energy in J, charge in C.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Quick method for collision problems:</b> (1) define the system, (2) write momentum before = after with signs, (3) decide whether KE is conserved, (4) solve, (5) check the answer makes physical sense (bodies cannot pass through each other).</p>\n<p><b>Past-paper reminder:</b> \"Which quantity is conserved when no external torque acts?\" is angular momentum, not angular velocity and not moment of inertia.</p>"}]},{"title":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Statement:</b> the force of attraction between two bodies (masses) is directly proportional to the product of the masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.</p><p><b>F = G M₁ M₂ / r²</b></p><ul><li>F = gravitational force (N); M₁, M₂ = the two masses (kg); r = distance between their <i>centres</i> (m).</li><li>G = universal gravitational constant = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻² (the lecture quotes 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹; 6.673 × 10⁻¹¹ also appears).</li><li>The force is always attractive, acts along the line joining the centres, and the two bodies pull on each other with equal and opposite forces (Newton's third law).</li><li>It is a <b>universal</b> force: it acts between any two masses, but is only noticeable when at least one mass is huge, e.g. a planet.</li></ul><p><b>Inverse-square law.</b> F ∝ 1/r². Double the distance and the force falls to 1/4; triple it and the force is 1/9; halve it and the force is 4 times larger. Lecture example: a force of 16 units becomes 16/2² = 4 units when the distance is doubled.</p><p><b>Common mistake:</b> 'directly proportional to the <i>sum</i> of the masses' is wrong, it is the <i>product</i>; and the force <i>decreases</i> as distance increases.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Rearranging F = G M₁M₂/r² gives G = F r² / (M₁ M₂).</p><ul><li><b>Unit:</b> N m² kg⁻² (equivalently m³ kg⁻¹ s⁻²).</li><li><b>Dimensions:</b> [M L T⁻²][L²] / [M²] = <b>[M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²]</b>.</li><li>The dimensions of the gravitational force itself are those of any force: <b>[M L T⁻²]</b>. (Past question: 'dimensional formula for gravitational attraction' = [M L T⁻²].)</li></ul><p>G is tiny, so everyday objects barely attract each other. Worked example (Bowen PES / tutorial): two cars 5 m apart, 1000 kg and 1200 kg:</p><p>F = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 1000 × 1200 / 5² = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 1.2×10⁶ / 25 = 3.2 × 10⁻⁶ N = 3.2 μN.</p><p>For comparison, the electron-proton gravitational force at an atomic separation of 10⁻¹⁰ m is only about 1.0 × 10⁻⁴⁷ N. The lecture slide prints the distance as 1 × 10¹⁰ m, but the printed answer 1.013 × 10⁻⁴⁷ N corresponds to 10⁻¹⁰ m. Gravity is always attractive and here absurdly small, so an exponent of +47 is impossible.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The weight of a mass m at the surface of a planet is the gravitational force on it: mg = GMm/R². The mass m cancels:</p><p><b>g = GM / R²</b> (M = planet's mass, R = its radius).</p><ul><li>Earth: g = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 5.97×10²⁴ / (6.371×10⁶)² ≈ 9.81 m/s².</li><li>Moon: g ≈ 1.62 m/s² (about one-sixth of Earth's).</li></ul><p><b>Variation of g</b></p><ul><li><b>With altitude:</b> at distance r from the centre, g' = GM/r², so g' = g (R/r)². At h = R (r = 2R), g' = g/4. At r = 3R (altitude 2R), g' = g/9. Always measure r from the <i>centre</i>.</li><li><b>With latitude:</b> g is a little larger at the poles (about 9.83 m/s²) than at the equator (about 9.78 m/s²) because the Earth bulges at the equator and its rotation reduces the effective g.</li><li><b>With depth:</b> g decreases below the surface (it is zero at the centre).</li></ul><p><b>Mass vs weight:</b> mass is constant; weight W = mg changes with g. A 70 kg person has W = 70 × 9.8 = 686 N at the surface, matching the worked example: F = 6.673×10⁻¹¹ × 5.98×10²⁴ × 70 / (6.38×10⁶)² ≈ 686 N. The Earth-ball example (0.3 kg, R = 6378 km) gives F ≈ 2.94 N; the Earth-Moon force is about 1.98 × 10²⁰ N.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Gravitational potential energy</b> of mass m at distance r from the centre of mass M (zero at infinity): <b>U = −GMm/r</b>. It is negative because gravity is attractive: work must be done <i>against</i> gravity to carry the mass out to infinity. The familiar mgh is only the small-change approximation near the surface.</p><p><b>Gravitational potential</b> at a point is the potential energy per unit mass: <b>V = U/m = −GM/r</b>.</p><ul><li>SI unit: J kg⁻¹ (= m² s⁻²).</li><li><b>Dimensions: [L² T⁻²]</b> (equivalently [M⁰ L² T⁻²]), the same as velocity². Check: [G][M]/[r] = [M⁻¹L³T⁻²][M]/[L] = [L²T⁻²].</li><li>At the Earth's surface V = −GM/R ≈ −6.25 × 10⁷ J/kg.</li></ul><p><b>Do not confuse:</b> potential V = −GM/r (no test mass), potential energy U = −GMm/r (has m), and field strength g = GM/r² (varies as 1/r²). In the tutorial question 'which equation best describes gravitational potential', pick the one of the form GM/r (magnitude); the minus sign records that the field is attractive.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>For a satellite (or planet) of mass m moving in a circle of radius r around a body of mass M, gravity supplies the centripetal force:</p><p>GMm/r² = mv²/r.</p><p>m cancels, giving the <b>orbital speed v = √(GM/r)</b>. With v = 2πr/T:</p><p><b>T² = 4π² r³ / (GM)</b>, i.e. T = 2π √(r³/GM).</p><ul><li>The orbital speed and period do <b>not</b> depend on the satellite's mass. A heavy and a light satellite at the same radius move identically.</li><li>Larger r means slower speed and longer period (v ∝ 1/√r, T ∝ r^{3/2}).</li><li>r is the distance from the centre of the Earth: r = R + h (R = 6.371 × 10⁶ m, h = altitude).</li><li>There is no separate 'centrifugal' force in the inertial frame: gravity alone is the unbalanced force.</li></ul><p><b>Worked example.</b> Satellite 400 km above the Earth. r = 6.371×10⁶ + 4×10⁵ = 6.771×10⁶ m. v = √(GM/r) = √(3.98×10¹⁴ / 6.771×10⁶) ≈ 7.67 km/s. T = 2πr/v ≈ 5.55×10³ s ≈ 92.5 min, a typical LEO period.</p><p><b>Worked example (Sun's mass).</b> Earth: r = 1.496×10¹¹ m, T = 3.156×10⁷ s. M = 4π²r³/(GT²) ≈ 2.0 × 10³⁰ kg.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Johannes Kepler summarised planetary motion in three laws (wording as in the module):</p><ol><li><b>First law (law of ellipses):</b> the orbit of a planet around the Sun is an ellipse with the Sun at one focus.</li><li><b>Second law (law of equal areas):</b> a line segment joining a planet and the Sun sweeps out equal areas during equal intervals of time, meaning that planets move faster when they are closer to the Sun.</li><li><b>Third law (harmonic law):</b> the square of the orbital period T of a planet is directly proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis r of its orbit: T² ∝ r³.</li></ol><ul><li>Nearest point to the Sun = perihelion (fastest); farthest = aphelion (slowest). At these two points v·r is the same (v_p r_p = v_a r_a), so a comet with r_a = 2 r_p moves twice as fast at perihelion.</li><li>The second law is a consequence of conservation of angular momentum (gravity is a central force).</li><li>The third law is derived above for circular orbits: T² = (4π²/GM) r³, so T²/r³ is the same for every planet orbiting the same star.</li><li>Using Earth's year and AU: T² = r³ with T in years and r in AU. Mars (1.524 AU): T = 1.524^{1.5} ≈ 1.88 years. Jupiter (5.2 AU): T ≈ 11.9 years.</li></ul><p><b>Exam tip:</b> in the past paper all three laws share one option block; match the wording: 'ellipse with the Sun at one focus' = 1st, 'equal areas' = 2nd, 'square of period / cube of semi-major axis' = 3rd.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Types of satellite:</b> <b>natural</b> satellites are moons that orbit planets; <b>artificial</b> satellites are man-made objects placed in orbit (communication, weather monitoring, navigation, imaging). There is no third category such as 'student satellite' in the module's classification.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Orbit</th><th>Altitude</th><th>Period</th><th>Typical uses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Low Earth Orbit (LEO)</td><td>about 160-2,000 km</td><td>short, about 90-120 min</td><td>imaging, communications, scientific research</td></tr><tr><td>Medium Earth Orbit (MEO)</td><td>about 2,000-35,786 km</td><td>longer than LEO (GPS about 12 h)</td><td>navigation (GPS)</td></tr><tr><td>Geostationary (GEO)</td><td>35,786 km above the equator</td><td>one sidereal day (23 h 56 min)</td><td>weather forecasting, telecommunications; appears fixed over one point</td></tr><tr><td>Polar orbit</td><td>usually LEO</td><td>about 100 min</td><td>passes over both poles; as the Earth rotates it covers the whole surface</td></tr><tr><td>Sun-synchronous (SSO)</td><td>near-polar</td><td>about 100 min</td><td>consistent solar lighting for imaging</td></tr><tr><td>GEO-synchronous transfer orbit (GTO)</td><td>elliptical, from low altitude out to 35,786 km</td><td>about 10-16 h</td><td>staging orbit used to reach GEO</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Geostationary needs three things: period equal to Earth's rotation period, orbit over the equator, circular (zero inclination). From T² = 4π²r³/GM with T = 86,164 s, r ≈ 4.22 × 10⁷ m, i.e. altitude ≈ 35,800 km (35,786 km with exact constants).</p><p><b>Trap:</b> telecommunications and weather forecasting are GEO uses, so they are 'not a feature of LEO' in the past question.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Definition:</b> the minimum speed an object must reach to break free from the gravitational influence of a body (planet or moon) without further propulsion, i.e. to reach infinity with zero speed left.</p><p><b>Derivation (energy).</b> Total energy at the surface = ½mv² − GMm/r. To just reach infinity the total energy must be zero: ½mv² = GMm/r, so</p><p><b>v_e = √(2GM/r)</b></p><ul><li><b>Independent of the escaping mass</b>: m cancels (a small rocket and a large spacecraft need the same speed).</li><li><b>Decreases with altitude</b>: r larger means smaller v_e (at r = 2R it is 11.2/√2 ≈ 7.9 km/s).</li><li><b>Comparison with orbital speed at the same radius:</b> v_e = √2 × v_orbit ≈ 1.414 v_orbit. At the Earth's surface v_orbit ≈ 7.9 km/s.</li><li>Direction of launch does not change the required speed (ignoring air and the planet's rotation).</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Body</th><th>Escape velocity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Earth</td><td>11.2 km/s (about 25,000 mph)</td></tr><tr><td>Moon</td><td>2.4 km/s (lecture calculation: 2.38 km/s)</td></tr><tr><td>Mars</td><td>about 5 km/s (5.03 km/s)</td></tr><tr><td>Jupiter</td><td>about 60 km/s</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Worked example (Earth).</b> v_e = √(2 × 6.674×10⁻¹¹ × 5.972×10²⁴ / 6.371×10⁶) = √(1.251×10⁸) ≈ 1.12×10⁴ m/s = 11.2 km/s. Scaling: v_e ∝ √M / √r, so a planet of Earth's radius and twice the mass has v_e = 15.8 km/s; Earth's mass but four times the radius gives 5.6 km/s.</p><p><b>Atmospheres.</b> A body with a low escape velocity (Moon) cannot hold an atmosphere because gas molecules can easily exceed that speed and drift away; Jupiter's high escape velocity lets it keep thick gas layers.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>At 400 km altitude gravity is still about 90% of its value at the surface (g' = 8.7 m/s²), so astronauts are <i>not</i> beyond gravity. They float because the spacecraft and everything in it are in continuous <b>free fall</b> around the Earth, all accelerating towards the Earth at the same rate. The contact force (normal reaction) between astronaut and floor is zero, and 'apparent weight' is that contact force: so they feel weightless.</p><p>Satellites do not fall to Earth because their sideways speed is so large that as they fall the Earth's surface curves away beneath them. Orbit = falling around the Earth.</p><p><b>Trap:</b> 'no gravity in space' is false; the force is the very thing keeping the satellite in orbit.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul><li><b>Squares and metres:</b> square r; convert km to m (6378 km = 6.378 × 10⁶ m). Using km gives an answer 10¹² too large.</li><li><b>r from the centre:</b> for a satellite use r = R + h, not h.</li><li><b>Potential vs potential energy vs field:</b> V = −GM/r (J/kg), U = −GMm/r (J), g = GM/r² (m/s² or N/kg).</li><li><b>Orbital speed</b> √(GM/r) vs <b>escape velocity</b> √(2GM/r): differ by √2; escape velocity is the larger.</li><li><b>Neither speed depends on the satellite's mass.</b></li><li><b>Sign of exponents:</b> G is 10⁻¹¹, so forces between small masses are tiny; check that your answer's power of ten is plausible.</li><li><b>Slide quirks:</b> the lecture slides use 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg in one line and 5.98 × 10²⁴ kg in the next for the Earth-Moon example; both give about 1.98 × 10²⁰ N. The slide's electron-proton distance prints as 1 × 10¹⁰ m but the quoted result needs 10⁻¹⁰ m. The slide's escape velocity formula is v_e = √(2GM/r) (printed only as an image-like fragment in the text).</li><li><b>Quick recall:</b> LEO 160-2,000 km; MEO 2,000-35,786 km; GEO 35,786 km above the equator; v_e(Earth) = 11.2 km/s; v_e = 1.414 × v_orbit.</li></ul>"}]},{"title":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An object moves in <b>circular motion</b> when its path is the circumference of a circle. The module distinguishes two cases.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Uniform circular motion:</b> constant <i>speed</i> around the circle. The velocity is still changing, because its direction changes continuously, so there is always an acceleration, directed towards the centre.</li>\n<li><b>Non-uniform circular motion:</b> the speed also changes. The acceleration then has two parts: a <b>centripetal</b> (radial) part v²/r pointing to the centre, and a <b>tangential</b> part dv/dt along the path.</li></ul>\n<p>Velocity is always <b>tangent</b> to the circle. If the inward force suddenly vanishes (string cut), the object flies off along that tangent in a straight line, by inertia. It does not fly outwards along the radius.</p>\n<p><b>Trap:</b> \"constant speed\" does not mean \"zero acceleration\". Velocity is a vector, so a change of direction is an acceleration.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The <b>radian</b> is the angle subtended at the centre by an arc equal in length to the radius: θ = s/r. It is a ratio of two lengths, so it is dimensionless; \"rad\" is only a label.</p>\n<p>One full turn = 360° = 2π rad = 1 revolution. Conversions:</p>\n<ul><li>radians = degrees × (π/180)</li><li>degrees = radians × (180/π)</li><li>1 rad = 57.3°; 90° = π/2; 180° = π; 45° = π/4</li></ul>\n<p><b>Angular displacement</b> Δθ is the angle through which the object turns, in rad. Arc length s = rθ (θ in radians only).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> 45° in radians: 45 × π/180 = π/4 = 0.785 rad.</p>\n<p><b>Trap:</b> calculators must be in the right mode, and the formulas v = rω and s = rθ only work with radians.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Angular velocity</b> ω is the rate of change of angular displacement:</p>\n<p><b>ω = Δθ/Δt</b> (average). Unit: rad/s. Dimension: [T⁻¹] (radian is dimensionless).</p>\n<ul><li><b>Average angular velocity:</b> over a time interval, Δθ/Δt.</li>\n<li><b>Instantaneous angular velocity:</b> at one moment, ω = lim(Δt→0) Δθ/Δt = dθ/dt.</li>\n<li>ω is a <b>vector</b>: its direction is along the axis of rotation, given by the right-hand rule (curl fingers the way it turns, thumb gives ω). Both the \"average\" and \"instantaneous\" kinds are types of angular velocity.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Linear and angular speed:</b> v = rω. Points farther from the axis move faster linearly, but all points of a rigid rotating body share the same ω.</p>\n<p><b>Period T</b> = time for one revolution (s). <b>Frequency f</b> = revolutions per second (Hz), f = 1/T.</p>\n<p><b>ω = 2π/T = 2πf.</b> From rpm: ω = rpm × 2π/60.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (class exercise).</b> A wheel turns through 30 rad in 5 s. ω = 30/5 = 6 rad/s.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (class exercise).</b> A wheel turns 45° in 22 s. Δθ = π/4 = 0.785 rad, so ω = 0.785/22 = 0.0357 rad/s. <i>Slide note:</i> the slide prints 0.393 rad/s, which is the answer if you divide by 2 s instead of 22 s. The correct value is 0.0357 rad/s.</p>\n<p><b>Angular acceleration</b> α = Δω/Δt (rad/s², dimension [T⁻²]). For constant α: ω = ω₀ + αt, θ = ω₀t + ½αt².</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>An object in circular motion accelerates <b>towards the centre</b>. The magnitude is</p>\n<p><b>a = v²/r = ω²r</b> (since v = rω). It grows with the square of the speed and falls as radius increases (at fixed v).</p>\n<p>By Newton's second law the net inward force is the <b>centripetal force</b>:</p>\n<p><b>F = mv²/r = mω²r</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Centripetal force is not a new kind of force.</b> It is the name for whatever net force points to the centre. Examples: tension (stone on a string), gravity (planets, satellites), friction (car on a flat bend), normal reaction (banked track, loop), or a combination. Never draw it as an extra force on a free-body diagram.</p>\n<p>Direction rule: the acceleration points inward. A statement that centripetal acceleration \"arises from a net force acting tangentially\" is false: a tangential force changes speed, not direction.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (class exercise).</b> A car on a 50 m radius track at 20 m/s: a = 20²/50 = 400/50 = 8 m/s².</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> Wheel ω = 4 rad/s, r = 0.75 m: a = ω²r = 16 × 0.75 = 12 m/s².</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> a = 10 m/s², v = 5 m/s: r = v²/a = 25/10 = 2.5 m.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> 2 kg at r = 5 m, v = 10 m/s: F = 2 × 100/5 = 40 N. (G is not needed; the constant printed in the tutorial question is a distractor.)</p>\n<p><b>Scaling:</b> double v, force ×4. Halve r at fixed v, force ×2.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Flat road:</b> friction supplies the centripetal force. Skidding starts when mv²/r exceeds μmg, so the maximum speed is v = √(μrg), independent of the car's mass.</p>\n<p><b>Banked curve without friction:</b> the road is tilted at angle θ to the horizontal. Only gravity (mg, down) and the normal reaction N (perpendicular to the surface) act.</p>\n<ul><li>Vertical: N cosθ = mg</li><li>Horizontal (towards centre): N sinθ = mv²/r</li></ul>\n<p>Divide: <b>tanθ = v²/(rg)</b>, so v = √(rg tanθ). Mass cancels, so the safe speed is the same for any vehicle.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (class exercise and PES).</b> r = 30 m, θ = 15°, g = 9.8 m/s²: v = √(30 × 9.8 × tan15°) = √(294 × 0.2679) = √78.8 = 8.88 m/s (the exam option reads 8.87 m/s, a truncated value).</p>\n<p><b>Trap:</b> do not use sinθ or cosθ alone; the result is tan. Faster than this speed and the car tends to slide up the bank; slower and it slides down (friction would be needed).</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Torque</b> (moment of force) measures the turning effect of a force about a pivot or axis.</p>\n<p><b>τ = r F sinθ</b>, where r is the distance from pivot to the point of application, F is the force, and θ is the angle between r and F. Equivalently τ = F × (perpendicular distance from the axis to the line of action of F), the <b>moment arm</b> = r sinθ. In vector form <b>τ = r × F</b>, perpendicular to both r and F (a vector).</p>\n<ul><li>Unit: newton metre (N m). Dimension: <b>[ML²T⁻²]</b>, the same as work and energy, though torque is not energy. (Pound-feet is a non-SI unit.)</li>\n<li>Maximum when F ⟂ r (θ = 90°, sinθ = 1). Zero if θ = 0° or 180° (force through the pivot) or r = 0.</li>\n<li>Sign convention: anticlockwise positive, clockwise negative.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Common false statement:</b> \"τ = F × r / sinθ\". The correct expression has sinθ in the numerator (multiplied), not the denominator.</p>\n<p><b>Rotational equilibrium:</b> net torque about any point = 0 (Στ = 0), so zero angular acceleration. A body can be at rest or spinning at constant ω. A rigid body is in full equilibrium when ΣF = 0 (translational) and Στ = 0 (rotational).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (PES).</b> Uniform rod 2 m, pivot at one end, 25 N perpendicular force at the free end: τ = 25 × 2 = 50 N m. (The question asks for the torque of the applied force.)</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> A see-saw balances when m₁ × d₁ = m₂ × d₂: 30 kg at 2 m balances 40 kg at 1.5 m.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Moment of inertia</b> I = Σmr² measures a body's resistance to changes in rotation, the rotational analogue of mass. Unit kg m², dimension [ML²]. It depends on the axis and on how mass is distributed: mass farther from the axis counts as r².</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Body</th><th>Axis</th><th>I</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Point mass</td><td>distance r</td><td>mr²</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thin ring / hoop</td><td>through centre, ⟂ plane</td><td>MR²</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Solid disc / cylinder</td><td>through centre</td><td>½MR²</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Uniform rod, length L</td><td>through centre</td><td>(1/12)ML²</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Uniform rod, length L</td><td>one end</td><td>(1/3)ML²</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Solid sphere</td><td>through centre</td><td>(2/5)MR²</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><b>Parallel-axis idea:</b> I = I_cm + Md², where d is the distance between the parallel axes. Check: rod about its end = (1/12)ML² + M(L/2)² = (1/3)ML².</p>\n<p><b>Newton's second law for rotation: τ = Iα</b> (net torque = moment of inertia × angular acceleration), analogous to F = ma.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> τ = 12 N m on I = 3 kg m²: α = 12/3 = 4 rad/s².</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> Rod M = 3 kg, L = 2 m about one end: I = (1/3)(3)(4) = 4 kg m²; about its centre: 1 kg m².</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Angular momentum</b> is the rotational analogue of linear momentum.</p>\n<ul><li>Rigid body about a fixed axis: <b>L = Iω</b>.</li>\n<li>Particle: <b>L = r × p = r × mv</b>; magnitude L = mvr sinθ (= mvr for circular motion).</li></ul>\n<p>Unit: kg m² s⁻¹ (same as J s). Dimension: [ML²T⁻¹], the same as Planck's constant. It is a vector along the rotation axis.</p>\n<p>Torque and angular momentum are linked by τ = dL/dt, just as F = dp/dt. If I is constant this gives τ = Iα.</p>\n<p><b>Conservation:</b> if the net external torque is zero, L stays constant: I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂. The conserved quantity is angular momentum, not angular velocity.</p>\n<p>Examples: an ice skater pulls in her arms, I decreases, so ω must increase. A diver tucks to spin faster. Planets move faster when nearer the Sun (Kepler's second law).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (PES).</b> Disk ω = 10 rad/s, I = 2 kg m²: L = 2 × 10 = 20 kg m²/s.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> Skater I = 4 kg m² at 3 rad/s pulls in to I = 1 kg m²: ω = 4 × 3/1 = 12 rad/s. (Her kinetic energy rises; she does work pulling her arms in.)</p>\n<p><i>Source note:</i> the lecture text breaks off at the start of this section, so the content above is standard physics.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A rotating rigid body has kinetic energy <b>KE_rot = ½Iω²</b> (joules), the analogue of ½mv². A rolling body has both translational and rotational energy.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Linear</th><th>Rotational</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>displacement s</td><td>angle θ (s = rθ)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>velocity v</td><td>ω (v = rω)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>acceleration a</td><td>α (a_tangential = rα)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>mass m</td><td>moment of inertia I</td></tr>\n<tr><td>force F</td><td>torque τ</td></tr>\n<tr><td>F = ma</td><td>τ = Iα</td></tr>\n<tr><td>momentum p = mv</td><td>L = Iω</td></tr>\n<tr><td>½mv²</td><td>½Iω²</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> I = 2 kg m² at ω = 10 rad/s: KE = ½ × 2 × 100 = 100 J.</p>\n<p><b>Rolling without slipping:</b> v = ωR for the centre of a wheel. A wheel of radius 0.3 m moving at 6 m/s has ω = 6/0.3 = 20 rad/s.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><b>Radians:</b> convert degrees before using v = rω or s = rθ. The PES wheel (45° in 22 s) needs π/4 first.</li>\n<li><b>Squares:</b> a = v²/r and F = mv²/r need v squared; ω²r needs ω squared.</li>\n<li><b>Torque:</b> τ = rF sinθ; the F × r / sinθ form is false. Torque is a vector, unit N m, dimension [ML²T⁻²].</li>\n<li><b>Dimension of angular velocity</b> is [T⁻¹]. \"rad/s\" is the unit of angular velocity (the resit question prints \"rads\", a typo for rad/s).</li>\n<li><b>Centripetal force</b> is a role played by real forces. It is never an additional force, and centripetal acceleration never comes from a tangential force.</li>\n<li><b>Banked curve:</b> tanθ = v²/(rg). Mass cancels.</li>\n<li><b>Class-exercise banked curve:</b> use g = 9.8 m/s². With g = 10 you would get 8.96 m/s and miss the option.</li>\n<li><b>Conservation:</b> L is conserved only if net external torque is zero. When I shrinks, ω grows.</li>\n<li>When a question gives extra data (like G in the 2 kg, 5 m, 10 m/s problem), ignore it if the physics does not need it.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Quick formula sheet:</b> ω = Δθ/Δt; ω = 2πf = 2π/T; v = rω; a = v²/r = ω²r; F = mv²/r; tanθ = v²/(rg); τ = rF sinθ; τ = Iα; L = Iω; KE = ½Iω².</p>"}]},{"title":"Gyroscopes and Precession","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <b>gyroscope</b> is a device built around a heavy, fast-spinning <b>rotor</b> (a disc or wheel) that can turn freely about one or more axes. Its usefulness comes from one property: a rapidly spinning rotor resists any attempt to change the direction of its spin axis, and when that axis is pushed it moves in a regular, predictable way rather than simply falling over.</p>\n<p>The module names three key parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rotor:</b> the spinning disc. It carries the <b>angular momentum</b> that gives the device its stability.</li>\n<li><b>Gimbal:</b> a pivoted support, usually a set of rings, that lets the rotor turn freely about several axes.</li>\n<li><b>Spin axis:</b> the line through the centre of the rotor about which it spins. Its direction is the direction of the angular momentum vector.</li>\n</ul>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>For a rigid body spinning about a fixed axis, the angular momentum is <b>L = Iω</b>, where I is the moment of inertia about the spin axis (kg m²) and ω is the angular velocity (rad s⁻¹). For a uniform disc or solid cylinder of mass M and radius R spinning about its central axis, <b>I = ½MR²</b>, so L = ½MR²ω. The SI unit of L is kg m² s⁻¹.</p>\n<p>Angular momentum is a <b>vector</b>. Its direction lies along the spin axis and follows the right-hand rule: curl the fingers of the right hand in the direction of spin, and the thumb points along L. The rotational form of Newton's second law is <b>τ = dL/dt</b>: the net external torque equals the rate of change of angular momentum. This is the rotational analogue of F = dp/dt.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A gyroscope works because of the <b>conservation of angular momentum</b>. If the net external torque is zero, L is constant in both magnitude and direction. The spin axis therefore keeps pointing the same way in space, however the base carrying it is turned. This is called <b>rigidity in space</b>.</p>\n<p>The gimbal makes this practical. Because the rotor is free to turn inside its rings, rotation of the base does not transmit a torque to the spin axis. The rotor's orientation is set by its own angular momentum, and the gimbal angles show how the base has moved relative to it. This is the principle behind inertial navigation.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Precession</b> is the slow turning of a spin axis about a second axis, caused by an external torque. Consider a rotor whose spin axis lies along a rod pivoted at one end. The centre of mass is a distance r from the pivot. Gravity acts at the centre of mass and exerts a torque <b>τ = rMg</b> about the pivot when the rod is horizontal. This torque is perpendicular to L.</p>\n<p>A naive guess is that the rotor should tip over. It does not. The torque does not tilt the spin axis downward. It changes L by dL = τ dt, in the direction of τ. Since dL is perpendicular to L, the magnitude of L is unchanged but its direction turns. The spin axis therefore moves sideways, in the horizontal plane, while staying at the same height. When the spin axis is inclined, its tip traces out a <b>cone</b> around the vertical instead of falling.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>In a short time dt the change in L is dL = τ dt, perpendicular to L. The angle through which L turns is dφ = dL/L = τ dt / L. Hence the precession angular velocity is</p>\n<p><b>ω<sub>P</sub> = τ/L = rMg/(Iω)</b></p>\n<p>For a general inclination θ between the pivot-to-centre-of-mass line and the vertical, the torque is τ = rMg sinθ, so ω<sub>P</sub> = rMg sinθ/(Iω). Setting θ = 90° (rod horizontal) gives the module's form, rMg/(Iω).</p>\n<p>Validity: the result holds when the rotor is rigid, its spin ω is much greater than ω<sub>P</sub>, and the torque is perpendicular to L. A unit check: (N m)/(kg m² s⁻¹) = s⁻¹, so ω<sub>P</sub> is in rad s⁻¹. <i>Note on the source:</i> the lecture text export garbles the symbols in this formula. The standard form rMg/(Iω) is used throughout this topic.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The precession vector ω<sub>P</sub> points along L × τ, by the right-hand rule. Equivalently, the tip of L moves in the direction of the torque. For example, if L points up (+z) and the torque points along +x, then L turns towards +x and ω<sub>P</sub> points along +y, because z × x = y. Reversing the torque reverses the sense of precession.</p>\n<p>Always find the torque direction first, then the direction in which the spin axis moves. The most common error is to assume the axis moves in the direction of the weight.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Nutation</b> is a small, rapid periodic wobble that can be superimposed on steady precession. It appears when the spin axis is initially tilted or the gyroscope is given a sudden disturbance. The module treats it only briefly. For exams, know its definition and that steady precession is the main effect, with nutation as a small wobble on top of it.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The Earth behaves like a giant spinning top. Its rotation bulges it at the equator, and the Sun and Moon exert gravitational torques on this equatorial bulge. These torques make the rotation axis precess. The axis is tilted about 23.5° from the perpendicular to the orbit, and it traces a cone once every <b>about 26,000 years</b>.</p>\n<p>Consequences: the celestial pole moves across the sky, so the Pole Star changes over millennia. The equinox points also drift westward along the ecliptic by roughly 50 arcseconds per year. This is the <b>precession of the equinoxes</b>. Check: 360° × 3600 ″/° ÷ 26000 yr ≈ 50 arcseconds per year.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><b>Navigation:</b> inertial navigation in aircraft, spacecraft and missiles tracks orientation without an external reference.</li>\n<li><b>Gyrocompass:</b> uses a gyroscope together with the Earth's rotation to find true north, independent of the magnetic field. Used on ships.</li>\n<li><b>Stabilisation:</b> gyrostabilisers reduce the rolling of ships and submarines in rough seas.</li>\n<li><b>Smartphones:</b> most phone gyroscopes are MEMS devices, tiny vibrating elements that sense rotation through the Coriolis effect. They are not spinning rotors. They detect rotation for screen orientation and motion sensing.</li>\n<li><b>Robotics:</b> balance and orientation control.</li>\n<li><b>Tops and bicycles:</b> a spinning top or bicycle wheel shows gyroscopic effects. A torque on the axle makes the spin axis turn sideways rather than tip over.</li>\n</ul>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Example 1 (a top).</b> M = 0.50 kg, r = 0.040 m, I = 1.0×10⁻³ kg m², ω = 100 rad s⁻¹, g = 9.8 m s⁻², spin axis horizontal.</p>\n<p>Torque: τ = rMg = 0.040 × 0.50 × 9.8 = 0.196 N m.<br>Angular momentum: L = Iω = 1.0×10⁻³ × 100 = 0.10 kg m² s⁻¹.<br>Precession: ω<sub>P</sub> = τ/L = 0.196 / 0.10 = <b>1.96 rad s⁻¹</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Example 2 (a uniform disc).</b> M = 2.0 kg, R = 0.10 m, ω = 200 rad s⁻¹, centre of mass r = 0.050 m from the pivot.</p>\n<p>I = ½MR² = ½ × 2.0 × 0.10² = 0.010 kg m².<br>L = Iω = 0.010 × 200 = 2.0 kg m² s⁻¹.<br>τ = rMg = 0.050 × 2.0 × 9.8 = 0.98 N m.<br>ω<sub>P</sub> = τ/L = 0.98 / 2.0 = 0.49 rad s⁻¹.<br>Period of one full precession circle: T = 2π/ω<sub>P</sub> = 2π/0.49 ≈ <b>12.8 s</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Example 3 (tilted lever arm).</b> r = 0.10 m, M = 1.5 kg, and the pivot-to-centre line makes 30° with the vertical. Then τ = rMg sinθ = 0.10 × 1.5 × 9.8 × sin 30° = 1.47 × 0.5 = <b>0.735 N m</b>. The torque is half its horizontal value, because only the component of the lever arm perpendicular to gravity contributes.</p>\n<p><b>Example 4 (Earth).</b> With a 26,000-year precession period and 1 year = 3.16×10⁷ s, ω<sub>P</sub> = 2π/(26000 × 3.16×10⁷ s) ≈ <b>7.6×10⁻¹² rad s⁻¹</b>. The equinox shift is 360° × 3600 ″/° ÷ 26000 ≈ <b>50 arcseconds per year</b>.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Slip</th><th>Correct idea</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Writing ω<sub>P</sub> = τ (forgetting L)</td><td>ω<sub>P</sub> = τ/L. A torque alone does not give the precession rate.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Using rev/min directly as rad/s</td><td>Convert first: rad s⁻¹ = rev/min × 2π/60. 3000 rev/min = 314 rad s⁻¹.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Using MR² for a disc</td><td>For a uniform disc or solid cylinder, I = ½MR².</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thinking faster spin means faster precession</td><td>ω<sub>P</sub> ∝ 1/(Iω). Faster spin gives slower precession.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thinking the gyroscope falls down</td><td>The torque is perpendicular to L, so the spin axis sweeps sideways.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Using cos for the torque angle</td><td>θ is measured from the vertical, and τ = rMg sinθ.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Confusing a perpendicular torque with a parallel one</td><td>A torque perpendicular to L changes its direction. Only a parallel torque changes its magnitude.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Write ω<sub>P</sub> = τ/L first, then substitute τ = rMg and L = Iω.</li>\n<li>Check units: τ in N m, L in kg m² s⁻¹, ω<sub>P</sub> in rad s⁻¹.</li>\n<li>Period T = 2π/ω<sub>P</sub>. A quarter turn takes (π/2)/ω<sub>P</sub>.</li>\n<li>For direction questions, find the torque, then L, then use L × τ.</li>\n<li>Precession is slow compared with spin. The spin ω must be much greater than ω<sub>P</sub> for the formula to apply.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"title":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>This part is supplementary to the Prof. Emetere mechanics syllabus.</b> The lecturer's PHY 101 mechanics course covers motion, forces, energy, gravitation and rotation. The topics here (fluids, pressure, elasticity and basic heat) come from the Bowen class tutorial sheets (Part A Q8 to Q10, Part B Q75) and from the IGSTA past-question bank. Study them alongside the lecture notes, and use them for practice rather than as a replacement for the syllabus.</p><p>Each section gives a definition, the key formula with its SI unit, one or two worked examples, and the traps that the past questions use. Numbers in the worked examples are checked, and g = 9.8 m/s² is used unless a question states otherwise.</p><p><b>Source notes.</b> The IGSTA paper prints a few garbled units (for example 'kgsecVm⁴' for density and 'MlL°T²' for surface-tension dimensions). The correct units are used here. Some IGSTA questions print no answer key, and their keys were worked out and flagged on each question.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <b>fluid</b> is a substance that cannot resist a shear stress at rest. It flows and takes the shape of its container. Liquids and gases are both fluids. A perfect (ideal) fluid is incompressible and has no viscosity.</p><ul><li><b>Density</b>: ρ = m/V, in kg/m³. Water at 4 °C has its maximum density, 1000 kg/m³.</li><li><b>Specific gravity</b>: SG = ρ/ρ<sub>water</sub>. It is a pure number, so mercury has SG 13.6.</li><li><b>Specific weight</b>: weight per unit volume, w = ρg, in N/m³. Water has about 9.8 × 10³ N/m³.</li><li><b>Viscosity</b> (dynamic): the internal friction of a fluid, η, in Pa s = N s m⁻². Liquids become less viscous as they warm; gases become more viscous.</li><li><b>Kinematic viscosity</b>: ν = η/ρ, in m² s⁻¹.</li><li><b>Surface tension</b>: γ = F/L, a force per unit length acting along the surface, in N/m. It is equal to energy per unit area (J/m²).</li><li><b>Cohesion</b>: attraction between like molecules (liquid to liquid). <b>Adhesion</b>: attraction between unlike molecules (liquid to solid). Water wets glass because adhesion beats cohesion; mercury does not wet glass because its cohesion is stronger.</li><li><b>Compressibility</b>: the fractional change in volume per unit pressure change, 1/B. Liquids are very hard to compress, so their bulk modulus B is very large (about 2.2 × 10⁹ Pa for water).</li></ul><p>A fluid at rest cannot sustain shear stress, so the only stress in a static fluid is normal (pressure). The pressure at a point in a static fluid is the same in all directions.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Pressure</b> is force per unit area: P = F/A, in pascals (Pa = N/m²). Other units: 1 kPa = 10³ Pa, 1 atm ≈ 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa.</p><p><b>Pressure at depth</b>: P = ρgh. It depends on the density, g and the depth h only. It does not depend on the width or shape of the container or on the total volume of liquid. Doubling the depth doubles the pressure.</p><p><b>Gauge and absolute pressure</b>: P<sub>absolute</sub> = P<sub>gauge</sub> + P<sub>atm</sub>. A tyre gauge or a manometer reads gauge pressure, so it reads zero when open to the air.</p><p><b>Pascal's principle</b>: a pressure change applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished to every part of the fluid and to the walls. In a hydraulic press, F<sub>1</sub>/A<sub>1</sub> = F<sub>2</sub>/A<sub>2</sub>, so the output force is multiplied by the area ratio.</p><p><b>U-tube manometer</b>: a difference in liquid levels Δh gives a gauge pressure ΔP = ρgΔh. Mercury (ρ = 13 600 kg/m³) gives a large pressure for a small height. The height of a water column needed for the same pressure is 13.6 times the mercury height, because h ∝ 1/ρ.</p><p><b>Worked example 1.</b> Gauge pressure at 2 m depth in fresh water (ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 9.8 m/s²): P = 1000 × 9.8 × 2 = 19 600 Pa = 19.6 kPa. Absolute pressure with P<sub>atm</sub> = 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa: 101 000 + 19 600 = 120 600 Pa = 120.6 kPa.</p><p><b>Units check.</b> Convert 100 mm to 0.100 m before using P = ρgh. Forgetting this gives a pressure 1000 times too small, which is the most common slip on the IGSTA pressure items.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Archimedes' principle</b>: a body wholly or partly immersed in a fluid experiences an upward force (<b>upthrust</b> or buoyant force) equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces: U = ρ<sub>fluid</sub> V<sub>submerged</sub> g.</p><p><b>Flotation</b> is the ability of a body to float. A body floats in equilibrium when the upthrust equals its weight, so the net force is zero. Then:</p><ul><li>weight &gt; upthrust: the body sinks</li><li>weight = upthrust: the body floats (in equilibrium)</li><li>weight &lt; upthrust: the body rises to the surface</li></ul><p>For a floating body, ρ<sub>body</sub> V g = ρ<sub>fluid</sub> V<sub>submerged</sub> g, so the fraction submerged is V<sub>submerged</sub>/V = ρ<sub>body</sub>/ρ<sub>fluid</sub>. A block of density 600 kg/m³ floats with 60% submerged in water.</p><p><b>Apparent weight</b>: W<sub>apparent</sub> = W − U. A 50 N object that reads 30 N when immersed has an upthrust of 20 N.</p><p><b>Worked example 2.</b> A block of volume 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³ is fully submerged in water. U = 1000 × 2.0 × 10⁻³ × 9.8 = 19.6 N.</p><p><b>Trap.</b> The upthrust equals the weight of the displaced fluid, not the weight of the body. The two are equal only when the body floats or hangs in equilibrium.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Elasticity</b> is the ability of a body to return to its original shape once the deforming force is removed, provided the force was not too large. The <b>elastic limit</b> is the largest stress up to which this recovery is complete. Beyond it the body keeps a permanent set.</p><ul><li><b>Stress</b> σ = F/A, in N/m² (Pa).</li><li><b>Strain</b> ε = ΔL/L, a dimensionless ratio. Bulk strain is ΔV/V; shear strain is the angle φ in radians.</li><li><b>Hooke's law</b>: within the elastic limit, stress is proportional to strain. For a wire, F = kx, where k is the force constant.</li></ul><p>The three moduli are the constants of proportionality (stress/strain), in Pa:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Modulus</th><th>Stress</th><th>Strain</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Young's Y</td><td>F/A (tensile)</td><td>ΔL/L</td><td>Y = FL/(AΔL)</td></tr><tr><td>Bulk B</td><td>ΔP</td><td>ΔV/V</td><td>B = −ΔP/(ΔV/V)</td></tr><tr><td>Shear G</td><td>F/A (tangential)</td><td>φ</td><td>G = F/(A φ)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>From Y, the extension of a wire is ΔL = FL/(AY), and its force constant is k = YA/L. The modulus of a material does not depend on its length or radius, but the extension does. Extension is proportional to length and inversely proportional to the area, so ΔL ∝ L/r².</p><p><b>Worked example 3.</b> A wire of length 2 m and area 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² carries 50 N, with Y = 2.0 × 10¹¹ Pa. ΔL = (50 × 2)/(1.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 2.0 × 10¹¹) = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.5 mm.</p><p><b>Scaling rules.</b> Halving the radius of a wire under the same load makes the extension four times bigger. Halving the length halves the extension. Y itself is unchanged by either change.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>Heat</b> is energy transferred between bodies because of a temperature difference. It is measured in joules (J). <b>Temperature</b> measures how hot a body is; it is not an energy. A large body at a low temperature can contain more heat than a small body at a high temperature.</p><p><b>Heat capacity</b>: C = Q/ΔT, in J/K, the heat needed to raise the whole body by 1 K.</p><p><b>Specific heat capacity</b>: c = Q/(mΔT), in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, the heat needed per kilogram per kelvin. The question wording 'raise the temperature of 1 kg by 1°C' means specific heat capacity.</p><p>The heating equation is <b>Q = mcΔT</b>. Water has c = 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, which is high. It is why water is used as a coolant and why coastal climates change slowly.</p><p><b>Calorie.</b> 1 cal = 4.2 J in the module's convention (4.184 J exactly, if you need the precise value). Use 4.2 J for exam calculations unless told otherwise.</p><p><b>Worked example 4.</b> Heat 2 kg of water from 20 °C to 35 °C. Q = 2 × 4200 × 15 = 126 000 J = 126 kJ.</p><p><b>Trap.</b> A rise of 1 K equals a rise of 1 °C, but the Kelvin and Celsius zeros differ. For a temperature difference, the two scales give the same number. For a temperature value, you must convert.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>Heat moves by three mechanisms:</p><ul><li><b>Conduction</b>: energy passes from particle to particle through a solid. The particles vibrate in place and do not travel. Metals conduct best because free electrons carry energy.</li><li><b>Convection</b>: heat is carried by the bulk movement of a liquid or gas. Warmer, less dense fluid rises and cooler fluid sinks. Land and sea breezes are convection.</li><li><b>Radiation</b>: heat is carried by electromagnetic waves. It needs no medium and works in a vacuum. The heat of the Sun reaches the Earth this way.</li></ul><p>The rate of conduction through a plane slab is:</p><p><b>Q/t = kAΔT/d</b></p><p>where k is the thermal conductivity (W m⁻¹ K⁻¹), A the area (m²), ΔT the temperature difference (K) and d the thickness (m). The rate is directly proportional to A, so doubling the area doubles the rate. It is inversely proportional to d, so doubling the thickness halves the rate. It does not depend on the square of the area.</p><p><b>Worked example 5.</b> A glass pane of area 2.0 m², thickness 4.0 mm, k = 0.8 W m⁻¹ K⁻¹, with ΔT = 20 K: Q/t = (0.8 × 2.0 × 20)/(4.0 × 10⁻³) = 8000 W.</p><p><b>Trap.</b> Using d in centimetres or millimetres. Always convert to metres before using the formula.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The <b>Kelvin</b> (absolute) scale starts at absolute zero, 0 K, which is about −273 °C. The conversion is T(K) = θ(°C) + 273 (or + 273.15 for more precise work). The size of one kelvin equals the size of one degree Celsius, so a temperature <i>change</i> has the same value in K and °C.</p><p><b>Perfect (ideal) gas.</b> The state of a fixed amount of gas is set by pressure, volume and temperature, through PV = nRT. Changing any one of them changes the physical properties of the gas, so all three are controlling variables (Part B Q75; its option (d) is printed cut off, read here as 'all of the above').</p><p>At constant temperature, the bulk modulus of an ideal gas equals its pressure (B = P), which is why IGSTA Q79 has the answer 'equal to its pressure'. A perfect gas has zero viscosity in the idealised model and is of theoretical interest.</p><p><b>Trap.</b> A temperature in kelvin is never negative. The zero of the Celsius scale is 273 K, not 0 K.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p><b>IGSTA Q70.</b> A force of 50 N acts normal to a surface of area 5 m². P = F/A = 50/5 = 10 Pa. The option 55 Pa adds, and 45 Pa subtracts.</p><p><b>IGSTA Q74.</b> Gauge pressure 70 kPa, atmospheric 100 kPa. Absolute = 70 + 100 = 170 kPa. Subtracting gives 30 kPa, the trap.</p><p><b>IGSTA Q78.</b> Same load and length, half the diameter. ΔL ∝ 1/d², so the extension becomes 4 × 1 cm = 4 cm. The naive answer 2 cm ignores the square.</p><p><b>IGSTA Q84.</b> Wires A and B of the same material. L<sub>A</sub>:L<sub>B</sub> = 1:2 and d<sub>A</sub>:d<sub>B</sub> = 2:1, so the area ratio A<sub>A</sub>:A<sub>B</sub> = 4:1. ΔL<sub>A</sub>/ΔL<sub>B</sub> = (L<sub>A</sub>/L<sub>B</sub>) × (A<sub>B</sub>/A<sub>A</sub>) = (1/2) × (1/4) = 1/8, so the ratio is 1:8.</p><p><b>IGSTA Q81.</b> The force constant of an atomic bond: k = YA/L with A = a² and L = a gives k = Ya = 2 × 10¹¹ × 3 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 60 N/m. This is a model estimate, not an exact law.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<ul><li><b>Units first.</b> Convert mm to m and cm² to m² before using any formula. Most wrong answers come from a power of ten.</li><li><b>Square correctly.</b> Area scales with the square of the diameter or radius. Do not square the length or the thickness.</li><li><b>Pressure is not force.</b> P = F/A is in Pa; the force is in N. A 50 N force on 5 m² gives 10 Pa.</li><li><b>Gauge vs absolute.</b> Check whether the question gives a gauge reading or an absolute value before adding or subtracting atmospheric pressure.</li><li><b>Strain has no unit.</b> Stress and the moduli are in Pa; strain is a pure ratio.</li><li><b>Heat is not temperature.</b> Heat is an energy, in J. Temperature is in K or °C.</li><li><b>Conduction, convection, radiation.</b> Solids: conduction. Fluids: convection. Vacuum: radiation only.</li><li><b>Read the option list.</b> Some IGSTA options use 'none of the above' or 'all of the above'. Check that the chosen option is actually true; several traps are near-synonyms (buoyant force vs upthrust).</li></ul><p><b>Exam routine.</b> Write down the formula, then the substitution with units, then the answer. If the answer is a power of ten away from your estimate, recheck the unit conversion first.</p>"}]}]},"questions":[{"i":"PHY101-SP-0001","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"______ is the time measured by an observer using a fixed reference frame.","o":["Absolute time","Relative time","Coordinate time","Universal time."],"a":2,"e":"Coordinate time is the time recorded by an observer using a fixed reference frame; it labels when and where events occur. Absolute time is Newton's idea of time flowing the same for everyone, so it is the usual trap.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q12"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0002","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is true about the speed of light?","o":["It is constant in all inertial frames","It varies in a vacuum","It depends on the observer's motion","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Einstein's postulate: the speed of light in vacuum (c, about 3 x 10^8 m/s) is the same for all inertial observers, whatever their motion or the motion of the source. This is why time and length must change instead.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q9"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0003","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"What is the term for a 3D mathematical structure formed by vectors?","o":["Outer Space","Hilbert Space","Vector Space","Minkowski Space"],"a":2,"e":"A vector space is a mathematical structure of vectors that can be added and scaled by numbers. Displacement, velocity and force vectors live in the 3D vector space. Outer space is a physical region, Minkowski space is 4D spacetime.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q10"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0004","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"What type of space is described by Euclidean geometry?","o":["Curved Space","Flat Space","Non-Euclidean Space","Minkowski Space"],"a":1,"e":"Euclidean geometry (parallel lines never meet, triangle angles sum to 180 degrees) describes flat space. Curved and non-Euclidean spaces break these rules.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q14"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0005","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Which type of time is associated with biological rhythms?","o":["Universal Time","Psychological Time","Biological Time","Clock Time"],"a":2,"e":"Biological time is the internal clock behind circadian and other biological rhythms (e.g. the roughly 24-hour sleep-wake cycle). Psychological time is only how time feels.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q45"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0006","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Which time system measures Earth's rotation relative to distant stars?","o":["Solar Time","Sidereal Time","Atomic Time","Event Time"],"a":1,"e":"Sidereal time tracks Earth's rotation against the distant stars. Solar time tracks it against the Sun, so a sidereal day is slightly shorter than a solar day.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q46"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0007","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Who introduced the concept of \"spacetime\"?","o":["Isaac Newton","Albert Einstein","Galileo Galilei","Max Planck"],"a":1,"e":"In the lecture, Einstein's relativity unified space and time into a single four-dimensional spacetime, so Einstein is the intended answer. Newton and Galileo treated them as separate.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q51"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0008","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"What is \"Planck Time\"?","o":["Time based on Earth's rotation","Smallest measurable unit of time in quantum mechanics","Time it takes for the moon to orbit the Earth","None of the above"],"a":1,"e":"Planck time (about 10^-43 s, precisely 5.39 x 10^-44 s) is the time light takes to cross one Planck length: the smallest meaningful time interval in quantum mechanics.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q58"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0009","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"What does \"absolute time\" mean in classical physics?","o":["Time varies based on motion","Time flows uniformly and is independent of external factors","Time depends on gravitational fields","Time stops in inertial frames"],"a":1,"e":"Newton's absolute time flows uniformly and is the same for all observers, independent of motion or gravity. The other options describe relative time from relativity.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q59"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0010","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Classwork (space identification): the displacement vector A runs from the point (6, 1) to the point (13, 4). Express A in component (i, j) notation.","o":["7i + 3j","19i + 5j","-7i - 3j","7i - 3j"],"a":0,"e":"Displacement = final - initial: (13 - 6) i + (4 - 1) j = 7i + 3j.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Space and Time classwork, vector A"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0011","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Classwork (space identification): the displacement vector B runs from the point (3, 7) to the point (3, 1). Express B in component (i, j) notation.","o":["6j","-6j","-6i","4j"],"a":1,"e":"Displacement = final - initial: (3 - 3) i + (1 - 7) j = 0i - 6j = -6j. Pure downward vector, magnitude 6.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Space and Time classwork, vector B"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0012","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Classwork (space identification): the displacement vector C runs from the point (-14, 4) to the point (-6, 6). Express C in component (i, j) notation.","o":["-8i - 2j","8i - 2j","-20i + 10j","8i + 2j"],"a":3,"e":"Displacement = final - initial: (-6 - (-14)) i + (6 - 4) j = 8i + 2j. Subtracting a negative is the usual slip (giving -20i).","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Space and Time classwork, vector C"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0013","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Who proposed the idea of absolute time and absolute space?","o":["Albert Einstein","Max Planck","Hermann Minkowski","Isaac Newton"],"a":3,"e":"Absolute space and time are the foundation of Newton's classical mechanics. Einstein replaced them with relativity.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0014","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"In the classical (Newtonian) view, space is best described as","o":["a flexible fabric bent by mass","a four-dimensional block joined with time","a quantity that shrinks with speed","a fixed, unchanging stage in which objects move"],"a":3,"e":"Absolute space exists independently of the objects in it and gives a reference frame for motion. Bending and shrinking belong to relativity.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0015","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Relative time, in Einstein's theory, depends on","o":["the observer's speed and position in a gravitational field","the colour of the light used","the observer's mood","the Earth's rotation only"],"a":0,"e":"In relativity the rate of a clock depends on its speed relative to the observer and on the gravity it sits in. Mood is psychological time.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0016","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Proper time is the time interval measured","o":["by a distant observer using fixed coordinates","by an atomic clock at Greenwich","by the Sun's position in the sky","by a clock travelling with the observer, in the observer's own frame"],"a":3,"e":"Proper time is what an observer's own clock records along their own path. A distant observer's fixed-frame reading is coordinate time.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0017","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"To an astronaut moving at very high speed, their own time seems to","o":["pass normally","stop completely","run backwards","run much faster than on Earth"],"a":0,"e":"In their own frame proper time always seems normal; it is the comparison with another observer that shows dilation.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0018","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Psychological time refers to","o":["time kept by cesium atoms","time since the Big Bang","the subjective experience of how time feels","time set by the Sun's position"],"a":2,"e":"It varies with emotion and attention: time seems fast when excited and slow when bored. It is not a measured quantity.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0019","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Universal Time (UT) is essentially the same as","o":["International Atomic Time (TAI)","Planck time","Sidereal time at midnight","Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)"],"a":3,"e":"UT is a standard based on Earth's rotation and is essentially GMT. TAI is the atomic standard.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0020","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"The SI second is defined using the radiation from which atom?","o":["Caesium-133","Hydrogen-1","Carbon-12","Rubidium-87"],"a":0,"e":"The second is 9 192 631 770 periods of the caesium-133 hyperfine transition; this is the basis of atomic time. Carbon-12 appears in the definition of the mole.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0021","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Compared with a solar day, a sidereal day is","o":["slightly longer","exactly equal","slightly shorter","about twice as long"],"a":2,"e":"Earth must turn a little extra each day to bring the Sun back to the same position, since it also moves along its orbit. So the star-based day (about 23 h 56 min) is shorter than 24 h.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0022","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Sundials measure which kind of time?","o":["Mean solar time","Atomic time","Dynamical time","Apparent solar time"],"a":3,"e":"Sundials follow the Sun's actual position (apparent solar time). Modern clocks use mean solar time, which averages out the variations caused by Earth's elliptical orbit.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0023","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Barycentric Dynamical Time (TDB) and Terrestrial Dynamical Time (TDT) are used for","o":["predicting the motion of planets and other solar-system bodies","measuring how long a day feels","recording the age of the universe","defining the kilogram"],"a":0,"e":"Dynamical time is a precise time scale for planetary motion and celestial mechanics.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0024","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"The age of the universe, about 13.8 billion years, is an example of","o":["clock time","event time","cosmological time","historical time"],"a":2,"e":"Cosmological time covers the largest scales, from the Big Bang to now. Historical time is human or geological eras.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0025","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"The Geological Time Scale and the Anthropocene Epoch are examples of","o":["proper time","atomic time","coordinate time","historical time"],"a":3,"e":"Historical time divides history into eras, epochs and centuries.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0026","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Clock time is","o":["everyday time in hours, minutes and seconds used in daily life and business","time measured by vibrating atoms only","time measured against distant stars","the smallest possible time interval"],"a":0,"e":"Clock time is the practical, conventional time on clocks, for example the 24-hour system.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0027","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Event time is described in the lecture as","o":["time that flows the same for everyone","time measured only in the Greenwich zone","the duration taken for an event to occur","time that runs backwards at light speed"],"a":2,"e":"It is the duration of an event, broken into hours, minutes and seconds, and depends on the time system adopted.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0028","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Circadian rhythms typically repeat about every","o":["24 minutes","24 hours","24 days","24 seconds"],"a":1,"e":"The human sleep-wake cycle follows a roughly 24-hour pattern.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0029","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"According to special relativity, a clock moving relative to an observer runs","o":["faster than a clock at rest","at exactly the same rate","backwards","slower than a clock at rest relative to that observer"],"a":3,"e":"This is time dilation: moving clocks tick slower as seen by the observer.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0030","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Length contraction means that an object moving relative to an observer appears","o":["shorter along its direction of motion","longer along its direction of motion","narrower only at right angles to its motion","unchanged in all directions"],"a":0,"e":"Only the length along the direction of relative motion contracts. The trap is thinking the object shrinks sideways.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0031","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"The relativity of simultaneity says that two events that are simultaneous for one observer","o":["are always simultaneous for everyone","can never be simultaneous","may not be simultaneous for an observer moving relative to the first","happen at the same place"],"a":2,"e":"Because time is not the same for all observers, simultaneity depends on the observer's motion.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0032","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"In general relativity, gravity is explained as","o":["a force that acts instantly across space","the curvature of spacetime caused by mass and energy","a purely electrical effect","an effect of absolute space"],"a":1,"e":"Massive bodies bend spacetime and other objects follow the curved paths, which we see as gravitational attraction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0033","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Gravitational time dilation means a clock close to a massive body ticks","o":["faster than one far from it","at exactly the same rate","only when it is moving","more slowly than one far from it"],"a":3,"e":"Clocks in strong gravitational fields run slower.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0034","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Minkowski space is","o":["the four-dimensional spacetime framework of special relativity","a space with only distance defined","an infinite-dimensional space of quantum states","the space of positions and momenta"],"a":0,"e":"Time is treated as a dimension alongside the three spatial dimensions, and c is constant.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0035","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"On the surface of a globe (spherical geometry), the angles of a triangle add up to","o":["exactly 180 degrees","less than 180 degrees","more than 180 degrees","exactly 360 degrees"],"a":2,"e":"A sphere is a non-Euclidean (curved) space where the angle sum exceeds 180 degrees; in hyperbolic geometry it is less.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0036","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Curved space (Riemannian space) is the type of space used in","o":["Newton's classical mechanics","Einstein's general relativity","Euclid's geometry","Bohr's atomic model"],"a":1,"e":"In general relativity, space is warped by mass and energy, which causes effects like gravitational lensing.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0037","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"In physics, Hilbert space is mainly associated with","o":["everyday three-dimensional movement","the bending of light by stars","the position of the Sun","quantum mechanics (states of a quantum system)"],"a":3,"e":"A Hilbert space is a (usually infinite-dimensional) vector space with an inner product, used for quantum states.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0038","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Phase space is the space whose points represent","o":["the positions and momenta of all parts of a system","only the time of day","only the colour of the object","distances between cities"],"a":0,"e":"Each point in phase space gives a complete state of a mechanical system.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0039","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A metric space is a set in which","o":["time is the only coordinate","all vectors are perpendicular","a distance between any two points is defined","only curved lines exist"],"a":2,"e":"'Metric' means there is a distance function for the points.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0040","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A topological space is mainly concerned with","o":["exact distances and angles","closeness and properties that survive continuous stretching","the speed of light","the age of the universe"],"a":1,"e":"Topology ignores exact distances; a coffee cup and a doughnut are topologically alike.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0041","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Configuration space is the space of","o":["all possible clock readings","all galaxies in the universe","all colours of light","all possible arrangements (configurations) of a system"],"a":3,"e":"Each point describes one arrangement of the parts of the system.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0042","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Physical (real) space is the three-dimensional continuum described by","o":["height, width and depth","mass, charge and spin","past, present and future","speed, force and power"],"a":0,"e":"It is the space around us where objects exist and events occur.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0043","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A key property of Euclidean space is that","o":["parallel lines meet at infinity","triangle angles sum to over 180 degrees","parallel lines never meet","time is its fourth dimension"],"a":2,"e":"Euclidean geometry also gives a 180 degree angle sum for triangles. Meeting parallels belong to curved geometries.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0044","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Outer space is","o":["the 3D structure formed by vectors","the vast expanse beyond Earth's atmosphere where stars, planets and galaxies exist","a space of quantum states","the geometry of a sheet of paper"],"a":1,"e":"Do not confuse outer space (a physical region) with vector space (a mathematical structure).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0045","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"In relativity, an event is located by","o":["three coordinates: x, y and z only","one coordinate: t only","two coordinates: x and y","four coordinates: x, y, z and t"],"a":3,"e":"Spacetime has three space coordinates and one time coordinate.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0046","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Observers moving relative to each other will disagree on time and space but all agree on","o":["the spacetime interval","the time between two events","the length of a moving rod","whether two events are simultaneous"],"a":0,"e":"The spacetime interval is the invariant quantity in relativity.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0047","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"An observer on a fast train shines a light beam forward. A person on the platform measures its speed to be","o":["c plus the speed of the train","c minus the speed of the train","c, the same as the train observer","zero"],"a":2,"e":"The constancy of c means no velocity addition is applied to light. The trap is classical addition of speeds.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0048","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"In the classical, relativity and quantum comparison, time in quantum mechanics is","o":["absolute and uniform for all","uncertain at small scales, perhaps linked with space in novel ways","fixed and never uncertain","curved by mass only"],"a":1,"e":"Classical: absolute. Relativity: relative, part of spacetime. Quantum: uncertain at small scales.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0049","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"In classical physics, space and time are","o":["a single unified entity","both curved by mass","both quantised at 10^-43 s","distinct and separate entities"],"a":3,"e":"Unification into spacetime is the relativity view.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0050","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"In the notation r = x i + y j + z k, the symbols i, j and k are","o":["unit vectors along the x, y and z axes","scalar constants equal to 1, 2, 3","the angles of the vector","time components"],"a":0,"e":"Each has magnitude 1 and points along one axis (i-hat, j-hat, k-hat).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0051","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A position vector locates a point P by giving","o":["only its distance from O","only its direction","its distance and direction from the chosen origin O","the time taken to reach it"],"a":2,"e":"r = (x, y, z) points from O to P. A vector needs both magnitude and direction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0052","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A displacement vector between two points is found by","o":["initial coordinates minus final coordinates","final coordinates minus initial coordinates","adding the two coordinate pairs","multiplying the coordinates"],"a":1,"e":"Subtract tail from tip: (x2 - x1, y2 - y1). Reversing the order flips both signs.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0053","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Average velocity is calculated as","o":["distance divided by time taken only","displacement multiplied by time","time divided by displacement","displacement divided by time taken"],"a":3,"e":"v = displacement / time, giving a vector with the direction of the displacement. Speed is a scalar.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0054","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is the vector component form of velocity?","o":["v = (vx, vy, vz)","v = (x, y, t)","v = (m, a, F)","v = (i, j, k) only"],"a":0,"e":"Velocity components are written vx, vy, vz, just as acceleration is (ax, ay, az).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0055","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A displacement vector goes from P(2, 3) to Q(9, 7). In (i, j) notation it is","o":["11i + 10j","-7i - 4j","7i + 4j","7i - 4j"],"a":2,"e":"(9 - 2) i + (7 - 3) j = 7i + 4j.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0056","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"The magnitude of A = 7i + 3j is about","o":["10.00","7.62","21.00","5.29"],"a":1,"e":"|A| = sqrt(7^2 + 3^2) = sqrt(58) = 7.62. The trap is adding the components (10) or forgetting the square root.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0057","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"What is the magnitude of the displacement from (1, 2) to (7, 10)?","o":["14","8","6","10"],"a":3,"e":"Components (6, 8), so |d| = sqrt(36 + 64) = 10.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0058","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Displacement C = 8i + 2j takes 5 s. The average velocity is","o":["1.6i + 0.4j (units of length per s)","40i + 10j","8i + 2j","0.625i + 2.5j"],"a":0,"e":"Divide each component by t: (8/5) i + (2/5) j = 1.6i + 0.4j.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0059","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Displacement B = -6j takes 3 s. The average velocity is","o":["-18j","2i","-2j (units of length per s)","-2i"],"a":2,"e":"(-6)/3 = -2 along j: it is directed in the negative y direction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0060","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A displacement A = 7i + 3j is covered in 2 s. The average speed along the straight line is about","o":["3.50","3.81","1.50","15.2"],"a":1,"e":"|A| = 7.62, divided by 2 s gives 3.81 (length units per s). Do not divide only one component.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0061","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"The position vector r = 3i - 2j + 5k has magnitude about","o":["6.00","10.00","38.00","6.16"],"a":3,"e":"|r| = sqrt(9 + 4 + 25) = sqrt(38) = 6.16. 38 is the square of the magnitude.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0062","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"The displacement from (-5, 2) to (4, -6) is","o":["9i - 8j","-9i + 8j","-1i - 4j","9i + 8j"],"a":0,"e":"(4 - (-5)) i + (-6 - 2) j = 9i - 8j.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0063","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A = 7i + 3j and C = 8i + 2j are two successive displacements. The total displacement is","o":["56i + 6j","-1i + j","15i + 5j","15i + 6j"],"a":2,"e":"Add components: (7 + 8) i + (3 + 2) j = 15i + 5j.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0064","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A particle starts at (2, 5) and is displaced by 4i - 3j. Its final position is","o":["(-2, 8)","(6, 2)","(6, 8)","(8, 15)"],"a":1,"e":"Final = initial + displacement = (2 + 4, 5 - 3) = (6, 2).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0065","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A particle ends at (10, 4) after a displacement of -3i + 2j. It started at","o":["(7, 6)","(7, 2)","(13, 6)","(13, 2)"],"a":3,"e":"Initial = final - displacement = (10 + 3, 4 - 2) = (13, 2).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0066","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Taking the Planck length as 1.6 x 10^-35 m and c = 3.0 x 10^8 m/s, the Planck time is about","o":["5.4 x 10^-44 s","4.8 x 10^-27 s","5.4 x 10^-43 s","1.6 x 10^-35 s"],"a":0,"e":"t = length / c = 1.616 x 10^-35 / 2.998 x 10^8 = 5.39e-44 s (about 10^-44 s; the lecture quotes the order 10^-43 s).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0067","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Light travels from the Moon (3.84 x 10^8 m away) to Earth. Taking c = 3.0 x 10^8 m/s, the time is about","o":["12.8 s","0.78 s","1.28 s","1.15 x 10^17 s"],"a":2,"e":"t = d/c = 3.84 x 10^8 / 3.0 x 10^8 = 1.28 s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0068","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Sunlight travels 1.5 x 10^11 m to Earth. With c = 3.0 x 10^8 m/s, it takes about","o":["50 s","500 s (about 8.3 min)","5000 s","500 min"],"a":1,"e":"t = 1.5 x 10^11 / 3.0 x 10^8 = 500 s = 8.3 min.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0069","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A sidereal day is 23 h 56 min 4 s and a solar day is 24 h. The difference is about","o":["4 s","56 min","24 min","236 s (about 3 min 56 s)"],"a":3,"e":"24 h = 86 400 s; 23 h 56 min 4 s = 86 164 s; difference = 236 s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0070","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"How long do 18 385 263 540 periods of the caesium-133 radiation take? (1 s = 9 192 631 770 periods)","o":["2 s","1 s","18 s","0.5 s"],"a":0,"e":"18 385 263 540 / 9 192 631 770 = 2, so 2 s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0071","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"The universe is 13.8 billion years old. Using 1 year = 3.16 x 10^7 s, its age is about","o":["4.4 x 10^14 s","1.4 x 10^10 s","4.4 x 10^17 s","4.4 x 10^21 s"],"a":2,"e":"13.8 x 10^9 x 3.156 x 10^7 = 4.36e+17 s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0072","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A body is displaced by 3i + 4j in 2 s. Its average velocity vector and its speed (length units per s) are","o":["3i + 4j and 5","1.5i + 2j and 2.5","1.5i + 2j and 3.5","6i + 8j and 10"],"a":1,"e":"v = (3/2) i + (4/2) j = 1.5i + 2j. Speed = sqrt(1.5^2 + 2^2) = 2.5, which equals 5/2.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0073","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"The unit vector in the direction of A = 3i + 4j is","o":["0.3i + 0.4j","3i + 4j","i + j","0.6i + 0.8j"],"a":3,"e":"|A| = 5, so unit vector = A/5 = 0.6i + 0.8j (magnitude 1).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0074","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Point r1 = (1, 2, 3) and r2 = (4, 6, 3). The magnitude of the displacement from r1 to r2 is","o":["5","7","25","3"],"a":0,"e":"d = (3, 4, 0), |d| = sqrt(9 + 16) = 5. The trap is forgetting the square root (25).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0075","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A walker goes from (0, 0) to (3, 0) and then to (3, 4). The distance walked and the magnitude of the displacement are","o":["5 and 7","7 and 7","7 and 5","5 and 5"],"a":2,"e":"Distance is the path length 3 + 4 = 7. Displacement is the straight line from start to end: sqrt(9 + 16) = 5.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0076","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"The direction of A = 7i + 3j, measured above the +x axis, is about","o":["66.8 degrees","23.2 degrees","23.2 radians","40.0 degrees"],"a":1,"e":"theta = arctan(3/7) = 23.2 degrees. Using arctan(7/3) gives the angle from the y axis instead.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0077","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"If A = 7i + 3j is the displacement from (6, 1) to (13, 4), the displacement for the return trip from (13, 4) to (6, 1) is","o":["7i + 3j","-7i + 3j","7i - 3j","-7i - 3j"],"a":3,"e":"Return = initial - final = -A = -7i - 3j: same magnitude, opposite direction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0078","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"If A = 7i + 3j, then 2A equals","o":["14i + 6j","9i + 5j","14i + 3j","7i + 6j"],"a":0,"e":"A scalar multiplies every component: 2 x 7 = 14 and 2 x 3 = 6.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0079","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"A displacement of (8, 2) is covered in 4 s. The average velocity is","o":["32i + 8j","8i + 2j","2i + 0.5j","0.5i + 2j"],"a":2,"e":"Divide each component by 4: 8/4 = 2 and 2/4 = 0.5.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0080","c":"PHY","u":"Space and Time","s":"mcq","q":"Displacement C = 8i + 2j is covered in 5 s. The average speed (straight line) is about","o":["2.00","1.65","1.60","8.25"],"a":1,"e":"|C| = sqrt(68) = 8.25; speed = 8.25/5 = 1.65.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0081","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"A force F is given by F = at + bt², where t is time. What are the dimensions of a and b?","o":["[MLT⁻¹] and [MLT⁰]","[MLT⁻³] and [ML²T⁴]","[MLT⁻⁴] and [MLT¹]","[MLT⁻³] and [MLT⁻⁴]"],"a":3,"e":"Every term must have the dimensions of force, [MLT⁻²]. [a] = [F]/[t] = [MLT⁻³]; [b] = [F]/[t²] = [MLT⁻⁴]. Trap: dividing by t only once for b.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q1"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0082","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"In a system of units if force (F), acceleration (A) and time (T) are taken as fundamental units then the dimensional formula of energy is","o":["[FA²T]","[FAT²]","[F²AT]","[FAT]"],"a":1,"e":"Energy = force × length. Length = acceleration × time², so L = AT². Hence energy = F·(AT²) = [FAT²]. Check: F·A·T² = MLT⁻²·LT⁻²·T² = ML²T⁻². Trap: forgetting that length must be rebuilt from A and T.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q2"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0083","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The acceleration due to gravity is 9.80 m/s². What is its value in ft/s²?","o":["32.4 ft/s²","28.4 ft/s²","20.4 ft/s²","3.24 ft/s²"],"a":0,"e":"1 ft = 0.3048 m, so 9.80 m/s² = 9.80/0.3048 = 32.2 ft/s². The nearest option is 32.4 ft/s². Trap: multiplying by 0.3048 instead of dividing gives about 3.0.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q3"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0084","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensional representation of Planck's constant is same as that of:","o":["Angular momentum","Momentum","Torque","Energy"],"a":0,"e":"E = hf gives [h] = [ML²T⁻²]/[T⁻¹] = [ML²T⁻¹], which is also [r][p] = L·MLT⁻¹, angular momentum. Torque and energy are [ML²T⁻²]; momentum is [MLT⁻¹].","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q4"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0085","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following pairs of physical quantities does not have same dimensional formula?","o":["Work and torque.","Angular momentum and Planck's constant.","Tension and surface tension.","Impulse and linear momentum."],"a":2,"e":"Tension is a force [MLT⁻²]; surface tension is force per length [MT⁻²]. The other pairs match: work and torque [ML²T⁻²]; angular momentum and h [ML²T⁻¹]; impulse and momentum [MLT⁻¹].","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q5"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0086","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The period P of a swinging pendulum depends only on the length of the pendulum d and the acceleration of gravity g. Which of the following formulas for P could be correct?","o":["P = 2(dg)²","P = 2π(d/g)","P = 2π(d/g)^0.5","None"],"a":2,"e":"P must have dimension [T]. (dg)² = L⁴T⁻⁴ and d/g = T² are both wrong; (d/g)^0.5 = (L/(LT⁻²))^0.5 = T is correct. The 2π cannot be checked by dimensions.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q8"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0087","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensional formula [ML²T⁻²] represents","o":["momentum","energy","acceleration","force."],"a":1,"e":"[ML²T⁻²] = force × length = energy (work, torque too). Momentum is [MLT⁻¹], acceleration [LT⁻²], force [MLT⁻²].","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q9"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0088","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"If the units of length and force are increased four times, then the unit of energy will","o":["increase 8 times","increase 16 times","decreases 16 times","increase 4 times."],"a":1,"e":"Energy = force × length, so the new unit is 4 × 4 = 16 times larger. Trap: the numerical value of a given energy would fall 16 times, but the question asks about the unit, which increases.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q10"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0089","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these statements is not a system of unit?","o":["FPS","CGS","MKS","SUP"],"a":3,"e":"FPS (foot-pound-second), CGS (centimetre-gram-second) and MKS (metre-kilogram-second) are systems of units. SUP is not.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q11"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0090","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensional formula for Planck's constant is","o":["ML²T⁻¹","ML²T³","ML⁻¹T⁻²","MLT⁻²"],"a":0,"e":"h = E/f, so [h] = [ML²T⁻²]·[T] = [ML²T⁻¹]. ML⁻¹T⁻² is pressure and MLT⁻² is force.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q13"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0091","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is not one of the seven fundamental SI units?","o":["Henry","Ampere","Candela","Mole"],"a":0,"e":"The seven base units are metre, kilogram, second, kelvin, ampere, candela, mole. The henry (unit of inductance) is a derived unit.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q15"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0092","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"From the following pairs of physical quantities, in which group dimensions are not same:","o":["Momentum and impulse","Torque and energy","Energy and work","Light year and minute"],"a":3,"e":"A light year is a unit of distance [L]; a minute is a unit of time [T]. The other pairs match: momentum and impulse [MLT⁻¹]; torque, energy and work [ML²T⁻²].","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q16"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0093","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"10 Fermi is equal to","o":["1 meter","100 micron","1 Angstrom","1 mm"],"a":2,"e":"1 fermi = 10⁻¹⁵ m, so 10 fermi = 10⁻¹⁴ m. No option equals this. The intended answer is probably 1 Angstrom, since 1 Å = 10⁻¹⁰ m = 10⁵ fermi; it is the only atomic-scale option.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q20"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0094","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"What is the dimension of power?","o":["[M¹L²T⁻³]","[M¹L¹T⁻²]","[M¹L³T⁻³]","[M⁰L⁰T⁰]"],"a":0,"e":"Power = work/time = [ML²T⁻²]/[T] = [ML²T⁻³]. [MLT⁻²] is force.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Dimension of power"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0095","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these quantities is dimensionless?","o":["Pressure","Refractive index","Force","Torque"],"a":1,"e":"Refractive index = speed of light in vacuum / speed in medium, a ratio of two speeds, so dimensionless. Pressure [ML⁻¹T⁻²], force [MLT⁻²], torque [ML²T⁻²].","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Which quantity is dimensionless"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0096","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"What is the unit of force in the CGS system?","o":["Newton","Dyne","Joule","Pascal"],"a":1,"e":"CGS force unit is the dyne = g cm s⁻². The newton is the SI unit; 1 N = 10⁵ dyne.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — CGS unit of force"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0097","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The principle used to check the correctness of equations is called?","o":["Dimensional Analysis","Vector Calculus","Kinematic Equation","Energy Conservation"],"a":0,"e":"Dimensional analysis, based on the principle of homogeneity: every term in a correct equation has the same dimensions.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Principle to check equations"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0098","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"How many base SI units are there?","o":["5","6","7","8"],"a":2,"e":"Seven: metre, kilogram, second, ampere, kelvin, candela, mole.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Number of base SI units"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0099","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"What is the dimensional formula for density?","o":["[M¹L⁻³T⁰]","[M¹L²T⁻²]","[M⁰L³T⁰]","[M⁰L⁻³T⁰]"],"a":0,"e":"Density = mass/volume = [M]/[L³] = [ML⁻³T⁰]. [M⁰L⁻³] forgets the mass.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Dimensional formula for density"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0100","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"What is the SI unit for energy?","o":["Newton","Joule","Erg","Watt"],"a":1,"e":"The joule (J = N m = kg m² s⁻²). The erg is the CGS unit; the watt is power; the newton is force.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — SI unit for energy"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0101","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"What is the SI unit of luminous intensity?","o":["Mole","Candela","Ampere","Kelvin"],"a":1,"e":"Candela (cd). Mole is amount of substance, ampere is current, kelvin is temperature.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — SI unit of luminous intensity"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0102","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which physical quantity has the dimensional formula [MLT⁻²]?","o":["Velocity","Acceleration","Force","Momentum"],"a":2,"e":"Force = mass × acceleration = [M][LT⁻²] = [MLT⁻²]. Momentum is [MLT⁻¹].","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Quantity with dimension [MLT⁻²]"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0103","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is a fundamental physical quantity?","o":["Volume","Time","Pressure","Energy"],"a":1,"e":"Time is a base quantity. Volume, pressure and energy are derived from length, mass and time.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Fundamental physical quantity"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0104","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"What is the SI base unit for the amount of substance?","o":["Mole","Kilogram","Candela","Kelvin"],"a":0,"e":"The mole (mol). Kilogram is mass, candela luminous intensity, kelvin temperature.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — SI unit for amount of substance"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0105","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which system of units uses \"foot, pound, second\"?","o":["MKS","CGS","FPS","SI"],"a":2,"e":"FPS = foot-pound-second. MKS and SI use metre-kilogram-second; CGS uses centimetre-gram-second.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — System using foot-pound-second"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0106","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The speed 'c' of ocean waves is proportional to the acceleration due to gravity 'g', wavelength 'λ', and density 'ρ' of the wave such that c = k gˣ λʸ ρᶻ, where k is a dimensionless constant. The correct equation for the speed of the ocean wave is?","o":["c = √(kgλρ)","c = k√(gλ)","c = k√(gλρ)","c = k g²λ²ρ²"],"a":1,"e":"LT⁻¹ = (LT⁻²)ˣ Lʸ (ML⁻³)ᶻ. Mass: z = 0. Time: −2x = −1 so x = ½. Length: x + y − 3z = 1 so y = ½. Hence c = k√(gλ); density does not appear.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q1"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0107","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Class exercise: using η = F/(A·dv/dx) (F = viscous force, A = area, dv/dx = velocity gradient), the dimensional formula of the coefficient of viscosity η is","o":["[MLT⁻²]","[ML⁻¹T⁻²]","[ML²T⁻¹]","[ML⁻¹T⁻¹]"],"a":3,"e":"[F] = MLT⁻², [A] = L², [dv/dx] = (LT⁻¹)/L = T⁻¹. So [η] = MLT⁻²/(L²·T⁻¹) = [ML⁻¹T⁻¹]. SI unit: Pa s.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Units and dimensions class exercise 1(iii)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0108","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Class exercise: the dimensional formula of a plane angle θ = (arc length)/(radius) is","o":["[L]","[L⁻¹]","[T⁻¹]","[M⁰L⁰T⁰] (dimensionless)"],"a":3,"e":"θ = length/length, a ratio of like quantities, so it has no dimensions. The radian is a dimensionless supplementary unit.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Units and dimensions class exercise 1(iv)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0109","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Class exercise: which of these pairs of physical quantities has the same dimensions?","o":["Work and power","Momentum and kinetic energy","Force and surface tension","Stress and pressure"],"a":3,"e":"Stress and pressure are both force/area = [ML⁻¹T⁻²]. Work [ML²T⁻²] differs from power [ML²T⁻³]; momentum [MLT⁻¹] differs from energy [ML²T⁻²]; surface tension [MT⁻²] differs from force [MLT⁻²].","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Units and dimensions class exercise 2(ii)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0110","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Class exercise: which of these pairs of physical quantities does NOT have the same dimensions?","o":["Stress and pressure","Momentum and impulse","Work and power","Torque and work"],"a":2,"e":"Work [ML²T⁻²] and power [ML²T⁻³] differ by a factor of time. Stress and pressure [ML⁻¹T⁻²], momentum and impulse [MLT⁻¹], torque and work [ML²T⁻²] match.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Units and dimensions class exercise 2(i)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0111","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Class exercise: V = √(E/d), where V is the speed of sound, E the elasticity (elastic modulus) and d the density of the medium. On the basis of dimensional analysis this equation is","o":["dimensionally incorrect, because the right side is [L²T⁻²]","dimensionally incorrect, because E and d have different dimensions","impossible to check, because of the square root","dimensionally correct, both sides being [LT⁻¹]"],"a":3,"e":"[E] = [ML⁻¹T⁻²], [d] = [ML⁻³], so [E/d] = [L²T⁻²] and √(E/d) = [LT⁻¹] = [V]. E and d having different dimensions is irrelevant; only the two sides must match.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Units and dimensions class exercise 3"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0112","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Class exercise: capillary rise is given by h = 2T cosθ/(rρg), with T = surface tension, ρ = density, r = radius, g = gravitational acceleration. By the principle of homogeneity this equation is","o":["dimensionally incorrect, because the right side is dimensionless","dimensionally incorrect, because the right side is [L²]","dimensionally correct, both sides having dimension [L]","impossible to check, because of cosθ"],"a":2,"e":"[T/(rρg)] = MT⁻²/(L·ML⁻³·LT⁻²) = MT⁻²/(ML⁻¹T⁻²) = [L]. The 2 and cosθ are dimensionless. Note the slide prints the formula garbled (density d in the numerator, cosθ in the denominator); the correct capillary formula has ρ in the denominator.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Units and dimensions class exercise 4"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0113","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Class exercise: the period P of a pendulum depends only on its length d and on g. Writing P = k dᵃ gᵇ and equating dimensions, the exponents are","o":["a = −½, b = ½","a = 1, b = −1","a = ½, b = ½","a = ½, b = −½"],"a":3,"e":"T = Lᵃ(LT⁻²)ᵇ. Time: −2b = 1 so b = −½. Length: a + b = 0 so a = ½. So P = k√(d/g); the exercise slide gives k = 2π (found by experiment or theory, not dimensions).","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Units and dimensions class exercise (pendulum)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0114","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"A car travels at 72 km/h. Its speed in m/s is","o":["259 m/s","7.2 m/s","20 m/s","200 m/s"],"a":2,"e":"72 km/h = 72 × 1000 m / 3600 s = 20 m/s (divide by 3.6). Multiplying by 3.6 gives 259, the common slip.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0115","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Using 1 inch = 2.54 cm, 15.0 inches equals","o":["5.91 cm","381 cm","12.5 cm","38.1 cm"],"a":3,"e":"15.0 in × 2.54 cm/in = 38.1 cm. Dividing by 2.54 is the usual slip.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0116","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Using 1 mile = 5280 ft and 1 ft = 0.3048 m, a speed of 60 miles per hour is about","o":["26.8 m/s","96.6 m/s","16.7 m/s","216 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"60 × 5280 × 0.3048 / 3600 = 26.8 m/s. (1 mph = 0.447 m/s.) 96.6 is km/h and 16.7 comes from wrongly treating 60 as km/h.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0117","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Using 1 lb = 454 g, a mass of 5 lb is","o":["227 g","90.8 g","2270 g","2.27 g"],"a":2,"e":"5 × 454 g = 2270 g. Dividing by 454 gives 90.8, and misplacing the decimal gives 227.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0118","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"A liquid has density 8 g/cm³. In SI units this is","o":["800 kg/m³","8 kg/m³","0.008 kg/m³","8000 kg/m³"],"a":3,"e":"8 g/cm³ = 8×10⁻³ kg / 10⁻⁶ m³ = 8×10³ kg/m³. Rule: g/cm³ → kg/m³ multiply by 1000.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0119","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Using 1 N = 10⁵ dyne, a force of 20 N equals","o":["2×10⁴ dyne","2×10⁶ dyne","2×10⁷ dyne","2×10⁻⁴ dyne"],"a":1,"e":"20 × 10⁵ = 2×10⁶ dyne. 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻² = 10³ g × 10² cm s⁻² = 10⁵ g cm s⁻².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0120","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The CGS unit of pressure is the dyne/cm². Since 1 N = 10⁵ dyne and 1 m² = 10⁴ cm², 1 dyne/cm² equals","o":["0.1 Pa","10 Pa","10⁻⁵ Pa","10⁵ Pa"],"a":0,"e":"1 dyne/cm² = 10⁻⁵ N / 10⁻⁴ m² = 0.1 N/m² = 0.1 Pa.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0121","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Light takes about how long to travel from the Sun to Earth? (distance 1.5×10¹¹ m, speed 3.0×10⁸ m/s)","o":["2.0×10⁻³ s","4.5×10¹⁹ s","500 s","50 s"],"a":2,"e":"t = d/c = 1.5×10¹¹ / 3.0×10⁸ = 5.0×10² s (about 8.3 minutes). Inverting the ratio gives 2.0×10⁻³ s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0122","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensional formula of pressure is","o":["[MLT⁻²]","[ML²T⁻²]","[ML⁻²T⁻²]","[ML⁻¹T⁻²]"],"a":3,"e":"Pressure = force/area = [MLT⁻²]/[L²] = [ML⁻¹T⁻²]. The same as stress and elastic modulus.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0123","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensional formula of surface tension (force per unit length) is","o":["[MLT⁻²]","[MT⁻²]","[ML⁻¹T⁻²]","[MT⁻¹]"],"a":1,"e":"Surface tension = F/L = [MLT⁻²]/[L] = [MT⁻²]. Same as a spring constant.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0124","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensional formula of the universal gravitational constant G (from F = Gm₁m₂/r²) is","o":["[M⁻¹L³T⁻²]","[ML³T⁻²]","[M⁻¹L²T⁻²]","[M⁻¹L³T²]"],"a":0,"e":"G = Fr²/(m₁m₂) = [MLT⁻²][L²]/[M²] = [M⁻¹L³T⁻²]. SI unit: N m² kg⁻². G is a dimensional constant.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0125","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The moment of inertia of a particle, I = mr², has dimensional formula","o":["[MLT⁻¹]","[ML²T⁻²]","[ML²]","[M²L]"],"a":2,"e":"[m][r²] = [M][L²] = [ML²]. No time dimension; [ML²T⁻²] is torque/energy.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0126","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Energy per unit volume has the same dimensions as","o":["power","momentum","force","pressure"],"a":3,"e":"[ML²T⁻²]/[L³] = [ML⁻¹T⁻²], the dimension of pressure. Power is [ML²T⁻³], momentum [MLT⁻¹], force [MLT⁻²].","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0127","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The quantity ρgh (density × g × height) has the same dimensions as","o":["force","pressure","energy","power"],"a":1,"e":"[ML⁻³][LT⁻²][L] = [ML⁻¹T⁻²], dimension of pressure (the hydrostatic pressure of a liquid column).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0128","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT dimensionless?","o":["Speed of light in vacuum","Plane angle","Strain","Refractive index"],"a":0,"e":"The speed of light has dimension [LT⁻¹] (the slide lists it as a dimensionless constant, which is wrong). Angle, strain and refractive index are ratios of like quantities.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0129","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The steradian (sr) is the SI supplementary unit of","o":["plane angle","luminous intensity","solid angle","angular velocity"],"a":2,"e":"Steradian: solid angle. Radian: plane angle. Both are dimensionless.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0130","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these equations is dimensionally INCORRECT? (s = displacement, u, v = speeds, a = acceleration, t = time)","o":["v = u + at","s = ut + ½at²","v² = u² + 2as","s = ut + ½at"],"a":3,"e":"In s = ut + ½at the term ut has dimension [L] but at has [LT⁻¹], so the terms cannot be added. The correct form has at².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0131","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"In the equation v = A + Bt², v is speed and t is time. The dimensional formula of B is","o":["[LT⁻²]","[LT⁻³]","[LT⁻¹]","[L²T⁻³]"],"a":1,"e":"[Bt²] must equal [v] = [LT⁻¹], so [B] = [LT⁻¹]/[T²] = [LT⁻³].","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0132","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The period T of a mass m on a spring of force constant k (dimension [MT⁻²]) is T = C mᵃ kᵇ. Dimensional analysis gives","o":["a = ½, b = −½","a = −½, b = ½","a = 1, b = −1","a = ½, b = ½"],"a":0,"e":"T = Mᵃ(MT⁻²)ᵇ. Time: −2b = 1, b = −½. Mass: a + b = 0, a = ½. So T = C√(m/k) (C = 2π comes from theory).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0133","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The viscous drag F on a small sphere of radius r moving at speed v through a fluid of viscosity η depends on η, r and v only. Dimensional analysis gives F ∝","o":["η r² v","η r v²","η r v","η² r v"],"a":2,"e":"[F] = MLT⁻². With [η] = ML⁻¹T⁻¹: ηrv = ML⁻¹T⁻¹·L·LT⁻¹ = MLT⁻². The other products give wrong dimensions. This is Stokes' law, F = 6πηrv (6π is not found by dimensions).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0134","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The fundamental frequency f of a stretched string depends on its length L, tension F and mass per unit length μ. Dimensional analysis gives f ∝","o":["L√(F/μ)","(1/L)√(Fμ)","√(F/(Lμ))","(1/L)√(F/μ)"],"a":3,"e":"Write f = Lᵃ Fᵇ μᶜ. Mass: b + c = 0. Time: −2b = −1, so b = ½, c = −½. Length: a + b − c = 0, so a = −1. Check: (1/L)√(F/μ) = (1/L)(LT⁻¹) = T⁻¹.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0135","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following can NOT be found by dimensional analysis?","o":["Whether T = 2π√(l/g) is dimensionally consistent","The numerical factor 2π in T = 2π√(l/g)","The power of l in the pendulum formula","The dimensions of g given T and l"],"a":1,"e":"Pure numbers such as 2π, ½ or 6π are dimensionless and invisible to dimensional analysis. The method can only check consistency and find powers.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0136","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Dimensional analysis cannot be used directly to derive a relation when the physical quantity","o":["is a sum of two or more terms (e.g. s = ut + ½at²)","is a product of powers of other quantities","depends on length and time only","has the dimensions of a force"],"a":0,"e":"The method assumes a single product-type dependence. Equations with several added terms, trig or exponential functions are outside its scope.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0137","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which statement about dimensions and units is correct?","o":["A quantity has different dimensional formulas in CGS and SI","Quantities with the same dimensions must have the same unit","A quantity has the same dimensional formula in all systems of units, but its unit differs from system to system","A dimensionless quantity cannot have a unit"],"a":2,"e":"Force is [MLT⁻²] in SI and CGS, but its units are newton and dyne. Dimensionless quantities such as angle can have units (radian).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0138","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"A wavelength of 450 nm expressed in metres is","o":["4.5×10⁻⁹ m","4.5×10⁻⁶ m","4.5×10⁻⁴ m","4.5×10⁻⁷ m"],"a":3,"e":"nano = 10⁻⁹, so 450 nm = 450×10⁻⁹ = 4.5×10⁻⁷ m (blue light, matching the module's table of lengths).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0139","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"1 Angstrom = 10⁻¹⁰ m and 1 fermi = 10⁻¹⁵ m. How many fermi make one Angstrom?","o":["10⁻⁵","10⁵","10¹⁰","10¹⁵"],"a":1,"e":"10⁻¹⁰ / 10⁻¹⁵ = 10⁵ fermi in one Angstrom.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0140","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The diameter of a hydrogen atom, about 1×10⁻¹⁰ m, is closest to","o":["1 Angstrom","1 fermi","1 nanometre","1 micrometre"],"a":0,"e":"1 Å = 10⁻¹⁰ m. A nucleus (proton) is about a fermi (10⁻¹⁵ m); 1 nm is 10 Å.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0141","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"How many significant figures does 0.00450 have?","o":["2","4","3","6"],"a":2,"e":"Leading zeros are not significant; 4, 5 and the trailing 0 are. So 3 significant figures.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0142","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The product 4.56 × 1.4, quoted with the correct number of significant figures, is","o":["6.38","6.384","6.3","6.4"],"a":3,"e":"4.56 × 1.4 = 6.384. The less precise factor (1.4) has 2 s.f., so the answer is 6.4.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0143","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The radius of a circle is measured as 4.0 ± 0.1 cm. The percentage uncertainty in the area πr² is about","o":["2.5%","5%","10%","0.1%"],"a":1,"e":"Relative error in r = 0.1/4.0 = 2.5%. Since area ∝ r², the relative errors add twice: 2 × 2.5% = 5%.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0144","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The SI second is defined as the duration of how many periods of the radiation from the hyperfine transition of caesium-133?","o":["9 192 631 770","299 792 458","540 × 10¹²","273 160"],"a":0,"e":"The second is 9 192 631 770 periods of the caesium-133 hyperfine radiation. 299 792 458 is the speed of light in m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0145","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"The kelvin is defined as the fraction 1/273.16 of the thermodynamic temperature of","o":["the boiling point of water at 1 atm","absolute zero","the triple point of water","the freezing point of water at 1 atm"],"a":2,"e":"The module defines the kelvin via the triple point of water (273.16 K = 0.01 °C).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0146","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"In the SI, the ampere is the constant current which, flowing in two infinitely long parallel conductors 1 m apart in vacuum, produces a force per metre of length of","o":["2×10⁷ N","1×10⁻⁷ N","9.8 N","2×10⁻⁷ N"],"a":3,"e":"The module's definition: force of 2×10⁻⁷ newton per metre of length. (The extracted slide shows the sign garbled.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0147","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which statement correctly describes SI as a coherent system?","o":["It assigns only one unit to each physical quantity","All derived units come from the base units by multiplication or division with no numerical factors","Multiples and sub-multiples are powers of ten","It has no gravitational units"],"a":1,"e":"Coherent = no extra conversion factors. The other statements describe SI being rational, metric and absolute respectively.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0148","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is a derived SI unit, not a base unit?","o":["newton","ampere","kelvin","kilogram"],"a":0,"e":"The newton is kg m s⁻². Ampere, kelvin and kilogram are base units.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0149","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"If the units of mass, length and time are all doubled, the new unit of force (kg·m·s⁻² type) is","o":["twice as large","four times as large","unchanged (same size as before)","half as large"],"a":2,"e":"Unit of force ∝ M L T⁻². Factor = 2 × 2 × 2⁻² = 1. Mass and length each double the unit, but time² in the denominator gives back a factor 4.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0150","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"Which expression for the speed v of an object falling freely from rest through height h is dimensionally consistent?","o":["v = 2gh","v = √(g/h)","v = √(2g/h)","v = √(2gh)"],"a":3,"e":"[gh] = LT⁻²·L = L²T⁻², so √(2gh) has dimension [LT⁻¹]. 2gh gives L²T⁻², and √(g/h) gives T⁻¹.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0151","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"A radio station broadcasts at 2.5 GHz. In hertz this is","o":["2.5×10⁶ Hz","2.5×10⁹ Hz","2.5×10¹² Hz","2.5×10⁻⁹ Hz"],"a":1,"e":"giga = 10⁹, so 2.5 GHz = 2.5×10⁹ Hz. Mixing up giga (10⁹) and mega (10⁶) is the usual slip.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0152","c":"PHY","u":"Units and Dimensions","s":"mcq","q":"A red blood cell has diameter about 7 μm. In metres this is","o":["7×10⁻⁶ m","7×10⁻³ m","7×10⁻⁹ m","7×10⁻¹² m"],"a":0,"e":"micro = 10⁻⁶, so 7 μm = 7×10⁻⁶ m. Milli is 10⁻³ and nano is 10⁻⁹.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0153","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Which one of the following is not a vector quantity:","o":["velocity","force","energy","momentum"],"a":2,"e":"Energy is a scalar (magnitude only, in joules). Velocity, force and momentum all have direction. Trap: energy is built from a force and a displacement, but the dot product of two vectors is a scalar.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q7"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0154","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"What is the vector quantity representing rate of change of velocity?","o":["Speed","Acceleration","Displacement","Momentum"],"a":1,"e":"Acceleration a = Δv/Δt is the rate of change of velocity and points along the change in velocity. Speed is a scalar; displacement and momentum are vectors but are not rates of change of velocity.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q56"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0155","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Which method can be used to calculate the resultant of two non-collinear vectors?","o":["Subtraction","Pythagorean Theorem","Sine rule","Both B and C"],"a":3,"e":"Non-collinear vectors are added by Pythagoras (if perpendicular) or by the cosine and sine rules (general triangle). 'Both B and C' is the closest option. Plain subtraction is only for collinear vectors.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q57"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0156","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these quantities has both magnitude and direction?","o":["Mass","Energy","Pressure","Electric Field"],"a":3,"e":"Electric field is a vector (force per unit charge, with direction). Mass, energy and pressure are scalars.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q58"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0157","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"What is the unit vector in the x-direction represented as?","o":["k̂","ĵ","î","l̂"],"a":2,"e":"î points along +x, ĵ along +y and k̂ along +z. There is no unit vector called l̂.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q59"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0158","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is a scalar quantity?","o":["Velocity","Force","Work","Displacement"],"a":2,"e":"Work = F·d is a dot product, so it is a scalar (joules). Velocity, force and displacement are vectors.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q60"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0159","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The cross product of two parallel vectors is always sine of zero?","o":["True","False","Cannot be said without the magnitudes","Only if both vectors are unit vectors"],"a":0,"e":"|A × B| = AB sin θ and θ = 0° for parallel vectors, so the cross product is AB sin 0° = 0 (the zero vector). The statement is loosely worded but True.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q14"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0160","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Given the vector A = 2i + j + 3k (m) and B = 3i + 2j + 6k (m). Find |A| + |B|","o":["10.72 m","10.70 m","10.74 m","10.73 m"],"a":2,"e":"|A| = √(4+1+9) = √14 = 3.742 m; |B| = √(9+4+36) = √49 = 7 m. Sum = 10.742 ≈ 10.74 m. Trap: this is the sum of magnitudes, not the magnitude of the sum.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q18"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0161","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Given the vector A = 2i + j + 3k (m) and B = 3i + 2j + 6k (m). Find |A + B|","o":["10.76 m","10.70 m","10.72 m","10.73 m"],"a":2,"e":"A + B = 5i + 3j + 9k. |A + B| = √(25+9+81) = √115 = 10.724 ≈ 10.72 m. It is less than |A|+|B| = 10.74 m, as the triangle inequality requires.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q20"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0162","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"When two vectors are perpendicular, their dot product is the cosine of 90°","o":["True","False","Only if they are unit vectors","Only in two dimensions"],"a":0,"e":"A·B = AB cos θ. At θ = 90° the factor cos 90° = 0, so A·B = 0. The statement says the dot product 'is the cosine of 90°' (a factor equal to zero), which is accepted as True.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q24"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0163","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"An insect is displaced on a straight track joining the base and the apex of two vertical poles each 2 cm high, and are 3 m apart. Calculate: (1) the straight track covered by the insect; (2) the angle above the horizontal.","o":["13 / 33.7°","3.61 / 33.7°","13 / 34°","3.61 / 34°"],"a":1,"e":"Track = √(3² + 2²) = √13 = 3.61 m; angle = tan⁻¹(2/3) = 33.7° above the horizontal. Trap: 13 is the square of the length, not the length.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q25"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0164","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these statements is true about the dot product of two vectors.","o":["It has both magnitude and direction","It has only magnitude","It has only direction","All of the above"],"a":1,"e":"The dot (scalar) product A·B = AB cos θ is a scalar: a number with magnitude (and sign) but no direction.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q34"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0165","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"When two vectors are perpendicular, their dot product is","o":["Cosine 90°","Cosine 180°","Cosine 45°","Cosine 360°"],"a":0,"e":"A·B = AB cos θ, and perpendicular means θ = 90°, so the product contains cos 90° (= 0). The others correspond to θ = 180°, 45°, 360°.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q35"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0166","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A boat moves north at 15 m/s in a river which flows west at 8.0 m/s. What is the boat's resultant velocity with respect to due north?","o":["17 m/s at 28.1° N of W","17 m/s at 28.1° W of N","23 m/s at 28.1° W of N","7 m/s at 28.1° W of N"],"a":1,"e":"R = √(15² + 8²) = 17 m/s. tan θ = 8/15 = 0.5333, θ = 28.1° measured from north towards west, i.e. 28.1° W of N.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Vectors class exercise 1 (boat and river)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0167","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A bear searching for food wanders 35 m east, then 20 m north, then 12 m west, then 6 m south. Calculate the bear's displacement.","o":["37 m","73 m","23 m","26.93 m"],"a":3,"e":"Net east = 35 − 12 = 23 m; net north = 20 − 6 = 14 m. R = √(23² + 14²) = 26.93 m at tan⁻¹(14/23) = 31.3° N of E. 73 m is the total distance walked.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Vectors class exercise 2 (bear)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0168","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A plane moves with a velocity of 63.5 m/s at 32° south of east. What is the plane's horizontal (eastward) velocity component?","o":["33.65 m/s","53.85 m/s","63.5 m/s","119.9 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"vₓ = 63.5 cos 32° = 53.85 m/s east (the vertical part is 63.5 sin 32° = 33.65 m/s south). Trap: using sin for the horizontal part.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Vectors class exercise 3 (plane components)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0169","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A car moves 80 km east, then 30 km west, then 50 km east. Calculate its displacement relative to where it started.","o":["160 km east","0 km","100 km east","100 km west"],"a":2,"e":"Take east as positive: 80 − 30 + 50 = +100 km, i.e. 100 km east. 160 km is the total distance travelled.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Vectors class exercise 4 (car)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0170","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Find the resultant of two displacements, 2.0 m at 40° and 4.0 m at 127° (angles from the +x axis). Using the cosine rule, to two significant figures:","o":["6.0 m","4.4 m","2.0 m","4.6 m"],"a":3,"e":"The angle between the vectors is 127° − 40° = 87°. R² = 2² + 4² + 2(2)(4)cos 87° = 20.84, R = 4.6 m. (The slide uses the interior triangle angle 93° with a minus sign; it is the same thing.)","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Vectors class exercise 5 (cosine rule)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0171","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A man walks 54.5 m east and then 30 m west. What is his displacement relative to where he started?","o":["84.5 m east","24.5 m west","54.5 m east","24.5 m east"],"a":3,"e":"Collinear vectors in opposite directions subtract: 54.5 − 30 = 24.5 m, in the direction of the larger one (east).","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Vectors class exercise 6 (man walking)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0172","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is a scalar quantity?","o":["Power","Torque","Momentum","Weight"],"a":0,"e":"Power (rate of doing work) has magnitude only, in watts. Torque, momentum and weight all carry a direction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0173","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is a vector quantity?","o":["Weight","Density","Volume","Electric charge"],"a":0,"e":"Weight is the gravitational force on a body (W = mg, directed downwards). Density, volume and charge are scalars.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0174","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A runner completes exactly one lap of a 400 m circular track and returns to the start. Her displacement is","o":["0 m","400 m","200 m","Cannot be found without the radius"],"a":0,"e":"Displacement is the straight-line vector from start to finish. Start and finish coincide, so it is zero, although the distance is 400 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0175","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A car travels round a circular track at a constant 20 m/s. Which of its quantities is constant?","o":["Velocity","Speed","Acceleration","Displacement from the start"],"a":1,"e":"Speed (scalar) stays at 20 m/s. The direction keeps changing, so the velocity (a vector) and the acceleration change.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0176","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The magnitude of a unit vector is","o":["0","9.8","Depends on its direction","1"],"a":3,"e":"A unit vector has magnitude exactly 1 and only specifies direction. Examples: î, ĵ, k̂.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0177","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The dot product of two vectors gives","o":["a scalar","a vector along the larger vector","a vector perpendicular to both","a vector along the smaller vector"],"a":0,"e":"A·B = AB cos θ is a number (no direction). A vector perpendicular to both is the cross product A × B.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0178","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The vector A × B is directed","o":["along A","perpendicular to the plane containing A and B","along the bisector of A and B","along B"],"a":1,"e":"The cross product is perpendicular to both A and B; the sense is given by the right-hand rule.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0179","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Which statement about vector products is correct?","o":["A × B = B × A","A · B = −(B · A)","A × B = −(B × A)","A × B is always a scalar"],"a":2,"e":"The cross product is anti-commutative: swapping the order reverses the direction. The dot product is commutative (A·B = B·A).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0180","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The value of î · ĵ is","o":["1","k̂","−1","0"],"a":3,"e":"î and ĵ are perpendicular, so î·ĵ = (1)(1)cos 90° = 0. Only î·î = 1.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0181","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"î × ĵ equals","o":["k̂","−k̂","0","1"],"a":0,"e":"Using the right-hand rule x → y gives +z, so î × ĵ = k̂. Reversing the order (ĵ × î) gives −k̂.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0182","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"ĵ × î equals","o":["k̂","−k̂","0","−1"],"a":1,"e":"ĵ × î = −(î × ĵ) = −k̂. The cross product reverses sign when the order is swapped.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0183","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Two vectors of fixed magnitude give the largest possible resultant when the angle between them is","o":["90°","180°","0°","45°"],"a":2,"e":"At 0° the vectors point the same way and their magnitudes add: R = A + B.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0184","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Two vectors of fixed magnitude give the smallest possible resultant when the angle between them is","o":["0°","90°","270° measured as 90° on the other side","180°"],"a":3,"e":"At 180° they are opposite and R = |A − B|. For 4 N and 5 N the minimum is 1 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0185","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Which operation is NOT commutative?","o":["Cross product of two vectors","Dot product of two vectors","Addition of two vectors","Multiplication of a vector by a scalar"],"a":0,"e":"A × B ≠ B × A (it changes sign). Vector addition and the dot product do not depend on order.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0186","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The direction of A × B is found using the","o":["parallelogram rule","right-hand rule","sine rule","left-hand rule for motors only"],"a":1,"e":"Curl the fingers of the right hand from A to B (through the smaller angle); the thumb gives the direction of A × B.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0187","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A vector A makes an angle θ with the +x axis. Its x-component is","o":["A sin θ","A tan θ","A cos θ","A / cos θ"],"a":2,"e":"Adjacent side = hypotenuse × cos θ. Trap: if θ is measured from the y axis, the roles of sin and cos swap.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0188","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Multiplying a vector by −2 gives a vector that is","o":["twice as long and in the same direction","half as long and opposite","the same length and opposite","twice as long and in the opposite direction"],"a":3,"e":"A scalar multiplies the magnitude (|−2| = 2) and a negative sign reverses the direction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0189","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A − B is equivalent to","o":["A + (−B)","B − A","−A − B","A × B"],"a":0,"e":"Subtracting a vector means adding its reverse. B − A would point the opposite way.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0190","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The direction '28.1° W of N' means","o":["start facing west and rotate 28.1° towards north","start facing north and rotate 28.1° towards west","28.1° measured anticlockwise from east","28.1° south of west"],"a":1,"e":"'X of Y' means: begin along Y, then turn towards X by the stated angle. 28.1° W of N is mostly north, tilted a little west.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0191","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"In the cosine rule c² = a² + b² − 2ab cos C, the angle C is","o":["the angle between a and the x-axis","the angle opposite side a","the angle opposite side c, inside the triangle","always 90°"],"a":2,"e":"C is the interior angle facing side c. When C = 90°, cos C = 0 and the rule reduces to Pythagoras.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0192","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The magnitude of the vector A = 3i + 4j is","o":["7","25","3.5","5"],"a":3,"e":"|A| = √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5. Trap: adding the components gives 7.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0193","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The magnitude of the vector A = 2i − 3j + 6k is","o":["7","5","11","√11"],"a":0,"e":"|A| = √(4 + 9 + 36) = √49 = 7. Signs vanish on squaring; the sum of components (5) is wrong.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0194","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"If A = 3i + 2j and B = i − 4j, then A + B is","o":["2i + 6j","4i − 2j","4i + 6j","3i − 8j"],"a":1,"e":"Add like components: (3+1)i + (2−4)j = 4i − 2j. 2i + 6j is A − B.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0195","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"If A = 3i + 2j and B = i − 4j, then A − B is","o":["4i − 2j","2i − 2j","2i + 6j","−2i − 6j"],"a":2,"e":"Subtract like components: (3−1)i + (2−(−4))j = 2i + 6j. Trap: the sign of the j part (2 + 4, not 2 − 4).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0196","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The dot product (2i + 3j − k)·(i − 2j + 4k) is","o":["8","−12","6i − 4j","−8"],"a":3,"e":"Multiply matching components and add: (2)(1) + (3)(−2) + (−1)(4) = 2 − 6 − 4 = −8. A dot product is a number, not a vector.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0197","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The z-component of (i + 2j) × (3i + j) is","o":["−5","5","7","1"],"a":0,"e":"(A × B)_z = AₓB_y − A_yBₓ = (1)(1) − (2)(3) = −5, so A × B = −5k. Trap: reversing the order gives +5.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0198","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Two vectors of magnitude 5 and 4 have an angle of 60° between them. Their dot product is","o":["20","10","17.3","9"],"a":1,"e":"A·B = AB cos 60° = (5)(4)(0.5) = 10. 17.3 comes from using sin 60°.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0199","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Two vectors of magnitude 6 and 4 have an angle of 30° between them. The magnitude of their cross product is","o":["20.8","24","12","10"],"a":2,"e":"|A × B| = AB sin 30° = (6)(4)(0.5) = 12. 20.8 comes from using cos 30°.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0200","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The angle between A = 3i + 4j and B = 4i + 3j is closest to","o":["73.7°","0°","36.9°","16.3°"],"a":3,"e":"A·B = 12 + 12 = 24; |A| = |B| = 5. cos θ = 24/25 = 0.96, so θ = 16.3°. 73.7° is 90° − 16.3°.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0201","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Two forces of 3.0 N and 4.0 N act at right angles to each other. The magnitude of the resultant is","o":["5.0 N","7.0 N","1.0 N","12 N"],"a":0,"e":"R = √(3² + 4²) = 5.0 N. Adding (7 N) is only correct if the forces are collinear and in the same direction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0202","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Two forces of 6 N and 8 N act with an angle of 60° between them. The magnitude of the resultant is","o":["10 N","12.2 N","14 N","7.5 N"],"a":1,"e":"R² = 6² + 8² + 2(6)(8)cos 60° = 36 + 64 + 48 = 148, R = 12.2 N. Using −2ab cos 60° would give 7.2 N (wrong angle convention).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0203","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Vectors of 4.0 N and 5.0 N act with 45° between their directions (tail to tail). The resultant magnitude is","o":["9.0 N","6.4 N","8.3 N","3.6 N"],"a":2,"e":"R² = 16 + 25 + 2(4)(5)cos 45° = 41 + 28.3 = 69.3, R = 8.3 N. 9.0 N is the 0° case, 6.4 N the 90° case, 3.6 N the 135° case.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0204","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Vectors of 4.0 N and 5.0 N act with 135° between their directions. The resultant magnitude is","o":["8.3 N","6.4 N","1.0 N","3.6 N"],"a":3,"e":"R² = 41 + 40 cos 135° = 41 − 28.3 = 12.7, R = 3.6 N. The resultant shrinks as the angle grows from 0° (9.0 N) to 180° (1.0 N).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0205","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Two forces of 10 N each act with an angle of 120° between them. The resultant is","o":["10 N","20 N","17.3 N","0 N"],"a":0,"e":"R² = 100 + 100 + 2(10)(10)cos 120° = 200 − 100 = 100, so R = 10 N (the resultant equals each force).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0206","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Two vectors have magnitudes 7 and 3. The magnitude of their resultant can be anywhere from","o":["0 to 10","4 to 10","3 to 7","4 to 21"],"a":1,"e":"Minimum at 180° is 7 − 3 = 4; maximum at 0° is 7 + 3 = 10. Any angle in between gives a value in that range.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0207","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"P(1, 2, 3) and Q(4, 6, 15) are points in metres. The displacement vector from P to Q has magnitude","o":["√13 m","5 m","13 m","9 m"],"a":2,"e":"PQ = (4−1)i + (6−2)j + (15−3)k = 3i + 4j + 12k. |PQ| = √(9 + 16 + 144) = √169 = 13 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0208","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A displacement starts at A(2, −1) and ends at B(−1, 3), in metres. In unit-vector form it is","o":["3i − 4j","i + 2j","−3i + 2j","−3i + 4j"],"a":3,"e":"Final minus initial: (−1 − 2)i + (3 − (−1))j = −3i + 4j (magnitude 5 m). Trap: initial minus final reverses the vector.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0209","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A force of 100 N acts at 30° above the horizontal. Its horizontal component is","o":["86.6 N","50 N","100 N","57.7 N"],"a":0,"e":"Fₓ = F cos 30° = 100 × 0.866 = 86.6 N. 50 N is the vertical component F sin 30°.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0210","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A plane moves at 63.5 m/s at 32° south of east. Its southward velocity component is","o":["53.85 m/s","33.65 m/s","63.5 m/s","40.3 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"v_y = 63.5 sin 32° = 33.65 m/s south. The eastward part is 63.5 cos 32° = 53.85 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0211","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A walker goes 3 km north then 4 km east. Her displacement is","o":["7 km at 53.1° E of N","5 km at 36.9° E of N","5 km at 53.1° E of N","5 km at 53.1° N of E"],"a":2,"e":"R = √(3² + 4²) = 5 km. tan θ = 4/3 gives θ = 53.1° measured from north towards east, i.e. 53.1° E of N (equivalent to 36.9° N of E).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0212","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The bear in the class exercise walked 35 m E, 20 m N, 12 m W and 6 m S. The total DISTANCE it walked is","o":["26.93 m","37 m","23 m","73 m"],"a":3,"e":"Distance adds every leg regardless of direction: 35 + 20 + 12 + 6 = 73 m. The displacement is only 26.93 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0213","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A swimmer heads straight across a river at 4 m/s relative to the water. The river flows at 3 m/s. Her speed relative to the bank is","o":["5 m/s","7 m/s","1 m/s","3.5 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"The two velocities are perpendicular: v = √(4² + 3²) = 5 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0214","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The unit vector in the direction of 3i + 4j is","o":["3i + 4j","0.6i + 0.8j","i + j","0.8i + 0.6j"],"a":1,"e":"Divide the vector by its magnitude (5): (3i + 4j)/5 = 0.6i + 0.8j. Check: 0.36 + 0.64 = 1.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0215","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The unit vector in the direction of 2i − 2j + k is","o":["(2i − 2j + k)/5","(2i − 2j + k)/9","(2i − 2j + k)/3","(i − j + k)/√3"],"a":2,"e":"|2i − 2j + k| = √(4 + 4 + 1) = 3. Divide each component by 3.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0216","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"For what value of p are A = 2i + pj + k and B = i − 2j + 4k perpendicular?","o":["p = −3","p = 1","p = 6","p = 3"],"a":3,"e":"Perpendicular means A·B = 0: 2 − 2p + 4 = 0, so p = 3.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0217","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"Find |A × B| for A = 2i + 4j and B = i + 2j.","o":["0","8","4","10"],"a":0,"e":"B = A/2, so the vectors are parallel and A × B = 0. Check: (2)(2) − (4)(1) = 0.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0218","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A force F = (10i + 5j) N moves a body through d = (4i + 2j) m. The work done F·d is","o":["20 J","50 J","10i + 10j J","40i + 10j J"],"a":1,"e":"W = F·d = (10)(4) + (5)(2) = 50 J. Work is a scalar, so options written with i and j cannot be right.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0219","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A force of 20 N acts at 30° to a lever arm of 0.5 m. The magnitude of the torque is","o":["8.7 N m","10 N m","5 N m","2.5 N m"],"a":2,"e":"|τ| = r F sin θ = (0.5)(20)(sin 30°) = 5 N m. Using cos 30° would give 8.7 N m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0220","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"The resultant of 3 N east and 3 N north has magnitude and direction","o":["6 N at 45° N of E","4.24 N at 30° N of E","3 N at 45° N of E","4.24 N at 45° N of E"],"a":3,"e":"R = √(3² + 3²) = 4.24 N; tan θ = 3/3 = 1, θ = 45° from east towards north.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0221","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"If |A + B| = |A − B|, the vectors A and B are","o":["perpendicular","parallel","antiparallel","equal in magnitude"],"a":0,"e":"Squaring: A² + B² + 2A·B = A² + B² − 2A·B, so A·B = 0, meaning perpendicular.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0222","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A = 2i + 3j and B = 3i − 2j. The vectors are","o":["parallel","perpendicular","antiparallel","at 45° to each other"],"a":1,"e":"A·B = 6 − 6 = 0, so they are perpendicular. Parallel vectors would have a zero cross product instead.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0223","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A·B = AB for two vectors. The angle between them is","o":["90°","180°","0°","45°"],"a":2,"e":"A·B = AB cos θ = AB requires cos θ = 1, so θ = 0° (parallel, same direction).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0224","c":"PHY","u":"Vectors and Scalars","s":"mcq","q":"A 5 m east displacement minus a 3 m west displacement (A − B, with B = 3 m west) equals","o":["2 m east","2 m west","8 m west","8 m east"],"a":3,"e":"A − B = A + (−B) = 5 m east + 3 m east = 8 m east. Trap: subtracting magnitudes without reversing B.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0225","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"What is d(x⁵)/dx?","o":["x⁴","5x⁴","5x⁵","4x⁵"],"a":1,"e":"Power rule: bring down the power and reduce it by one. Trap: leaving the power unchanged or not reducing it.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0226","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"What is the derivative of a constant, such as d(7)/dx?","o":["0","7","1","7x"],"a":0,"e":"A constant does not change with x, so its slope is zero. That is why constant terms in x(t) do not appear in v.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0227","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d(sin x)/dx equals","o":["−cos x","cos x","−sin x","sin x"],"a":1,"e":"The derivative of sin x is cos x (x in radians). The minus sign belongs to the derivative of cos x.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0228","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d(cos x)/dx equals","o":["−sin x","sin x","cos x","−cos x"],"a":0,"e":"Differentiating cos x gives −sin x. Trap: forgetting the minus sign.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0229","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d(tan x)/dx equals","o":["cot x","−csc² x","1/(1 + x²)","sec² x"],"a":3,"e":"d(tan x)/dx = sec² x = 1/cos² x. The expression 1/(1 + x²) is the derivative of tan⁻¹ x, not of tan x.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0230","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d(eˣ)/dx equals","o":["x eˣ⁻¹","eˣ ln x","eˣ","1/x"],"a":2,"e":"eˣ is its own derivative. The power rule does not apply because the variable is in the exponent.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0231","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d(ln x)/dx equals","o":["x","1/x","eˣ","ln x / x"],"a":1,"e":"The derivative of the natural logarithm is 1/x.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0232","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d(sin⁻¹ x)/dx equals","o":["1/√(1 − x²)","1/(1 + x²)","−1/√(1 − x²)","1/√(1 + x²)"],"a":0,"e":"Standard result for the inverse sine. The negative of it is the derivative of cos⁻¹ x, and 1/(1 + x²) belongs to tan⁻¹ x.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0233","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d(tan⁻¹ x)/dx equals","o":["1/√(1 − x²)","1/(1 + x²)","sec² x","−1/(1 + x²)"],"a":1,"e":"Standard result: d(tan⁻¹ x)/dx = 1/(1 + x²).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0234","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d(aˣ)/dx, for a constant a > 0, equals","o":["x aˣ⁻¹","aˣ","aˣ/ln a","aˣ ln a"],"a":3,"e":"Write aˣ = e^(x ln a) and use the chain rule, giving aˣ ln a. Trap: using the power rule, or dividing by ln a (that is the integral).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0235","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The velocity of a particle with position vector r(t) is defined as","o":["v = r/t","v = ∫r dt","v = dr/dt","v = d²r/dt²"],"a":2,"e":"Velocity is the time derivative of position. r/t is only an average and d²r/dt² is acceleration.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0236","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The acceleration of a particle is given by","o":["a = dr/dt","a = ∫v dt","a = v/t","a = dv/dt = d²r/dt²"],"a":3,"e":"Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, the second derivative of position.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0237","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"Given that the displacement of a body is described by the equation x = Ct + Dt², where C = 2 m/s and D = 0.1 m/s², while x is in meters and t in seconds. Calculate (a) the displacement between the time interval t = 2s and t = 5s; (b) the average velocity in the intervals.","o":["8.5m/2.8ms-1","8.2m/2.7ms-1","8.1 m/2.7ms-1","8.1m/2.8ms-1"],"a":2,"e":"x(5) = 2(5) + 0.1(25) = 12.5 m; x(2) = 4 + 0.4 = 4.4 m. Displacement = 8.1 m. Average velocity = 8.1 m / 3 s = 2.7 m/s. Option C. Trap: 8.1/3 gives 2.7, not 2.8.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q21"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0238","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The speed of a particle moving in a plane with velocity v = vₓ i + v_y j is","o":["vₓ + v_y","√(vₓ² + v_y²)","vₓ² + v_y²","vₓ v_y"],"a":1,"e":"Speed is the magnitude of velocity. Trap: adding components without squaring and rooting.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0239","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The derivative of the fixed unit vector i with respect to time is","o":["i","j","zero","1"],"a":2,"e":"i has constant magnitude and constant direction, so it does not change with time.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0240","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"If a vector A has constant magnitude, then dA/dt is","o":["perpendicular to A","parallel to A","zero","equal in size to A"],"a":0,"e":"A·A = constant, so differentiating gives 2A·dA/dt = 0, hence dA/dt ⟂ A (unless it is zero). Example: a ⟂ v in uniform circular motion.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0241","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The product rule for the derivative of a dot product of two vector functions is","o":["d(A·B)/dt = A·(dB/dt) + (dA/dt)·B","d(A·B)/dt = (dA/dt)·(dB/dt)","d(A·B)/dt = A×(dB/dt) + (dA/dt)×B","d(A·B)/dt = A·B/t"],"a":0,"e":"Same form as the ordinary product rule. The cross-product version has crosses and is a vector. Trap: differentiating both factors only.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0242","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"In d(A×B)/dt = A×(dB/dt) + (dA/dt)×B, the one point that must be respected is","o":["the order of the vectors in each cross product","the result is a scalar","A and B must be perpendicular","the dot product of the two terms must vanish"],"a":0,"e":"A×B = −B×A, so the order of factors must be kept. The result is a vector, and no perpendicularity is needed.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0243","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The direction of the velocity vector of a particle at a point on its path is","o":["along the normal to the path","along the position vector","along the tangent to the path at that point","along the acceleration"],"a":2,"e":"v = dr/dt is the limit of Δr/Δt, and the chord Δr approaches the tangent. Trap: assuming v is along r or a.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0244","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The slope of a velocity–time graph at an instant gives the","o":["displacement","speed squared","acceleration","position"],"a":2,"e":"Slope of v–t = dv/dt = a. The area under it gives displacement.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0245","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"Integrating a constant acceleration a once with respect to time gives","o":["v = at²","v = u + at","v = u + ½at²","v = u − at²"],"a":1,"e":"∫a dt = at + C, and the constant C equals the initial velocity u.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0246","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The equation v² = u² + 2as is obtained from a = dv/dt by writing","o":["a = ds/dt and differentiating","s = ut and squaring","a = v/t and integrating","a = v dv/ds and integrating"],"a":3,"e":"Use the chain rule dv/dt = (dv/ds)(ds/dt) = v dv/ds, so v dv = a ds; integrating gives ½v² − ½u² = as.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0247","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d(cos⁻¹ x)/dx equals","o":["1/√(1 − x²)","−1/√(1 − x²)","−1/(1 + x²)","sin x"],"a":1,"e":"It is the negative of d(sin⁻¹ x)/dx.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0248","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"What is the value of d/dx (3x⁴ − 2x² + 5) at x = 2?","o":["100","88","40","45"],"a":1,"e":"Derivative: 12x³ − 4x. At x = 2: 12(8) − 4(2) = 96 − 8 = 88.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0249","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The derivative of x² sin x evaluated at x = π/2 (radians) is","o":["2.47","0","1.57","3.14"],"a":3,"e":"Product rule: 2x sin x + x² cos x. At π/2, sin = 1 and cos = 0, so the value is 2(π/2)(1) + 0 = π ≈ 3.14.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0250","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d/dx [x/(x² + 1)] at x = 2 equals","o":["-0.12","0.12","-0.60","0.20"],"a":0,"e":"Quotient rule: [(x²+1)(1) − x(2x)]/(x²+1)² = (1 − x²)/(x²+1)². At x = 2: (1 − 4)/25 = −0.12. Trap: reversing the numerator terms gives +0.12.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0251","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"Using the chain rule, d/dx [sin(3x²)] equals","o":["cos(3x²)","3x² cos(3x²)","6x cos(3x²)","6x sin(3x²)"],"a":2,"e":"Outside: cos(3x²). Inside: d(3x²)/dx = 6x. Multiply: 6x cos(3x²).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0252","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d/dt (e^(−3t)) equals","o":["−3e^(−3t)","e^(−3t)","−3t e^(−3t−1)","3e^(−3t)"],"a":0,"e":"d(e^(kt))/dt = k e^(kt) with k = −3. Trap: dropping the minus sign of the inner derivative.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0253","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d/dx [ln(5x)] equals","o":["1/(5x)","1/x","5/x","ln 5"],"a":1,"e":"Chain rule: (1/5x)(5) = 1/x. Equivalent: ln 5x = ln 5 + ln x, whose derivative is 1/x.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0254","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"What is the value of d/dx [tan⁻¹(2x)] at x = 0?","o":["1","0","1/2","2"],"a":3,"e":"Chain rule: 2/(1 + 4x²). At x = 0 this is 2.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0255","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The derivative of sin⁻¹(x/2) at x = 1 is","o":["0.289","0.866","1.155","0.577"],"a":3,"e":"d/dx sin⁻¹(x/2) = (1/2)/√(1 − x²/4) = 1/√(4 − x²). At x = 1: 1/√3 = 0.577.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0256","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d/dx (x² eˣ) at x = 1 equals","o":["5.44","2.72","10.87","8.15"],"a":3,"e":"Product rule: (2x + x²)eˣ. At x = 1: 3e = 8.15.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0257","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"What is d/dx [ln(x² + 1)] at x = 1?","o":["1","1/2","2","0"],"a":0,"e":"Chain rule: 2x/(x² + 1). At x = 1: 2/2 = 1.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0258","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"d/dt [cos 2t] at t = π/4 (radians) is","o":["−2","−1","2","0"],"a":0,"e":"−2 sin 2t, and sin(π/2) = 1, so the value is −2. Trap: forgetting the factor 2 gives −1.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0259","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves along a line with x = 4t³ − 2t (SI units). Its velocity at t = 2 s is","o":["30 m/s","46 m/s","28 m/s","92 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"v = dx/dt = 12t² − 2. At t = 2: 48 − 2 = 46 m/s. (28 is the position x(2) = 32 − 4.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0260","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For x = 4t³ − 2t (SI units), the acceleration at t = 2 s is","o":["48 m/s²","46 m/s²","24 m/s²","12 m/s²"],"a":0,"e":"a = d²x/dt² = 24t. At t = 2: 48 m/s².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0261","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves with x = 3t + 0.5t² (SI units). Its average velocity from t = 1 s to t = 3 s is","o":["6 m/s","4.5 m/s","5 m/s","10 m/s"],"a":2,"e":"x(3) = 13.5 m, x(1) = 3.5 m, so Δx = 10 m over Δt = 2 s: 5 m/s. (6 is the instantaneous velocity at t = 3; 10 forgets to divide by Δt.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0262","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For x = 3t + 0.5t² (SI units) the instantaneous velocity at t = 3 s is","o":["6 m/s","13.5 m/s","5 m/s","4.5 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"v = 3 + t = 6 m/s at t = 3. 13.5 m is the position, 5 m/s the average over 1–3 s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0263","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has x = t² − 4t (SI units). Its displacement from t = 0 to t = 4 s, and its velocity at t = 2 s, are respectively","o":["0 and −4 m/s","16 m and 0","−4 m and 0","0 and 0"],"a":3,"e":"x(4) = 0 = x(0), so the displacement is 0 (it returned to the start). v = 2t − 4 = 0 at t = 2, where it turns round (x = −4 m there).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0264","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves with x = 2t³ − 9t² + 12t (SI units). It first comes momentarily to rest at a position of","o":["4 m","0 m","5 m","12 m"],"a":2,"e":"v = 6t² − 18t + 12 = 6(t − 1)(t − 2) = 0 gives t = 1 s and 2 s. First time t = 1: x = 2 − 9 + 12 = 5 m (at t = 2, x = 4 m).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0265","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For x = 2t³ − 9t² + 12t (SI units) the acceleration at t = 2 s is","o":["0 m/s²","−6 m/s²","12 m/s²","6 m/s²"],"a":3,"e":"a = 12t − 18. At t = 2: 24 − 18 = 6 m/s².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0266","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has position r = (3t² + 2) i + (t³ − t) j (SI units). Its velocity at t = 2 s is","o":["12 i + 11 j m/s","14 i + 6 j m/s","6 i + 12 j m/s","12 i + 6 j m/s"],"a":0,"e":"v = dr/dt = 6t i + (3t² − 1) j. At t = 2: 12 i + (12 − 1) j = 12 i + 11 j. (14 i + 6 j is the position.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0267","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For r = (3t² + 2) i + (t³ − t) j (SI units), the speed at t = 2 s is","o":["16.3 m/s","23 m/s","12 m/s","265 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"|v| = √(12² + 11²) = √265 = 16.3 m/s. 23 adds the components; 265 forgets the square root.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0268","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For r = (3t² + 2) i + (t³ − t) j (SI units), the magnitude of the acceleration at t = 2 s is","o":["18 m/s²","13.4 m/s²","12 m/s²","6 m/s²"],"a":1,"e":"a = dv/dt = 6 i + 6t j. At t = 2: 6 i + 12 j, |a| = √(36 + 144) = √180 = 13.4 m/s².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0269","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For r = (3t² + 2) i + (t³ − t) j (SI units), the position at t = 2 s is","o":["12 i + 11 j m","14 i + 8 j m","8 i + 6 j m","14 i + 6 j m"],"a":3,"e":"x = 3(4) + 2 = 14; y = 8 − 2 = 6.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0270","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The co-ordinates of a moving particle at any time t are given by x = ct² and y = bt². The speed of the particle is given by...","o":["t√(c² + b²)","2t√(c² + b²)","2t(c + b)","2√(c² + b²)"],"a":1,"e":"vₓ = dx/dt = 2ct and v_y = dy/dt = 2bt. Speed = √(vₓ² + v_y²) = √(4t²(c² + b²)) = 2t√(c² + b²). Traps: dropping the factor 2 or the t, or adding components without squaring.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Differentiation class exercise"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0271","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has x = 3t² and y = 4t² (SI units). Its speed at t = 2 s is","o":["10 m/s","28 m/s","14 m/s","20 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"Speed = 2t√(c² + b²) = 2(2)√(9 + 16) = 4 × 5 = 20 m/s. 28 adds 12 + 16 (vₓ + v_y).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0272","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For x = 3t² and y = 4t² (SI units), the magnitude of the acceleration is","o":["5 m/s²","10 m/s²","14 m/s²","20 m/s²"],"a":1,"e":"a = 6 i + 8 j, constant. |a| = √(36 + 64) = 10 m/s².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0273","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For x = ct² and y = bt², the path of the particle is","o":["a straight line y = (b/c)x","a parabola y = (b/c²)x²","a circle","an ellipse"],"a":0,"e":"Dividing, y/x = b/c, which is constant, so the path is a straight line through the origin although the motion is accelerated.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0274","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has r = 2cos(3t) i + 2sin(3t) j (SI units). Its speed is","o":["2 m/s (constant)","6t m/s","6 m/s (constant)","12 m/s (constant)"],"a":2,"e":"v = −6 sin 3t i + 6 cos 3t j, so |v| = 6√(sin² + cos²) = 6 m/s. Speed = rω = 2 × 3. Trap: missing the chain-rule factor 3.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0275","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For r = 2cos(3t) i + 2sin(3t) j (SI units), the magnitude of the acceleration is","o":["6 m/s²","12 m/s²","0","18 m/s²"],"a":3,"e":"a = −18 cos 3t i − 18 sin 3t j = −9r, so |a| = 9 × 2 = 18 m/s² (= rω² = v²/r = 36/2). Constant speed does not mean zero acceleration.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0276","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For r = R cos ωt i + R sin ωt j, the scalar product r·v is","o":["Rω","R²ω","zero","R²ω²"],"a":2,"e":"r·v = ½ d(r·r)/dt = ½ d(R²)/dt = 0. The velocity is perpendicular to the position vector.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0277","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has r = R cos ωt i + R sin ωt j. Which statement is correct?","o":["|dr/dt| = Rω but d|r|/dt = 0","|dr/dt| = 0 and d|r|/dt = Rω","both are zero","both equal Rω"],"a":0,"e":"|r| = R is constant, so d|r|/dt = 0, but the speed |dr/dt| = Rω. The two quantities are different.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0278","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A projectile has r = 5t i + (10t − 5t²) j (SI units). Its velocity at t = 1 s is","o":["5 i + 5 j m/s","5 i + 0 j m/s","5 i − 10 j m/s","0 i + 0 j m/s"],"a":1,"e":"v = 5 i + (10 − 10t) j. At t = 1: 5 i + 0 j, the top of the flight (vertical velocity zero, horizontal 5 m/s).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0279","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For r = 5t i + (10t − 5t²) j (SI units), the acceleration is","o":["5 i − 10 j m/s²","10 j m/s²","−10t j m/s²","−10 j m/s² (constant)"],"a":3,"e":"a = dv/dt = d(5 i + (10 − 10t) j)/dt = −10 j, constant and downward. The horizontal component of velocity is constant so it contributes no acceleration.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0280","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown upward with y = 20t − 4.9t² (SI units). It reaches its highest point at t =","o":["2.04 s","4.08 s","1.02 s","2.45 s"],"a":0,"e":"v = dy/dt = 20 − 9.8t = 0 gives t = 20/9.8 = 2.04 s. 4.08 s is the total flight time.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0281","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has r = t² i + t j (SI units). The scalar product v·a at t = 1 s is","o":["2","0","5","4"],"a":3,"e":"v = 2t i + j = 2 i + j; a = 2 i. v·a = 4 > 0 so the particle is speeding up. (5 would be v·v.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0282","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"In uniform circular motion, v·a is","o":["positive, because the particle accelerates","zero, because a is perpendicular to v","negative, because the particle turns","equal to v²/r"],"a":1,"e":"Constant speed means d(v²/2)/dt = v·a = 0. The acceleration only changes the direction of the velocity.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0283","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The speed of a particle is increasing at an instant when","o":["v·a < 0","v·a > 0","v·a = 0","v × a = 0"],"a":1,"e":"d(v²/2)/dt = v·a. Positive means speed rising. v × a = 0 means a is parallel to v (it could be speeding up or slowing down).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0284","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"If A = t i + t² j and B = t² i + t j, then d(A·B)/dt at t = 2 is","o":["16","24","12","8"],"a":1,"e":"A·B = t³ + t³ = 2t³. Derivative 6t², at t = 2 gives 24. (Using the product rule term by term gives the same.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0285","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"If A = t i and B = t² j, then d(A×B)/dt at t = 2 is","o":["8 k","−12 k","12 k","6 k"],"a":2,"e":"i × j = k, so A×B = t³ k. Derivative: 3t² k = 12 k at t = 2. Trap: reversing the order flips the sign.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0286","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"If r = t i + t² j, then d(r·r)/dt at t = 1 is","o":["6","2","4","3"],"a":0,"e":"r·r = t² + t⁴, so the derivative is 2t + 4t³ = 6 at t = 1. This equals 2 r·v = 2(1·1 + 1·2) = 6.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0287","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A unit vector n̂ = cosθ i + sinθ j rotates with θ = 2t (rad, t in s). The magnitude of dn̂/dt is","o":["1 s⁻¹","2t s⁻¹","2 s⁻¹","0"],"a":2,"e":"dn̂/dt = (dθ/dt)(−sinθ i + cosθ j), a unit vector times dθ/dt = 2. Its direction changes but its magnitude stays constant.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0288","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has r = t² i + t³ j. The angle its velocity makes with the x-axis at t = 1 s is","o":["56.3°","33.7°","45.0°","71.6°"],"a":0,"e":"v = 2t i + 3t² j = 2 i + 3 j at t = 1. tanθ = 3/2, θ = 56.3°. (33.7° is tan⁻¹(2/3), the x/y mix-up.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0289","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For r = t² i + t³ j, the unit vector along the direction of motion at t = 1 s is","o":["0.707 i + 0.707 j","0.832 i + 0.555 j","0.555 i + 0.832 j","0.667 i + 1.000 j"],"a":2,"e":"v = 2 i + 3 j, |v| = √13 = 3.606. v/|v| = 0.555 i + 0.832 j. Swapping the components is a common slip.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0290","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A spherical tumour model grows so that its radius increases at 0.5 cm/s. When r = 2 cm, the volume grows at","o":["16.8 cm³/s","8.4 cm³/s","50.3 cm³/s","25.1 cm³/s"],"a":3,"e":"V = (4/3)πr³ ⇒ dV/dt = 4πr² dr/dt = 4π(4)(0.5) = 8π = 25.1 cm³/s. Trap: using the volume formula instead of its derivative.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0291","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A circular ripple has radius r = 10 cm growing at 0.2 cm/s. The area is increasing at","o":["6.3 cm²/s","62.8 cm²/s","12.6 cm²/s","1.3 cm²/s"],"a":2,"e":"A = πr² ⇒ dA/dt = 2πr dr/dt = 2π(10)(0.2) = 4π = 12.6 cm²/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0292","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A drug concentration in blood is C = 20e^(−0.1t) mg/L (t in hours). The rate dC/dt at t = 0 is","o":["−20 mg/L per hour","−0.1 mg/L per hour","−2 mg/L per hour","+2 mg/L per hour"],"a":2,"e":"dC/dt = −0.1 × 20e^(−0.1t) = −2e^(−0.1t). At t = 0 the exponential is 1 so the rate is −2 mg/L/h (negative: concentration falling).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0293","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"For C = 20e^(−0.1t) mg/L (t in hours), the rate dC/dt at t = 10 h is","o":["-0.74 mg/L per hour","−2.00 mg/L per hour","−0.20 mg/L per hour","−7.36 mg/L per hour"],"a":0,"e":"dC/dt = −2e^(−0.1t); at t = 10, −2e⁻¹ = −0.74. The rate of fall slows as the concentration falls.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0294","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has a = 6t i (SI units) and v = 2 i at t = 0. Its velocity at t = 2 s is","o":["12 i m/s","26 i m/s","6 i m/s","14 i m/s"],"a":3,"e":"v = ∫6t dt = 3t² + C, with C = 2. At t = 2: 12 + 2 = 14 m/s. Trap: omitting the initial velocity (gives 12).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0295","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has a = (4 − 2t) m/s² and starts from rest. Its velocity at t = 3 s is","o":["−2 m/s","3 m/s","6 m/s","9 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"v = 4t − t² (+0 as v(0) = 0). At t = 3: 12 − 9 = 3 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0296","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has v = (3t² + 2) m/s and x = 0 at t = 0. How far has it moved by t = 2 s?","o":["14 m","16 m","12 m","8 m"],"a":2,"e":"x = ∫(3t² + 2) dt = t³ + 2t. At t = 2: 8 + 4 = 12 m. (14 is v(2); 8 drops the 2t term.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0297","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has v = (3t² + 2) m/s and x = 5 m at t = 0. Its position at t = 2 s is","o":["12 m","7 m","19 m","17 m"],"a":3,"e":"x = t³ + 2t + 5. At t = 2: 8 + 4 + 5 = 17 m. Trap: forgetting the initial position.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0298","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has v = 2t i + 3 j (SI units) and r = i at t = 0. Its position at t = 2 s is","o":["4 i + 6 j m","5 i + 3 j m","5 i + 6 j m","3 i + 6 j m"],"a":2,"e":"r = (t² + 1) i + 3t j. At t = 2: (4 + 1) i + 6 j = 5 i + 6 j. Trap: dropping the initial i.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0299","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has a = 6t i − 2 j, v(0) = 4 i and r(0) = i + j (SI units). Its position at t = 2 s is","o":["17 i − 3 j m","16 i − 4 j m","17 i − 4 j m","13 i − 3 j m"],"a":0,"e":"v = (3t² + 4) i − 2t j; r = (t³ + 4t + 1) i + (1 − t²) j. At t = 2: (8 + 8 + 1) i + (1 − 4) j = 17 i − 3 j.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0300","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A car with u = 5 m/s accelerates uniformly at 2 m/s² for 4 s. Its displacement is","o":["28 m","52 m","20 m","36 m"],"a":3,"e":"s = ut + ½at² = 5(4) + ½(2)(16) = 20 + 16 = 36 m. (20 omits the acceleration term; 28 uses ½at without squaring... that is, 20 + 8.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0301","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle starts from rest with a = 3 m/s². Its speed after covering 24 m is","o":["144 m/s","8.5 m/s","72 m/s","12 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"v² = u² + 2as = 0 + 2(3)(24) = 144, so v = 12 m/s. Trap: stopping at v² (144).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0302","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A car moving at 20 m/s brakes uniformly to rest in 50 m. Its acceleration is","o":["−8 m/s²","−4 m/s²","−2 m/s²","−0.4 m/s²"],"a":1,"e":"0 = u² + 2as ⇒ a = −u²/2s = −400/100 = −4 m/s². Trap: using s instead of 2s gives −8.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0303","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown upward at 20 m/s. Taking a = −9.8 m/s², its velocity after 1.5 s is","o":["34.7 m/s upward","5.3 m/s downward","5.3 m/s upward","9.8 m/s upward"],"a":2,"e":"v = u + at = 20 − 9.8(1.5) = 5.3 m/s, positive so still upward.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0304","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has u = 2 m/s and constant a = 4 m/s². Its displacement after 3 s is","o":["20 m","24 m","30 m","18 m"],"a":1,"e":"s = ut + ½at² = 6 + ½(4)(9) = 6 + 18 = 24 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0305","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"The equation s = ut + ½at² is valid when","o":["the velocity is constant","the acceleration is any function of time","the acceleration is constant","the motion is circular"],"a":2,"e":"It was derived by integrating a constant a twice. If a varies with time, integrate a(t) instead.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0306","c":"PHY","u":"Differentiation of Vectors","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves with a = 2t m/s², starting from rest at the origin. Its position at t = 3 s is","o":["27 m","18 m","9 m","4.5 m"],"a":2,"e":"v = t², x = t³/3. At t = 3: 27/3 = 9 m. Using ½at² with a = 6 would wrongly give 27.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0307","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A body is moving from height x=0.1 m to x=1.2 m in 1 sec under constant acceleration of 0.5 m/s². What was the initial velocity with which it started? (Correct to significant digits)","o":["0.85 m/s","0.9 m/s","1.0 m/s","0.8 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"s = 1.2 − 0.1 = 1.1 m. From s = ut + ½at²: 1.1 = u(1) + ½(0.5)(1)², so u = 1.1 − 0.25 = 0.85 m/s.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q14"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0308","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A bus is moving with a constant velocity v1 along a horizontal road. A man standing inside the bus throws a ball upward with velocity v0 and catches the ball. The magnitude of displacement of the ball with respect to ground is","o":["v0²/g","zero","2v0v1/g","v0v1/g"],"a":2,"e":"Flight time T = 2v0/g. The ball keeps the bus's horizontal speed v1, so ground displacement = v1T = 2v0v1/g. Trap: v0²/g is the vertical range-type quantity, not the ground displacement.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q17"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0309","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle is projected vertically upwards and it attains maximum height H. If the ratio of times to attain height h (<H) is 1/3, then h equals","o":["(2/3) H","(3/4) H","(4/3) H","(3/2) H"],"a":1,"e":"Taking the up-pass and down-pass times through h as t1 and t2 with t1/t2 = 1/3: t1 + t2 = 2u/g, so t1 = u/(2g). Then h = u t1 − ½g t1² = u²/(2g) − u²/(8g) = 3u²/(8g) = (3/4)H, since H = u²/(2g).","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q18"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0310","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle starts moving with constant acceleration and covers a distance x in first t seconds. The distance travelled by it in the next 2t seconds is","o":["8x","3x","x","None of these"],"a":0,"e":"x = ½at². In 3t seconds the distance is ½a(3t)² = 9x. Distance in the next 2t = 9x − x = 8x.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q19"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0311","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"What is the formula for displacement under constant acceleration?","o":["v = u + at","s = ut + 1/2 at²","v² = u² + 2as","s = vt − 1/2 at²"],"a":1,"e":"The standard displacement formula is s = ut + ½at². v = u + at gives velocity, and v² = u² + 2as relates velocity and displacement.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q1"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0312","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"What is the acceleration due to gravity on Earth (approx.)?","o":["8.9 m/s²","9.8 m/s²","10.2 m/s²","7.9 m/s²"],"a":1,"e":"g ≈ 9.8 m/s² near Earth's surface (often rounded to 10).","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q2"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0313","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"What does a negative acceleration signify?","o":["Increasing Velocity","Deceleration","Constant velocity","Zero displacement"],"a":1,"e":"Negative acceleration (retardation) is called deceleration: it reduces velocity when motion is in the positive direction. Trap: it only means slowing if the velocity is positive; constant velocity means zero acceleration.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q3"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0314","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown upwards with an initial velocity of 10 m/s. What is the maximum height reached?","o":["2.5 m","5 m","10 m","20 m"],"a":1,"e":"H = u²/(2g) = 100/(2 × 10) = 5 m with g = 10 (g = 9.8 gives 5.10 m). Forgetting the factor 2 gives 10 m; forgetting to square gives 0.5 m.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q4"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0315","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"What is the relationship between velocity (v) and displacement (s)?","o":["v = s/t","v = u + as","v² = u² + 2as","None of the above"],"a":2,"e":"The suvat relation connecting velocity and displacement (no time) is v² = u² + 2as. v = u + as is dimensionally wrong.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q5"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0316","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A body exhibit motion when its position changes continuously","o":["True","False","True only for straight-line paths","False, because motion needs a force"],"a":0,"e":"Motion is, by definition, a continuous change of position with time. A force is not needed to describe motion (that is kinematics) and the path need not be straight.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q23"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0317","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"The speed of the trust is 40m/s, after 10 seconds, its speed decreases to 20 m/s. what is the acceleration?","o":["-1 m/s","-2 m/s","-4 m/s","-5 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"a = (v − u)/t = (20 − 40)/10 = −2 m/s². The sign is negative because the body is slowing.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q39"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0318","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"Velocity – time graph can give you","o":["Velocity of the moving object","Acceleration of the moving object","Displacement of the moving object","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"A v-t graph gives velocity (the height), acceleration (the slope) and displacement (the area under it).","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q42"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0319","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A car covers a distance of 5 km in 5 minutes, its average speed is equal to?","o":["1 km/h","25 km/h","60 km/h","None of the above"],"a":2,"e":"Average speed = 5 km / (5/60 h) = 60 km/h. Trap: 1 km/h comes from dividing without converting minutes.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q43"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0320","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A bus traveling eastward at 60 m/s takes 10 s to come to halt, its deceleration is equal to?","o":["6 m/s eastward","– 6 m/s eastward","6 m/s² eastward","– 6 m/s² eastward"],"a":3,"e":"a = (0 − 60)/10 = −6 m/s². Only options C and D have the right unit (m/s²); the acceleration is negative, so D matches.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q44"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0321","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"Velocity - time graph can give you","o":["Velocity of the moving object","Acceleration of the moving object","Displacement of the moving object","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"A v-t graph gives velocity (the height), acceleration (the slope) and displacement (the area under it).","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q3"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0322","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"The negative acceleration is also known as?","o":["Retardation","Relaxation","Escalation","All of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Negative acceleration is called retardation (or deceleration). Relaxation and escalation are not kinematic terms.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q4"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0323","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"The magnitude of the displacement from initial position to final position is the?","o":["Straight line","Curved line","Circle line","Total distance"],"a":0,"e":"Displacement is the straight-line separation of the start and end points, so its magnitude is the straight-line distance. Total distance is the path length and is not the same.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q8"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0324","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A stone is thrown upward with an initial velocity of 20 m/s. what is the height the stone will reach?","o":["20 m","30 m","40 m","50 m"],"a":0,"e":"H = u²/(2g) = 400/(2 × 10) = 20 m with g = 10 m/s². With g = 9.8 the result is 20.4 m, closest to 20 m.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q10"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0325","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"The horizontal component of a bouncing ball is:","o":["Affected by gravity","Unaffected by gravity","Affected by weight","Affected by the contact force"],"a":1,"e":"Gravity acts vertically, so the horizontal component of velocity is unchanged in flight (air resistance neglected). Trap: the contact force during the bounce can change it, but it is the flight that is asked about.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q11"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0326","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves in a horizontal line from a point A to a point C, via point B. It has a constant acceleration of 1 m/s² and passes point B after 6 seconds and point C after a further 4 seconds. Its velocity at C is 50 m/s. Calculate the velocity at A.","o":["44 m/s","46 m/s","56 m/s","40 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"From A to C the time is 6 + 4 = 10 s. v = u + at gives 50 = u + 1(10), so u = 40 m/s. (46 m/s is the velocity at B.)","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Kinematics class exercise 1 (velocity at A)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0327","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves in a horizontal line from A to C via B with constant acceleration 1 m/s². It passes B after 6 s and C after a further 4 s, and its velocity at C is 50 m/s. Calculate the distance AB.","o":["240 m","276 m","258 m","222 m"],"a":2,"e":"u at A = 40 m/s. AB: s = ut + ½at² = 40(6) + ½(1)(36) = 240 + 18 = 258 m. Forgetting the ½at² term gives 240 m.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Kinematics class exercise 1 (distance AB)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0328","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves in a horizontal line from A to C via B with constant acceleration 1 m/s². It passes B after 6 s and C after a further 4 s, and its velocity at C is 50 m/s. Calculate the distance BC.","o":["184 m","200 m","192 m","176 m"],"a":2,"e":"Velocity at B = 40 + 6 = 46 m/s. BC: s = 46(4) + ½(1)(16) = 184 + 8 = 192 m. Check with s = vt − ½at² = 50(4) − 8 = 192 m.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Kinematics class exercise 1 (distance BC)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0329","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A ball falls off a cliff and lands on the beach 3.2 seconds later. How high is the cliff? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["100.4 m","31.4 m","25.1 m","50.2 m"],"a":3,"e":"Free fall from rest: s = ½gt² = ½(9.8)(3.2)² = 50.2 m. Omitting the ½ gives 100.4 m; v = gt = 31.4 is a speed, not a height.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Kinematics class exercise 2"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0330","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown vertically upwards with an initial velocity of u m/s from a point 1.2 m above the ground. It reaches a maximum height of 23 m above the ground. Calculate the initial velocity. (g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["21.2 m/s","20.0 m/s","20.7 m/s","10.4 m/s"],"a":2,"e":"Rise above the throw point = 23 − 1.2 = 21.8 m. v² = u² − 2gs gives u = √(2 × 9.8 × 21.8) = 20.7 m/s. Using 23 m instead of 21.8 m gives 21.2 m/s.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Kinematics class exercise 3a"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0331","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown vertically upwards from a point 1.2 m above the ground and reaches a maximum height of 23 m above the ground. Calculate the velocity with which the ball strikes the ground. (g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["20.7 m/s","21.8 m/s","10.6 m/s","21.2 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"Fall from rest through 23 m: v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 23) = 21.2 m/s downward. Adding the 1.2 m to 23 m would be wrong; the fall starts from the top at 23 m and ends at the ground (0 m).","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Kinematics class exercise 3b"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0332","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown vertically upwards from a point 1.2 m above the ground and reaches a maximum height of 23 m above the ground. Calculate the total time the ball is in the air. (g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["4.28 s","4.22 s","2.17 s","8.56 s"],"a":0,"e":"Up: t = u/g = 20.67/9.8 = 2.11 s. Down 23 m: t = √(2 × 23/9.8) = 2.17 s. Total = 4.28 s. Using 2u/g = 4.22 s ignores the extra 1.2 m below the launch point.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Kinematics class exercise 3c"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0333","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A stone is thrown vertically upwards with a speed of 10 m/s from a point 15 m above the ground. Find the maximum height above the ground that the stone reaches. (g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["5.10 m","15.0 m","20.1 m","25.1 m"],"a":2,"e":"0 = 10² + 2(−9.8)s gives s = 5.10 m above the launch point. Height above ground = 15 + 5.10 = 20.1 m. 5.10 m alone forgets the starting height.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Kinematics class exercise 4a (Example 1)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0334","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A stone is thrown vertically upwards with a speed of 10 m/s from a point 15 m above the ground. Find the time taken for the stone to reach the ground. (g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["1.02 s","2.04 s","4.07 s","3.05 s"],"a":3,"e":"Take up as positive: −15 = 10t − 4.9t², so 4.9t² − 10t − 15 = 0. t = [10 + √(100 + 294)]/9.8 = 3.05 s (the negative root, −1.01 s, is rejected). 2.04 s is just the time to return to the launch level.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Kinematics class exercise 4b (Example 1)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0335","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle starts moving from rest along a circle of radius 4 m with a constant tangential acceleration of 3 m/s². The total acceleration of the particle at t = 2 seconds makes an angle φ with the tangential acceleration, where φ is equal to","o":["18.4°","45°","60°","71.6°"],"a":3,"e":"At t = 2 s, v = aₜt = 6 m/s, so aᵣ = v²/r = 36/4 = 9 m/s². tan φ = aᵣ/aₜ = 9/3 = 3, giving φ = 71.6°. 18.4° comes from inverting the ratio.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Kinematics class exercise 5"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0336","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"Which pair of quantities consists of two vectors?","o":["Displacement and velocity","Distance and speed","Speed and displacement","Distance and velocity"],"a":0,"e":"Displacement and velocity both have direction. Distance and speed are scalars.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0337","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"The branch of mechanics concerned only with how bodies move, without considering the forces causing the motion, is called","o":["kinetics","statics","kinematics","dynamics"],"a":2,"e":"Kinematics describes the geometry of motion. Kinetics analyses the forces causing motion; statics deals with equilibrium; dynamics covers bodies in motion generally.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0338","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"Which branch of mechanics analyses the forces that cause motion?","o":["Kinematics","Kinetics","Statics","Geometry of motion"],"a":1,"e":"Kinetics = analysis of the forces causing motion; kinematics is only the description of the motion.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0339","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A runner completes one full lap of a 400 m track in 50 s. Her average velocity for the lap is","o":["8 m/s","0 m/s","4 m/s","400 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"Displacement after a full lap is zero, so average velocity = 0/50 = 0. Her average speed would be 400/50 = 8 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0340","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"Which suvat equation does NOT contain time t?","o":["v² = u² + 2as","v = u + at","s = ut + ½at²","s = ½(u + v)t"],"a":0,"e":"v² = u² + 2as links u, v, a and s only; the others all contain t.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0341","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"Which suvat equation does NOT contain the acceleration a?","o":["v = u + at","v² = u² + 2as","s = ½(u + v)t","s = ut + ½at²"],"a":2,"e":"s = ½(u + v)t uses the average velocity (u + v)/2 times t, so a is not needed.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0342","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"On a velocity-time graph, the slope of the line gives the","o":["displacement","distance","average speed","acceleration"],"a":3,"e":"Slope = Δv/Δt = acceleration; the area under the graph gives displacement.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0343","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"On a displacement-time graph, the slope at an instant gives the","o":["acceleration","displacement","instantaneous velocity","distance"],"a":2,"e":"Slope = ds/dt = velocity.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0344","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"Using the chain rule, acceleration can be written as a = v dv/ds. This follows from","o":["a = dv/dt = (dv/ds)(ds/dt) with ds/dt = v","a = ds/dt multiplied by v","v = dv/dt divided by ds","integrating v = ds/dt with respect to s"],"a":0,"e":"dv/dt = (dv/ds)(ds/dt) and ds/dt = v, giving a = v dv/ds.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0345","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"For a given launch speed, the launch angle that gives the maximum range on level ground is","o":["30°","60°","90°","45°"],"a":3,"e":"R = u² sin 2θ / g is largest when sin 2θ = 1, i.e. θ = 45°.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0346","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves on a circle of radius r at speed v. Its radial (centripetal) acceleration is","o":["v/r","v²/r","vr","v²r"],"a":1,"e":"aᵣ = v²/r (equivalently ω²r), directed toward the centre.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0347","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"The area under an acceleration-time graph represents","o":["the change in velocity","the displacement","the change in acceleration","the average speed"],"a":0,"e":"∫a dt = Δv.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0348","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown vertically upward. At its highest point, its velocity and acceleration are (up positive, g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["v = 0 and a = 0","v = 0 and a = −9.8 m/s²","v = 9.8 m/s and a = 0","v = 0 and a = +9.8 m/s²"],"a":1,"e":"At the top velocity is zero for an instant, but gravity still acts, so a = −g. Trap: zero velocity does not mean zero acceleration.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0349","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A stone is thrown straight up, rises 20 m and falls back to the thrower's hand. Its distance travelled and displacement are","o":["20 m and 20 m","0 and 40 m","40 m and 40 m","40 m and 0"],"a":3,"e":"Distance = 20 up + 20 down = 40 m; displacement = final − initial position = 0.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0350","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A displacement-time graph is a curve that bends upward (getting steeper). The body is","o":["moving at constant velocity","slowing down","speeding up","at rest"],"a":2,"e":"Increasing slope of the s-t graph means increasing velocity, i.e. acceleration.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0351","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A velocity-time graph is a straight line sloping downward but staying above the time axis. The body is","o":["moving backward and speeding up","moving forward and slowing down","moving at constant velocity","at rest"],"a":1,"e":"Velocity stays positive (moving forward) while the negative slope shows deceleration.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0352","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A heavy ball and a light ball are dropped together from the same height in a vacuum. They","o":["hit the ground at the same time","land with the heavy ball first","land with the light ball first","land at the same time only if they have equal radius"],"a":0,"e":"Free-fall acceleration g is independent of mass, so they land together.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0353","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"At the highest point of a projectile's path (launch angle θ > 0, no air resistance), the velocity is","o":["zero","u sin θ, vertical","u cos θ, horizontal","u, at angle θ"],"a":2,"e":"The vertical component vanishes at the top but the horizontal component u cos θ is unchanged.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0354","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"Two projectiles are launched with the same speed, one at 30° and one at 60°. Their ranges are","o":["greater for 30°","greater for 60°","zero for both","equal"],"a":3,"e":"R = u² sin 2θ / g, and sin 60° = sin 120°. Complementary angles give equal ranges.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0355","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"Inside a train moving at constant velocity, a passenger tosses a coin straight up. Relative to the passenger, the coin","o":["lands behind the passenger","moves straight up and down and returns to the hand","lands in front of the passenger","follows a parabola"],"a":1,"e":"The coin keeps the train's horizontal velocity, so in the train frame it moves vertically only.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0356","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves uniformly (constant speed) around a circle. Its tangential and radial accelerations are","o":["tangential zero, radial v²/r toward the centre","tangential v²/r, radial zero","both zero","both equal to v²/r"],"a":0,"e":"Constant speed means dv/dt = 0, so aₜ = 0; direction still changes, giving aᵣ = v²/r.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0357","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has acceleration a = 2t (m/s²). Why can the equation v = u + at NOT be used directly?","o":["Because the motion is not in a straight line","Because the velocity is zero","Because the acceleration is not constant","Because time is not constant"],"a":2,"e":"The suvat equations need constant acceleration. Here v = u + ∫2t dt = u + t².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0358","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A stone is dropped from a cliff while another stone is thrown horizontally from the same height at the same instant. They","o":["reach the ground at different times, with the dropped one first","reach the ground at different times, with the thrown one first","reach the ground together only if the speed is 9.8 m/s","reach the ground at the same time"],"a":3,"e":"Vertical motion is identical for both (zero initial vertical velocity); horizontal speed does not affect fall time.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0359","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A body is momentarily at rest (v = 0). Which statement must be true?","o":["Its acceleration is zero","Its acceleration is positive","Nothing can be concluded about its acceleration","Its acceleration is constant"],"a":2,"e":"At the top of a throw, v = 0 yet a = −g. Zero velocity does not imply zero acceleration.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0360","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A car starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 3 m/s² for 8 s. How far does it travel?","o":["96 m","24 m","192 m","72 m"],"a":0,"e":"s = ut + ½at² = 0 + ½(3)(8)² = 96 m. Forgetting the ½ gives 192 m; 24 m is the final speed.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0361","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A car travelling at 20 m/s brakes uniformly and stops in 50 m. Its acceleration is","o":["−2 m/s²","−4 m/s²","−8 m/s²","−0.4 m/s²"],"a":1,"e":"v² = u² + 2as: 0 = 400 + 2a(50) so a = −400/100 = −4 m/s².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0362","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A stone is dropped from rest from a height of 45 m. Taking g = 10 m/s², the time to reach the ground is","o":["4.5 s","9 s","1.5 s","3 s"],"a":3,"e":"s = ½gt²: 45 = 5t² so t² = 9 and t = 3 s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0363","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown vertically upward at 15 m/s. Taking g = 9.8 m/s², the maximum height is","o":["23.0 m","11.5 m","7.7 m","1.53 m"],"a":1,"e":"H = u²/(2g) = 225/19.6 = 11.5 m. Forgetting the 2 gives 23.0 m; 1.53 s is the time to the top.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0364","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown vertically upward at 24.5 m/s. Taking g = 9.8 m/s², the total time before it returns to the thrower's hand is","o":["2.5 s","10 s","5.0 s","2.0 s"],"a":2,"e":"T = 2u/g = 2 × 24.5/9.8 = 5.0 s. 2.5 s is only the time to the top.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0365","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is kicked with speed 20 m/s at 30° above the horizontal on level ground (g = 10 m/s²). Its range is about","o":["40 m","20 m","17.3 m","34.6 m"],"a":3,"e":"R = u² sin 2θ / g = 400 × sin 60°/10 = 34.6 m. Using sin 30° instead gives 20 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0366","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A projectile is launched at 40 m/s at 30° above the horizontal (g = 10 m/s²). Its maximum height is","o":["20 m","80 m","40 m","10 m"],"a":0,"e":"H = (u sin θ)²/(2g) = (20)²/20 = 20 m. Using u² / 2g without sin gives 80 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0367","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A projectile is launched at 50 m/s at an angle θ with sin θ = 0.6 (g = 10 m/s²). Its time of flight on level ground is","o":["3 s","8 s","6 s","10 s"],"a":2,"e":"T = 2u sin θ / g = 2 × 50 × 0.6/10 = 6 s. Using cos θ = 0.8 gives 8 s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0368","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown horizontally at 15 m/s from the top of an 80 m cliff (g = 10 m/s²). How far from the base of the cliff does it land?","o":["15 m","60 m","120 m","80 m"],"a":1,"e":"Fall time t = √(2h/g) = √16 = 4 s. Horizontal distance = 15 × 4 = 60 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0369","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"The position of a particle is x = 3t² + 2t (x in m, t in s). Its velocity at t = 2 s is","o":["14 m/s","16 m/s","12 m/s","8 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"v = dx/dt = 6t + 2 = 6(2) + 2 = 14 m/s. 16 is the position (12 + 4).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0370","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle starts from rest at s = 0 with acceleration a = 6t (m/s²). Its velocity at t = 3 s is","o":["18 m/s","54 m/s","9 m/s","27 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"v = ∫6t dt = 3t² = 27 m/s at t = 3 s. Using v = at with a = 18 gives 54 m/s (invalid, a not constant).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0371","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle has velocity v = 3t² − 2t (m/s) and is at s = 0 at t = 0. Its displacement at t = 2 s is","o":["8 m","8 m/s","4 m","12 m"],"a":2,"e":"s = ∫(3t² − 2t) dt = t³ − t² = 8 − 4 = 4 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0372","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves so that its velocity is v = 2s (v in m/s, s in m). Its acceleration at s = 3 m is","o":["12 m/s²","6 m/s²","2 m/s²","36 m/s²"],"a":0,"e":"a = v dv/ds = (2s)(2) = 4s = 12 m/s² at s = 3. Using a = dv/ds alone gives 2 m/s².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0373","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"Car A moves east at 25 m/s and car B moves west at 15 m/s. The speed of A relative to B is","o":["10 m/s","25 m/s","15 m/s","40 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"v_AB = v_A − v_B = 25 − (−15) = 40 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0374","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves on a circle at a constant 6 m/s and radius 3 m. Its radial acceleration is","o":["2 m/s²","12 m/s²","18 m/s²","6 m/s²"],"a":1,"e":"aᵣ = v²/r = 36/3 = 12 m/s². Dividing v by r gives 2 m/s².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0375","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"On a circle a particle has tangential acceleration 4 m/s² and radial acceleration 3 m/s² at some instant. The magnitude of its total acceleration is","o":["5 m/s²","7 m/s²","1 m/s²","3.5 m/s²"],"a":0,"e":"a = √(4² + 3²) = 5 m/s². The components are perpendicular, so they add in quadrature, not directly.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0376","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A body speeds up from rest to 10 m/s in 5 s and then moves at constant velocity for 10 s. The total displacement is","o":["150 m","100 m","75 m","125 m"],"a":3,"e":"Area: triangle ½ × 5 × 10 = 25 m plus rectangle 10 × 10 = 100 m, total 125 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0377","c":"PHY","u":"Kinematics","s":"mcq","q":"A man walks 30 m east and then 40 m north in 10 s. The magnitude of his average velocity is","o":["7 m/s","3 m/s","5 m/s","4 m/s"],"a":2,"e":"Displacement = √(30² + 40²) = 50 m. Average velocity = 50/10 = 5 m/s (average speed would be 70/10 = 7 m/s).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0378","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Mechanics is best defined as the study of","o":["the motion of objects and the forces acting on them","the flow of heat between bodies","the structure of atoms and nuclei","the behaviour of light and sound waves"],"a":0,"e":"Mechanics deals with motion and the forces that cause or change it. Heat, atoms and waves belong to other branches of physics.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0379","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Newtonian mechanics is the branch of mechanics based on","o":["the quantisation of energy levels","the application of Newton's laws of motion","the constancy of the speed of light for all observers","the statistical behaviour of gas molecules only"],"a":1,"e":"Newtonian (classical) mechanics applies Newton's laws, with gravitation, to bodies much larger than atoms and much slower than light.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0380","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"In the module, motion is described as","o":["a change in the mass of an object with time","the force needed to keep an object still","a change in position or direction of an object with time","the total distance between two fixed points"],"a":2,"e":"Motion is the change in position (or direction) of an object considered against time. Mass change and force are not the definition.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0381","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Which pair of quantities is used to describe a change in position?","o":["Mass and weight","Force and pressure","Charge and current","Distance and displacement"],"a":3,"e":"Distance (total path length) and displacement (straight-line change in position) both describe change of position.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0382","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"The module classifies types of motion as","o":["linear, thermal and magnetic","linear, rotary and oscillatory","rotary, elastic and nuclear","oscillatory, chemical and linear"],"a":1,"e":"Linear (rectilinear or curvilinear), rotary and oscillatory are the three types listed. The other options mix in non-motion words.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0383","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A car driving along a perfectly straight road shows","o":["curvilinear motion","rotary motion","rectilinear motion","oscillatory motion"],"a":2,"e":"A straight path makes it rectilinear (a kind of linear motion). Curvilinear would need a curved path.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0384","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A cricket ball thrown at an angle follows a curved path under gravity. This is","o":["rectilinear motion","oscillatory motion","rotary motion about its own axis only","curvilinear motion"],"a":3,"e":"The path is a parabola, so the motion is curvilinear. The trap is to call it rotary because the ball may also spin.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0385","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"The daily spinning of the Earth about its own axis is an example of","o":["rotary motion","oscillatory motion","rectilinear motion","curvilinear translation only"],"a":0,"e":"Rotation about the body's own axis is rotary motion. Going round the Sun is a separate motion (revolution).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0386","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"The bob of a simple pendulum swinging to and fro about its lowest point is an example of","o":["rotary motion","rectilinear motion","oscillatory motion","uniform linear motion"],"a":2,"e":"To-and-fro motion about a mean position is oscillatory. The bob's path is curved but it repeatedly passes through the mean position.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0387","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is an example of oscillatory motion?","o":["A train moving on a straight track","The Earth rotating on its axis","A stone falling freely","The vibrating string of a guitar"],"a":3,"e":"Guitar strings vibrate to and fro about their rest position. The others are rectilinear, rotary and rectilinear.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0388","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A wheel of a moving vehicle (about its own axle) undergoes","o":["rotary motion","only rectilinear motion","only oscillatory motion","no motion"],"a":0,"e":"A wheel turns about its axle, which is rotary motion. Its axle also moves in a line, so the wheel's overall motion is combined.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0389","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"An object of ordinary size that contains a huge number of molecules is called a","o":["microscopic system","macroscopic system","isolated particle","quantum state"],"a":1,"e":"Ordinary-sized objects (about 10²³ molecules or more) are macroscopic systems, described by collective quantities.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0390","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is an example of a system of particles?","o":["A single electron in vacuum treated alone","A single point of zero mass","A geometrical line","The solar system"],"a":3,"e":"Atoms and molecules, galaxies and the solar system are the module's examples: collections of many interacting objects.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0391","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"An isolated system is one in which","o":["there is no exchange of matter or energy with the surroundings","matter is exchanged but not energy","energy is exchanged but not matter","both matter and energy are freely exchanged"],"a":0,"e":"Isolated means no external interaction at all. Energy-only exchange is a closed system; exchange of both is an open system.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0392","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A closed system allows exchange of","o":["matter only, not energy","energy only, not matter","both matter and energy","neither matter nor energy"],"a":1,"e":"Closed: no matter exchange but energy can cross the boundary (a sealed pot being heated).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0393","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"An open pot of boiling water is best described as","o":["an isolated system","a closed system","an open system","a system of zero mass"],"a":2,"e":"Steam (matter) and heat (energy) both leave, so both are exchanged: an open system.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0394","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is NOT one of the general laws governing systems of particles listed in the module?","o":["Ohm's law","Law of conservation of momentum","Law of conservation of energy","Law of conservation of angular momentum"],"a":0,"e":"Ohm's law is a circuit relation. The module lists Newton's laws and the conservation of momentum, energy and angular momentum.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0395","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"The centre of mass of a system is best described as","o":["the point where the heaviest particle sits","the point where the whole mass can be considered concentrated when analysing translational motion","the geometric midpoint of the system whatever the masses","the point where the net external force is zero"],"a":1,"e":"COM is the mass-weighted average position. It is not the midpoint unless the masses are equal, and it need not coincide with any particle.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0396","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"For n particles, the position vector of the centre of mass is","o":["r_cm = Σ rᵢ / n","r_cm = Σ mᵢ rᵢ / n","r_cm = Σ mᵢrᵢ / Σ mᵢ","r_cm = Σ mᵢ / Σ rᵢ"],"a":2,"e":"Weight each position by its mass and divide by the TOTAL mass. Dividing by n gives a simple average (right only for equal masses).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0397","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"For two particles of unequal mass, the centre of mass lies","o":["closer to the lighter particle","exactly midway","outside the line joining them","closer to the heavier particle"],"a":3,"e":"From m₁d₁ = m₂d₂, the larger mass has the smaller distance. The COM always lies on the line between two particles.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0398","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"The centre of mass of a uniform thin rod is at","o":["one third of its length from one end","its midpoint","one quarter of its length from one end","its end"],"a":1,"e":"Symmetry places the COM of a uniform rod at its midpoint.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0399","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"The centre of mass of a uniform circular ring is located","o":["on the rim","halfway between the centre and the rim","at its geometric centre, where there is no material","at a point depending on the ring's mass"],"a":2,"e":"Symmetry puts it at the centre of the circle, which lies in empty space: the COM need not be inside the body.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0400","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"The centre of mass of a uniform triangular lamina lies","o":["half of the height above the base","two thirds of the height above the base","at the vertex","one third of the height above the base"],"a":3,"e":"The COM is at the centroid, one third of the height above the base (two thirds from the apex).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0401","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"The centre of mass moves as if","o":["all the mass were concentrated there and all the external forces acted on it","only the internal forces acted on it","all the mass were at the heaviest particle","no force acted on any part"],"a":0,"e":"M a_cm = F_ext. Internal forces cancel in pairs and do not affect the COM.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0402","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Internal forces between the particles of a system","o":["always change the acceleration of the centre of mass","double the total momentum","cannot change the acceleration of the centre of mass","are the only forces that matter for a_cm"],"a":2,"e":"Internal forces occur in equal opposite pairs (third law) and sum to zero, so only external forces enter M a_cm = F_ext.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0403","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"If no external force acts on a system whose centre of mass is initially at rest, the centre of mass","o":["moves with increasing speed","moves in a circle","moves with constant acceleration","remains at rest"],"a":3,"e":"a_cm = F_ext/M = 0, so the COM velocity stays zero.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0404","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"If no external force acts on a system whose centre of mass is moving, the centre of mass","o":["moves with constant velocity","slows to rest","accelerates uniformly","oscillates"],"a":0,"e":"a_cm = 0 means v_cm is constant in magnitude and direction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0405","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A shell at rest explodes into fragments in empty space. The centre of mass of the fragments","o":["moves in the direction of the heaviest fragment","stays at the original position","moves in the direction of the fastest fragment","moves in a parabola"],"a":1,"e":"The explosion forces are internal, and no external force acts, so momentum is zero before and after and the COM remains at rest.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0406","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A projectile explodes in mid-air into several fragments. Until the first fragment lands, the centre of mass of the fragments","o":["moves vertically downwards","moves in a straight line at constant velocity","stops at the point of explosion","continues along the original parabolic path"],"a":3,"e":"Gravity is the only external force, as before the explosion; the explosion forces are internal. The COM path is unchanged.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0407","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"The centre of gravity coincides with the centre of mass when","o":["the gravitational field is uniform over the body","the body is rotating","the body is in free fall in space far from any mass","the body is non-uniform in density"],"a":0,"e":"CG uses weights mᵢg. If g is the same everywhere over the body it cancels from the average, giving the COM.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0408","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"The SI unit of the position of the centre of mass is the","o":["kilogram","metre","newton","kilogram metre"],"a":1,"e":"r_cm is a length: a mass-weighted average of positions, since the masses cancel in Σmr/Σm.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0409","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A man walks along a boat at rest on calm water (no horizontal water resistance). The centre of mass of the man-boat system","o":["moves in the direction the man walks","moves opposite to the man's walk","does not move horizontally","moves with the boat's speed"],"a":2,"e":"The man's push on the boat is internal and there is no horizontal external force, so the COM stays put; the boat moves back as he moves forward.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0410","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 2 kg mass is at x = 0 and a 6 kg mass at x = 4 m. The centre of mass is at x =","o":["3 m","2.0 m","1.0 m","2.4 m"],"a":0,"e":"x_cm = (2×0 + 6×4)/(2+6) = 24/8 = 3.0 m. The simple midpoint (2 m) ignores the masses.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0411","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Masses of 3 kg and 1 kg are at x = 0 and x = 4 m. How far from the 3 kg mass is the centre of mass?","o":["2.0 m","1 m","3.0 m","0.5 m"],"a":1,"e":"x_cm = (3×0 + 1×4)/4 = 1.0 m from the heavy mass; check 3×1 = 1×3.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0412","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Two 5 kg particles are at x = −2 m and x = +6 m. The centre of mass is at x =","o":["4.0 m","2.0 m","2 m","−2.0 m"],"a":2,"e":"x_cm = (5(−2) + 5(6))/10 = 20/10 = 2.0 m, the midpoint of the two positions (equal masses).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0413","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Masses 1 kg, 2 kg and 3 kg are at (0, 0), (3 m, 0) and (0, 4 m) respectively. The centre of mass is at","o":["(1.5 m, 2 m)","(2 m, 1 m)","(3 m, 4 m)","(1 m, 2 m)"],"a":3,"e":"x_cm = (0 + 6 + 0)/6 = 1 m; y_cm = (0 + 0 + 12)/6 = 2 m. Handle each coordinate separately.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0414","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 2 kg mass is at (1 m, 2 m) and a 3 kg mass at (4 m, −1 m). The y-coordinate of the centre of mass is","o":["0.5 m","0.2 m","1.5 m","−0.2 m"],"a":1,"e":"y_cm = (2×2 + 3×(−1))/5 = 1/5 = 0.2 m. The negative y of the second mass reduces the sum.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0415","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"For the same masses (2 kg at (1 m, 2 m), 3 kg at (4 m, −1 m)), the x-coordinate of the centre of mass is","o":["2.5 m","1.2 m","2.8 m","3.0 m"],"a":2,"e":"x_cm = (2×1 + 3×4)/5 = 14/5 = 2.8 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0416","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Masses 1 kg, 2 kg and 3 kg are at (0,0,0), (3,0,0) and (0,3,3) (metres). The z-coordinate of the centre of mass is","o":["3.0 m","1.0 m","0.5 m","1.5 m"],"a":3,"e":"z_cm = (1×0 + 2×0 + 3×3)/6 = 9/6 = 1.5 m (the y-coordinate is also 1.5 m).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0417","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Masses 1 kg, 1 kg and 2 kg are at x = 0, 4 m and 2 m respectively. The centre of mass is at x =","o":["2 m","1.0 m","2.5 m","3.0 m"],"a":0,"e":"x_cm = (0 + 4 + 2×2)/4 = 2.0 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0418","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 2 kg mass is at x = 0 and an unknown mass m at x = 3 m. The centre of mass is at x = 1 m. What is m?","o":["2 kg","0.5 kg","1 kg","3 kg"],"a":2,"e":"1 = 3m/(2 + m) gives 2 + m = 3m, so m = 1 kg.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0419","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 3 kg mass is at x = 0. Where must a 1 kg mass be placed so that the centre of mass is at x = 1 m?","o":["x = 1 m","x = 3 m","x = 2 m","x = 4 m"],"a":3,"e":"1 = (3×0 + 1×x)/4 gives x = 4 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0420","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A uniform 1.0 m rod of mass 0.40 kg has a 0.10 kg lump at one end. How far from the lump is the centre of mass?","o":["0.4 m","0.5 m","0.25 m","0.6 m"],"a":0,"e":"x_cm = (0.40×0.5 + 0.10×0)/0.50 = 0.4 m from the lump end.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0421","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A uniform metre rule of mass 200 g balances at 50 cm. A 100 g mass is clamped at the 90 cm mark. The new centre of mass is at","o":["70.0 cm","63.3 cm","60.0 cm","66.7 cm"],"a":1,"e":"x_cm = (200×50 + 100×90)/300 = 19000/300 = 63.3 cm.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0422","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"An L-shape is made of two uniform rods of the same material: 4 m along the x-axis and 2 m along the y-axis, joined at the origin. The x-coordinate of its centre of mass is","o":["1.00 m","2.00 m","0.67 m","1.33 m"],"a":3,"e":"Mass is proportional to length: 4 and 2 units. x_cm = (4×2 + 2×0)/6 = 8/6 = 1.33 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0423","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"For the same L-shape (4 m rod on the x-axis, 2 m rod on the y-axis), the y-coordinate of the centre of mass is","o":["0.33 m","0.67 m","1.00 m","1.33 m"],"a":0,"e":"y_cm = (4×0 + 2×1)/6 = 2/6 = 0.33 m, since the vertical rod's COM is at y = 1 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0424","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A thin rod of length 3.0 m has linear density λ = kx (x measured from one end). Its centre of mass is at x =","o":["1.5 m","2 m","1.0 m","2.25 m"],"a":1,"e":"x_cm = ∫x·kx dx/∫kx dx = (L³/3)/(L²/2) = 2L/3 = 2.0 m. Heavier far end, so beyond the midpoint.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0425","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A uniform disc of radius 12 cm has a circular hole of radius 6 cm cut out, its centre 6 cm from the disc's centre. How far is the new centre of mass from the disc's centre, and on which side?","o":["2 cm, on the side of the hole","6 cm, on the side away from the hole","2 cm, on the side away from the hole","3 cm, on the side away from the hole"],"a":2,"e":"Treat the hole as negative mass. Hole area fraction = (6/12)² = 1/4. Shift = (1/4)(6)/(3/4) = 2 cm away from the hole.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0426","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"The Earth (5.97×10²⁴ kg) and Moon (7.35×10²² kg) are 3.84×10⁸ m apart (centre to centre). How far from the Earth's centre is their common centre of mass?","o":["4.67×10⁶ m","3.84×10⁸ m","1.92×10⁸ m","4.67×10⁷ m"],"a":0,"e":"d₁ = d·m_M/(m_E + m_M) = 3.84×10⁸ × 7.35×10²²/6.04×10²⁴ = 4.67×10⁶ m, inside the Earth (radius 6.4×10⁶ m).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0427","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"In a carbon monoxide molecule the C (12 u) and O (16 u) nuclei are 1.13 Å apart. How far is the centre of mass from the carbon nucleus?","o":["0.48 Å","0.65 Å","0.565 Å","0.80 Å"],"a":1,"e":"d_C = d·m_O/(m_C + m_O) = 1.13×16/28 = 0.646 Å. The COM is nearer the heavier oxygen, so it is further from the lighter carbon (0.48 Å would be the distance from oxygen).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0428","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 1 kg ball is at height 0 and a 3 kg ball at height 8 m. The height of their centre of mass is","o":["4 m","2 m","6 m","7 m"],"a":2,"e":"h_cm = (1×0 + 3×8)/4 = 6 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0429","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Two skaters of mass 60 kg and 40 kg stand 10 m apart on frictionless ice and pull on a rope between them. How far does the 60 kg skater move before they meet?","o":["6 m","5 m","10 m","4 m"],"a":3,"e":"They meet at the COM, which is (40×10)/100 = 4 m from the 60 kg skater (the heavier one moves less).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0430","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 3 kg block moves at 2 m/s and a 1 kg block at 6 m/s in the same direction. The speed of the centre of mass is","o":["4 m/s","3 m/s","2 m/s","8 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"v_cm = (3×2 + 1×6)/4 = 12/4 = 3 m/s. The simple average (4 m/s) ignores the masses.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0431","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 3 kg particle moves at +4 m/s and a 2 kg particle at −1 m/s along a line. The total momentum is","o":["14 kg m/s","12 kg m/s","10 kg m/s","2 kg m/s"],"a":2,"e":"P = 3×4 + 2×(−1) = 10 kg m/s, so v_cm = 10/5 = 2 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0432","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"For the same two particles (3 kg at +4 m/s, 2 kg at −1 m/s), the velocity of the centre of mass is","o":["2.5 m/s","1.5 m/s","10 m/s","2 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"v_cm = P/M = 10/5 = 2 m/s. Dividing by the number of particles (2) would give the wrong 5 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0433","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A system of total mass 10 kg has its centre of mass moving at 2 m/s. Its total momentum is","o":["20 kg m/s","5 kg m/s","40 kg m/s","12 kg m/s"],"a":0,"e":"P = M v_cm = 10×2 = 20 kg m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0434","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Two blocks of mass 2 kg and 3 kg are joined by a light string on a smooth horizontal table. A horizontal 10 N force pulls the 3 kg block directly away from the other. The acceleration of their centre of mass is","o":["3.3 m/s²","5 m/s²","2 m/s²","0.5 m/s²"],"a":2,"e":"M a_cm = F_ext: a_cm = 10/5 = 2 m/s². Both blocks share the same acceleration; the string tension is internal.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0435","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 2 kg body accelerates at 3 m/s² and a 4 kg body at the same instant has zero acceleration. The acceleration of their centre of mass is","o":["1.5 m/s²","3 m/s²","0.75 m/s²","1 m/s²"],"a":3,"e":"a_cm = Σmᵢaᵢ/M = (2×3 + 4×0)/6 = 1 m/s². Equivalent to F_ext = 6 N on 6 kg.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0436","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A system of mass 10 kg, initially at rest, experiences a constant net external force of 20 N for 4 s. The speed of its centre of mass is then","o":["8 m/s","2 m/s","80 m/s","5 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"a_cm = 20/10 = 2 m/s²; v = at = 2×4 = 8 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0437","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 6 kg shell at rest explodes into a 4 kg piece and a 2 kg piece. The 2 kg piece moves at 12 m/s. The speed of the 4 kg piece is","o":["3 m/s","6 m/s","12 m/s","24 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"0 = 4v + 2×12 gives v = −6 m/s: 6 m/s in the opposite direction. Equal and opposite momenta (24 kg m/s each).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0438","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 4 kg gun fires a 0.02 kg bullet at 400 m/s. The recoil speed of the gun is","o":["0.5 m/s","8 m/s","1 m/s","2 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"Momentum conservation: 4×v = 0.02×400 = 8, so v = 2 m/s, opposite to the bullet.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0439","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 50 kg man stands in a 100 kg canoe at rest on still water and walks 3 m towards the bow relative to the canoe. How far does the canoe move relative to the water?","o":["1 m backwards","3 m backwards","0.5 m backwards","2 m backwards"],"a":0,"e":"No external horizontal force: 50(3 − x) = 100x, so x = 150/150 = 1 m backwards (x = md/(M+m)).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0440","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"In the same situation (50 kg man, 100 kg canoe, 3 m walk relative to the canoe), how far does the man move relative to the water?","o":["3 m forwards","2 m forwards","1 m forwards","1.5 m forwards"],"a":1,"e":"The relative displacement is 3 m = man's + canoe's: man = 3 − 1 = 2 m forwards (check 50×2 = 100×1).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0441","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A shell would land 200 m away on level ground. At the top of its flight it explodes into two equal fragments; one falls vertically from the point of explosion (100 m horizontally). Where does the other land?","o":["200 m","400 m","300 m from the launch point","150 m"],"a":2,"e":"The COM still lands at 200 m: (100 + x)/2 = 200 gives x = 300 m. (Ignoring air resistance, same landing level.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0442","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Three equal masses sit at the corners of an equilateral triangle of side 6 m. How far is the centre of mass above the base?","o":["1.73 m","2.60 m","3.46 m","5.20 m"],"a":0,"e":"Height = 6·√3/2 = 5.196 m; the COM is one third of that: 1.73 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0443","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Four equal masses sit at the corners of a square of side 2 m. Taking one corner as origin with sides along the axes, the centre of mass is at","o":["(2 m, 2 m)","(1 m, 1 m)","(0.5 m, 0.5 m)","(0 m, 0 m)"],"a":1,"e":"x_cm = (0 + 2 + 0 + 2)/4 = 1 m and y_cm = 1 m: the centre of the square, as symmetry demands.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0444","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"Two particles of equal mass m are at x = −a and x = +3a. Where is their centre of mass?","o":["x = +2a","x = 0","x = +a","x = +1.5a"],"a":2,"e":"x_cm = (m(−a) + m(3a))/2m = a.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0445","c":"PHY","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass","s":"mcq","q":"A 4 kg mass is at the origin and a 1 kg mass at (5 m, 5 m). The y-coordinate of the centre of mass is","o":["2.5 m","4 m","5 m","1 m"],"a":3,"e":"y_cm = (4×0 + 1×5)/5 = 1 m: close to the heavy mass at the origin.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0446","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Calculate the force acting on a body of mass 5 kg that will produce a velocity of 10 m/s in time 5s.","o":["10N","20N","15N","30N"],"a":0,"e":"From rest, a = (v − u)/t = 10/5 = 2 m/s². F = ma = 5 × 2 = 10 N. Trap: using F = mv = 50 N or t = 10.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q12"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0447","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A body of mass 5 kg is acted upon by a horizontal force of 20N. Calculate the acceleration produced if the first force is opposed by the second force of 5N.","o":["5.0ms-2","6.0 ms-2","3.0 ms-2","2.0 ms-2"],"a":2,"e":"Net force = 20 − 5 = 15 N, so a = 15/5 = 3.0 m/s². Trap: dividing 20 N by 5 kg gives 4, or adding the forces gives 5.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q13"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0448","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A block of mass 4 kg is released from rest on a frictionless incline plane at an angle of 30° with the horizontal. What is its acceleration down the plane?","o":["5.0 ms-2","3.0 ms-2","2.0 ms-2","6.0 ms-2"],"a":0,"e":"a = g sinθ = g sin30° = g/2. With g = 10 m/s² this is 5.0 m/s² (with g = 9.8 it is 4.9, still nearest to 5.0). The mass of 4 kg cancels.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q15"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0449","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"When the elevator is moving down with acceleration 'a', the reaction of the elevator floor on the man's feet is given as:","o":["R < 0","R > 0","R = 0","None of the Above"],"a":1,"e":"Downward acceleration a gives R = m(g − a). This is positive whenever a < g (R is never negative). R = 0 only in free fall (a = g).","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q16"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0450","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The following statements are true about Newton's laws of motion except","o":["It implies the idea of inertia","It involves the mutual interaction","The mass (m) of a body is the ratio of force to acceleration","The net force is zero"],"a":3,"e":"Inertia (first law), mutual interaction (third law) and m = F/a (second law) are all parts of Newton's laws. 'The net force is zero' is only the special condition of the first law, not a general statement of the laws.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q26"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0451","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Newton's laws of motion were published in the year?","o":["1687","1787","1887","1987"],"a":0,"e":"Newton published the three laws in the Principia Mathematica in 1687.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q7"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0452","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 50 kg box is placed on a flat surface. A rope is attached to the box and pulled to move it. The rope is pulled at an angle of 25 degrees to the horizontal, and the box accelerates at 2 m/s². Calculate: a) The force applied to the rope b) The torque produced by this force if the rope is 1.5 meters long the force is applied perpendicular to the rope.","o":["100N / 63.39Nm","100N / 70.1Nm","110.4N / 63.39Nm","110.4N / 70.1Nm"],"a":3,"e":"With no friction, F cos25° = ma = 100 N, so F = 100/cos25° = 110.3 ≈ 110.4 N. For the torque the options use τ = F sin25° × 1.5 = 70.0 ≈ 70.1 N m. Option 110.4 N / 70.1 N m is the best match.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Rope and box (50 kg, 25°)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0453","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A body of mass m is moving with a velocity v in a straight line. If the mass and the velocity are both doubled, (i) find the ratio of the initial momentum to the final momentum, and (ii) find the ratio of the final kinetic energy to the initial kinetic energy.","o":["1 : 2 and 4 : 1","1 : 4 and 8 : 1","1 : 4 and 4 : 1","1 : 2 and 8 : 1"],"a":1,"e":"Initial p = mv; final p = (2m)(2v) = 4mv, so p(i) : p(f) = 1 : 4. Initial KE = ½mv²; final KE = ½(2m)(2v)² = 4mv², so KE(f) : KE(i) = 4mv² / (½mv²) = 8 : 1. Trap: forgetting that v is squared in KE gives 4 : 1.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Newton's laws class exercise 1"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0454","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The displacement of an object is proportional to the square of time. This implies that the object moves with increasing velocity. TRUE OR FALSE?","o":["False, the velocity stays constant","True, because v = ds/dt = 2kt increases with time","False, because displacement and velocity are unrelated","True, but only if the object starts from rest at a height"],"a":1,"e":"If s = kt², v = ds/dt = 2kt, which grows linearly with t. The velocity is increasing (constant acceleration 2k). TRUE.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Newton's laws class exercise 2"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0455","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A passenger in a moving bus is thrown forward when the bus suddenly stops. What law of motion explains this?","o":["Newton's first law (inertia)","Newton's second law","Newton's third law","Newton's law of gravitation"],"a":0,"e":"The passenger's body tends to keep its state of uniform motion while the bus stops: that is the law of inertia (first law).","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Newton's laws class exercise 3"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0456","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A ball of mass 200 g is moving with a speed of 30 m/s. When it strikes a wall, it returns along the same path with the same speed. If the time of contact is 0.2 s, what is the force exerted by the ball on the wall?","o":["30 N","120 N","60 N","6 N"],"a":2,"e":"m = 0.2 kg. Δp = m[30 − (−30)] = 0.2 × 60 = 12 kg m/s. F = Δp/Δt = 12/0.2 = 60 N. Trap: using Δv = 30 gives 30 N; forgetting grams to kg gives 60 000.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Newton's laws class exercise 4"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0457","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A bus starts suddenly and the passengers are pushed back. This is an example of which law of motion?","o":["Newton's third law (action and reaction)","Newton's second law (F = ma)","Newton's law of gravitation","Newton's first law (inertia)"],"a":3,"e":"The passengers' bodies tend to stay at rest while the bus moves forward, so they seem pushed back: the first law (inertia). They are not acted on by a backward force.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Newton's laws class exercise 5"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0458","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Which statement is Newton's first law?","o":["Force equals mass times acceleration","A body stays at rest or in uniform straight-line motion unless acted on by an external force","Every action has an equal and opposite reaction","Force is inversely proportional to the square of distance"],"a":1,"e":"The first law is the law of inertia. F = ma is the second law and action-reaction is the third.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0459","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Newton's second law in its most general form is","o":["F = mv","F = p × t","F = m/a","F = dp/dt"],"a":3,"e":"The net force equals the rate of change of momentum. F = ma is the special case of constant mass.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0460","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The two forces of an action-reaction pair","o":["act on different bodies and are equal and opposite","act on the same body and cancel","act on different bodies and always cancel","are equal in size but both act on the same body"],"a":0,"e":"Third-law pairs act on different bodies, so they cannot cancel. Trap: the weight and normal reaction on a book are not a pair.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0461","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A book rests on a table. Which force is the reaction partner (third-law pair) of the Earth's pull on the book?","o":["The normal force of the table on the book","The weight of the table","The book's gravitational pull on the Earth","The book's push on the table"],"a":2,"e":"The pair of 'Earth pulls book' is 'book pulls Earth'. The normal force balances the weight but acts on the same body, so is not its pair.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0462","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The SI unit of impulse is","o":["N m","N s⁻¹","kg m s⁻²","N s"],"a":3,"e":"J = FΔt, so the unit is N × s = kg m s⁻¹. N m is the unit of work or torque.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0463","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensions of impulse are","o":["[M L T⁻²]","[M L T⁻¹]","[M L² T⁻²]","[M L² T⁻¹]"],"a":1,"e":"Impulse = force × time = [M L T⁻²][T] = [M L T⁻¹], the same as momentum.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0464","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The area under a force-time graph represents","o":["work done","power","impulse","acceleration"],"a":2,"e":"∫F dt = impulse = change in momentum. Work is the area under a force-displacement graph.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0465","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Which is true about the relationship between force and impulse?","o":["They have different dimensions; impulse is force multiplied by the time of action","Force is always the same as impulse","Impulse is force divided by time","Force and impulse have the same unit"],"a":0,"e":"Force is [M L T⁻²], impulse [M L T⁻¹]. A force is not always an impulse; J = FΔt.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0466","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Impulse is equal to","o":["the change in kinetic energy","the product of mass and acceleration","the change in momentum of the body","the rate of change of momentum"],"a":2,"e":"J = FΔt = Δp. The rate of change of momentum is the force itself.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0467","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A cricketer draws his hands back while catching a fast ball. This","o":["reduces the momentum of the ball","increases the impulse needed to stop the ball","decreases the contact time so the force is smaller","increases the contact time so the average force is smaller"],"a":3,"e":"Δp is fixed by the ball's initial speed; F = Δp/Δt, so a longer Δt reduces F. The impulse is unchanged.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0468","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Mass is best described as","o":["a force that varies with g","a measure of inertia and a scalar that does not vary with location","a vector pointing downward","a quantity measured in newtons"],"a":1,"e":"Weight (a force) varies with g; mass is invariant. Mass is a scalar measured in kg.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0469","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"An astronaut travels from Earth to the Moon. Her","o":["mass stays the same and her weight decreases","mass decreases and weight stays the same","mass and weight both stay the same","mass and weight both decrease equally"],"a":0,"e":"W = mg and g on the Moon is about 1/6 of g on Earth. Mass is unchanged.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0470","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A dust-laden carpet is beaten and the dust falls out. This is mainly explained by","o":["action and reaction (third law)","the equations of motion at constant acceleration","impulse being zero","inertia (Newton's first law)"],"a":3,"e":"The carpet moves suddenly, the dust tends to stay at rest and so is left behind.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0471","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The normal reaction on a body from a surface acts","o":["parallel to the surface","along the direction of motion","perpendicular to the surface","vertically downward always"],"a":2,"e":"Normal means perpendicular. On a slope the normal reaction tilts with the surface; it is not always vertical.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0472","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The tension in a light string passing over a smooth pulley is","o":["larger on the heavier side","the same at every point along the string","zero if the masses are unequal","always equal to the weight of the lighter mass"],"a":1,"e":"A light string and smooth pulley transmit the same tension throughout. T lies between m₂g and m₁g.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0473","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A free-body diagram should show","o":["only the forces acting on the chosen body","all forces including those the body exerts on others","only the weight of the body","the velocity but no forces"],"a":0,"e":"Isolate the body; draw only the forces exerted on it. Forces it exerts on other bodies belong on their diagrams.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0474","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The equations of motion v = u + at, s = ut + ½at² and v² = u² + 2as are valid when","o":["the velocity is constant","the acceleration is constant","the force is zero","the motion is circular"],"a":1,"e":"They were derived assuming constant a. Variable acceleration needs calculus.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0475","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A body moving in a straight line is acted on by zero net force. It will","o":["slow down gradually","come to rest immediately","accelerate uniformly","continue with constant velocity"],"a":3,"e":"Zero net force implies zero acceleration (first law), so velocity (including zero) is constant.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0476","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A person in a lift feels weightless when the lift","o":["is in free fall","moves upward at constant speed","moves downward at constant speed","accelerates upward at g"],"a":0,"e":"Free fall means a = g downward, so R = m(g − g) = 0.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0477","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A lift moves upward but is slowing down. A passenger's apparent weight is","o":["greater than the true weight","equal to the true weight","less than the true weight","zero"],"a":2,"e":"Slowing while moving up means acceleration is downward, so R = m(g − a) < mg. Direction of velocity is irrelevant.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0478","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Which statement about force and acceleration is correct for a body of constant mass?","o":["Acceleration is opposite to the net force","Acceleration is perpendicular to the net force","Acceleration depends only on the velocity","Acceleration is in the direction of the net force"],"a":3,"e":"F = ma is a vector equation: a points along the net force.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0479","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Newton's laws of motion were first published in which book?","o":["Opticks (1704)","Principia Mathematica (1687)","Dialogue Concerning Two World Systems (1632)","Almagest (about 150 AD)"],"a":1,"e":"The three laws appeared in the Principia Mathematica in 1687.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0480","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Momentum is","o":["a scalar quantity with unit N","a vector quantity with unit kg m s⁻²","a vector quantity with unit kg m s⁻¹","a scalar quantity with unit J"],"a":2,"e":"p = mv has the direction of velocity; unit kg m s⁻¹ = N s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0481","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"On a smooth incline of angle θ, the component of weight acting down the slope is","o":["mg sinθ","mg cosθ","mg tanθ","mg"],"a":0,"e":"Along the plane: mg sinθ; perpendicular to it: mg cosθ. Check θ = 90°: all of mg acts along the 'slope'.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0482","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The acceleration of a body sliding down a smooth plane inclined at θ depends on","o":["mass and θ only","g, θ and the mass","g and θ only, not on the mass","the normal reaction only"],"a":2,"e":"ma = mg sinθ, so a = g sinθ; the mass cancels.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0483","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A net force acts on a 12 kg body and gives it an acceleration of 3.5 m/s². What is the force?","o":["3.43 N","15.5 N","411.6 N","42 N"],"a":3,"e":"F = ma = 12 × 3.5 = 42 N. Trap: dividing m by a, or multiplying by g.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0484","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Two horizontal forces of 30 N and 12 N act in opposite directions on a 4 kg block on a smooth table. What is its acceleration?","o":["7.5 m/s²","4.5 m/s²","10.5 m/s²","3 m/s²"],"a":1,"e":"Net force = 30 − 12 = 18 N, a = 18/4 = 4.5 m/s². Trap: using only one force or adding them.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0485","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"What is the weight of a 60 kg person on Earth? Take g = 9.8 m/s².","o":["588 N","60 N","6.12 N","98 N"],"a":0,"e":"W = mg = 60 × 9.8 = 588 N. Dividing by g is a common slip.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0486","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 70 kg astronaut stands on the Moon where g = 1.6 m/s². Her weight there is","o":["686 N","43.75 N","70 N","112 N"],"a":3,"e":"W = mg = 70 × 1.6 = 112 N. Her mass remains 70 kg.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0487","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A constant force of 50 N acts on a body for 0.4 s. What is the impulse?","o":["125 N s","50.4 N s","20 N s","2 N s"],"a":2,"e":"J = FΔt = 50 × 0.4 = 20 N s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0488","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 0.5 kg ball is at rest. It receives an impulse of 4 N s. What speed does it acquire?","o":["2 m/s","8 m/s","4 m/s","4.5 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"J = Δp = mv, so v = J/m = 4/0.5 = 8 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0489","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 0.16 kg cricket ball moving at 25 m/s is brought to rest by a fielder's hands in 0.05 s. What is the average force?","o":["80 N","4 N","0.2 N","8 N"],"a":0,"e":"Δp = 0.16 × 25 = 4 kg m/s; F = Δp/Δt = 4/0.05 = 80 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0490","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 1000 kg car moving at 20 m/s is braked uniformly to rest in 5 s. The braking force is","o":["20000 N","4000 N","250 N","4 N"],"a":1,"e":"a = 20/5 = 4 m/s², F = ma = 1000 × 4 = 4000 N (impulse: 20000 N s ÷ 5 s).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0491","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A triangular force pulse rises uniformly from 0 to 100 N and back to 0 over a total time of 0.2 s. The impulse is","o":["20 N s","500 N s","50 N s","10 N s"],"a":3,"e":"Impulse is the area of the triangle = ½ × 0.2 × 100 = 10 N s. Trap: using the area of the full rectangle.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0492","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A body moving at 5 m/s accelerates uniformly at 2 m/s² for 6 s. Its final velocity is","o":["17 m/s","60 m/s","12 m/s","5.33 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"v = u + at = 5 + 2 × 6 = 17 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0493","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A body starts from rest and accelerates at 3 m/s². How far does it travel in 4 s?","o":["12 m","48 m","24 m","6 m"],"a":2,"e":"s = ut + ½at² = 0 + ½ × 3 × 4² = 24 m. Trap: forgetting to square t or the ½.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0494","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A trolley moving at 10 m/s is brought to rest by a constant retardation of 2 m/s². The distance travelled before stopping is","o":["5 m","50 m","20 m","25 m"],"a":3,"e":"v² = u² + 2as gives 0 = 100 − 2(2)s, s = 100/4 = 25 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0495","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A car of mass 800 kg is pushed from rest by a net force of 2400 N. Distance covered in the first 10 s?","o":["300 m","150 m","120000 m","30 m"],"a":1,"e":"a = F/m = 2400/800 = 3 m/s²; s = ½at² = ½ × 3 × 100 = 150 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0496","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A block slides down a frictionless plane inclined at 45° to the horizontal. Take g = 9.8 m/s². Its acceleration is","o":["4.9 m/s²","9.8 m/s²","6.93 m/s²","3.46 m/s²"],"a":2,"e":"a = g sin45° = 9.8 × 0.7071 = 6.93 m/s². Option A (4.9) is g sin30°, a common mix-up of angles; option D (3.46) is g sin45° × ½, which is wrong. tan45° = 1 would give 9.8 and is also wrong.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0497","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A block is released from rest at the top of a smooth 30° slope 10 m long. Take g = 9.8 m/s². Its speed at the bottom is about","o":["9.9 m/s","14 m/s","4.9 m/s","49 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"a = g sin30° = 4.9 m/s²; v² = 2as = 2 × 4.9 × 10 = 98, v = 9.9 m/s. Using a = g gives 14 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0498","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 4 kg block rests on a smooth 30° incline. Take g = 9.8 m/s². The normal reaction is","o":["19.6 N","39.2 N","33.9 N","22.6 N"],"a":2,"e":"N = mg cosθ = 4 × 9.8 × 0.866 = 33.9 N. Trap: using sin gives 19.6 N, the down-slope component.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0499","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 60 kg person stands in a lift accelerating upward at 2 m/s². Take g = 9.8 m/s². The floor reaction is","o":["468 N","588 N","120 N","708 N"],"a":3,"e":"R = m(g + a) = 60 × 11.8 = 708 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0500","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The same 60 kg person is in a lift accelerating downward at 2 m/s². Take g = 9.8 m/s². The floor reaction is","o":["708 N","468 N","588 N","120 N"],"a":1,"e":"R = m(g − a) = 60 × 7.8 = 468 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0501","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A rope lifts a 5 kg load upward with acceleration 2 m/s². Take g = 9.8 m/s². The tension in the rope is","o":["59 N","49 N","39 N","10 N"],"a":0,"e":"T − mg = ma, so T = m(g + a) = 5 × 11.8 = 59 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0502","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 5 kg load hangs at rest from a light rope. Take g = 9.8 m/s². The tension is","o":["5 N","0.51 N","0 N","49 N"],"a":3,"e":"Equilibrium: T = mg = 5 × 9.8 = 49 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0503","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 75 kg person stands on a scale in a lift whose cable has snapped (free fall). Ignoring air resistance, the scale reads","o":["735 N","75 N","0 N","1470 N"],"a":2,"e":"R = m(g − a) with a = g gives zero. The person is apparently weightless.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0504","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"In an Atwood machine, masses of 5 kg and 3 kg hang over a light smooth pulley. Take g = 9.8 m/s². The acceleration is","o":["39.2 m/s²","2.45 m/s²","6.12 m/s²","19.6 m/s²"],"a":1,"e":"a = (m₁ − m₂)g/(m₁ + m₂) = 2 × 9.8/8 = 2.45 m/s².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0505","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"For the same Atwood machine (5 kg and 3 kg, g = 9.8 m/s²), the tension in the string is","o":["36.75 N","29.4 N","49 N","39.2 N"],"a":0,"e":"T = 2m₁m₂g/(m₁ + m₂) = 2 × 5 × 3 × 9.8/8 = 36.75 N. It lies between 29.4 N and 49 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0506","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"Blocks of 3 kg and 2 kg are joined by a light string on a smooth table. A horizontal force of 20 N pulls the 3 kg block, which drags the 2 kg block behind it. The tension in the string is","o":["20 N","8 N","12 N","6.67 N"],"a":1,"e":"a = 20/5 = 4 m/s². The string only has to accelerate the 2 kg block: T = 2 × 4 = 8 N. Using 3 kg gives 12 N, which is the net force on that block's own part.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0507","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 4 kg block on a smooth table is joined by a string over a pulley to a 1 kg mass hanging freely. Take g = 9.8 m/s². The acceleration is","o":["2.45 m/s²","9.8 m/s²","5.88 m/s²","1.96 m/s²"],"a":3,"e":"Driving force = m₂g; total mass = 5 kg. a = 1 × 9.8/5 = 1.96 m/s².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0508","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 4 kg block on a smooth horizontal surface is pulled by a 20 N force directed 60° above the horizontal. Its acceleration is","o":["2.5 m/s²","4.33 m/s²","5 m/s²","8.66 m/s²"],"a":0,"e":"Only the horizontal component acts: a = F cos60°/m = 20 × 0.5/4 = 2.5 m/s². Using sin gives 4.3.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0509","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 10 kg box on a floor is pulled by a rope with 30 N at 30° above the horizontal. Take g = 10 m/s². The normal reaction from the floor is","o":["115 N","100 N","85 N","74.02 N"],"a":2,"e":"Vertically: N + F sin30° = mg, so N = 100 − 15 = 85 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0510","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"The momentum of a 2 kg body moving at 3 m/s is","o":["9 kg m/s","0.67 kg m/s","18 kg m/s","6 kg m/s"],"a":3,"e":"p = mv = 2 × 3 = 6 kg m/s. 9 J would be the kinetic energy.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0511","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A 0.1 kg ball hits a wall at 20 m/s and rebounds at 20 m/s. Contact time is 0.01 s. The average force on the ball is","o":["200 N","400 N","0.02 N","0.04 N"],"a":1,"e":"Δp = 0.1(20 + 20) = 4 kg m/s; F = 4/0.01 = 400 N. Using half of Δp gives 200 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0512","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A bat exerts an average force of 2000 N on a ball for 5 ms (0.005 s). The impulse is","o":["10000 N s","400000 N s","10 N s","100 N s"],"a":2,"e":"J = FΔt = 2000 × 0.005 = 10 N s. Convert milliseconds to seconds first.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0513","c":"PHY","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse","s":"mcq","q":"A force of 12 N gives a body an acceleration of 4 m/s². Under the same force, a body of twice the mass has acceleration","o":["2 m/s²","8 m/s²","1 m/s²","24 m/s²"],"a":0,"e":"m = F/a = 12/4 = 3 kg. Twice the mass is 6 kg, so a = 12/6 = 2 m/s². Acceleration is inversely proportional to mass.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0514","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"________ state that the work done by the resultant external force acting on a body is equal to the change in kinetic energy of the body.","o":["Mechanical Energy","Kinetics Energy","Resultant force","Work – Energy Theorem"],"a":3,"e":"This is the work-energy theorem: W_net = ΔKE = ½mv² − ½mu². Mechanical energy is KE + PE, a different quantity.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q2"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0515","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A rigid body is said to be in equilibrium if it is in _________ Equilibriums.","o":["Translational and Circular","Rotational and Oscillatory","Translational and Rotation","Oscillatory and Circular"],"a":2,"e":"A rigid body is in (complete) equilibrium when both translational equilibrium (ΣF = 0) and rotational equilibrium (Στ = 0) hold. Wording of the option is 'Translational and Rotation' in the original.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q3"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0516","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The two types of friction are Static and Dynamic","o":["True","False","True only for static friction","Cannot be determined"],"a":0,"e":"Friction is classed as static (between surfaces at rest relative to each other) and dynamic/kinetic (surfaces sliding). 'Dynamic' is the older name for kinetic friction.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q5"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0517","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The moment is a positive quantity if the force tends to produce a counterclockwise angular acceleration about the axis, and negative if the force tends to produce a clockwise acceleration.","o":["True","False","True only for clockwise moments","Cannot be determined"],"a":0,"e":"This is the standard sign convention for moments: anticlockwise positive, clockwise negative.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q11"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0518","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"_______ is defined as the angle which the resultant (total reaction) makes with the normal reaction, if the normal reaction, N and the frictional force Fr is compounded into a single force.","o":["Angle of Motion","Angle of friction","Angle of Static","All of the Above"],"a":1,"e":"The angle of friction λ is the angle between the total (resultant) reaction and the normal reaction, with tan λ = F/R = μ at limiting friction.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q17"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0519","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The _________ about a given point is the product of the force and the perpendicular distance drawn from the given point to the line of action of the force.","o":["Equilibrium","Angular force","Moment of a force","Impulse"],"a":2,"e":"Moment = force × perpendicular distance from the point to the line of action of the force.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q19"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0520","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"All these are true about friction except:","o":["Is independent of the area of the surface in contact","Oppose motion between the surfaces","Depends on the nature of the surface","Is dependent of relative velocity between the surfaces"],"a":3,"e":"Friction opposes relative motion, depends on the nature of the surfaces (through μ) and is independent of contact area. Kinetic friction is, to a good approximation, independent of relative speed, so (d) is the false statement.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q22"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0521","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the statement is not true about Moment of force","o":["The moment is a positive quantity if the force tends to produce a counter-clockwise acceleration about the axis, and negative if the force tends to produce a clockwise acceleration.","When a number of forces are acting on a body, the algebraic sum of their moments is obtained by giving the value of the moment of each force its proper sign and adding them together.","The moment Vector quantity","The moments a Scalar quantity"],"a":2,"e":"Statements (a) and (b) treat moments as signed scalars about an axis (in-plane problems), so (d) fits the module's usage and (c) is the 'not true' statement. In full 3D, torque τ = r × F is a vector, so (c) is defensible physics too.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q28"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0522","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"_______________ about a given point is the product of the force and the perpendicular distance drawn from the given point to the line of action of the force.","o":["Moment of force","A couple of force","Equilibrium position","Angle of friction"],"a":0,"e":"Definition of the moment of a force about a point. A couple is a pair of equal, opposite, non-collinear forces.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q29"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0523","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The net force of anti-parallel body is ____________","o":["∑F > 0","∑F < 0","∑F = 0","∑F ≠ 0"],"a":2,"e":"Anti-parallel forces forming a couple (equal in magnitude, opposite in direction, not collinear) have zero net force, ΣF = 0, yet a non-zero turning effect. This matches the equilibrium theme of Q31 and Q32.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q30"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0524","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A rigid body is in translational equilibrium if its translational acceleration is zero, that is:","o":["∑Fx = 0 and ∑Fy ≠ 0","∑Fx ≠ 0 and ∑Fy = 0","∑Fx = 0 and ∑Fy = 0","∑Fx > 0 and ∑Fy < 0"],"a":2,"e":"Zero translational acceleration needs the force components along each axis to sum to zero: ΣFx = 0 and ΣFy = 0.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q31"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0525","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A rigid body is in rotational equilibrium if its angular acceleration is zero, that is:","o":["∑τ ≠ 0","∑τ = 0","∑τ < 0","∑τ > 0"],"a":1,"e":"Zero angular acceleration means the sum of torques (moments) about any axis is zero: Στ = 0.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q32"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0526","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A rigid body is said to be in equilibrium if it is in _________ Equilibriums","o":["Translational and Circular","Rotational and Oscillatory","Translational and Rotation","Oscillatory and Circular"],"a":2,"e":"Equilibrium of a rigid body requires ΣF = 0 (translational) and Στ = 0 (rotational).","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q37"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0527","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Given that the normal reaction is constant, the friction force is?","o":["Directly proportional to the area of the contact of the given surfaces","Inversely proportional to the area of the contact of the given surfaces","Directly proportional to the square of the area of the contact of the given surfaces","Independent of the area of the contact of the given surfaces"],"a":3,"e":"Law of friction: for a given normal reaction, friction does not depend on the area of contact.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q40"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0528","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The coefficient of static friction (limiting friction force and normal reaction force) is equal to _____________","o":["F × R","F/R","R/F","None of the above"],"a":1,"e":"μ = F/R, the ratio of the limiting friction force F to the normal reaction R. The option layout is garbled in the source (read as F × R, F/R, R/F, none).","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q41"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0529","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The potential energy and kinetic energy are types of?","o":["Electrical energy","Magnetic energy","Thermal energy","Mechanical energy"],"a":3,"e":"Mechanical energy = kinetic energy + potential energy.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q45"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0530","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Two types of the moment are?","o":["Clockwise and Anti-clockwise","Stable and unstable","Natural and charged","Forward and backward"],"a":0,"e":"Moments are classed by their sense of turning: clockwise (negative) and anticlockwise (positive). Stable/unstable describe equilibrium.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q46"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0531","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"When work done on a body equals the change in its kinetic energy, this principle is known as:","o":["Work done principle","Work-Energy Principle","Work-Velocity Principle","Work-Displacement Principle"],"a":1,"e":"W_net = ΔKE is the work-energy principle (theorem).","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q47"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0532","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Given that the normal reaction is constant, the friction force is? (Part B)","o":["Directly proportional to the area of the contact of the given surfaces","Inversely proportional to the area of the contact of the given surfaces","Directly proportional to the square of the area of the contact of the given surfaces","Independent of the area of the contact of the given surfaces"],"a":3,"e":"Friction depends on the normal reaction and the nature of the surfaces, not on the contact area.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q1"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0533","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The coefficient of static friction (limiting friction force and normal reaction force) is equal to _____________ (Part B)","o":["F × R","F/R","R/F","None of the above"],"a":1,"e":"μ = F/R (limiting friction over normal reaction).","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q2"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0534","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Reference to the ability of an object to return to its original position after it has been tilted slightly is termed as? (Part B)","o":["Stability","Equilibrium","Center of gravity","Turning effect"],"a":0,"e":"Stability is the ability of a body to return to its original position after a small tilt (stable equilibrium).","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q5"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0535","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Two types of the moment are? (Part B)","o":["Clockwise and Anti-clockwise","Stable and unstable","Natural and charged","Forward and backward"],"a":0,"e":"Clockwise and anticlockwise moments.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q6"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0536","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"When work done on a body equals the change in its kinetic energy, this principle is known as: (Part B)","o":["Work done principle","Work-Energy Principle","Work-Velocity Principle","Work-Displacement Principle"],"a":1,"e":"Work-energy principle: W_net = ΔKE.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q9"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0537","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A constant force of 50 N acts at 60° above the horizontal on a trolley that moves 4 m horizontally. How much work does the force do?","o":["100 J","173 J","200 J","50 J"],"a":0,"e":"W = F d cosθ = 50 × 4 × cos 60° = 50 × 4 × 0.5 = 100 J. Trap: using sin 60° (173 J) or leaving out cosθ (200 J).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0538","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A car of mass 1200 kg moves at 20 m/s. What is its kinetic energy?","o":["480 kJ","240 kJ","12 kJ","24 kJ"],"a":1,"e":"KE = ½mv² = 0.5 × 1200 × 400 = 240 000 J = 240 kJ. Forgetting ½ gives 480 kJ; forgetting to square v gives 12 kJ.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0539","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Take g = 9.8 m/s². How much gravitational potential energy does a 5 kg mass gain when lifted 12 m?","o":["60 J","294 J","588 J","58.8 J"],"a":2,"e":"ΔPE = mgh = 5 × 9.8 × 12 = 588 J. Using g = 1 gives 60 J; halving it (confusing with KE) gives 294 J.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0540","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A spring of force constant 200 N/m is stretched 0.10 m from its natural length. How much elastic potential energy does it store?","o":["2 J","10 J","20 J","1 J"],"a":3,"e":"U = ½kx² = 0.5 × 200 × 0.01 = 1 J. kx² = 2 J forgets the ½; kx = 20 N is a force, not an energy.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0541","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A net force increases the speed of a 2 kg body from 3 m/s to 7 m/s. How much net work was done?","o":["40 J","16 J","58 J","20 J"],"a":0,"e":"W = ΔKE = ½(2)(7² − 3²) = 49 − 9 = 40 J. Using (7 − 3)² = 16 is the classic slip: KE depends on v², so the speeds must be squared separately.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0542","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Take g = 9.8 m/s². A motor lifts a 60 kg load through 10 m in 20 s at constant speed. What is its useful power output?","o":["588 W","294 W","30 W","2940 W"],"a":1,"e":"Work = mgh = 60 × 9.8 × 10 = 5880 J; P = W/t = 5880/20 = 294 W. 588 W comes from dividing by 10 s; 2940 W from dropping the time.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0543","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A car engine exerts a driving force of 2000 N while the car moves at a steady 15 m/s. What power is developed?","o":["133 W","7.5 kW","30 kW","3 kW"],"a":2,"e":"P = Fv = 2000 × 15 = 30 000 W = 30 kW. Dividing (F/v ≈ 133) is the usual slip.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0544","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Using conservation of mechanical energy and g = 10 m/s², what is the speed of a stone just before it hits the ground after being dropped from rest from a height of 20 m (ignore air resistance)?","o":["40 m/s","14 m/s","400 m/s","20 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"mgh = ½mv² so v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 × 20) = √400 = 20 m/s. 400 is v² (forgot the square root); 14 m/s is √(gh).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0545","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Take g = 9.8 m/s². A 10 kg block rests on a horizontal surface with μ = 0.4 between them. What is the limiting friction force?","o":["39.2 N","245 N","98 N","3.92 N"],"a":0,"e":"R = mg = 98 N, so F = μR = 0.4 × 98 = 39.2 N. Dividing by μ gives 245 N; forgetting μ gives 98 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0546","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A horizontal force of 30 N just makes a block slide on a rough floor where the normal reaction is 60 N. What is the coefficient of static friction?","o":["2.0","0.5","1800","30"],"a":1,"e":"μ = F/R = 30/60 = 0.5. Inverting the ratio (R/F = 2) is the common slip.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0547","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The coefficient of friction between two surfaces is 0.75. What is the angle of friction?","o":["53.1°","48.6°","36.9°","0.75°"],"a":2,"e":"tan λ = μ so λ = tan⁻¹(0.75) = 36.9°. 53.1° is the complement (tan⁻¹(1/0.75)).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0548","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A block on a rough plank is just on the point of sliding when the plank is tilted to 25° to the horizontal. What is μ?","o":["0.42","0.91","2.14","0.47"],"a":3,"e":"At the point of slipping tanθ = μ, so μ = tan 25° = 0.47. sin 25° = 0.42 and cos 25° = 0.91 are the wrong ratios.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0549","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Take g = 10 m/s². A block slides down a 30° incline with coefficient of kinetic friction 0.2. What is its acceleration?","o":["3.27 m/s²","6.73 m/s²","5.00 m/s²","1.73 m/s²"],"a":0,"e":"a = g(sinθ − μcosθ) = 10(0.5 − 0.2 × 0.866) = 10(0.3268) = 3.27 m/s². 6.73 uses + (friction wrongly aiding motion); 5.00 ignores friction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0550","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Take g = 9.8 m/s². What is the smallest horizontal force that will start a 50 kg crate moving on a floor with μs = 0.5?","o":["490 N","245 N","98 N","24.5 N"],"a":1,"e":"F = μs mg = 0.5 × 50 × 9.8 = 245 N. Using 2μs mg gives 490 N; 98 N is the weight mg with μ left out; 24.5 N has a misplaced decimal.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0551","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Take g = 9.8 m/s². A 20 kg crate slides 5 m along a floor with μk = 0.3. What work does friction do on the crate?","o":["+294 J","−58.8 J","−294 J","−980 J"],"a":2,"e":"f = μk mg = 0.3 × 196 = 58.8 N; W = −f d = −58.8 × 5 = −294 J (negative because friction opposes the motion). −980 J forgets μk; −58.8 J forgets the distance.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0552","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A force of 20 N acts perpendicular to a door at 0.5 m from the hinge. What is its moment about the hinge?","o":["40 N m","20.5 N m","2.5 N m","10 N m"],"a":3,"e":"τ = F × perpendicular distance = 20 × 0.5 = 10 N m. Dividing (20/0.5 = 40) is the slip.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0553","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A 40 N force is applied to a 0.25 m spanner, at 30° to the line of the spanner. What is the moment about the nut?","o":["5 N m","8.7 N m","10 N m","20 N m"],"a":0,"e":"τ = F r sinθ = 40 × 0.25 × sin 30° = 5 N m. cos 30° gives 8.7 N m; ignoring the angle gives 10 N m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0554","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A 40 kg child sits 2.0 m from the pivot of a uniform see-saw. Where must a 50 kg child sit on the other side to balance it?","o":["2.5 m","1.6 m","1.0 m","4.0 m"],"a":1,"e":"Principle of moments: 40 g × 2.0 = 50 g × d, so d = 80/50 = 1.6 m. The heavier child sits closer; 2.5 m inverts the ratio.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0555","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A uniform 6 m beam of weight 200 N rests on supports at its two ends. A 300 N load is placed 2 m from the left end. What is the reaction at the right support?","o":["300 N","250 N","200 N","400 N"],"a":2,"e":"Moments about the left end: R_R × 6 = 200 × 3 + 300 × 2 = 1200, so R_R = 200 N (and R_L = 500 − 200 = 300 N). 300 N is the left reaction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0556","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Two equal and opposite parallel forces of 15 N each act 0.4 m apart. What is the moment of this couple?","o":["0 N m","12 N m","30 N m","6 N m"],"a":3,"e":"Moment of a couple = F × perpendicular separation = 15 × 0.4 = 6 N m. The net force is zero but the turning effect is not; 0 N m confuses the two.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0557","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Two anti-parallel forces of 30 N and 10 N act on a rod at points 2 m apart. At what distance from the 30 N force, on the side away from the 10 N force, does their 20 N resultant act?","o":["1 m","2 m","0.67 m","3 m"],"a":0,"e":"The resultant lies outside the two forces, nearer the larger. Moments about the resultant's line: 30 x = 10 (x + 2), so 20x = 20 and x = 1 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0558","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A 100 N weight hangs from two identical ropes, each making 30° with the horizontal. What is the tension in each rope?","o":["50 N","100 N","200 N","86.6 N"],"a":1,"e":"Vertical equilibrium: 2T sin 30° = 100, so T = 100/(2 × 0.5) = 100 N. Dividing by 2 only gives 50 N; 86.6 N uses cosine.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0559","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Masses of 2 kg and 6 kg are fixed at x = 0 and x = 4 m on a light rod. Where is the centre of gravity?","o":["2 m","1 m","3 m","2.67 m"],"a":2,"e":"x = (2 × 0 + 6 × 4)/(2 + 6) = 24/8 = 3 m, nearer the heavier mass. 2 m is the simple midpoint.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0560","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Take g = 9.8 m/s². A pendulum bob is released from rest at a height of 0.20 m above its lowest point. What is its speed at the lowest point (ignore air resistance)?","o":["3.96 m/s","0.98 m/s","1.40 m/s","1.98 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"mgh = ½mv² gives v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 0.20) = √3.92 = 1.98 m/s. 3.96 forgets the square root; 1.40 is √(gh).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0561","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A frictionless spring (k = 400 N/m) is compressed 0.10 m and launches a 0.20 kg block. What is the launch speed?","o":["4.47 m/s","6.32 m/s","2.0 m/s","10 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"½kx² = ½mv²: v = x√(k/m) = 0.10 × √(400/0.20) = 0.10 × 44.7 = 4.47 m/s. 6.32 m/s results from dropping the ½ on the spring only.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0562","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Take g = 9.8 m/s². How much work is needed to push a 50 kg load at constant speed up 4 m of a smooth 30° incline (4 m measured along the slope)?","o":["1960 J","980 J","1697 J","490 J"],"a":1,"e":"Force along the slope = mg sin30° = 245 N; W = 245 × 4 = 980 J (equals mgh with h = 4 sin30° = 2 m). 1960 J treats 4 m as the vertical rise; 1697 J uses cos.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0563","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A spring of constant 100 N/m is stretched from 0.10 m to 0.30 m extension. What work is done on it?","o":["2 J","5 J","4 J","8 J"],"a":2,"e":"W = ½k(x₂² − x₁²) = 50(0.09 − 0.01) = 4 J. 2 J uses (x₂ − x₁)² = 0.04 (½k × 0.04); 8 J is k(x₂² − x₁²) with the ½ forgotten; 5 J is not the result of any correct step.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0564","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Take g = 10 m/s². A car skids to rest from 20 m/s on a level road with μk = 0.5. How far does it skid?","o":["20 m","80 m","10 m","40 m"],"a":3,"e":"Work-energy: μk m g d = ½mv², so d = v²/(2μk g) = 400/(2 × 0.5 × 10) = 40 m. Mass cancels.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0565","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A 5 kW pump does 100 kJ of work. How long does it take at full power?","o":["20 s","0.05 s","500 s","5 s"],"a":0,"e":"t = W/P = 100 000/5000 = 20 s. Inverting gives 0.05 s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0566","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"What are the SI base-unit dimensions of work and energy?","o":["[M L T⁻²]","[M L² T⁻²]","[M L² T⁻³]","[M L⁻¹ T⁻²]"],"a":1,"e":"Work = force × distance = (M L T⁻²)(L) = M L² T⁻². [M L² T⁻³] is power; [M L T⁻²] is force.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0567","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"One watt is equal to","o":["one joule per metre","one newton per second","one joule per second","one joule-second"],"a":2,"e":"Power is the rate of doing work, P = W/t, so 1 W = 1 J s⁻¹.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0568","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A porter carries a suitcase along a level corridor at constant velocity. The work done by the upward force he exerts on the suitcase is","o":["positive, equal to mgd","negative, because it opposes gravity","zero, because the speed is constant, regardless of direction","zero, because the force is perpendicular to the displacement"],"a":3,"e":"W = F d cosθ with θ = 90° gives zero. The trap is the last option: constant speed matters only because net force is zero, the zero work comes from the perpendicular geometry.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0569","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"In uniform circular motion, the work done by the centripetal force in one revolution is","o":["zero","2πr F","πr F","F r"],"a":0,"e":"The centripetal force is always perpendicular to the instantaneous velocity (and displacement), so W = F d cos 90° = 0 and KE stays constant.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0570","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Which statement about the usual relation between the two coefficients of friction is correct?","o":["μk is always greater than μs","μk is usually less than μs","μk = μs always","μk depends on contact area, μs does not"],"a":1,"e":"It is harder to start a body moving than to keep it moving, so limiting static friction exceeds kinetic friction: μs > μk for most surfaces.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0571","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A 10 kg block on a rough floor, with limiting friction 20 N, is pushed horizontally with 12 N and does not move. The friction force acting is","o":["20 N","0 N","12 N","98 N"],"a":2,"e":"Static friction is self-adjusting: it equals the applied force (12 N) until the limit of 20 N is exceeded. Using the limiting value is the trap.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0572","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The angle of friction λ is related to the coefficient of friction μ by","o":["sin λ = μ","cos λ = μ","λ = μ","tan λ = μ"],"a":3,"e":"Resultant reaction S makes angle λ with the normal R and F = S sinλ, R = S cosλ, so F/R = tan λ = μ.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0573","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A body rests on a rough inclined plane of angle θ and angle of friction λ. It stays at rest provided","o":["θ ≤ λ","θ > λ","θ = 90° − λ only","sin θ ≥ μ"],"a":0,"e":"Equilibrium on the incline needs tan θ ≤ μ = tan λ, so θ ≤ λ. When θ = λ the body is in limiting equilibrium.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0574","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A ball resting at the bottom of a bowl, if nudged slightly, returns to its position. This is an example of","o":["unstable equilibrium","stable equilibrium","neutral equilibrium","no equilibrium"],"a":1,"e":"In stable equilibrium the potential energy is a minimum and the centre of gravity rises when displaced, so a restoring moment/force acts.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0575","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A cone balanced on its tip is in","o":["stable equilibrium","neutral equilibrium","unstable equilibrium","translational equilibrium only"],"a":2,"e":"Any small displacement lowers the centre of gravity (PE falls), so the weight produces a moment that increases the displacement. Neutral equilibrium (a cone lying on its side) has PE unchanged.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0576","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Which change makes an object more stable?","o":["Raising its centre of gravity","Narrowing its base","Making its mass smaller","Lowering its centre of gravity and widening its base"],"a":3,"e":"Stability increases when the vertical line through the centre of gravity stays inside the base over larger tilts: low CG and wide base.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0577","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The torque of a force about a point is given by τ = r × F. The direction of τ is","o":["perpendicular to the plane containing r and F","along F","along r","parallel to the displacement"],"a":0,"e":"The cross product is perpendicular to both r and F, with sense given by the right-hand rule. Magnitude is rF sinθ.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0578","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A couple acting on a rigid body","o":["produces translation only","produces rotation only, since its resultant force is zero","produces both translation and rotation","has a moment that depends on the chosen pivot point"],"a":1,"e":"A couple has ΣF = 0 so the centre of mass does not accelerate, but its moment F × d is non-zero and, being independent of the pivot, causes pure rotation.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0579","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A body is acted on by a couple only. Which statement is correct?","o":["ΣF = 0 and Στ = 0, so it is in equilibrium","ΣF ≠ 0 and Στ = 0","ΣF = 0 but Στ ≠ 0, so it is not in equilibrium","It is in translational and rotational equilibrium"],"a":2,"e":"Both conditions are needed for full equilibrium. A couple satisfies only the translational condition.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0580","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The moment of a force about a point is zero when","o":["the force is perpendicular to the position vector","the force is large","the force is vertical","the line of action of the force passes through the point"],"a":3,"e":"τ = F × (perpendicular distance), which is zero when that distance is zero, i.e. the line of action passes through the pivot.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0581","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"When the net work done on a moving body is negative, its kinetic energy","o":["decreases","increases","stays constant","becomes negative"],"a":0,"e":"W_net = ΔKE, so negative W means ΔKE < 0. KE itself (½mv²) can never be negative.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0582","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"If the speed of a body is doubled, its kinetic energy","o":["doubles","becomes four times as large","becomes eight times as large","is halved"],"a":1,"e":"KE ∝ v², so (2v)² = 4v² and KE becomes four times larger.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0583","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The change in gravitational potential energy of a body moved between two points depends on","o":["the path taken","the time taken","only the vertical height difference","the speed during the move"],"a":2,"e":"Gravity is conservative: ΔPE = mgΔh regardless of path or time.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0584","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"Mechanical energy (KE + PE) of a system is conserved when","o":["friction acts","an external force does net work","the speed is constant","only conservative forces do work"],"a":3,"e":"If friction or other non-conservative forces do work, mechanical energy is converted to heat; with only gravity/spring forces, KE + PE is constant.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0585","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"The resultant of two unequal anti-parallel forces acts","o":["outside the two forces, on the side of the larger force","between the two forces","midway between the forces","at the point where the smaller force acts"],"a":0,"e":"The resultant equals the difference of the forces, has the direction of the larger force, and lies outside the pair on the larger-force side, since moments about it must balance.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0586","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"A car's engine delivers constant power P. If the car's speed doubles, the driving force becomes","o":["twice as large","half as large","unchanged","four times as large"],"a":1,"e":"P = Fv, so for constant P, F ∝ 1/v and doubling v halves F.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0587","c":"PHY","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium","s":"mcq","q":"When the force F = 20 N applied to a lever is increased while its perpendicular distance from the pivot is halved, the moment","o":["always doubles","always halves","stays the same only if F is doubled","is unaffected whatever F becomes"],"a":2,"e":"τ = F × d; halving d requires doubling F to keep τ the same.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0588","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"An isolated system is one that","o":["exchanges neither matter nor energy with its surroundings","exchanges energy but not matter","exchanges matter but not energy","exchanges both matter and energy"],"a":0,"e":"Isolated means no exchange of matter or energy and no net external influence. The option 'energy but not matter' describes a closed system, which is the common trap.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0589","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A conservation law states that, for an isolated system, a certain measurable quantity","o":["always increases with time","always decreases with time","changes only when the system is at rest","does not change as the system evolves in time"],"a":3,"e":"A conserved quantity stays constant over time (for an isolated system).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0590","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"The SI unit of linear momentum is","o":["kg m/s²","kg m/s","kg m²/s","kg m²/s²"],"a":1,"e":"p = mv, so the unit is kg × m/s. kg m²/s is angular momentum; kg m²/s² is the joule.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0591","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensions of linear momentum are","o":["[M L T⁻²]","[M L² T⁻¹]","[M L T⁻¹]","[M L² T⁻²]"],"a":2,"e":"[p] = [m][v] = M × L T⁻¹. [M L T⁻²] is force; [M L² T⁻¹] is angular momentum.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0592","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"The total linear momentum of a system is conserved when","o":["the net internal force is zero","the system has zero kinetic energy","the net external force on the system is zero","the system contains only one body"],"a":2,"e":"Internal forces always cancel in pairs (Newton's third law), so only the net external force can change the total momentum. Trap: it is the external, not internal, force.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0593","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"In an elastic collision between two bodies in an isolated system, which quantities are conserved?","o":["Both momentum and kinetic energy","Momentum only","Kinetic energy only","Neither momentum nor kinetic energy"],"a":0,"e":"Elastic means KE is conserved in addition to the momentum that is conserved in every collision.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0594","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A collision in which the bodies stick together and move off with a common velocity is called","o":["elastic","explosive","reversible","perfectly inelastic"],"a":3,"e":"Sticking together is the perfectly inelastic case: maximum KE loss consistent with momentum conservation.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0595","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Electric charge is best described as a","o":["vector quantity","scalar quantity","tensor with direction","dimensionless ratio"],"a":1,"e":"Charge has sign and size but no direction, so it is a scalar and adds algebraically.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0596","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Angular momentum is a","o":["vector quantity","scalar quantity","dimensionless quantity","quantity that has no direction"],"a":0,"e":"L = r × p points along the axis of rotation (right-hand rule). The module states it is a vector and is additive.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0597","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"The SI unit of angular momentum is","o":["kg m/s","kg m²/s²","N m","kg m²/s"],"a":3,"e":"L = Iω = (kg m²)(rad/s). N m is the unit of torque; kg m²/s² is energy.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0598","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Which one of the following is a non-conservative force?","o":["Gravity","Kinetic friction","Spring (elastic) force","Electrostatic force"],"a":1,"e":"Friction's work depends on path length and is not zero around a closed loop. The other three are conservative.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0599","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"The work done by a conservative force around any closed path is","o":["always positive","always negative","zero","equal to the path length"],"a":2,"e":"Path independence implies zero net work for a round trip.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0600","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"The relation that expresses the interconversion of mass and energy is","o":["E = mv²","E = ½mc²","E = mc²","E = mgh"],"a":2,"e":"Einstein's mass-energy relation. Trap: ½ appears only in kinetic energy ½mv².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0601","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following quantities is conserved when no external torque acts on a system?","o":["Linear Velocity","Angular velocity","Angular momentum","Moment of inertia"],"a":2,"e":"With zero net external torque, ΔL = 0. Angular velocity and moment of inertia can individually change (the skater) so long as their product Iω stays fixed. Trap: confusing angular velocity with angular momentum.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q28"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0602","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Consider an ice skater with a moment of inertia of 3.0 kg·m² spinning at an angular velocity of 2.0 rad/s. If she pulls her arms in and her new moment of inertia becomes 1.5 kg·m², what will be her new angular velocity?","o":["16.2 rad/s","4.0 rad/s","9.2 rad/s","6.0 rad/s"],"a":1,"e":"No external torque, so I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂: ω₂ = (3.0 × 2.0)/1.5 = 4.0 rad/s. Halving I doubles ω.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q39"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0603","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Rocket propulsion in empty space is explained by conservation of","o":["linear momentum","electric charge","angular momentum only","gravitational potential energy"],"a":0,"e":"Exhaust goes backward, so the rocket gains equal forward momentum. It does not push on the air.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0604","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"In the absence of air resistance, the sum KE + PE of a freely falling body","o":["increases steadily","decreases steadily","is zero","stays constant"],"a":3,"e":"Only gravity (conservative) does work, so mechanical energy is conserved: PE converts to KE.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0605","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is a conservative force?","o":["Air drag on a falling parachute","The restoring force of a stretched spring","Sliding friction on a rough floor","Viscous force on a ball in oil"],"a":1,"e":"Spring force does work independent of path and stores recoverable PE. The other three dissipate energy as heat.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0606","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A skater spinning on ice pulls her arms in. Which statement is correct?","o":["Her angular velocity increases while her angular momentum stays the same","Her angular velocity stays the same while her moment of inertia falls","Her angular momentum increases because she rotates faster","Her kinetic energy stays the same"],"a":0,"e":"I falls, L = Iω is constant, so ω rises. KE = ½Iω² actually rises because she does work pulling her arms in.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0607","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A diver performs a somersault by tucking into a ball. The reason she spins faster in the tuck is that","o":["her angular momentum increases","her mass decreases","gravity exerts a torque that speeds her up","her moment of inertia decreases while angular momentum is conserved"],"a":3,"e":"Gravity acts at the centre of mass and gives no torque about it in flight, so L is constant. Smaller I gives larger ω.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0608","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A planet moves faster when it is nearer the Sun. This is a consequence of conservation of","o":["linear momentum","angular momentum","electric charge","mass only"],"a":1,"e":"The Sun's gravity gives no torque about the Sun, so L = mvr (perpendicular) is constant: smaller r, larger v. Linear momentum of the planet is not constant, since gravity is a net external force.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0609","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A gun recoils when a bullet is fired. Immediately after firing, the momentum of the gun and the momentum of the bullet are","o":["equal in magnitude and in the same direction","zero for both","equal in magnitude and opposite in direction","equal only if the masses are equal"],"a":2,"e":"The system starts at rest so total momentum is zero: mv = MV in magnitude. Their speeds differ but their momenta cancel.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0610","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Two bodies collide in an isolated system. Whether the collision is elastic or inelastic, the total momentum","o":["is greater after","is smaller after","is the same before and after","is zero after"],"a":2,"e":"Momentum is conserved in all collisions when no net external force acts. Only KE depends on the type.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0611","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"In a perfectly inelastic collision, compared with any other collision with the same initial momenta, the kinetic energy lost is","o":["the largest possible","zero","the smallest possible","negative"],"a":0,"e":"Sticking together gives zero relative velocity after the collision, the maximum loss compatible with momentum conservation.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0612","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Why is the momentum of a single falling ball not conserved, while the momentum of the ball + Earth system is?","o":["Momentum is never conserved for falling bodies","The ball has no mass in free fall","The Earth has no weight","Gravity from the Earth is an external force on the ball alone but internal to the ball + Earth system"],"a":3,"e":"Conservation needs zero net external force on the chosen system. Enlarging the system turns the gravitational pair into internal forces. This is the standard 'isolated system' trap.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0613","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A block slides across a rough floor and comes to rest. Its momentum is not conserved because","o":["momentum is only conserved for rolling bodies","friction is an external force acting on the block","the block's mass has decreased","kinetic energy is always conserved"],"a":1,"e":"The net external force (friction) is non-zero, so Δp ≠ 0. Momentum passes to the Earth through friction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0614","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A simple pendulum is released from rest at one end of its swing. At the lowest point of its path, ignoring air resistance, its","o":["kinetic energy is maximum and its potential energy is minimum","kinetic energy is zero and potential energy is maximum","kinetic and potential energies are both maximum","kinetic and potential energies are both zero"],"a":0,"e":"PE = mgh is lowest at the bottom, so by conservation KE is highest there.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0615","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"The work done by friction when a box is dragged between two points","o":["is independent of the path","is zero for every path","is always positive","depends on the path taken"],"a":3,"e":"Friction is non-conservative: a longer path means more negative work. Trap: the work is negative, never positive, for kinetic friction on the moving body.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0616","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Which statement about the work done by a conservative force is correct?","o":["It always dissipates energy as heat","It is reversible: the energy transferred can be fully recovered on the return trip","It depends strongly on the path","It is nonzero around a closed loop"],"a":1,"e":"Conservative work changes a stored potential energy which can be given back, so the round trip gives zero total work.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0617","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A glass rod is rubbed with silk and becomes positively charged. The silk","o":["remains neutral","becomes positively charged","becomes negatively charged with equal magnitude of charge","loses all its charge"],"a":2,"e":"Electrons move from rod to silk. Total charge of the isolated pair stays zero.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0618","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"In a nuclear reaction the products have slightly less total rest mass than the reactants. By conservation of mass-energy, the 'missing' mass","o":["is destroyed completely","is converted into charge","appears as energy, with E = Δm c²","has no effect on the energy of the system"],"a":2,"e":"Only total mass-energy is conserved: the mass defect is released as kinetic and radiant energy.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0619","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A body of mass m moving with speed v collides head-on elastically with an identical body at rest. After the collision","o":["the first body stops and the second moves off with speed v","both move off with speed v/2","both stop","the first rebounds with speed v and the second stays at rest"],"a":0,"e":"Equal masses exchange velocities in a head-on elastic collision. Check: momentum mv conserved and KE ½mv² conserved. Option 'both v/2' conserves momentum but loses half the KE (that is the perfectly inelastic case).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0620","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A stationary shell explodes into two fragments. Immediately after the explosion","o":["the total kinetic energy is zero","the fragments must have equal speeds","the fragments must move in the same direction","the vector sum of the fragments' momenta is zero"],"a":3,"e":"Total momentum was zero before, so it is zero after. KE is not conserved (it rises, from stored chemical energy), and speeds are equal only if the masses are equal.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0621","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"In an inelastic collision the 'lost' kinetic energy","o":["is destroyed","is converted into other forms such as heat, sound and deformation energy","is converted into extra momentum","is converted into electric charge"],"a":1,"e":"Total energy is conserved. KE decreases because it becomes internal (thermal) energy etc.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0622","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Two equal and opposite forces act at different points on a rigid free body (a couple). Which statement is correct?","o":["Its total linear momentum is unchanged but its angular momentum changes","Both its linear and angular momentum are unchanged","Its linear momentum changes but its angular momentum is unchanged","Neither is conserved because the forces cancel"],"a":0,"e":"Net force is zero, so p is constant. The forces produce a net torque, so L changes. Trap: assuming zero net force implies zero net torque.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0623","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Which expression correctly describes the exhaust of a rocket that has a larger exhaust speed vₑ, with everything else the same?","o":["It gives a smaller speed gain","It makes no difference to Δv","It violates conservation of momentum","It gives a larger speed gain Δv = vₑ ln(m₀/m)"],"a":3,"e":"Δv is proportional to vₑ. The mass ratio only enters through the logarithm.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0624","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A smooth frictionless ramp can be either straight or curved, with the same height drop. A block released from rest at the top reaches the bottom with","o":["a larger speed on the longer curved path","the same speed for both, because gravity is conservative","a smaller speed on the curved path","a speed that depends on the block's mass"],"a":1,"e":"Work by gravity depends only on the height change: v = √(2gh) regardless of path or mass.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0625","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A pendulum swings in air and gradually comes to rest. The mechanical energy","o":["was destroyed","was converted into angular momentum","was converted to thermal energy by non-conservative air drag, so total energy is conserved","shows that energy is not conserved"],"a":2,"e":"Drag is non-conservative. Total energy (including heat in the air) is constant.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0626","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A car of mass 1200 kg travels at 25 m/s. What is its linear momentum?","o":["3.0 × 10³ kg m/s","7.5 × 10⁵ kg m/s","3.0 × 10⁴ kg m/s","1.5 × 10⁴ kg m/s"],"a":2,"e":"p = mv = 1200 × 25 = 30 000 kg m/s. 1.5 × 10⁴ comes from using ½mv/…; 7.5 × 10⁵ from ½mv² type slips.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0627","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A 4.0 kg rifle fires a 0.020 kg bullet at 400 m/s. What is the recoil speed of the rifle?","o":["2.0 m/s","0.50 m/s","20 m/s","8.0 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"0 = (0.020)(400) − (4.0)V ⇒ V = 8.0/4.0 = 2.0 m/s opposite to the bullet.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0628","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A 2 kg trolley moving at 6 m/s collides with a 4 kg trolley at rest and they stick together. Their common velocity is","o":["3.0 m/s","1.5 m/s","4.0 m/s","2.0 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"2 × 6 = (2 + 4)v ⇒ v = 12/6 = 2.0 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0629","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"For the collision of the 2 kg trolley (6 m/s) with the stationary 4 kg trolley that stick together, how much kinetic energy is lost?","o":["12 J","24 J","36 J","6 J"],"a":1,"e":"KE before = ½ × 2 × 6² = 36 J; after = ½ × 6 × 2² = 12 J; loss = 24 J. 36 J is the initial KE, 12 J the final KE.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0630","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A 3 kg body moving at +4 m/s collides head-on elastically with a 3 kg body moving at −2 m/s. What is the velocity of the first body after the collision?","o":["−2 m/s","+4 m/s","+1 m/s","0 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"Equal masses exchange velocities in a one-dimensional elastic collision, so the first body leaves at −2 m/s (and the second at +4 m/s).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0631","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A stone falls from rest through 20 m. Ignoring air resistance and taking g = 9.8 m/s², its speed on reaching the ground is about","o":["392 m/s","9.9 m/s","14.0 m/s","19.8 m/s"],"a":3,"e":"v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 20) = √392 = 19.8 m/s. 392 forgets the square root.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0632","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A pendulum bob is released from rest when it is 0.45 m above its lowest point. Take g = 10 m/s². Its speed at the lowest point is","o":["4.5 m/s","3.0 m/s","9.0 m/s","1.5 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 × 0.45) = √9 = 3.0 m/s. The mass is not needed.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0633","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A ball is thrown vertically upward at 12 m/s. Take g = 9.8 m/s² and ignore air resistance. The maximum height reached is","o":["14.7 m","1.22 m","7.35 m","3.67 m"],"a":2,"e":"½mv² = mgh ⇒ h = v²/2g = 144/19.6 = 7.35 m. 14.7 m forgets the factor 2 in the denominator.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0634","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Taking c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s, the energy equivalent of 1.0 g of mass is","o":["9.0 × 10¹⁶ J","3.0 × 10⁵ J","9.0 × 10¹³ J","4.5 × 10¹³ J"],"a":2,"e":"m = 1.0 × 10⁻³ kg: E = mc² = 10⁻³ × (3.0 × 10⁸)² = 9.0 × 10¹³ J. 9.0 × 10¹⁶ J is for 1 kg (grams not converted).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0635","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Two identical conducting spheres carry charges +6 μC and −2 μC. They touch and are then separated. The charge on each is","o":["+2 μC","+4 μC","+3 μC","−1 μC"],"a":0,"e":"Total charge = +6 − 2 = +4 μC, shared equally: +2 μC each. Charge is conserved and additive.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0636","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"Three small bodies in an isolated system carry charges +4 nC, −7 nC and +2 nC. The total charge is","o":["+13 nC","+1 nC","−5 nC","−1 nC"],"a":3,"e":"Algebraic sum: 4 − 7 + 2 = −1 nC. 13 nC adds magnitudes, ignoring signs.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0637","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A disc has moment of inertia 0.50 kg m² and rotates at 8.0 rad/s. Its angular momentum is","o":["16 kg m²/s","4.0 kg m²/s","2.0 kg m²/s","64 kg m²/s"],"a":1,"e":"L = Iω = 0.50 × 8.0 = 4.0 kg m²/s. 16 would come from ½Iω.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0638","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A skater has I = 4.0 kg m² and ω = 1.5 rad/s. She draws in her arms so that I becomes 1.2 kg m². Her new angular velocity is","o":["5.0 rad/s","1.8 rad/s","0.45 rad/s","3.2 rad/s"],"a":0,"e":"ω₂ = I₁ω₁/I₂ = (4.0 × 1.5)/1.2 = 5.0 rad/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0639","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A student sits on a frictionless turntable holding weights. The system spins at 2.0 rad/s with I = 6.0 kg m². She pulls the weights in so I becomes 4.0 kg m². The new angular velocity is","o":["1.3 rad/s","2.0 rad/s","4.5 rad/s","3.0 rad/s"],"a":3,"e":"ω₂ = 6.0 × 2.0/4.0 = 3.0 rad/s. 1.3 results from multiplying instead of dividing by the ratio.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0640","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"In the skater problem with I = 4.0 kg m², ω = 1.5 rad/s changing to I = 1.2 kg m², ω = 5.0 rad/s, how much work does she do pulling her arms in?","o":["0 J","10.5 J","4.5 J","15 J"],"a":1,"e":"ΔKE = ½(1.2)(5.0)² − ½(4.0)(1.5)² = 15 − 4.5 = 10.5 J. Angular momentum is conserved but KE is not. 15 J is only the final KE.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0641","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A 3 kg body moving at +5 m/s collides with a 2 kg body moving at −5 m/s and they stick together. Their common velocity is","o":["0 m/s","+5 m/s","+1.0 m/s","+2.5 m/s"],"a":2,"e":"Momentum: 3(5) + 2(−5) = 5 kg m/s; v = 5/(3 + 2) = +1.0 m/s. Forgetting the minus sign gives 5 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0642","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A 6 kg object at rest explodes into 2 kg and 4 kg pieces. The 2 kg piece moves at 12 m/s. The speed of the 4 kg piece is","o":["3 m/s","12 m/s","6 m/s","24 m/s"],"a":2,"e":"0 = 2(12) + 4V ⇒ V = −6 m/s, so the speed is 6 m/s in the opposite direction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0643","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A rocket's exhaust speed relative to the rocket is 2000 m/s and the ratio m₀/m of initial to final mass is 3. Using Δv = vₑ ln(m₀/m) with ln 3 = 1.10, the speed gained is about","o":["2.2 × 10³ m/s","6.0 × 10³ m/s","6.7 × 10² m/s","1.8 × 10³ m/s"],"a":0,"e":"Δv = 2000 × 1.0986 ≈ 2197 m/s. 6000 m/s uses the ratio 3 directly instead of its natural logarithm.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0644","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"An engine expels exhaust at 2500 m/s relative to the rocket, burning 40 kg of fuel each second. The thrust is","o":["6.3 × 10⁻² N","6.3 × 10¹ N","2.5 × 10³ N","1.0 × 10⁵ N"],"a":3,"e":"F = vₑ (dm/dt) = 2500 × 40 = 1.0 × 10⁵ N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0645","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A 0.010 kg bullet embeds in a 1.99 kg block hanging from a string. The block and bullet rise 0.20 m. Take g = 9.8 m/s². The speed of the bullet was about","o":["1.98 m/s","396 m/s","198 m/s","790 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"After impact: v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 0.20) = 1.98 m/s. Momentum: 0.010 u = 2.00 × 1.98 ⇒ u ≈ 396 m/s. (Energy is conserved only after the impact, not during it.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0646","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A 1 kg body moving at 6 m/s collides elastically with a 2 kg body at rest. After the collision the 2 kg body moves at","o":["4.0 m/s","2.0 m/s","6.0 m/s","3.0 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"v₂ = 2m₁u₁/(m₁ + m₂) = 2 × 1 × 6/3 = 4.0 m/s (and v₁ = −2 m/s). Check KE: 18 J before and 18 J after.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0647","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A planet's speed is 3.0 × 10⁴ m/s at a distance of 1.5 × 10¹¹ m from the Sun. What is its speed (perpendicular to the radius) at 1.2 × 10¹¹ m?","o":["2.4 × 10⁴ m/s","3.0 × 10⁴ m/s","4.7 × 10⁴ m/s","3.75 × 10⁴ m/s"],"a":3,"e":"L = mvr is constant: v₂ = v₁r₁/r₂ = 3.0 × 10⁴ × 1.5/1.2 = 3.75 × 10⁴ m/s. 2.4 × 10⁴ inverts the ratio.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0648","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A spring (k = 200 N/m) is compressed by 0.10 m and launches a 0.50 kg block on a frictionless surface. The block's speed after release is","o":["4.0 m/s","2.0 m/s","0.20 m/s","20 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"½kx² = ½mv² ⇒ v = x√(k/m) = 0.10 × √400 = 2.0 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0649","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A frictionless roller coaster starts from rest at the top of a 40 m drop. Take g = 10 m/s². Its speed at the bottom is about","o":["20 m/s","800 m/s","28 m/s","14 m/s"],"a":2,"e":"v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 × 40) = √800 ≈ 28.3 m/s. 800 forgets the square root.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0650","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A body strikes an identical stationary body and they stick together. What fraction of the initial kinetic energy remains?","o":["1/4","1/3","1/2","3/4"],"a":2,"e":"Momentum: mv = 2mV ⇒ V = v/2. KE after = ½(2m)(v/2)² = ¼mv², which is half of ½mv².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0651","c":"PHY","u":"Conservation Principles","s":"mcq","q":"A 0.15 kg ball is hit and leaves the bat at 40 m/s from rest. What is the momentum it acquires?","o":["6.0 kg m/s","3.0 kg m/s","1.2 × 10² kg m/s","600 kg m/s"],"a":0,"e":"p = mv = 0.15 × 40 = 6.0 kg m/s. 120 would come from ½mv².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0652","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"If two cars 5m apart have masses 1000kg and 1200kg respectively, what is the gravitational attraction between them?","o":["32.0 × 10⁻⁶ N","3.20 μN","5.20 × 10⁻⁶ N","5.20 μN"],"a":1,"e":"F = Gm₁m₂/r² = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 1000 × 1200 / 5² = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 1.2×10⁶ / 25 = 3.20×10⁻⁶ N = 3.20 μN. Trap: 32.0×10⁻⁶ N is ten times too big (a slip in the power of ten), and forgetting to square r gives 16 μN.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q17"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0653","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"What is the dimensional formula for Gravitational attraction?","o":["[M L² T⁻¹]","[M L T⁻²]","[M² L² T⁻²]","[M² L⁻¹ T]"],"a":1,"e":"Gravitational attraction is a force, so its dimensions are those of force: mass × acceleration = M × L T⁻² = [M L T⁻²]. Check with F = Gm₁m₂/r², where G has [M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²].","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q18"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0654","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is Kepler’s first law?","o":["A line segment joining a planet and the Sun sweeps out equal areas during equal intervals of time, meaning that planets move faster when they are closer to the Sun.","The square of the orbital period T of a planet is directly proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis r of its orbit.","The orbit of a planet around the Sun is an ellipse with the Sun at one focus.","None of the above"],"a":2,"e":"Kepler's first law (law of ellipses): the orbit of a planet around the Sun is an ellipse with the Sun at one focus. The first option is the second law and the second option is the third law.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q23"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0655","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is Kepler’s third law?","o":["A line segment joining a planet and the Sun sweeps out equal areas during equal intervals of time, meaning that planets move faster when they are closer to the Sun.","The square of the orbital period T of a planet is directly proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis r of its orbit.","The orbit of a planet around the Sun is an ellipse with the Sun at one focus.","None of the above"],"a":1,"e":"Kepler's third law (harmonic law): T² ∝ r³, where r is the semi-major axis. The first option is the equal-areas law and the third option is the law of ellipses.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q24"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0656","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is the law of equal areas ?","o":["A line segment joining a planet and the Sun sweeps out equal areas during equal intervals of time, meaning that planets move faster when they are closer to the Sun.","The square of the orbital period T of a planet is directly proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis r of its orbit.","The orbit of a planet around the Sun is an ellipse with the Sun at one focus.","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"The law of equal areas is Kepler's second law: a line joining planet and Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal times, so the planet moves fastest when closest to the Sun.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q25"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0657","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is not a type of satellite?","o":["Student Satellite","Artificial Satellite","Natural Satellite","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"The module classifies satellites as natural (moons) or artificial (man-made). 'Student satellite' is not a category in that classification, so it is the odd one out.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q26"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0658","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"What is the dimensional formula for Gravitational potential?","o":["[M⁰ L² T⁻²]","[M⁰ L²]","[M⁰ L T⁻¹]","[M L³ T⁻²]"],"a":0,"e":"Gravitational potential V = −GM/r is energy per unit mass: [M L² T⁻²]/[M] = [L² T⁻²]. Check: G has [M⁻¹L³T⁻²], times M gives [L³T⁻²], divided by r gives [L²T⁻²].","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q27"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0659","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Newton’s law of universal gravitation states that","o":["Newton’s law of universal gravitation states that the force of gravity between two objects is independent of the medium through which they interact.","Newton’s law of universal gravitation states that the force of gravity between two objects is directly proportional to the sum of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers.","Newton’s law of universal gravitation states that the force of attraction between two bodies (masses) is directly proportional to the product of the masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.","Newton’s law of universal gravitation states that the force of gravity between two objects increases as the distance between them increases."],"a":2,"e":"The correct statement is F ∝ m₁m₂/r²: proportional to the PRODUCT of the masses, inversely proportional to the SQUARE of the distance. Traps: 'sum of the masses', and force increasing with distance.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q34"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0660","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is not a feature of Low Earth Orbit satellites?","o":["Have an altitude of approximately 160 to 2,000 km above Earth’s surface.","Is used for imaging, communications, scientific research.","Has short orbital periods.","Is used for telecommunications and weather forecasting."],"a":3,"e":"Telecommunications and weather forecasting from a fixed spot above the equator is the job of geostationary (GEO) satellites. LEO satellites sit at about 160-2,000 km, have short periods (about 90-120 min) and are used for imaging, communications and research.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q35"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0661","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following satellite orbits has an altitude of about 35,786km above the earth’s equator?","o":["Low earth Orbit","Sun- synchronous orbit","Geostationary Orbit","GEO – Synchronous Transfer orbit"],"a":2,"e":"A satellite 35,786 km above the equator has a period equal to one sidereal day and stays over the same spot: the geostationary orbit (GEO).","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q36"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0662","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is not a characteristic of escape velocity?","o":["is independent of the mass of the object attempting to escape","It increases with altitude","Escape velocity is higher on Jupiter than on the earth.","It is is approximately 1.414 times greater than the orbital velocity required to maintain a stable orbit at the same altitude."],"a":1,"e":"Escape velocity v = √(2GM/r) DEcreases with altitude because r grows. The other three statements are true: it does not depend on the escaping mass, Jupiter's (about 60 km/s) exceeds Earth's (11.2 km/s), and v_e = √2 × v_orbit ≈ 1.414 v_orbit.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q37"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0663","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Calculate the gravitational force of attraction between the Earth and a 70 kg person standing at sea Level, at a distance of 6.38 × 10^6 m from the Earth’s center. Mass of the Earth, m1 = 5.98 × 10^24 kg, Mass of the person, m2= 70 kg, Distance from the center of the Earth, r = 6.38 × 10^6 m, Gravitational constant, G = 6.673 × 10^-11 N m² kg^-2.","o":["1.2 × 10³ N","686.2 N","9800 N","48.6 N"],"a":1,"e":"F = Gm₁m₂/r² = 6.673×10⁻¹¹ × 5.98×10²⁴ × 70 / (6.38×10⁶)² = 2.793×10¹⁶ / 4.070×10¹³ ≈ 686.2 N. This is the person's weight (70 × 9.8 = 686 N).","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q38"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0664","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Calculate the gravitational force between two small masses: one mass is 9.1 × 10^−31 kg (electron), and another is 1.67 × 10^−27 kg (proton), at a distance of 1 × 10^10 m. Given: Mass of electron, M1 = 9.1 × 10^−31 kg, Mass of proton, M2 = 1.67 × 10^−27 kg, Distance, r = 1 × 10^10 m.","o":["1.013×10^47","1.017×10^-47 N","1.013×10^-47 N","1.017×10^47 N"],"a":2,"e":"F = Gm₁m₂/r². With r = 10⁻¹⁰ m (atomic separation) F = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 9.1×10⁻³¹ × 1.67×10⁻²⁷ / (10⁻¹⁰)² ≈ 1.013×10⁻⁴⁷ N. Gravity is always attractive and its magnitude here is tiny, so a positive exponent (10⁴⁷) is impossible.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q39"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0665","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which one of these is not a type of satellite orbit?","o":["Sun-synchronous Orbit","Geostationary Orbit","GEO – Synchronous Transfer orbit","None of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Sun-synchronous, geostationary and GEO-synchronous transfer are all genuine satellite orbits (as are LEO, MEO and polar), so none of the listed ones is 'not a type of satellite orbit'.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q44"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0666","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Calculate the force between the Sun and Jupiter. Assume the mass of the Sun is equals 2 x 10³⁰ kg, the mass of Jupiter equals 1.89 x 10²⁷ kg and the radius of Jupiter orbit equals 7.7 x 10¹¹ m.","o":["4.22 × 10²³ N","3.22 × 10²³ N","4.44 × 10²³ N","2.24 × 10²³ N"],"a":0,"e":"F = GMm/r² = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 2×10³⁰ × 1.89×10²⁷ / (7.7×10¹¹)² = 2.52×10⁴⁷ / 5.93×10²³ ≈ 4.25×10²³ N, closest to 4.22×10²³ N.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q7"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0667","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Calculate the gravitational attraction of two cars 5 m apart if the masses of the cars are 1000 kg and 1200 kg.","o":["3.2 × 10⁻⁴ N","3.2 × 10⁻⁶ N","3.2 × 10⁻¹⁰ N","3.2 × 10⁻⁸ N"],"a":1,"e":"F = Gm₁m₂/r² = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 1000 × 1200 / 25 = 3.2×10⁻⁶ N.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q27"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0668","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which of this equation best describe escape velocity.","o":["v = √(2GMr)","v = √(GM/r)","v = √(2GM/r²)","v = √(2GM/r)"],"a":3,"e":"Equating kinetic energy at the surface to the energy needed to reach infinity: ½mv² = GMm/r gives v_e = √(2GM/r). √(GM/r) is the circular orbital speed (smaller by a factor √2).","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q33"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0669","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these equations is best describes the gravitational potential.","o":["V = GMr²","V = GMm/r","V = GM/r","V = GM/r²"],"a":2,"e":"Gravitational potential is the potential energy per unit mass: V = −GM/r (magnitude GM/r), dimensions [L² T⁻²]. GMm/r is potential energy (it contains the test mass m) and GM/r² is the field strength g.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q36"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0670","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Lecture example 1: a 70 kg person stands at sea level, 6.38 × 10⁶ m from the Earth's centre. Taking M(Earth) = 5.98 × 10²⁴ kg and G = 6.673 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the gravitational force between the Earth and the person is closest to","o":["68.6 N","6.86 × 10⁴ N","1.09 × 10² N","686 N"],"a":3,"e":"F = GMm/r² = 6.673×10⁻¹¹ × 5.98×10²⁴ × 70 / (6.38×10⁶)² ≈ 686 N. Dividing by 70 kg gives g = 9.8 m/s², confirming it is the person's weight.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Gravitation class exercise 1"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0671","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Lecture example 2: find the gravitational force between the Earth (5.98 × 10²⁴ kg) and the Moon (7.35 × 10²² kg) at a distance of 3.844 × 10⁸ m. Use G = 6.673 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².","o":["7.6 × 10²⁸ N","1.98 × 10²⁸ N","1.98 × 10¹⁶ N","1.98 × 10²⁰ N"],"a":3,"e":"F = GMm/r² = 6.673×10⁻¹¹ × 5.98×10²⁴ × 7.35×10²² / (3.844×10⁸)² = 2.933×10³⁷ / 1.478×10¹⁷ ≈ 1.98×10²⁰ N. Trap: forgetting to square r gives 7.6×10²⁸ N; also watch the powers of ten.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Gravitation class exercise 2"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0672","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Lecture example 3: what is the gravitational pull the Earth (5.97 × 10²⁴ kg, radius 6378 km) exerts on a 0.3 kg ball lying on its surface? Use G = 6.673 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻².","o":["2.94 N","2.94 × 10⁻³ N","18.8 N","9.8 N"],"a":0,"e":"F = GMm/r² = 6.673×10⁻¹¹ × 5.97×10²⁴ × 0.3 / (6.378×10⁶)² ≈ 2.94 N (= 0.3 × 9.8). Trap: using r = 6378 (km) instead of metres gives a force 10¹² too large; 9.8 N is g, not a force.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Gravitation class exercise 3"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0673","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Lecture example 4: the gravitational force between an electron (9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg) and a proton (1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg) at an atomic-scale separation of 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m is closest to","o":["1.01 × 10⁻⁶⁷ N","1.01 × 10⁻²⁷ N","1.01 × 10⁴⁷ N","1.01 × 10⁻⁴⁷ N"],"a":3,"e":"F = Gm₁m₂/r² = 6.673×10⁻¹¹ × 9.1×10⁻³¹ × 1.67×10⁻²⁷ / (10⁻¹⁰)² ≈ 1.01×10⁻⁴⁷ N. 10⁻⁶⁷ N results if you forget to square (or divide by) r. Gravity between particles is utterly negligible next to the electric force.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Gravitation class exercise 4"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0674","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Lecture example 5: two objects attract each other with a gravitational force of 16 units. What is the new force if the distance between them is doubled?","o":["4 units","8 units","32 units","64 units"],"a":0,"e":"Inverse-square law: F ∝ 1/r². Doubling r divides F by 2² = 4, so 16/4 = 4 units. Trap: dividing by 2 (treating it as inverse, not inverse-square).","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Gravitation class exercise 5"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0675","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Lecture example: using M = 5.972 × 10²⁴ kg, r = 6.371 × 10⁶ m and G = 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the escape velocity from the Earth's surface is approximately","o":["7.9 km/s","5.0 km/s","11.2 km/s","2.4 km/s"],"a":2,"e":"v_e = √(2GM/r) = √(2 × 6.674×10⁻¹¹ × 5.972×10²⁴ / 6.371×10⁶) = √(1.251×10⁸) ≈ 1.12×10⁴ m/s = 11.2 km/s. 7.9 km/s is the circular orbital speed at the surface (v_e/√2).","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Gravitation class exercise 6 (escape velocity of Earth)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0676","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Lecture example: the Moon has mass 7.348 × 10²² kg and radius 1.7371 × 10⁶ m. Its surface escape velocity is approximately (G = 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻²)","o":["1.68 km/s","11.2 km/s","2.38 km/s","0.238 km/s"],"a":2,"e":"v_e = √(2GM/r) = √(2 × 6.674×10⁻¹¹ × 7.348×10²² / 1.7371×10⁶) = √(5.65×10⁶) ≈ 2.38×10³ m/s = 2.38 km/s. 1.68 km/s is the slip of dropping the factor 2 (√(GM/r)).","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Gravitation class exercise 7 (escape velocity of the Moon)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0677","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Lecture example: Mars has mass 6.4171 × 10²³ kg and radius 3.3895 × 10⁶ m. Its surface escape velocity is approximately (G = 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻²)","o":["3.6 km/s","11.2 km/s","25 km/s","5.0 km/s"],"a":3,"e":"v_e = √(2GM/r) = √(2 × 6.674×10⁻¹¹ × 6.4171×10²³ / 3.3895×10⁶) = √(2.527×10⁷) ≈ 5.03×10³ m/s ≈ 5 km/s. 3.6 km/s is the √(GM/r) slip.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Gravitation class exercise 8 (escape velocity of Mars)"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0678","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"If the distance between two masses is tripled and the masses are unchanged, the gravitational force between them becomes","o":["1/9 of its original value","1/3 of its original value","3 times its original value","9 times its original value"],"a":0,"e":"F ∝ 1/r², so r → 3r gives F → F/3² = F/9. Trap: using 1/r instead of 1/r².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0679","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Both masses in a two-body system are doubled while the separation is kept fixed. The gravitational force between them","o":["doubles","increases by a factor of 4","stays the same","increases by a factor of 8"],"a":1,"e":"F = Gm₁m₂/r². Doubling both m₁ and m₂ gives 2 × 2 = 4 times the force. The force depends on the product of the masses, not their sum.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0680","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The SI unit of the universal gravitational constant G is","o":["N m kg⁻¹","N kg² m⁻²","N m² kg⁻²","kg m⁻³"],"a":2,"e":"From F = Gm₁m₂/r²: G = Fr²/(m₁m₂), so the unit is N m² / kg² = N m² kg⁻² (equivalently m³ kg⁻¹ s⁻²).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0681","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensional formula of the universal gravitational constant G is","o":["[M L³ T⁻²]","[M⁻¹ L² T⁻²]","[M L T⁻²]","[M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²]"],"a":3,"e":"G = Fr²/(m₁m₂) → [M L T⁻²][L²]/[M²] = [M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²]. Trap: forgetting that the masses are in the denominator.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0682","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which statement about the gravitational force between two masses is correct?","o":["It is always attractive and acts along the line joining their centres","It can be attractive or repulsive depending on the masses","It acts perpendicular to the line joining their centres","It exists only if one of the masses is a planet"],"a":0,"e":"Gravity between any two masses is attractive, equal and opposite on the two bodies (Newton's third law), and acts along the line joining their centres. It is a universal force.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0683","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which expression gives the acceleration due to gravity g at the surface of a planet of mass M and radius R?","o":["GM/R","GM/R²","GMR","G M²/R²"],"a":1,"e":"Set weight = gravitational force: mg = GMm/R², so g = GM/R². The mass m of the falling object cancels, so all objects fall with the same g in the absence of air.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0684","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Using G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², M = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and R = 6.371 × 10⁶ m, the value of g at the Earth's surface is about","o":["62.5 m/s²","1.54 m/s²","9.81 m/s²","98.1 m/s²"],"a":2,"e":"g = GM/R² = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 5.97×10²⁴ / (6.371×10⁶)² = 3.982×10¹⁴ / 4.059×10¹³ ≈ 9.81 m/s². Forgetting to square R gives 6.25×10⁷ (the surface potential's size), not g.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0685","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The Moon has mass 7.35 × 10²² kg and radius 1.737 × 10⁶ m. Using G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the acceleration due to gravity on its surface is about","o":["2.82 m/s²","9.8 m/s²","0.16 m/s²","1.62 m/s²"],"a":3,"e":"g = GM/R² = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 7.35×10²² / (1.737×10⁶)² = 4.903×10¹² / 3.017×10¹² ≈ 1.62 m/s², about one-sixth of Earth's.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0686","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A satellite is at a height h = R above the Earth's surface, where R is the Earth's radius. The acceleration due to gravity there is","o":["g/4","g/2","g/9","g/√2"],"a":0,"e":"Distance from the centre is r = R + h = 2R. Since g ∝ 1/r², g' = g/2² = g/4. Trap: measuring distance from the surface only, or using 1/r.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0687","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Where on the Earth's surface is the measured value of g greatest?","o":["At the equator","At the poles","At the tropics","It is the same everywhere"],"a":1,"e":"The Earth bulges at the equator (larger r) and its rotation reduces the effective g there. Hence g is larger at the poles (about 9.83 m/s²) than at the equator (about 9.78 m/s²).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0688","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"As an object is taken from the Earth's surface to a great height, its mass and weight","o":["both stay constant","mass decreases, weight stays constant","mass stays constant, weight decreases","both decrease"],"a":2,"e":"Mass is the amount of matter and does not change. Weight W = mg and g = GM/r² falls with distance from the centre, so weight decreases.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0689","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The gravitational potential at a point a distance r from a mass M (zero potential at infinity) is","o":["+GM/r","−GM/r²","−GMm/r","−GM/r"],"a":3,"e":"V = −GM/r: negative because gravity is attractive and V is defined as zero at infinity (work must be done to move a mass away). −GMm/r is the potential ENERGY of a mass m; −GM/r² has the wrong form (it is like the field).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0690","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The gravitational potential energy of a mass m at distance r from the centre of the Earth (mass M) is","o":["−GMm/r","+GMm/r","−GMm/r²","mgr"],"a":0,"e":"U = −GMm/r, the work done by gravity in bringing m from infinity to r. The mgh form is only valid for small heights near the surface, where g is constant.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0691","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The SI unit of gravitational potential is","o":["J","J/kg","N/kg²","J kg"],"a":1,"e":"Gravitational potential is energy per unit mass, V = U/m, so the unit is J kg⁻¹ (= m² s⁻²). Joule alone is the unit of potential energy.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0692","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Using G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², M = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and R = 6.371 × 10⁶ m, the gravitational potential at the Earth's surface is about","o":["+6.25 × 10⁷ J/kg","−9.81 J/kg","−6.25 × 10⁷ J/kg","−6.25 × 10⁷ J"],"a":2,"e":"V = −GM/R = −6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 5.97×10²⁴ / 6.371×10⁶ ≈ −6.25×10⁷ J/kg. The sign is negative (attractive field, V = 0 at infinity) and the unit is J/kg, not J.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0693","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A satellite of mass m orbits the Earth in a circle of radius r. Which statement is correct?","o":["The orbital speed depends on the mass m of the satellite","A separate centrifugal force balances gravity on the satellite","The satellite moves at constant velocity","Gravity provides the centripetal force, so GMm/r² = mv²/r"],"a":3,"e":"GMm/r² = mv²/r gives v = √(GM/r), in which m cancels. The satellite accelerates towards the Earth continuously (velocity changes direction), so the velocity is not constant.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0694","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A heavy satellite and a light satellite orbit the Earth at the same radius. Compared with the light satellite, the heavy one has","o":["the same orbital speed and the same period","a greater orbital speed","a smaller orbital speed","the same speed but a longer period"],"a":0,"e":"v = √(GM/r) and T = 2π√(r³/GM) contain only the Earth's mass M and the radius r; the satellite's mass cancels.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0695","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Using G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², M = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and R = 6.371 × 10⁶ m, the orbital speed of a satellite 400 km above the Earth's surface is about","o":["7.91 km/s","7.67 km/s","11.2 km/s","6.43 km/s"],"a":1,"e":"r = R + h = 6.371×10⁶ + 4×10⁵ = 6.771×10⁶ m. v = √(GM/r) = √(3.982×10¹⁴ / 6.771×10⁶) ≈ 7.67×10³ m/s. 7.91 km/s results from forgetting to add the altitude to R.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0696","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A satellite orbits at r = 6.771 × 10⁶ m from the Earth's centre, with GM = 3.98 × 10¹⁴ m³ s⁻². Its period T = 2π√(r³/GM) is about","o":["1.5 minutes","24 hours","92.5 minutes","5.55 hours"],"a":2,"e":"r³ = 3.104×10²⁰; r³/GM = 7.80×10⁵; √ = 883 s; T = 2π × 883 ≈ 5.55×10³ s ≈ 92.5 min. The option 5.55 hours is the number 5.55×10³ s misread as hours; this is a typical LEO period (90-120 min).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0697","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A planet orbits the Sun at 4 times the orbital radius of another planet. By Kepler's third law (T² ∝ r³), its period is","o":["4 times as long","16 times as long","2 times as long","8 times as long"],"a":3,"e":"T² ∝ r³ gives T ∝ r^(3/2). With r × 4: T × 4^(3/2) = 4 × 2 = 8. Trap: taking T ∝ r.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0698","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Mars orbits the Sun at a mean distance of 1.524 AU (1 AU = Earth-Sun distance, Earth's period = 1 year). Using T² ∝ r³, its orbital period is about","o":["1.88 years","2.32 years","1.52 years","3.54 years"],"a":0,"e":"T² = r³ = 1.524³ = 3.540, so T = √3.540 ≈ 1.88 years. 3.54 is T², and 2.32 is the slip of using r² as T².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0699","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Jupiter orbits at 5.2 AU from the Sun. Using T² ∝ r³ with T in years and r in AU, its period is about","o":["27 years","11.9 years","5.2 years","140.6 years"],"a":1,"e":"T² = 5.2³ = 140.6, so T = √140.6 ≈ 11.9 years. 140.6 is T² (not T), and 27 is r².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0700","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"According to Kepler's second law, a planet moving in an elliptical orbit travels fastest when it is","o":["farthest from the Sun","at the end of the minor axis","closest to the Sun","at a constant speed throughout"],"a":2,"e":"The line joining planet and Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal times. A short radius needs a long arc in the same time, so the planet moves fastest at its closest approach (perihelion) and slowest at aphelion.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0701","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A comet's distance from the Sun at aphelion is twice its distance at perihelion. Kepler's second law (equal areas, i.e. v·r = constant at the two extremes) implies its speed at perihelion is","o":["half its speed at aphelion","four times its speed at aphelion","the same as its speed at aphelion","twice its speed at aphelion"],"a":3,"e":"At the extremes the velocity is perpendicular to the radius, so the areal rate is ½vr = constant. v_p r_p = v_a r_a gives v_p = v_a (r_a/r_p) = 2 v_a.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0702","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The weight of a 60 kg person standing on the Earth's surface is (take g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["588 N","60 N","6.0 N","5880 N"],"a":0,"e":"W = mg = 60 × 9.8 = 588 N. Mass (kg) is not weight (N).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0703","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A 60 kg astronaut is in a spacecraft at a height of one Earth radius above the surface. Taking g = 9.8 m/s² at the surface, the gravitational force on the astronaut is","o":["294 N","147 N","588 N","65 N"],"a":1,"e":"At r = 2R, g' = g/4 = 2.45 m/s². F = 60 × 2.45 = 147 N. (588/4 = 147.)","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0704","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which orbit has a period equal to one sidereal day and lies about 35,786 km above the equator, so that the satellite appears fixed in the sky?","o":["Low Earth orbit","Medium Earth orbit","Geostationary orbit","Polar orbit"],"a":2,"e":"Only the geostationary orbit has period 23 h 56 min (one sidereal day), is circular, equatorial and has the right radius to keep pace with the Earth's rotation.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0705","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Global navigation satellites such as GPS are placed in","o":["Low Earth orbit","Geostationary orbit","Polar orbit below 400 km","Medium Earth orbit (about 2,000-35,786 km)"],"a":3,"e":"GPS and similar navigation constellations sit in MEO (about 20,200 km altitude, period about 12 h), giving wide coverage with many satellites.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0706","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A near-polar orbit that keeps consistent solar lighting conditions for imaging is called a","o":["Sun-synchronous orbit","Geostationary orbit","Medium Earth orbit","Equatorial orbit"],"a":0,"e":"A Sun-synchronous orbit passes over each latitude at the same local solar time, giving consistent lighting; it is a near-polar orbit.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0707","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A satellite in a polar orbit is useful because","o":["it stays above one point on the equator","as the Earth rotates beneath it, it can scan the whole surface","it has no orbital period","it needs no centripetal force"],"a":1,"e":"A polar orbit passes over both poles; the Earth turns beneath it so, over time, every part of the surface comes into view. Staying over a fixed point is the geostationary orbit.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0708","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Astronauts inside an orbiting spacecraft float because","o":["there is no gravity at that height","the spacecraft is outside the Earth's gravitational field","they and the spacecraft are in free fall, accelerating together towards the Earth","air resistance supports them"],"a":2,"e":"At about 400 km the Earth's gravity is still roughly 90% of its surface value. Astronauts feel weightless because they and the craft fall together around the Earth, so the contact (normal) force is zero.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0709","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The Moon is an example of a","o":["artificial satellite","geostationary satellite","polar satellite","natural satellite"],"a":3,"e":"Satellites are natural (moons orbiting planets) or artificial (man-made). The Moon is the Earth's natural satellite.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0710","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The escape velocity from a planet depends on","o":["the planet's mass and radius only","the mass of the escaping object and the planet's mass","the direction of launch only","the mass of the escaping object only"],"a":0,"e":"v_e = √(2GM/r) contains the planet's mass M and the starting distance r; the escaping object's mass cancels because both its kinetic and potential energies are proportional to it.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0711","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The escape velocity at the Earth's surface is 11.2 km/s. The speed of a circular orbit just above the surface is therefore about","o":["15.8 km/s","7.9 km/s","5.6 km/s","11.2 km/s"],"a":1,"e":"v_e = √2 × v_orbit, so v_orbit = 11.2/1.414 ≈ 7.9 km/s. Multiplying by √2 instead of dividing gives 15.8 km/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0712","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A planet has the same radius as the Earth but twice its mass. Compared with Earth's 11.2 km/s, its escape velocity is about","o":["22.4 km/s","7.9 km/s","15.8 km/s","11.2 km/s"],"a":2,"e":"v_e ∝ √M for fixed r, so doubling M multiplies v_e by √2: 11.2 × 1.414 ≈ 15.8 km/s. Doubling v_e (22.4 km/s) would need four times the mass.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0713","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A planet has the same mass as the Earth but four times its radius. Its escape velocity, compared with Earth's 11.2 km/s, is","o":["2.8 km/s","22.4 km/s","44.8 km/s","5.6 km/s"],"a":3,"e":"v_e ∝ 1/√r. A radius four times as large gives v_e/√4 = 11.2/2 = 5.6 km/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0714","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A rocket is at a height equal to the Earth's radius (r = 2R) above the surface. Compared with its surface value of 11.2 km/s, the escape velocity at that point is about","o":["7.9 km/s","5.6 km/s","11.2 km/s","15.8 km/s"],"a":0,"e":"v_e = √(2GM/r). With r = 2R, v_e = 11.2/√2 ≈ 7.9 km/s. It decreases with altitude because gravity weakens.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0715","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Using v_e = 11.2 km/s, the minimum kinetic energy needed to launch a 1 kg object to escape the Earth from its surface (ignoring air resistance) is about","o":["1.25 × 10⁸ J","6.3 × 10⁷ J","9.8 J","6.3 × 10⁴ J"],"a":1,"e":"KE = ½mv² = 0.5 × 1 × (1.12×10⁴)² ≈ 6.3×10⁷ J. This equals GMm/R, the magnitude of the potential energy at the surface. Forgetting the ½ gives 1.25×10⁸ J.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0716","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The Moon has almost no atmosphere mainly because","o":["it has no gravity","it is too cold for gas to exist","its low escape velocity (2.4 km/s) lets gas molecules escape easily","it is too close to the Earth"],"a":2,"e":"The Moon's surface escape velocity is only 2.4 km/s, so fast-moving gas molecules can exceed it and drift away. A low escape velocity means poor atmosphere retention; Jupiter, with about 60 km/s, holds a thick atmosphere.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0717","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The Earth orbits the Sun at a mean radius of 1.496 × 10¹¹ m with a period of 3.156 × 10⁷ s. Using T² = 4π²r³/(GM) and G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the Sun's mass is about","o":["6.0 × 10²⁴ kg","2.0 × 10²⁷ kg","4.0 × 10³⁰ kg","2.0 × 10³⁰ kg"],"a":3,"e":"M = 4π²r³/(GT²) = 39.48 × 3.348×10³³ / (6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 9.96×10¹⁴) ≈ 1.99×10³⁰ kg. 6.0×10²⁴ kg is the Earth's mass; the other values come from powers-of-ten slips.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0718","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Using g = 9.8 m/s², R = 6.371 × 10⁶ m and G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², the mass of the Earth is about","o":["6.0 × 10²⁴ kg","6.0 × 10²⁰ kg","9.4 × 10¹⁷ kg","1.5 × 10²⁵ kg"],"a":0,"e":"From g = GM/R²: M = gR²/G = 9.8 × (6.371×10⁶)² / 6.67×10⁻¹¹ = 3.978×10¹⁴ / 6.67×10⁻¹¹ ≈ 5.96×10²⁴ kg ≈ 6.0×10²⁴ kg.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0719","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Using G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻², M(Earth) = 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg and an Earth-Moon distance of 3.844 × 10⁸ m, the Moon's acceleration towards the Earth is about","o":["9.8 m/s²","2.7 × 10⁻³ m/s²","2.7 × 10⁻⁶ m/s²","1.6 m/s²"],"a":1,"e":"a = GM/r² = 3.982×10¹⁴ / (3.844×10⁸)² = 3.982×10¹⁴ / 1.478×10¹⁷ ≈ 2.7×10⁻³ m/s². This is 1/3600 of g, since the Moon is about 60 Earth radii away (60² = 3600).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0720","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"At what distance from the Earth's centre (in terms of Earth's radius R) is the acceleration due to gravity equal to g/9?","o":["9R","R/3","3R","√3 R"],"a":2,"e":"g ∝ 1/r². For g/9, r² = 9R², so r = 3R (an altitude of 2R, not 3R). Trap: taking r = 9R.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0721","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"A satellite's orbital radius is increased to 4 times its original value. Its orbital speed becomes","o":["1/4 of the original","2 times the original","1/16 of the original","1/2 of the original"],"a":3,"e":"v = √(GM/r) ∝ 1/√r. With r × 4: v × 1/2.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0722","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"Which quantity is the same for the Earth in a circular orbit around the Sun and for a space probe of one-thousandth the mass in the same orbit?","o":["orbital speed","centripetal force","kinetic energy","angular momentum"],"a":0,"e":"v = √(GM_sun/r) is independent of the orbiting mass. Force, kinetic energy and angular momentum are all proportional to the orbiting body's mass.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0723","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensional formula of GM/r (with G the gravitational constant, M a mass, r a distance) is","o":["[M L² T⁻²]","[L² T⁻²]","[L T⁻²]","[M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²]"],"a":1,"e":"[G][M]/[r] = [M⁻¹L³T⁻²][M]/[L] = [L²T⁻²], the dimensions of velocity², which is why it equals the potential (energy per mass). [M L² T⁻²] is energy.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0724","c":"PHY","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity","s":"mcq","q":"The Earth moves around the Sun in an almost circular orbit of radius 1.496 × 10¹¹ m in 3.156 × 10⁷ s. Its orbital speed is about","o":["11.2 km/s","7.9 km/s","29.8 km/s","9.5 km/s"],"a":2,"e":"v = 2πr/T = 2π × 1.496×10¹¹ / 3.156×10⁷ ≈ 2.98×10⁴ m/s ≈ 29.8 km/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0725","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"rads is the unit of","o":["Angular displacement","Angular velocity","Angular acceleration","Angular momentum"],"a":1,"e":"Unit rad/s measures angular velocity (ω = Δθ/Δt). Angular displacement is in rad, angular acceleration in rad/s², angular momentum in kg m²/s. The printed 'rads' is a typo for rad/s.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Resit (Edited) — Q6"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0726","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is false about Torque?","o":["It is often referred to as the moment of force.","It is a measure of the rotational force applied to an object.","Mathematically, torque is defined as τ = F x r / sin(θ).","The SI unit of torque may be expressed In pound-feet (lb-ft)"],"a":2,"e":"Torque is τ = rF sinθ; sinθ multiplies, it does not divide. The other statements match the lecture text (pound-feet is mentioned as a non-SI unit).","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q16"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0727","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following are types of angular velocity?","o":["None of the below","Both of the below","Average angular velocity","Instantaneous angular velocity"],"a":1,"e":"Both average (Δθ/Δt over an interval) and instantaneous (limit as Δt → 0) angular velocity are types, so 'Both of the below' is correct.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q19"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0728","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A rotating disk has an angular velocity of 10 rad/s and a radius of 0.50m. What is the linear velocity of a point on the edge of the disk?","o":["5 m/s","10 m/s","15 m/s","20 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"v = rω = 0.50 × 10 = 5 m/s.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q20"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0729","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A car is traveling around a banked curve with a radius of 30 m at an angle of 15°. What is the speed at which the car should travel to avoid sliding?","o":["8.87 m/s","16.67 m/s","32.37 m/s","4.47 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"tanθ = v²/(rg), so v = √(30 × 9.8 × tan15°) = √78.8 = 8.88 m/s, matching 8.87 m/s (truncated), taking g = 9.8 m/s².","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q21"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0730","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"What is the dimensional formula for angular velocity?","o":["[T⁻²]","[M T⁻¹]","[L T⁻¹]","[T⁻¹]"],"a":3,"e":"ω = Δθ/Δt, and the radian is dimensionless, so the dimension is [T⁻¹].","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q22"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0731","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A uniform rod of length 2 m and weight 50 N is pivoted at one end. A force of 25 N acts at the free end perpendicular to the rod. What is the torque about the pivot?","o":["50 Nm","25 Nm","75 Nm","0 Nm"],"a":0,"e":"τ = rF sin90° = 2 × 25 = 50 N m (torque of the applied force).","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q29"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0732","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is false about centripetal acceleration?","o":["Centripetal acceleration always points toward the center of rotation","It is proportional to the square of velocity and inversely proportional to the radius","The net force causing centripetal acceleration must act inward; otherwise, an object will move off in a straight line due to inertia","Centripetal acceleration is a fundamental acceleration that arises from a net force acting tangentially to the circular path"],"a":3,"e":"The false one is the last: centripetal acceleration comes from a net force acting inward (radially), not tangentially. A tangential force changes speed, not direction.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q30"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0733","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A disk is spinning with an angular velocity of 10 rad/s and has a moment of inertia of 2 kg·m². What is its angular momentum?","o":["5 kg·m²/s","20 kg·m²/s","10 kg·m²/s","0 kg·m²/s"],"a":1,"e":"L = Iω = 2 × 10 = 20 kg m²/s.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q31"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0734","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A wheel rotates through an angle of 45 degrees in 22 seconds. What is its angular velocity in radians per second?","o":["0.393 rad/s","0.0357 rad/s","0.03925 rad/s","0.392 rad/s"],"a":1,"e":"45° = π/4 = 0.7854 rad. ω = 0.7854/22 = 0.0357 rad/s. The lecture slide prints 0.393 rad/s, but that comes from dividing by 2 s, not 22 s.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q40"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0735","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"An object has a centripetal acceleration of 10 m/s² while moving at a speed of 5 m/s. What is the radius of its circular path?","o":["2.5 m","50 m","2 m","25.0 m"],"a":0,"e":"a = v²/r, so r = v²/a = 25/10 = 2.5 m.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q41"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0736","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A rotating wheel has an angular velocity of 4 rad/s and a radius of 0.75 m. Calculate its centripetal acceleration.","o":["8 m/s²","48 m/s²","16 m/s²","12 m/s²"],"a":3,"e":"a = ω²r = 4² × 0.75 = 12 m/s². Trap: forgetting to square gives 3 m/s²; v = rω = 3 m/s then a = v²/r = 12 as well.","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q42"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0737","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is the dimensional formula for Torque?","o":["[M·L²·T⁻²]","[M⁻²·L⁻²]","[M·L·T⁻²]","[M·L²·T²]"],"a":0,"e":"τ = force × distance = [MLT⁻²][L] = [ML²T⁻²].","f":false,"src":"Bowen Physics PES Exam 24/25 — Q44"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0738","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A body of mass 2kg moves around a circle of radius 5m with a speed of 10 ms-1. Calculate the force towards the center, (G = 6.67 X 10-11).","o":["40N","500N","8N","3N"],"a":0,"e":"F = mv²/r = 2 × 100/5 = 40 N. G is a distractor and is not needed.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q4"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0739","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Which equations defines a force required to keep an object m moving in a circular path?","o":["F = GMm/r²","F = mv²/r","F = mrω² (as garbled in the source)","F = mv²/r²"],"a":1,"e":"The centripetal force is F = mv²/r. Option D lacks the correct power of r.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q6"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0740","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"An object moving in a circle of radius 'r' with a constant speed 'v' has a constant acceleration toward the center equal to _____","o":["v²/r","v/r","v² × r","v × r"],"a":0,"e":"Centripetal acceleration a = v²/r. (Magnitude is constant; direction keeps changing.)","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q38"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0741","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A car is moving around a circular track with a radius of 50 meters at a speed of 20 meters per second. What is its centripetal acceleration?","o":["0.4 m/s²","2.5 m/s²","8 m/s²","20 m/s²"],"a":2,"e":"a = v²/r = 400/50 = 8 m/s². Trap: 20/50 = 0.4 forgets to square.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Circular motion class exercise 1"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0742","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A wheel rotates through an angle of 30 radians in 5 seconds. What is its angular velocity?","o":["150 rad/s","25 rad/s","35 rad/s","6 rad/s"],"a":3,"e":"ω = Δθ/Δt = 30/5 = 6 rad/s.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Angular velocity class exercise 1"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0743","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Classwork: A rotating disk has an angular velocity of 10 rad/s and a radius of 0.50 m. What is the linear velocity of a point on the edge of the disk?","o":["20 m/s","0.05 m/s","5 m/s","5 rad/s"],"a":2,"e":"v = rω = 0.50 × 10 = 5 m/s.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Angular velocity classwork 1"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0744","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Classwork: A car is traveling around a banked curve with a radius of 30 m at an angle of 15°. What speed avoids sliding? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["9.8 m/s","8.9 m/s","11.8 m/s","29.4 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"v = √(rg tanθ) = √(30 × 9.8 × 0.2679) = 8.88 ≈ 8.9 m/s.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Lecture Class Exercise — Angular velocity classwork 2"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0745","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Convert 90° to radians.","o":["π rad (3.14 rad)","π/2 rad (1.57 rad)","π/4 rad (0.785 rad)","90 rad"],"a":1,"e":"rad = deg × π/180 = 90π/180 = π/2 = 1.57 rad.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0746","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Convert 3π/4 rad to degrees.","o":["135°","270°","45°","75°"],"a":0,"e":"deg = rad × 180/π = (3π/4)(180/π) = 135°.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0747","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A flywheel makes 2 complete revolutions. Its angular displacement is","o":["6.28 rad","720 rad","12.57 rad","3.14 rad"],"a":2,"e":"2 rev × 2π rad/rev = 4π = 12.57 rad. Trap: 2π (6.28) is only one revolution.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0748","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A motor turns at 120 rpm. Its angular velocity in rad/s is","o":["120 rad/s","4π rad/s (12.57 rad/s)","2π rad/s (6.28 rad/s)","240π rad/s"],"a":1,"e":"ω = 120 × 2π/60 = 4π = 12.57 rad/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0749","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A body moves in a circle with period 0.50 s. Its angular velocity is","o":["3.14 rad/s","6.28 rad/s","2.0 rad/s","12.57 rad/s"],"a":3,"e":"ω = 2π/T = 2π/0.5 = 12.57 rad/s. Trap: 1/T = 2 is the frequency in Hz.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0750","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A turntable rotates at a frequency of 50 Hz. Its angular velocity is","o":["50 rad/s","157 rad/s","314 rad/s","7.96 rad/s"],"a":2,"e":"ω = 2πf = 2π × 50 = 314 rad/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0751","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A particle moves in a circle of radius 2 m with ω = 5 rad/s. Its centripetal acceleration is","o":["10 m/s²","50 m/s²","25 m/s²","5 m/s²"],"a":1,"e":"a = ω²r = 25 × 2 = 50 m/s². Trap: ωr = 10 m/s is the speed, not acceleration.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0752","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A 0.5 kg stone is whirled on a 0.8 m string at 4 m/s in a horizontal circle (ignore gravity). The tension is","o":["10 N","8 N","2.5 N","16 N"],"a":0,"e":"T = mv²/r = 0.5 × 16/0.8 = 10 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0753","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A point at r = 0.20 m on a wheel turning at 15 rad/s has linear speed","o":["3 m/s","75 m/s","0.013 m/s","300 m/s"],"a":0,"e":"v = rω = 0.20 × 15 = 3 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0754","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A 1000 kg car rounds a flat bend of radius 50 m at 10 m/s. The friction force needed is","o":["200 N","20 000 N","500 N","2000 N"],"a":3,"e":"F = mv²/r = 1000 × 100/50 = 2000 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0755","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"On a flat road with μ = 0.5, the maximum speed on a bend of radius 40 m is (g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["196 m/s","7 m/s","14 m/s","20 m/s"],"a":2,"e":"v = √(μrg) = √(0.5 × 40 × 9.8) = √196 = 14 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0756","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A frictionless banked track of radius 50 m is inclined at 20°. The design speed is (g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["17.9 m/s","13.4 m/s","5.9 m/s","25 m/s"],"a":1,"e":"v = √(rg tanθ) = √(50 × 9.8 × 0.364) = √178.3 = 13.4 m/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0757","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A 20 N force acts perpendicular to a spanner at 0.30 m from the nut. The torque is","o":["60 N m","0.015 N m","20.3 N m","6 N m"],"a":3,"e":"τ = rF sin90° = 0.30 × 20 = 6 N m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0758","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A 40 N force acts at 0.5 m from a pivot, at 30° to the line from the pivot. The torque is","o":["17.3 N m","20 N m","10 N m","40 N m"],"a":2,"e":"τ = rF sinθ = 0.5 × 40 × sin30° = 10 N m. Trap: using cos30° gives 17.3.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0759","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Which quantity has the same dimensions as torque?","o":["Force","Power","Energy","Momentum"],"a":2,"e":"Torque = force × distance = [ML²T⁻²], the same as energy (work). They are still different quantities (torque is a vector, N m not J).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0760","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"The moment of inertia of a 2 kg point mass at 0.5 m from the axis is","o":["0.5 kg m²","1.0 kg m²","0.25 kg m²","2.0 kg m²"],"a":0,"e":"I = mr² = 2 × 0.25 = 0.5 kg m².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0761","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A solid disc of mass 6 kg and radius 0.4 m has moment of inertia about its central axis","o":["0.96 kg m²","0.24 kg m²","2.4 kg m²","0.48 kg m²"],"a":3,"e":"I = ½MR² = ½ × 6 × 0.16 = 0.48 kg m². Trap: MR² (ring formula) gives 0.96.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0762","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A uniform rod of mass 3 kg and length 2 m rotates about one end. Its moment of inertia is","o":["1 kg m²","4 kg m²","12 kg m²","2 kg m²"],"a":1,"e":"I = (1/3)ML² = (1/3)(3)(4) = 4 kg m².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0763","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"The same rod (3 kg, 2 m) is rotated about its centre. Its moment of inertia is","o":["1 kg m²","4 kg m²","0.5 kg m²","2 kg m²"],"a":0,"e":"I = (1/12)ML² = (1/12)(3)(4) = 1 kg m².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0764","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A disc has I_cm = 0.5 kg m² and mass 4 kg. About a parallel axis 0.3 m from the centre, I is","o":["0.54 kg m²","1.7 kg m²","0.86 kg m²","0.36 kg m²"],"a":2,"e":"I = I_cm + Md² = 0.5 + 4 × 0.09 = 0.86 kg m².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0765","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A net torque of 12 N m acts on a body of moment of inertia 3 kg m². The angular acceleration is","o":["36 rad/s²","4 rad/s²","0.25 rad/s²","9 rad/s²"],"a":1,"e":"α = τ/I = 12/3 = 4 rad/s².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0766","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A wheel starts from rest with α = 2 rad/s². After 5 s its angular velocity is","o":["10 rad/s","25 rad/s","2.5 rad/s","7 rad/s"],"a":0,"e":"ω = ω₀ + αt = 0 + 2 × 5 = 10 rad/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0767","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A flywheel with I = 0.4 kg m² spins at 15 rad/s. Its angular momentum is","o":["3 kg m²/s","0.027 kg m²/s","6 kg m²/s","60 kg m²/s"],"a":2,"e":"L = Iω = 0.4 × 15 = 6 kg m²/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0768","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A skater has I = 4 kg m² at 3 rad/s, then pulls in her arms so I = 1 kg m². Her new angular velocity is","o":["0.75 rad/s","3 rad/s","9 rad/s","12 rad/s"],"a":3,"e":"I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂ → ω₂ = 4 × 3/1 = 12 rad/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0769","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A rotor has I = 2 kg m² and ω = 10 rad/s. Its rotational kinetic energy is","o":["200 J","20 J","100 J","50 J"],"a":2,"e":"KE = ½Iω² = ½ × 2 × 100 = 100 J. Trap: forgetting ½ gives 200 J; Iω gives 20.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0770","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A 0.2 kg particle moves at 5 m/s in a circle of radius 2 m. Its angular momentum about the centre is","o":["0.5 kg m²/s","10 kg m²/s","5 kg m²/s","2 kg m²/s"],"a":3,"e":"L = mvr = 0.2 × 5 × 2 = 2 kg m²/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0771","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"The SI unit of angular momentum is","o":["N m","kg m/s","kg m²/s","rad/s"],"a":2,"e":"L = Iω = kg m² × rad/s = kg m²/s (equivalently J s). N m is torque; kg m/s is linear momentum.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0772","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensional formula of angular momentum is","o":["[MLT⁻¹]","[ML²T⁻²]","[ML²T⁻¹]","[ML⁻¹T⁻¹]"],"a":2,"e":"L = mvr = [M][LT⁻¹][L] = [ML²T⁻¹]. [ML²T⁻²] is torque.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0773","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"The direction of the torque vector τ = r × F is","o":["along r","perpendicular to the plane containing r and F","along F","tangent to the circle of motion"],"a":1,"e":"The cross product is perpendicular to both vectors, given by the right-hand rule.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0774","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A child of 30 kg sits 2 m from the pivot of a see-saw. Where must a 40 kg child sit to balance it? (distance from pivot)","o":["2.0 m","2.7 m","1.0 m","1.5 m"],"a":3,"e":"30 × 2 = 40 × d → d = 1.5 m. The heavier child sits closer.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0775","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A light beam is balanced about a pivot with 20 N at 0.4 m on one side. What force at 0.1 m on the other side balances it?","o":["5 N","20 N","80 N","8 N"],"a":2,"e":"20 × 0.4 = F × 0.1 → F = 80 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0776","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"In uniform circular motion, which quantity stays constant?","o":["Speed","Velocity","Acceleration vector","Momentum vector"],"a":0,"e":"Speed is constant by definition; velocity and momentum change direction, and the acceleration vector always points to the centre, so it changes direction too.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0777","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Centripetal force is best described as","o":["the net inward force, supplied by real forces such as tension or gravity","a separate force that must be added to all other forces","an outward force that balances gravity","a force along the tangent"],"a":0,"e":"Centripetal force is the name for the net inward force; real forces (tension, gravity, friction, normal reaction) supply it. It is never an extra force on a free-body diagram.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0778","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A 1 kg ball on a string swings in a vertical circle of radius 1 m. At the lowest point its speed is 3 m/s. The tension is (g = 10 m/s²)","o":["9 N","19 N","10 N","1 N"],"a":1,"e":"T − mg = mv²/r → T = 10 + 9 = 19 N. Trap: forgetting the weight gives 9 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0779","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"If the speed in a circle of fixed radius doubles, the centripetal force becomes","o":["2 times","4 times","half","unchanged"],"a":1,"e":"F = mv²/r, so doubling v multiplies F by 2² = 4.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0780","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"If the radius is halved at constant speed, the centripetal force becomes","o":["half as large","four times as large","unchanged","twice as large"],"a":3,"e":"F = mv²/r is inversely proportional to r, so halving r doubles F.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0781","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"On a frictionless banked curve, the design speed depends on","o":["the radius and banking angle but not the car's mass","the car's mass and radius only","the car's mass and banking angle only","the mass, radius and angle equally"],"a":0,"e":"tanθ = v²/(rg): mass cancels, so only r and θ matter.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0782","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"In non-uniform circular motion, the total acceleration","o":["points only to the centre","is zero","points only along the tangent","has both a centripetal and a tangential component"],"a":3,"e":"Changing speed gives a tangential component dv/dt; changing direction gives the centripetal component v²/r.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0783","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A body moves with constant angular velocity. Its average and instantaneous angular velocities are","o":["different, average larger","different, instantaneous larger","equal","both zero"],"a":2,"e":"If ω is constant, Δθ/Δt is the same over any interval, so average equals instantaneous.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0784","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"The direction of the angular velocity vector of a spinning wheel is","o":["tangent to the rim","radially outward","towards the centre of the rim","along the axis of rotation"],"a":3,"e":"ω is an axial vector along the rotation axis, direction by the right-hand rule.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0785","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"The torque of a force about a point is zero when","o":["the force is perpendicular to the lever arm","the line of action passes through the point","the force is large","the lever arm is large"],"a":1,"e":"τ = rF sinθ is zero when the line of action goes through the pivot (moment arm zero, θ = 0° or 180°).","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0786","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"The unit of angular acceleration is","o":["rad/s","rad/s²","N m","kg m²"],"a":1,"e":"α = Δω/Δt = (rad/s)/s = rad/s².","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0787","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"The dimensional formula of moment of inertia is","o":["[ML²T⁻²]","[MLT⁻²]","[M L T⁻¹]","[ML²]"],"a":3,"e":"I = Σmr² = [M][L²] = [ML²].","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0788","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"If the net external torque on a system is zero, which quantity is constant?","o":["Angular velocity","Moment of inertia","Angular momentum","Kinetic energy"],"a":2,"e":"τ_net = dL/dt = 0, so angular momentum is conserved. ω changes if I changes, and kinetic energy may change too.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0789","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Four bodies have equal mass M and radius R: a ring, a solid disc, a solid sphere, a point mass at R. About a central axis, the largest moment of inertia belongs to the","o":["solid disc","solid sphere","none, all equal","ring (and point mass at R, MR²)"],"a":3,"e":"Ring and point mass at R both have I = MR², the largest; disc ½MR², sphere (2/5)MR². More mass far from the axis means larger I.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0790","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"Earth rotates once in 86 400 s. Its angular velocity is about","o":["7.3 × 10⁻³ rad/s","6.28 rad/s","7.3 × 10⁻⁵ rad/s","1.16 × 10⁻⁵ rad/s"],"a":2,"e":"ω = 2π/86400 = 7.27 × 10⁻⁵ rad/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0791","c":"PHY","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum","s":"mcq","q":"A wheel of radius 0.3 m rolls without slipping at 6 m/s. Its angular velocity is","o":["1.8 rad/s","2 rad/s","0.05 rad/s","20 rad/s"],"a":3,"e":"ω = v/R = 6/0.3 = 20 rad/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0792","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Which part of a gyroscope is the spinning disc that generates its angular momentum?","o":["Rotor","Gimbal","Pivot stand","Housing"],"a":0,"e":"The rotor is the spinning disc. Its angular momentum L = Iω gives the gyroscope its stability. The gimbal is the pivoted support, not the spinning part.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0793","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"What is the function of the gimbal in a gyroscope?","o":["It stores the spin energy of the rotor","A pivoted support that lets the rotor turn freely about several axes","It is the axis about which the rotor spins","It supplies the torque that drives the rotor"],"a":1,"e":"The gimbal is a set of pivoted rings. It isolates the rotor from base rotations so the base cannot apply a torque to the spin axis.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0794","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The spin axis of a gyroscope is:","o":["The axis of the gimbal rings","The vertical line through the pivot","The line through the rotor's centre about which it spins","The direction of the applied torque"],"a":2,"e":"The spin axis is the line about which the rotor rotates. Its direction gives the direction of L. The gimbal rings and the applied torque are different things.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0795","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"For a rigid body spinning about a fixed axis, the magnitude of its angular momentum is:","o":["L = ½Iω²","L = I/ω","L = Mv","L = Iω"],"a":3,"e":"L = Iω is the definition for a rigid body spinning about a fixed axis. ½Iω² is rotational kinetic energy, I/ω has the wrong dimensions, and Mv is linear momentum.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0796","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The SI unit of angular momentum is:","o":["kg m² s⁻¹","kg m s⁻¹","kg m² s⁻²","kg m⁻² s"],"a":0,"e":"L = Iω has units (kg m²)(s⁻¹) = kg m² s⁻¹. kg m s⁻¹ is linear momentum; kg m² s⁻² is energy.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0797","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"In the precession relation ω_P = rMg/(Iω), the right-hand side is equal to:","o":["L/τ","τ/L","Iω/τ","τ × L"],"a":1,"e":"The torque is τ = rMg and the angular momentum is L = Iω, so rMg/(Iω) = τ/L. Inverting this gives the wrong quantity, and τ × L is a vector product, not a rate.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0798","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Nutation is:","o":["A slow sideways drift caused by air friction","A change in the rotor's spin speed caused by gravity","A small periodic wobble of the spin axis superimposed on steady precession","A torque acting parallel to the spin axis"],"a":2,"e":"Nutation is a small, rapid periodic wobble added to steady precession. It is not caused by friction or by a parallel torque.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0799","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Earth's axis completes one full precession cycle in roughly:","o":["26 years","26,000 days","260,000 years","26,000 years"],"a":3,"e":"The axial precession of the Earth takes about 26,000 years. The other options are wrong by factors of about 1000 or 10.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0800","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The main cause of the precession of Earth's axis is:","o":["Gravitational torques from the Sun and Moon acting on Earth's equatorial bulge","The solar wind pushing on the atmosphere","Continental drift moving the poles","Magnetic torque from the molten core"],"a":0,"e":"The equatorial bulge is pulled by the Sun and Moon, producing a torque perpendicular to the spin axis. That torque makes the axis precess.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0801","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The physical principle that gives a gyroscope its rigidity in space is:","o":["Conservation of linear momentum","Conservation of angular momentum","Conservation of energy alone","Conservation of electric charge"],"a":1,"e":"With no external torque, the angular momentum of the rotor is conserved, so its spin axis keeps its direction in space.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0802","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Most smartphone gyroscopes sense rotation using:","o":["A spinning steel rotor in a gimbal","A magnetised needle aligned with the Earth's field","Tiny vibrating elements (MEMS) that respond to the Coriolis effect","A camera tracking the horizon"],"a":2,"e":"Phone gyroscopes are MEMS devices with vibrating micro-structures, not spinning rotors. The Coriolis effect on the vibration gives the rotation rate.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0803","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A gyrocompass finds true north mainly by using:","o":["The Earth's magnetic field","The position of the Sun","The position of the Pole Star","The rotation of the Earth"],"a":3,"e":"A gyrocompass uses the gyroscope's rigidity together with the Earth's rotation. It works without the magnetic field, which is why it is used on ships.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0804","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these quantities is a vector?","o":["Angular momentum","Moment of inertia","Rotational kinetic energy","Mass"],"a":0,"e":"Angular momentum has a direction along the spin axis, so it is a vector. The others are scalars.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0805","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The SI unit of the precession angular velocity ω_P is:","o":["rad s⁻²","rad s⁻¹","m s⁻¹","kg m² s⁻¹"],"a":1,"e":"ω_P is an angular velocity, so its unit is rad s⁻¹. rad s⁻² is angular acceleration, and kg m² s⁻¹ is angular momentum.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0806","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"When a tilted gyroscope precesses, the tip of its spin axis traces:","o":["A straight line toward the ground","A spiral that shrinks to zero","A cone","A figure-eight"],"a":2,"e":"With the spin axis inclined, precession makes the tip move around a cone about the vertical. The axis does not fall straight down.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0807","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The tilt of Earth's axis from the perpendicular to its orbit is about:","o":["66.5°","45°","90°","23.5°"],"a":3,"e":"Earth's axial tilt is about 23.5°. The precession traces a cone of this half-angle. 66.5° is the complement, not the tilt.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0808","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The formula ω_P = rMg/(Iω) is valid when:","o":["The rotor spins much faster than it precesses (ω ≫ ω_P)","The rotor spins much slower than it precesses","The torque is parallel to the spin axis","The rotor has zero mass"],"a":0,"e":"The formula assumes a fast spin relative to the precession rate, a rigid rotor, and a torque perpendicular to L.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0809","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A torque acting on a body is equal to:","o":["Its angular momentum divided by its angular velocity","The rate of change of its angular momentum","The rate of change of its linear momentum","The product of its mass and angular velocity"],"a":1,"e":"The rotational form of Newton's second law is τ = dL/dt. The others do not describe a torque.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0810","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The rotational analogue of mass, which measures resistance to change in rotation, is:","o":["Torque","Angular velocity","Moment of inertia","Angular momentum"],"a":2,"e":"Moment of inertia I plays the role of mass in rotation, and it sets the resistance to angular acceleration.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0811","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A torque is applied to a spinning rotor parallel to its angular momentum L. What happens?","o":["It makes the spin axis precess in a cone","It changes only the direction of L","It has no effect on L at all","It changes only the magnitude of L, speeding up or slowing the spin, with no precession"],"a":3,"e":"A torque parallel to L gives dL parallel to L, so only |L| changes. Precession needs a component of torque perpendicular to L.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0812","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Why does a spinning gyroscope with its centre of mass offset from the pivot not simply fall over under gravity?","o":["Gravity's torque is perpendicular to L, so it changes the direction of L and the axis sweeps sideways","Angular momentum cancels the weight of the rotor","Friction at the pivot holds the axis up","The rotor is too light for gravity to act on it"],"a":0,"e":"The gravitational torque is perpendicular to L. It turns L sideways (precession) rather than tilting the spin axis downward.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0813","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The spin rate of a gyroscope is doubled, while its mass, moment of inertia and r stay the same. Its precession rate:","o":["Doubles","Halves","Stays the same","Quadruples"],"a":1,"e":"ω_P = rMg/(Iω). Doubling ω halves ω_P.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0814","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The moment of inertia I of a rotor is increased, with M, r and ω unchanged. Its precession rate:","o":["Increases","Stays the same","Decreases","Drops to zero"],"a":2,"e":"ω_P = rMg/(Iω) is inversely proportional to I. A larger I gives a slower precession.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0815","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A rotor slows down because of friction, while M, r and I stay fixed. Its precession rate:","o":["Decreases","Stays the same","Stops at once","Increases"],"a":3,"e":"ω_P is inversely proportional to ω. As ω falls, ω_P rises. The precession speeds up as the spin dies away.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0816","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"When a torque acts on a spinning rotor, the tip of its angular momentum vector moves:","o":["In the direction of the torque","Opposite to the torque","Along L, making the rotor spin faster","Straight down toward the ground"],"a":0,"e":"dL = τ dt, so L moves in the direction of τ. The tip of L follows the torque, and the rotor is not tipped downward.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0817","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Which statement about precession is FALSE?","o":["ω_P is proportional to the torque and inversely proportional to L","Precession is produced by a torque acting parallel to the spin axis","The spin axis of a tilted gyroscope sweeps out a cone","A faster spin gives a slower precession"],"a":1,"e":"Precession requires a torque perpendicular to L. A torque parallel to the spin axis changes only the spin rate. The other three statements are correct.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0818","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A gyroscope's pivot-to-centre-of-mass line is exactly vertical (θ = 0), with the spin axis along that line. The gravitational torque about the pivot is:","o":["Maximum, so precession is fastest","rMg, the full value","Zero, so there is no precession","rMg/ω"],"a":2,"e":"τ = rMg sinθ. With θ = 0, sinθ = 0, so the torque vanishes and the gyroscope does not precess.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0819","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The gimbal improves a gyroscope's performance mainly because:","o":["It adds to the rotor's moment of inertia","It supplies energy to keep the rotor spinning","It cancels gravity on the rotor","It isolates the rotor so that base rotations do not apply torques to the spin axis"],"a":3,"e":"Freedom in the gimbal rings means the base can rotate without transmitting a torque to the spin axis, so the rotor keeps its orientation.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0820","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A gyroscope spins very fast. Why does its precession appear slow?","o":["Its large angular momentum L makes τ/L small","Friction at high speed cancels the torque","Fast-spinning rotors have a very small mass","Gravity acts more weakly on spinning objects"],"a":0,"e":"ω_P = τ/L. A fast spin makes L large, so the precession rate is small.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0821","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A large spinning rotor is used as a ship stabiliser. It resists rolling because:","o":["The rotor's weight pulls the ship back upright","Its angular momentum resists changes in the direction of its spin axis","Spinning rotors repel water","The rotor increases the ship's mass"],"a":1,"e":"The rotor's angular momentum keeps its axis fixed in direction, so a roll that tries to tilt the axis meets a large resisting torque.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0822","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The Pole Star has not always been the same star because:","o":["The Pole Star itself moves across the sky","Earth's spin speed changes gradually","Earth's axis precesses, slowly moving the direction of the celestial pole over about 26,000 years","Continental drift moves the North Pole"],"a":2,"e":"The precession of Earth's axis changes which point in the sky lies near the celestial pole. The stars themselves barely move on this timescale.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0823","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A spinning bicycle wheel is held by its axle, and a horizontal torque is applied to the axle. The wheel turns sideways in the horizontal plane because:","o":["The torque is parallel to L and speeds up the wheel","Friction at the hub pushes the wheel sideways","The wheel's centre of mass moves toward the torque","The torque is perpendicular to L, so L turns in the direction of the torque"],"a":3,"e":"A horizontal torque perpendicular to the wheel's L makes L, and so the spin axis, turn in the direction of the torque. That is precession.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0824","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Two identical gyroscopes have the same torque applied. Gyroscope A spins faster than B. Which precesses more slowly, and why?","o":["A, because ω_P is inversely proportional to the spin rate","B, because the slower spin gives more torque","Both precess at the same rate, because the torque is the same","Neither, because a fast spin stops precession"],"a":0,"e":"ω_P = τ/L with L = Iω. A larger spin gives a larger L and so a smaller ω_P.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0825","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A rotor has its angular momentum L along +z, and a torque acts along +x. The precession vector ω_P points:","o":["Along −y","Along +y","Along +x","Along +z"],"a":1,"e":"ω_P is along L × τ. Here z × x = y, so ω_P points along +y. The sign comes from the right-hand rule.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0826","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"If the pivot-to-centre-of-mass distance r is doubled, with M, I and ω fixed, the precession rate:","o":["Halves","Stays the same","Doubles","Quadruples"],"a":2,"e":"ω_P = rMg/(Iω) is proportional to r. Doubling r doubles the torque and so doubles ω_P.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0827","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Nutation is most noticeable when:","o":["The rotor has exactly zero spin","The torque is exactly zero","The rotor is perfectly balanced at its centre","The spin axis is initially tilted or the gyroscope is given a sudden disturbance"],"a":3,"e":"Nutation is the wobble superimposed on steady precession. It arises from an initial tilt or a disturbance of the spin axis.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0828","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Earth's precession causes the equinox points to:","o":["Slowly shift westward along the ecliptic, about 50 arcseconds per year","Remain fixed relative to the stars","Move eastward at 1° per year","Shift once every 26 years"],"a":0,"e":"The equinox points drift westward by about 50 arcseconds per year because of the precession of Earth's axis.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0829","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Inertial navigation systems can track orientation without outside reference because:","o":["They receive constant signals from satellites","Gyroscopes keep a fixed reference direction in space from inside the vehicle","The Earth's magnetic field is always uniform","They use the rotor's weight as a reference"],"a":1,"e":"A gyroscope's rigidity in space gives a fixed reference direction. Changes in orientation are measured relative to it without any external signal.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0830","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A brief torque acts on a rotor perpendicular to its angular momentum. Which property of L changes?","o":["Only its magnitude","Both magnitude and direction equally","Only its direction","Neither"],"a":2,"e":"A torque perpendicular to L gives dL perpendicular to L, so |L| stays the same while its direction turns.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0831","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Which change increases a rotor's moment of inertia for the same mass?","o":["Moving the mass closer to the spin axis","Increasing the spin rate","Lowering the pivot","Moving the mass farther from the spin axis (a rim-heavy design)"],"a":3,"e":"I = Σmr². Moving mass away from the axis increases r and hence I.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0832","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Gyroscopes A and B have the same angular momentum. The torque on A is twice that on B. A's precession rate is:","o":["Twice B's","Half B's","The same as B's","Four times B's"],"a":0,"e":"ω_P = τ/L. With the same L and twice the torque, A's precession rate is twice B's.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0833","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A rotor has M = 0.50 kg, a centre of mass r = 0.040 m from the pivot, I = 1.0×10⁻³ kg m² and spins at ω = 100 rad/s. Take g = 9.8 m/s². With the spin axis horizontal, what is the precession angular velocity ω_P?","o":["196 rad/s","1.96 rad/s","0.00196 rad/s","0.196 rad/s"],"a":1,"e":"Working: τ = rMg = 0.040 × 0.50 × 9.8 = 0.196 N m; L = Iω = 1.0×10⁻³ × 100 = 0.10 kg m² s⁻¹; ω_P = τ/L = 1.96 rad/s. Forgetting ω gives 196 rad/s; forgetting I and ω gives 0.196 rad/s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0834","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A uniform disc of mass 2.0 kg and radius 0.10 m spins about its central axis at 200 rad/s. Using I = ½MR², what is its angular momentum?","o":["0.010 kg m² s⁻¹","4.0 kg m² s⁻¹","2.0 kg m² s⁻¹","200 kg m² s⁻¹"],"a":2,"e":"Working: I = ½ × 2.0 × 0.10² = 0.010 kg m²; L = Iω = 0.010 × 200 = 2.0 kg m² s⁻¹. Using I = MR² gives 4.0; forgetting ω gives 0.010.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0835","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A horizontal disc spinning with angular momentum L = 2.0 kg m² s⁻¹ has mass 2.0 kg, and its centre of mass is 0.050 m from the pivot. Take g = 9.8 m/s². What is its precession angular velocity?","o":["0.98 rad/s","98 rad/s","0.0049 rad/s","0.49 rad/s"],"a":3,"e":"Working: τ = rMg = 0.050 × 2.0 × 9.8 = 0.98 N m; ω_P = τ/L = 0.98/2.0 = 0.49 rad/s. Using τ alone gives 0.98; dividing by I instead of L gives 98.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0836","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A torque of 0.98 N m acts perpendicular to a rotor's angular momentum of 2.0 kg m² s⁻¹. How long does the spin axis take to complete one full precession circle?","o":["12.8 s","6.4 s","3.2 s","25.6 s"],"a":0,"e":"Working: ω_P = τ/L = 0.98/2.0 = 0.49 rad/s; T = 2π/ω_P = 6.283/0.49 ≈ 12.8 s. Half a circle takes 6.4 s, a quarter 3.2 s, and doubling gives 25.6 s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0837","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The line from a gyroscope's pivot to its centre of mass is 0.10 m long and makes 30° with the vertical. The mass is 1.5 kg, the spin axis lies along this line, and g = 9.8 m/s². What is the torque of gravity about the pivot?","o":["1.47 N m","0.735 N m","1.27 N m","0.0735 N m"],"a":1,"e":"Working: τ = rMg sinθ = 0.10 × 1.5 × 9.8 × sin 30° = 1.47 × 0.5 = 0.735 N m. Omitting sinθ gives 1.47; using cos 30° gives 1.27; a unit slip on r gives 0.0735.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0838","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Earth's axis precesses with a period of about 26,000 years, which makes the equinox points drift. Roughly how far does the equinox move each year, as an angle?","o":["About 5 arcseconds","About 500 arcseconds","About 50 arcseconds","About 1 degree"],"a":2,"e":"Working: 360° × 3600 ″/° ÷ 26000 yr = 49.8 arcseconds per year, about 50 arcseconds. Off by a factor of 10 gives 5 or 500 arcseconds; 1 degree per year is far too large.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0839","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"Take Earth's axis precession period as 26,000 years, with 1 year = 3.16×10⁷ s. What is the precession angular velocity ω_P in rad/s?","o":["2.4×10⁻⁴ rad/s","7.6×10⁻⁹ rad/s","7.6×10⁻¹⁴ rad/s","7.6×10⁻¹² rad/s"],"a":3,"e":"Working: T = 26000 × 3.16×10⁷ s = 8.22×10¹¹ s; ω_P = 2π/T = 6.283/8.22×10¹¹ ≈ 7.6×10⁻¹² rad/s. Leaving the period in years gives 2.4×10⁻⁴ rad per year; the other options are powers of ten away.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0840","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A rotor spins at 3000 rev/min and has moment of inertia I = 2.0×10⁻³ kg m² about its spin axis. What is its angular momentum?","o":["0.63 kg m² s⁻¹","6.3 kg m² s⁻¹","37.7 kg m² s⁻¹","314 kg m² s⁻¹"],"a":0,"e":"Working: ω = 2π × 3000/60 = 314 rad/s; L = Iω = 0.0020 × 314 = 0.63 kg m² s⁻¹. Forgetting to divide by 60 gives 37.7; forgetting I gives 314.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0841","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A rotor of mass 4.0 kg has its centre of mass 0.020 m from a horizontal pivot. Its I = 4.0×10⁻³ kg m² and it spins at ω = 50 rad/s. Take g = 9.8 m/s². What is ω_P?","o":["0.157 rad/s","3.92 rad/s","0.784 rad/s","0.0392 rad/s"],"a":1,"e":"Working: τ = rMg = 0.020 × 4.0 × 9.8 = 0.784 N m; L = Iω = 0.0040 × 50 = 0.20 kg m² s⁻¹; ω_P = 0.784/0.20 = 3.92 rad/s. Multiplying by L instead of dividing gives 0.157; forgetting L gives 0.784.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0842","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A torque of 0.50 N m acts perpendicular to the spin angular momentum of a rotor whose L = 2.0 kg m² s⁻¹. What is the period of precession?","o":["12.6 s","0.25 s","25.1 s","4.0 s"],"a":2,"e":"Working: ω_P = τ/L = 0.50/2.0 = 0.25 rad/s; T = 2π/0.25 = 25.1 s. Forgetting L gives ω_P = 0.5 and T = 12.6 s; using L/τ gives 4.0 s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0843","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A rotor has angular momentum L = 0.50 kg m² s⁻¹. A torque of 0.20 N m acts perpendicular to L for 3.0 s. Through what angle does L turn?","o":["About 1.2°","About 34°","About 172°","About 69°"],"a":3,"e":"Working: angle = ω_P t = (τ/L) t = (0.20/0.50) × 3.0 = 1.2 rad = 68.8°, about 69°. Reading 1.2 rad as degrees gives 1.2°; forgetting to divide by L gives 0.6 rad = 34°; treating 3 rad as the angle gives 172°.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0844","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A gyroscope's spin rate ω is doubled and its mass M is doubled, with I, r and g unchanged. What happens to its precession rate ω_P?","o":["It is unchanged","It doubles","It halves","It quadruples"],"a":0,"e":"ω_P = rMg/(Iω). The factor 2 from M cancels the factor 1/2 from ω, so ω_P stays the same.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0845","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A gyroscope has ω_P = 2.0 rad/s, I = 0.010 kg m², spin ω = 60 rad/s, and its centre of mass is 0.050 m from a horizontal pivot. Take g = 9.8 m/s². What mass M is needed?","o":["24 kg","2.45 kg","0.122 kg","0.0408 kg"],"a":1,"e":"Working: M = ω_P I ω/(rg) = 2.0 × 0.010 × 60/(0.050 × 9.8) = 1.2/0.49 ≈ 2.45 kg. Forgetting g gives 24 kg; forgetting r gives 0.122 kg; forgetting ω gives 0.0408 kg.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0846","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A rotor's spin angular momentum is 1.6 kg m² s⁻¹ and it precesses at 0.50 rad/s. What torque acts perpendicular to L?","o":["0.32 N m","3.2 N m","0.80 N m","0.50 N m"],"a":2,"e":"Working: τ = L ω_P = 1.6 × 0.50 = 0.80 N m. Dividing instead of multiplying gives 3.2; using ω_P/L gives 0.32; forgetting L gives 0.50.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0847","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A horizontal gyroscope has mass 0.30 kg, with its centre of mass 0.050 m from the pivot along the spin axis. Take g = 9.8 m/s². What is the gravitational torque about the pivot?","o":["0.015 N m","0.294 N m","1.47 N m","0.147 N m"],"a":3,"e":"Working: τ = rMg = 0.050 × 0.30 × 9.8 = 0.147 N m. Forgetting g gives 0.015; using the diameter 0.10 m gives 0.294; a factor 10 error gives 1.47.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0848","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A gyroscope precesses at ω_P = 0.49 rad/s. How long does its spin axis take to turn through 90°? Use π = 3.14.","o":["3.2 s","6.4 s","12.8 s","0.77 s"],"a":0,"e":"Working: t = (π/2)/ω_P = 1.57/0.49 ≈ 3.2 s. A half turn (180°) takes 6.4 s and a full turn 12.8 s. Multiplying by ω_P instead of dividing gives 0.77 s.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0849","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A rotor has angular momentum L = 1.2 kg m² s⁻¹ and moment of inertia I = 0.030 kg m² about its spin axis. What is its spin angular velocity?","o":["0.025 rad/s","40 rad/s","0.036 rad/s","1.2 rad/s"],"a":1,"e":"Working: ω = L/I = 1.2/0.030 = 40 rad/s. Inverting gives 0.025 rad/s; multiplying gives 0.036; forgetting I gives 1.2.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0850","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A uniform solid disc of mass 4.0 kg and radius 0.30 m spins about its central axis. What is its moment of inertia about that axis?","o":["0.36 kg m²","0.045 kg m²","0.18 kg m²","0.72 kg m²"],"a":2,"e":"Working: I = ½MR² = ½ × 4.0 × 0.30² = 0.18 kg m². MR² gives 0.36; using R/2 gives 0.045; using 2MR² gives 0.72.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0851","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A gyroscope of mass 2.0 kg has I = 0.010 kg m² and spins at 200 rad/s. Horizontal, its precession rate is 0.50 rad/s (g = 9.8 m/s²). How far is its centre of mass from the pivot?","o":["0.10 m","0.0255 m","0.0051 m","0.051 m"],"a":3,"e":"Working: r = ω_P I ω/(Mg) = 0.50 × 0.010 × 200/(2.0 × 9.8) = 1.0/19.6 ≈ 0.051 m. Doubling the result gives 0.10 m; halving gives 0.0255 m; a decimal slip gives 0.0051 m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0852","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"The equinox drifts at about 50 arcseconds per year. Roughly how far does it move in 100 years, in degrees?","o":["About 1.4°","About 0.14°","About 14°","About 0.014°"],"a":0,"e":"Working: 50 × 100 = 5000 arcseconds = 5000/3600 ≈ 1.39°, about 1.4°. A decimal slip in the answer gives 0.14° or 14°; 0.014° is the value for 1 year in degrees.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0853","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A gyroscope's moment of inertia is tripled and its spin rate doubled, with M, r and g unchanged. Its precession rate becomes:","o":["Six times the original","One-sixth of the original","Two-thirds of the original","Three-halves of the original"],"a":1,"e":"ω_P ∝ 1/(Iω). Tripling I and doubling ω multiplies the denominator by 6, so ω_P becomes 1/6 of its original value.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0854","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A rotor turns at 1500 rev/min. What is its angular velocity in rad/s?","o":["25 rad/s","1500 rad/s","157 rad/s","9420 rad/s"],"a":2,"e":"Working: ω = 1500 × 2π/60 = 157 rad/s. Using rev/s (1500/60 = 25) forgets the 2π; leaving the value unconverted gives 1500; multiplying by 2π alone gives 9420.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0855","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A gyroscope of mass 3.0 kg has its centre of mass 0.20 m from the pivot, with the spin axis horizontal. Take g = 9.8 m/s². What torque does gravity exert about the pivot?","o":["0.59 N m","2.94 N m","58.8 N m","5.88 N m"],"a":3,"e":"Working: τ = rMg = 0.20 × 3.0 × 9.8 = 5.88 N m. A decimal slip on r gives 0.59 N m; using r = 0.10 gives 2.94; a factor 10 error gives 58.8 N m.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0856","c":"PHY","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession","s":"mcq","q":"A gyroscope has L = 0.80 kg m² s⁻¹. A torque of 0.20 N m acts perpendicular to L for 4.0 s. Through what angle does L turn, in degrees?","o":["57.3°","14.3°","45.8°","229°"],"a":0,"e":"Working: angle = (τ/L) t = (0.20/0.80) × 4.0 = 1.0 rad = 57.3°. Forgetting t gives 0.25 rad = 14.3°; forgetting L gives 0.8 rad = 45.8°; using 4 rad gives 229°.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0857","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"For an object to float in fluid, the ______________________","o":["upthrust force must be greater than the weight of the object","upthrust force must be lesser than the weight of the object","upthrust force must be equal to the weight of the object","None of the above"],"a":2,"e":"A body floats in equilibrium when the upthrust balances its weight. Greater upthrust makes it rise and lesser upthrust makes it sink. 'None of the above' is the trap, since option (c) is correct.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q8"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0858","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The ability of an object to float when it is placed in a fluid is called?","o":["Buoyant Force","Flotation","Upthrust","Viscosity"],"a":1,"e":"Flotation is the ability to float. Buoyant force and upthrust name the upward push from the fluid, which is the cause of flotation rather than the ability itself. Viscosity is resistance to flow.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q9"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0859","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The ability of a body to return to its equilibrium position, provided that the applied force was not too great is?","o":["Stress Release","Elastic Moduli","Distortion","Elasticity"],"a":3,"e":"Elasticity is the property that brings a deformed body back to its original shape once the load is removed, as long as the elastic limit is not exceeded. Elastic moduli are the constants that measure elasticity; distortion is the deformation itself.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part A (ERP 2021/22) — Q10"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0860","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following variables controls the physical properties of a perfect gas","o":["Pressure","Temperature","Volume","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"For a fixed amount of perfect gas, pressure, volume and temperature together fix its state (PV = nRT). Changing any one of them changes the physical properties, so all three are correct.","f":false,"src":"Class Tutorial Questions Part B (ERP 2021/22) — Q75"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0861","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"1 cal. =?","o":["1.2 joule","3.2 joule","4.2 joule","none of the above"],"a":2,"e":"1 calorie = 4.2 J in the module's convention (4.184 J more precisely). Printed key C agrees.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q47"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0862","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The amount of heat required to raise temperature of a substance by 1°C is called as:","o":["work capacity","heat capacity","energy capacity","none of the above"],"a":1,"e":"Heat capacity is C = Q/ΔT, the heat needed per 1 K (or 1°C) rise for the whole body. Per kilogram it becomes specific heat capacity, so do not confuse the two. Printed key B agrees.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q50"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0863","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The amount of heat required to raise the temperature of 1 kg by 1°C is called as:","o":["heat capacity","work capacity","specific heat capacity","energy capacity"],"a":2,"e":"Specific heat capacity c = Q/(mΔT) is the heat per kilogram per degree. The '1 kg' in the stem is the clue. Printed key C agrees.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q54"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0864","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The process of transfer of heat in solids is called as:","o":["Convection","Radiation","Conduction","none of the above"],"a":2,"e":"Conduction passes heat from particle to particle in a solid; the particles vibrate in place and do not travel. Convection needs fluid motion. Printed key C agrees.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q56"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0865","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The process of transfer of heat in liquids & gases is called as:","o":["Conduction","Radiation","Convection","absorption"],"a":2,"e":"Convection moves heat by bulk movement of the fluid, so warmer, less dense parts rise. Radiation needs no medium at all. Printed key C agrees.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q58"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0866","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"It is the process of heat transfer from a hot body to a colder body without heating the space between the two is called as:","o":["Conduction","Radiation","Convection","absorption"],"a":1,"e":"Heat from the Sun crosses empty space by radiation, so the space between does not get heated. 'Absorption' is a fate of radiation, not a transfer mode. Printed key B agrees.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q60"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0867","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"If the area through which heat is transferred is increased by a factor of 2, then the rate of heat transfer is ……………….","o":["increased by a factor of 2","decreased by a factor of 2","increased by a factor of 4","decreased by a factor of 4"],"a":0,"e":"Q/t = kAΔT/d, so the rate is directly proportional to A. Doubling A doubles the rate. The factor-4 option is the trap of squaring the area, which does not happen here. Printed key A agrees.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q66"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0868","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The equation relating the heat transfer rate to these variables is: (heat flows through a glass window of area A and thickness d, from inside at T1 to outside at T2)","o":["Rate = k•(T1 – T2)/d","Rate = k•A•(T1 – T2)/d","Rate = A•(T1 – T2)/d","Rate = k•A•(T1 – T2)/r"],"a":1,"e":"Conduction law: Q/t = kAΔT/d, with k the thermal conductivity, A the area, ΔT = T1 – T2 and d the thickness. Dropping A or writing r for d are the traps. Printed key B agrees.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q67"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0869","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A force of 50 N acts uniformly over and at right angles to a surface. When the area of the surface is 5 m², the pressure on the area is:","o":["250 Pa","10 Pa","45 Pa","55 Pa"],"a":1,"e":"P = F/A = 50 N ÷ 5 m² = 10 Pa. Adding the force and area (55 Pa) or subtracting them (45 Pa) are the slips.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q70"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0870","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following statements is false? The pressure at a given depth in a fluid","o":["is equal in all directions","is independent of the shape of the container","acts at right angles to the surface containing the fluid","depends on the area of the surface"],"a":3,"e":"At a given depth P = ρgh, which depends only on density, g and depth. Pressure is the same in all directions, acts normal to a surface, and does not depend on container shape. It does not depend on the area, so (d) is false.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q71"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0871","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A container holds water of density 1000 kg/m³. Taking the gravitational acceleration as g = 10 m/s², the pressure at a depth of 100 mm is:","o":["1 kPa","1 MPa","100 Pa","1 Pa"],"a":0,"e":"P = ρgh = 1000 × 10 × 0.100 = 1000 Pa = 1 kPa. Forgetting to convert 100 mm to 0.100 m gives 100 Pa, which is the trap.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q72"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0872","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The gauge pressure of fluid in a pipe is 70 kPa and the atmospheric pressure is 100 kPa. The absolute pressure of the fluid in the pipe is:","o":["7 MPa","30 kPa","170 kPa","10/7 kPa"],"a":2,"e":"Absolute = gauge + atmospheric = 70 + 100 = 170 kPa. Subtracting gives 30 kPa, the usual trap.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q74"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0873","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A U-tube manometer contains mercury of density 13600 kg/m³. When the difference in the height of the mercury levels is 100 mm and taking the gravitational acceleration as g = 10 m/s², the gauge pressure is:","o":["13.6 Pa","13.6 MPa","13 710 Pa","13.6 kPa"],"a":3,"e":"Gauge pressure = ρgΔh = 13 600 × 10 × 0.100 = 13 600 Pa = 13.6 kPa. The 13 710 Pa option is the trap of adding 110 Pa.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q75"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0874","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The mercury in the U-tube of Question 6 is to be replaced by water of density 1000 kg/m³. The height of the tube to contain the water for the same gauge pressure is:","o":["(1/13.6) of the original height","13.6 times the original height","13.6 m more than the original height","13.6 m less than the original height"],"a":1,"e":"For the same pressure, ρ_w h_w = ρ_Hg h_Hg, so h_w = (13600/1000) h_Hg = 13.6 times the original height. Water is far less dense, so the column must be much taller. The 'm more or less' options mix up a ratio with a difference.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q76"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0875","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"When a certain weight is suspended from a long uniform wire, its length increases by one cm. If the same weight is suspended from another wire of the same material and length but having a diameter half of the first one then the increase in length will be.","o":["0.5 cm","4 cm","2 cm","8 cm"],"a":1,"e":"ΔL = FL/(AY). Area A ∝ d², so halving the diameter quarters the area and multiplies ΔL by 4: 1 cm becomes 4 cm. Thinking 'half the diameter, so twice the extension' (2 cm) is the trap.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q78"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0876","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The area of cross-section of a wire of length 1.1 metre is 1 mm². It is loaded with 1 kg. If Young's modulus of copper is 1.1 × 10¹¹ N/m², then the increase in length will be (take g = 10 m/s²).","o":["0.01 mm","0.1 mm","0.075 mm","0.15 mm"],"a":1,"e":"F = mg = 1 × 10 = 10 N; A = 1 mm² = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m². ΔL = FL/(AY) = (10 × 1.1)/(1.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 1.1 × 10¹¹) = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.1 mm.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q80"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0877","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"If Young's modulus of iron is 2 × 10¹¹ N/m² and the interatomic spacing between two molecules is 3 × 10⁻¹⁰ metre, the interatomic force constant is:","o":["60 N/m","30 N/m","120 N/m","180 N/m"],"a":0,"e":"k = YA/L with A = a² and L = a gives k = Ya = 2 × 10¹¹ × 3 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 60 N/m. The other options come from slips in the powers of ten or the arithmetic.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q81"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0878","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The ratio of the lengths of two wires A and B of same material is 1 : 2 and the ratio of their diameters is 2 : 1. They are stretched by the same force. The ratio of their increases in length (A : B) will be:","o":["2 : 1","1 : 8","1 : 4","8 : 1"],"a":1,"e":"For the same F and Y, ΔL ∝ L/d². So ΔL_A/ΔL_B = (L_A/L_B) × (d_B/d_A)² = (1/2) × (1/2)² = 1/8, giving 1 : 8. Ignoring the diameter gives 1 : 2, which is the trap.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q84"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0879","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The Young's modulus of a wire of length L and radius r is Y N/m². If the length and radius are reduced to L/2 and r/2, then its Young's modulus will be:","o":["Y/2","2Y","Y","4Y"],"a":2,"e":"Young's modulus is a property of the material. Changing the length and radius changes the extension but not Y.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q85"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0880","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The extension of a wire by the application of a load is 3 mm. The extension in a wire of the same material and length but half the radius, by the same load, is:","o":["12 mm","15 mm","0.75 mm","6 mm"],"a":0,"e":"ΔL ∝ 1/A ∝ 1/r². Halving the radius multiplies the area's reciprocal by 4, so ΔL = 4 × 3 mm = 12 mm. The 0.75 mm answer comes from dividing by 4 instead.","f":false,"src":"PHY 101 Past Quests - IGSTA — Q89"},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0881","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A water tank is filled to a depth of 3 m. Take ρ = 1000 kg/m³ and g = 9.8 m/s². The gauge pressure at the bottom is:","o":["3 kPa","9.8 kPa","29.4 kPa","58.8 kPa"],"a":2,"e":"Gauge pressure = ρgh = 1000 × 9.8 × 3 = 29 400 Pa = 29.4 kPa. Leaving out g gives 3 kPa; leaving out h gives 9.8 kPa; doubling h gives 58.8 kPa.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0882","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The same tank as the previous question is open to the air, where the atmospheric pressure is 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa. The absolute pressure at the bottom is:","o":["101 kPa","71.6 kPa","29.4 kPa","130.4 kPa"],"a":3,"e":"Absolute = atmospheric + gauge = 101 000 + 29 400 = 130 400 Pa = 130.4 kPa. 29.4 kPa is the gauge value only; 71.6 kPa comes from subtracting instead of adding.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0883","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"In a hydraulic press, a force of 200 N acts on a small piston of area 0.01 m². The large piston has area 0.25 m². Ignoring friction, the force on the large piston is:","o":["5000 N","8 N","500 N","50 N"],"a":0,"e":"Pascal's principle: F₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂, so F₂ = F₁A₂/A₁ = 200 × 0.25 / 0.01 = 5000 N. Inverting the ratio gives 8 N, which is the trap.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0884","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A solid block of volume 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³ is fully submerged in water (ρ = 1000 kg/m³). Take g = 9.8 m/s². The upthrust on the block is:","o":["0.0196 N","196 N","1.96 N","19.6 N"],"a":3,"e":"Upthrust = weight of water displaced = ρ_w V g = 1000 × 2.0 × 10⁻³ × 9.8 = 19.6 N. Using 2.0 × 10⁻¹ m³ (a power-of-ten slip) gives 196 N.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0885","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A block of density 600 kg/m³ floats freely in water (ρ = 1000 kg/m³). The fraction of its volume that is below the surface is:","o":["40%","167%","60%","100%"],"a":2,"e":"Floating: weight = upthrust, so ρ_block V g = ρ_w V_sub g, giving V_sub/V = ρ_block/ρ_w = 600/1000 = 0.60 = 60%. Inverting the ratio gives 167%, which is not physical for a floating body.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0886","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"An object weighs 50 N in air. When fully immersed in water its apparent weight is 30 N. The upthrust on the object is:","o":["30 N","50 N","20 N","80 N"],"a":2,"e":"Apparent weight = true weight − upthrust, so upthrust = 50 − 30 = 20 N. The 30 N option is the apparent weight, not the upthrust.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0887","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A steel wire of length 2 m and cross-sectional area 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² is stretched by a force of 50 N. Take Y = 2.0 × 10¹¹ N/m². The extension is:","o":["0.25 mm","0.5 mm","5 mm","0.05 mm"],"a":1,"e":"ΔL = FL/(AY) = (50 × 2)/(1.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 2.0 × 10¹¹) = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.5 mm. Forgetting L gives 0.25 mm.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0888","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A wire of original length 0.5 m is stretched by 0.1 mm. Its strain is:","o":["2 × 10⁻³","5 × 10⁻⁵","2 × 10⁻⁵","2 × 10⁻⁴"],"a":3,"e":"Strain = ΔL/L = (0.1 × 10⁻³ m)/0.5 m = 2 × 10⁻⁴. Strain has no units, so the answer is a pure number.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0889","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A fluid's pressure is increased by 2.0 × 10⁶ Pa, and its volume decreases by 0.1%. The bulk modulus of the fluid is:","o":["2 × 10⁹ Pa","2 × 10⁶ Pa","2 × 10³ Pa","5 × 10⁸ Pa"],"a":0,"e":"B = ΔP / (ΔV/V) = 2.0 × 10⁶ / 0.001 = 2.0 × 10⁹ Pa. Using the volume change itself, not the fractional change, gives the wrong power of ten.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0890","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A force of 100 N stretches a wire of length 1 m and cross-sectional area 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² by 0.5 mm. The Young's modulus of the material is:","o":["2 × 10¹¹ Pa","4 × 10¹¹ Pa","2 × 10¹⁰ Pa","1 × 10¹¹ Pa"],"a":0,"e":"Y = FL/(AΔL) = (100 × 1)/(1.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 5.0 × 10⁻⁴) = 2.0 × 10¹¹ Pa. Using 1 mm instead of 0.5 mm gives 1 × 10¹¹ Pa.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0891","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A 0.5 kg mass of water (c = 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) is heated through 20 K. The heat absorbed is:","o":["42 kJ","4.2 kJ","420 kJ","10.5 kJ"],"a":0,"e":"Q = mcΔT = 0.5 × 4200 × 20 = 42 000 J = 42 kJ. Dropping the 1000 when converting to kJ gives 42 000, and using ΔT = 5 K gives 10.5 kJ.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0892","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Heat of 500 cal is converted to joules using 1 cal = 4.2 J. The energy is:","o":["119 J","4200 J","21 J","2100 J"],"a":3,"e":"500 × 4.2 = 2100 J. Dividing instead of multiplying gives 119 J; doubling gives 4200 J.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0893","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A room is at 27 °C. Its temperature on the Kelvin scale is:","o":["327 K","300 K","27 K","246 K"],"a":1,"e":"T(K) = θ(°C) + 273 = 27 + 273 = 300 K. The 246 K answer comes from 273 − 27, the sign slip.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0894","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Water is heated from 20 °C to 80 °C. The temperature rise in kelvin is:","o":["60 K","−60 K","353 K","333 K"],"a":0,"e":"A change of 1 K equals a change of 1 °C, so ΔT = 80 − 20 = 60 K. The 353 K option is the final temperature converted to kelvin, not the rise.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0895","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A glass pane of area 2.0 m² and thickness 4.0 mm has inside and outside temperatures differing by 20 K. Its thermal conductivity is 0.8 W m⁻¹ K⁻¹. The rate of heat conduction through the pane is:","o":["800 W","8000 W","80 W","8 W"],"a":1,"e":"Q/t = kAΔT/d = (0.8 × 2.0 × 20)/(4.0 × 10⁻³) = 8000 W. Using d = 0.04 m instead of 4.0 mm gives 800 W.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0896","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A body of mass 1 kg and specific heat capacity 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ absorbs 4200 J of heat. Its temperature rises by:","o":["1 K","0.1 K","4.2 K","10 K"],"a":0,"e":"Q = mcΔT, so ΔT = Q/(mc) = 4200/(1 × 4200) = 1 K. Dividing by the mass only, or by 10, are the slips.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0897","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Specific gravity of a substance is:","o":["its weight per unit volume","the product of its density and g","its mass per unit area","the ratio of its density to the density of water"],"a":3,"e":"Specific gravity = ρ/ρ_water, a pure number (about 13.6 for mercury). Weight per unit volume is specific weight, in N/m³, which is a different quantity.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0898","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The SI unit of dynamic viscosity is:","o":["m² s⁻¹","N m⁻²","N s m⁻²","N m⁻¹"],"a":2,"e":"Dynamic viscosity η = shear stress / velocity gradient, so its unit is Pa s = N s m⁻². m² s⁻¹ is kinematic viscosity, and N m⁻² is a pressure.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0899","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Surface tension of a liquid is best described as:","o":["the energy per unit mass of the liquid","the pressure inside a drop","a force per unit length acting in the plane of the surface","a force per unit volume of the liquid"],"a":2,"e":"γ = F/L, in N/m, acting along the surface and normal to any line drawn in it. The energy-per-area form (J/m²) is equivalent, but energy per unit mass is not surface tension.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0900","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Water wets clean glass, but mercury does not. The reason is that:","o":["water has a higher viscosity than mercury","mercury is more compressible than water","water's adhesion to glass is greater than its cohesion, while mercury's cohesion is greater than its adhesion to glass","mercury is denser than glass"],"a":2,"e":"Wetting depends on the balance between adhesion (liquid to solid) and cohesion (liquid to liquid). Water clings to glass; mercury's strong internal cohesion pulls it into droplets. Density and viscosity are not the cause.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0901","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"As the temperature of a liquid increases, its viscosity generally:","o":["stays the same","is independent of temperature","increases","decreases"],"a":3,"e":"Liquids flow more easily when warmer, so viscosity falls. Gases behave the other way, which is why the question asks about liquids.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0902","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Pascal's principle states that a pressure change applied to an enclosed fluid is:","o":["transmitted only in the direction of the applied force","zero at the container walls","transmitted undiminished to every part of the fluid and to the walls of the container","reduced with depth"],"a":2,"e":"A change in pressure at one point is passed equally throughout an enclosed fluid. This is the principle behind hydraulic lifts and presses.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0903","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Gauge pressure is defined as:","o":["absolute pressure minus atmospheric pressure","absolute pressure plus atmospheric pressure","atmospheric pressure minus absolute pressure","the total pressure at the surface of a liquid"],"a":0,"e":"P_gauge = P_absolute − P_atm. A tyre gauge reads gauge pressure, which is why the reading is lower than the absolute value.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0904","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"If the depth of a liquid is doubled, the pressure at the bottom of the container (same liquid, g unchanged):","o":["quadruples","stays the same","doubles","halves"],"a":2,"e":"P = ρgh is directly proportional to depth, so doubling h doubles the pressure. Only the depth matters, not the width of the container.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0905","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Archimedes' principle states that the upthrust on a body in a fluid equals:","o":["the volume of the body multiplied by g","the mass of the fluid in the container","the weight of the body","the weight of the fluid displaced by the body"],"a":3,"e":"Upthrust = weight of fluid displaced = ρ_fluid V_submerged g. The weight of the body equals the upthrust only when the body floats or is in equilibrium, which is a trap.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0906","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A block floats in water with exactly half its volume below the surface. Its density is:","o":["500 kg/m³","1000 kg/m³","2000 kg/m³","250 kg/m³"],"a":0,"e":"Floating: ρ_block V g = ρ_w (V/2) g, so ρ_block = 1000/2 = 500 kg/m³. Inverting the ratio gives 2000 kg/m³.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0907","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A body sinks in a fluid when:","o":["the fluid is less dense than air","its weight is greater than the upthrust on it","its weight is less than the upthrust on it","its weight equals the upthrust on it"],"a":1,"e":"Net downward force = weight − upthrust. A positive net force makes the body sink. Equal forces give equilibrium, as in floating.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0908","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The elastic limit of a material is the:","o":["ratio of stress to strain","strain at zero load","maximum stress up to which the body returns fully to its original shape when the load is removed","stress at which the material breaks"],"a":2,"e":"Beyond the elastic limit the body keeps a permanent set. The breaking stress is a larger value, and the ratio of stress to strain is a modulus.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0909","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Hooke's law states that, within the elastic limit:","o":["stress is inversely proportional to strain","extension is independent of the load","stress is directly proportional to strain","strain is directly proportional to stress squared"],"a":2,"e":"Stress ∝ strain within the proportional region, so the modulus (stress/strain) is constant. Extension is proportional to the load, not independent of it.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0910","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Strain is:","o":["measured in N/m²","measured in pascals","measured in metres","dimensionless"],"a":3,"e":"Strain = change in length / original length (or the fractional change in volume or angle). Both quantities are lengths, so strain has no unit. Stress is the one measured in N/m² or Pa.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0911","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Young's modulus has the SI unit:","o":["m/N","N m","it has no unit","N/m²"],"a":3,"e":"Y = stress/strain. Stress is in N/m² (Pa) and strain is dimensionless, so Y is in Pa. Students who forget that strain has no unit may choose 'no unit'.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0912","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A rubber band and a steel wire of the same length and cross-section are stretched by the same force. Which statement is correct?","o":["The rubber stretches more, because its Young's modulus is smaller","The steel stretches more, because its Young's modulus is smaller","They stretch equally, because the force is the same","The steel stretches more, because it is heavier"],"a":0,"e":"ΔL = FL/(AY). With F, L and A equal, the extension is inversely proportional to Y, so the material with the smaller Y stretches more. Rubber has a much smaller Y than steel.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0913","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The shear modulus of a material measures its:","o":["resistance to a change of volume","ratio of the change in length to the original length","stretching under tension","resistance to a change of shape without a change of volume"],"a":3,"e":"G = shear stress / shear strain. Shear changes shape but not volume. Resistance to volume change is the bulk modulus, and stretching is Young's modulus.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0914","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Heat is best defined as:","o":["a fluid that flows from hot to cold","energy transferred between bodies because of a temperature difference","the average kinetic energy of molecules","a measure of how hot a body is"],"a":1,"e":"Heat is energy in transit, driven by a temperature difference. Temperature measures how hot a body is and is not an energy. Heat is measured in joules.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0915","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Two bodies at the same temperature are placed in thermal contact. Which statement is correct?","o":["No net heat flows between them","Heat flows from the larger body to the smaller one","Heat flows from the body with less mass","Heat flows from the body with more heat energy"],"a":0,"e":"Heat flows only when there is a temperature difference. Size, mass and the total energy stored do not decide the direction of flow.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0916","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"The SI unit of specific heat capacity is:","o":["J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹","J K","J kg K","W m⁻¹ K⁻¹"],"a":0,"e":"c = Q/(mΔT), so its unit is J/(kg K) = J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. W m⁻¹ K⁻¹ is the unit of thermal conductivity, a different quantity.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0917","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Water is widely used as a coolant in engines mainly because it has:","o":["a low density","a low boiling point","a higher thermal conductivity than metals","a high specific heat capacity"],"a":3,"e":"A large c means water absorbs a lot of heat for a small temperature rise. Water is a poor conductor compared with metals, so the thermal-conductivity option is false.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0918","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Heat reaches the handle of a metal spoon left in hot soup mainly by:","o":["convection through the metal","radiation from the soup only","evaporation","conduction along the metal"],"a":3,"e":"Metals conduct heat well by particle-to-particle transfer. Convection needs fluid flow, and the handle is solid, so the answer is conduction.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0919","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Heat transfer by radiation:","o":["needs no medium","needs a solid medium","needs a fluid medium","is slower in vacuum than in air"],"a":0,"e":"Radiation is electromagnetic and travels through a vacuum, which is how the Sun's heat reaches the Earth. The other options are conduction or convection ideas.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0920","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Land and sea breezes are examples of heat transfer by:","o":["evaporation","convection","conduction","radiation"],"a":1,"e":"Air warmed over land rises and is replaced by cooler sea air during the day, and the reverse happens at night. This bulk movement of a fluid is convection.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0921","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"Doubling the thickness of a wall, with all other factors unchanged, changes the rate of heat conduction through it by:","o":["halving it","doubling it","quadrupling it","no change at all"],"a":0,"e":"Q/t = kAΔT/d, so the rate is inversely proportional to thickness d. Doubling d halves the rate.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0922","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"On the Kelvin scale, absolute zero is:","o":["−100 K","0 K","−273 K","273 K"],"a":1,"e":"Absolute zero is the lowest possible temperature, 0 K (about −273 °C). Negative kelvin values are not allowed on this scale.","f":false,"src":""},{"i":"PHY101-SP-0923","c":"PHY","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)","s":"mcq","q":"A temperature interval of 1 K is equal to a change of:","o":["10 °C","1 °C","273 °C","0.5 °C"],"a":1,"e":"The kelvin and the degree Celsius have the same size, so a change of 1 K equals a change of 1 °C. Only the zero points differ, which is why 273 °C is a trap.","f":false,"src":""}],"cards":[{"front":"Absolute time","back":"Newton's idea: time flows uniformly regardless of external factors and is the same for all observers.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Relative time","back":"Einstein: time depends on the observer's speed and position in a gravitational field.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Proper time","back":"Time interval measured by an observer along their own trajectory, in their own reference frame.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Coordinate time","back":"Time measured by an observer using a fixed reference frame; describes events at different locations.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Psychological time","back":"Subjective experience of time; varies with emotion and mental state.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Biological time","back":"Natural cycles of living organisms (circadian rhythms); e.g. the 24-hour sleep-wake cycle.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Universal time (UT)","back":"Standard based on Earth's rotation; essentially Greenwich Mean Time.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Atomic time","back":"Time measured by vibrations of caesium atoms; defines the SI second; used by TAI and GPS.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Sidereal time","back":"Earth's rotation relative to distant stars; a sidereal day is slightly shorter than a solar day.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Solar time","back":"Earth's rotation relative to the Sun. Apparent (sundial) vs mean (modern clocks).","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Planck time","back":"Smallest meaningful unit of time in quantum mechanics; light time over a Planck length; about 10⁻⁴³ s (5.39 × 10⁻⁴⁴ s).","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Historical time","back":"Eras, epochs and centuries, such as the Geological Time Scale.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Cosmological time","back":"Largest scales; the age of the universe is about 13.8 billion years.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Clock time","back":"Everyday time in hours, minutes and seconds, e.g. the 24-hour clock.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Dynamical time","back":"TDB and TDT; precise time for planetary motion and celestial mechanics.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Event time","back":"The duration an event takes; depends on the time system adopted.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Physical (real) space","back":"The 3D continuum of height, width and depth where objects exist and events occur.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Euclidean space","back":"Flat space: parallel lines never meet, triangle angles add to 180°.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Non-Euclidean space","back":"Euclid's rules fail; spherical (angle sum above 180°) or hyperbolic geometry.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Curved (Riemannian) space","back":"Non-Euclidean space of general relativity, warped by mass and energy.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Minkowski space","back":"Four-dimensional spacetime of special relativity; time as a fourth dimension; c constant.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Vector space","back":"Mathematical structure of vectors that can be added and scaled; the 3D space of displacement, velocity and force.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Hilbert space","back":"Infinite-dimensional vector space with inner product used for quantum states (the slide wrongly repeats curved-space text).","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Phase space","back":"Space whose points are the complete states (positions and momenta) of a system.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Metric space vs topological space","back":"Metric: distance defined between points. Topological: only closeness and continuity matter.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Outer space","back":"Region beyond Earth's atmosphere where stars, planets and galaxies exist.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Classical view of space and time","back":"Both absolute and separate: fixed stage for space, uniform flow for time.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Relativity view of space and time","back":"Unified into 4D spacetime; observers agree on spacetime intervals, not on separate space and time.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Spacetime curvature","back":"In general relativity gravity is the curvature of spacetime by mass and energy.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Constancy of c","back":"Speed of light in vacuum is the same for all inertial observers (about 3 × 10⁸ m/s).","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Time dilation","back":"Moving clocks run slower than clocks at rest, as seen by the observer.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Length contraction","back":"Moving objects appear shorter along their direction of motion only.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Relativity of simultaneity","back":"Events simultaneous for one observer may not be for another in relative motion.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Position vector","back":"r = x i + y j + z k = (x, y, z), from origin O to point P.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Unit vectors i, j, k","back":"Magnitude 1 along +x, +y, +z.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Displacement from coordinates","back":"Final minus initial: (x₂ − x₁) i + (y₂ − y₁) j.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Average velocity","back":"Displacement divided by time taken; a vector in the displacement direction.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Magnitude of a vector","back":"|A| = √(x² + y²) in 2D; include the square root.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Classwork A, B, C answers","back":"A = 7i + 3j; B = −6j; C = 8i + 2j.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Sidereal vs solar day","back":"Sidereal about 23 h 56 min 4 s; solar 24 h; difference about 3 min 56 s.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"Who introduced spacetime (exam answer)","back":"Albert Einstein per the lecture; Minkowski gave the geometric form in 1908.","u":"Space and Time"},{"front":"How many SI base units are there?","back":"Seven: metre (length), kilogram (mass), second (time), ampere (current), kelvin (temperature), candela (luminous intensity), mole (amount of substance).","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"SI unit of luminous intensity","back":"Candela (cd).","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"SI unit of amount of substance","back":"Mole (mol).","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"SI supplementary units","back":"Radian (plane angle) and steradian (solid angle). Both are dimensionless.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Which is NOT a base SI unit: henry, ampere, candela, mole?","back":"Henry. It is the derived unit of inductance.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Definition of the metre (module)","back":"Length of the path travelled by light in vacuum in 1/299 792 458 of a second.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Definition of the second","back":"Duration of 9 192 631 770 periods of radiation from the hyperfine transition of the caesium-133 ground state.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Definition of the kelvin","back":"The fraction 1/273.16 of the thermodynamic temperature of the triple point of water.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Definition of the ampere","back":"Constant current that gives a force of 2×10⁻⁷ N per metre between two infinite parallel conductors 1 m apart in vacuum.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Definition of the mole","back":"Amount of substance containing as many entities as there are atoms in 0.012 kg of carbon-12.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"CGS, FPS and MKS base units","back":"CGS: centimetre, gram, second. FPS: foot, pound, second. MKS: metre, kilogram, second.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"CGS unit of force; of energy","back":"Force: dyne (g cm s⁻²). Energy: erg.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"1 N in dyne; 1 J in erg","back":"1 N = 10⁵ dyne. 1 J = 10⁷ erg.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Length and mass conversions","back":"1 ft = 30.48 cm, 1 in = 2.54 cm, 1 lb = 454 g, 1 mile = 5280 ft = 1.61 km.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"1 mph in m/s","back":"0.447 m/s. Also 9.8 m/s² = 980 cm/s² = 32.2 ft/s².","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Four advantages of SI","back":"Coherent (no numerical factors), rational (one unit per quantity), absolute (no gravitational units, no g factor), metric (powers of ten).","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Fundamental vs derived quantities","back":"Fundamental ones are independent (length, mass, time in mechanics). Derived ones are built from them: area, force, work, pressure.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Prefixes: giga, mega, kilo, micro, nano, pico, femto","back":"10⁹, 10⁶, 10³, 10⁻⁶, 10⁻⁹, 10⁻¹², 10⁻¹⁵.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Angstrom and fermi","back":"1 Å = 10⁻¹⁰ m (atom size). 1 fermi = 10⁻¹⁵ m (nucleus size). 1 Å = 10⁵ fermi.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of force","back":"[MLT⁻²] (mass × acceleration). SI unit: newton.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of energy, work, torque","back":"[ML²T⁻²]. All three share the same dimensions (joule, N m).","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of power","back":"[ML²T⁻³] = energy/time. SI unit: watt.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of pressure and stress","back":"[ML⁻¹T⁻²] = force/area. Same as elastic modulus and energy per unit volume.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of density","back":"[ML⁻³] = mass/volume.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of coefficient of viscosity","back":"[ML⁻¹T⁻¹], from η = F/(A dv/dx). SI unit: Pa s.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of surface tension","back":"[MT⁻²] = force/length. Same as a spring constant. Not the same as tension (a force).","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of momentum and impulse","back":"Both [MLT⁻¹]. Impulse = F × t = change in momentum.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of Planck's constant","back":"[ML²T⁻¹], from E = hf. Same as angular momentum.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of angular momentum","back":"[ML²T⁻¹] (r × p, or Iω).","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of G","back":"[M⁻¹L³T⁻²], from F = Gm₁m₂/r². Unit: N m² kg⁻².","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of gravitational potential","back":"[L²T⁻²] = energy per unit mass (J/kg).","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of angular velocity","back":"[T⁻¹] (angle is dimensionless). Unit: rad/s.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Dimensions of angle","back":"Dimensionless [M⁰L⁰T⁰] (arc/radius). Radian is its unit.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Which quantities are dimensionless?","back":"Pure numbers, angles, refractive index, strain, relative density, and arguments of sin, log, exp.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Principle of homogeneity","back":"Only quantities with the same dimensions can be added or subtracted; every term in a correct equation has the same dimensions.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Three uses of dimensional analysis","back":"Check an equation, derive a relation between quantities, and convert from one system of units to another.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Limitations of dimensional analysis","back":"Cannot give numerical constants (2π, ½); fails for trig/exponential functions and sums of terms; cannot handle too many variables.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Pendulum period by dimensions","back":"T = k√(d/g). Dimensions fix the powers (½, −½), but k = 2π needs theory.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Ocean wave speed by dimensions","back":"c = k√(gλ). Density's exponent comes out zero.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Unit of energy if length and force units are each 4 times bigger","back":"4 × 4 = 16 times bigger, since energy = force × length.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Significant figures in a product","back":"The result has as many s.f. as the factor with the fewest. For 0.00450, leading zeros do not count: 3 s.f.","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Percentage error in A = πr²","back":"Twice the percentage error in r (power multiplies the relative error).","u":"Units and Dimensions"},{"front":"Scalar","back":"A quantity with magnitude only, no direction. Examples: mass, time, speed, distance, energy, work, power, density, charge, pressure, temperature, volume.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Vector","back":"A quantity with both magnitude and direction. Examples: displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, weight, momentum, torque, electric field.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Distance vs displacement","back":"Distance is the total path length (scalar). Displacement is the straight-line change in position with direction (vector). After a full lap, displacement is zero.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Speed vs velocity","back":"Speed = distance/time (scalar). Velocity = displacement/time (vector). Circular motion at constant speed has changing velocity.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Same-direction collinear vectors","back":"Add the magnitudes; the resultant keeps the same direction.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Opposite-direction collinear vectors","back":"Subtract the magnitudes; the resultant points along the larger vector.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Man walks 54.5 m E then 30 m W","back":"Displacement = 54.5 − 30 = 24.5 m east.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Car: 80 km E, 30 km W, 50 km E","back":"Net displacement = 80 − 30 + 50 = 100 km east (distance is 160 km).","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Resultant of perpendicular vectors","back":"R = √(A² + B²); direction tan θ = opposite/adjacent.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"What does '28.1° W of N' mean?","back":"Start pointing north and turn 28.1° towards west.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Boat 15 m/s N, river 8.0 m/s W","back":"R = √(15² + 8²) = 17 m/s; tan θ = 8/15, θ = 28.1° W of N.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Component formulas","back":"Aₓ = A cos θ, A_y = A sin θ (θ from +x axis); A = √(Aₓ² + A_y²); tan θ = A_y/Aₓ.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Plane 63.5 m/s at 32° S of E","back":"Horizontal 63.5 cos 32° = 53.85 m/s east; vertical 63.5 sin 32° = 33.65 m/s south.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Unit vector","back":"A vector of magnitude 1 that gives direction only. Â = A/|A|.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"î, ĵ, k̂","back":"Unit vectors along +x, +y and +z respectively (i-hat, j-hat, k-hat).","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Magnitude of A = Aₓi + A_yj + A_zk","back":"|A| = √(Aₓ² + A_y² + A_z²).","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Adding vectors in component form","back":"Add like components: A + B = (Aₓ + Bₓ)i + (A_y + B_y)j + (A_z + B_z)k. Subtract likewise for A − B.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"|A| for A = 2i + j + 3k","back":"√(4 + 1 + 9) = √14 = 3.74.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Displacement vector from P to Q","back":"PQ = Q − P = (x₂ − x₁)i + (y₂ − y₁)j + (z₂ − z₁)k (final minus initial).","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Tail-to-tip method","back":"Draw B from the tip of A; the resultant goes from the tail of A to the tip of B.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Parallelogram method","back":"Draw A and B from a common point, complete the parallelogram; the diagonal from that point is the resultant.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Cosine rule","back":"a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A (A is the angle opposite a).","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Sine rule","back":"a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Resultant of A and B at angle θ","back":"R² = A² + B² + 2AB cos θ, where θ is the angle between the vectors, tail to tail.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"4 N and 5 N: resultant at 0°, 90°, 180°","back":"9.0 N at 0°; 6.4 N at 90°; 1.0 N at 180°. At 45° it is 8.3 N and at 135° it is 3.6 N.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Range of resultant of A and B","back":"|A − B| ≤ R ≤ A + B. Maximum at 0°, minimum at 180°.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Dot product definition","back":"A·B = AB cos θ = AₓBₓ + A_yB_y + A_zB_z. The result is a scalar.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Dot product of perpendicular vectors","back":"Zero, because cos 90° = 0.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Dot product of parallel vectors","back":"A·B = AB (cos 0° = 1). For anti-parallel vectors it is −AB.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Is the dot product commutative?","back":"Yes: A·B = B·A.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Cross product magnitude","back":"|A × B| = AB sin θ. The result is a vector perpendicular to both A and B.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Cross product direction","back":"Right-hand rule: curl the fingers from A to B; the thumb gives A × B.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Cross product of parallel vectors","back":"Zero, because sin 0° = 0 (and sin 180° = 0).","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Is the cross product commutative?","back":"No: A × B = −(B × A).","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"î × ĵ, ĵ × k̂, k̂ × î","back":"k̂, î and ĵ respectively. Reversing the order gives a minus sign.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"î·î and î·ĵ","back":"î·î = 1 and î·ĵ = 0.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Work as a vector product","back":"W = F·d is a dot product, so work is a scalar (joules).","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Torque as a vector product","back":"τ = r × F, magnitude rF sin θ; it is a vector.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Subtracting vectors","back":"A − B = A + (−B): reverse B and add.","u":"Vectors and Scalars"},{"front":"Power rule","back":"d(xⁿ)/dx = n xⁿ⁻¹. Example: d(x⁴)/dx = 4x³. Works for negative and fractional n.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Derivative of a constant","back":"Zero. Constants vanish on differentiation, so a constant term in x(t) does not appear in v.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Product rule","back":"d(uv)/dx = u dv/dx + v du/dx.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Quotient rule","back":"d(u/v)/dx = (v du/dx − u dv/dx) / v². Order in the numerator matters.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Chain rule","back":"If y = f(u), u = g(x): dy/dx = (dy/du)(du/dx). Differentiate the outside, then multiply by the derivative of the inside.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"d(sin x)/dx","back":"cos x (x in radians). With chain rule: d(sin ωt)/dt = ω cos ωt.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"d(cos x)/dx","back":"−sin x. With chain rule: d(cos ωt)/dt = −ω sin ωt.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"d(tan x)/dx","back":"sec² x.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"d(sec x)/dx","back":"sec x tan x.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"d(cot x)/dx","back":"−csc² x.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"d(eˣ)/dx","back":"eˣ. And d(e^(kt))/dt = k e^(kt).","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"d(aˣ)/dx","back":"aˣ ln a, for a constant a > 0.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"d(ln x)/dx","back":"1/x. And d[ln g(x)]/dx = g′(x)/g(x), so d[ln 5x]/dx = 1/x.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"d(sin⁻¹ x)/dx","back":"1/√(1 − x²), for |x| < 1.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"d(cos⁻¹ x)/dx","back":"−1/√(1 − x²).","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"d(tan⁻¹ x)/dx","back":"1/(1 + x²).","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Differentiating r(t) = x i + y j + z k","back":"Differentiate each component separately: dr/dt = (dx/dt) i + (dy/dt) j + (dz/dt) k, since i, j, k are constant.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Velocity from position","back":"v = dr/dt. A vector, tangent to the path, in m/s.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Acceleration from velocity","back":"a = dv/dt = d²r/dt². A vector, in m/s².","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Speed from velocity components","back":"Speed = |v| = √(vₓ² + vy² + vz²). A scalar, never negative.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Speed of x = ct², y = bt²","back":"vₓ = 2ct, v_y = 2bt, so speed = 2t√(c² + b²). Acceleration is constant, 2√(c² + b²).","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Is |dr/dt| equal to d|r|/dt?","back":"No. |dr/dt| is the speed; d|r|/dt is the rate of change of distance from the origin. In circular motion about the origin the second is zero.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Derivative of a dot product","back":"d(A·B)/dt = A·dB/dt + dA/dt·B. The result is a scalar.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Derivative of a cross product","back":"d(A×B)/dt = A×dB/dt + dA/dt×B. Keep the order of the factors; reversing flips the sign.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Derivative of a fixed unit vector","back":"Zero. i, j, k never change. A rotating unit vector n̂ has dn̂/dt perpendicular to n̂ with magnitude |dθ/dt|.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Vector of constant magnitude","back":"A·A is constant, so A·dA/dt = 0: the derivative is perpendicular to the vector (v ⟂ r, a ⟂ v in uniform circular motion).","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"When does speed increase?","back":"When v·a > 0. It decreases when v·a < 0 and stays constant when v·a = 0.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Direction of the tangent to a path","back":"Along v. Unit tangent = v/|v|; angle with x-axis from tanθ = v_y/vₓ.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Integration as reverse of differentiation","back":"v = ∫a dt + v₀ and r = ∫v dt + r₀. Always add the constant and fix it with initial conditions.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Derive v = u + at","back":"For constant a: v = ∫a dt = at + C. At t = 0, v = u, so C = u and v = u + at.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Derive s = ut + ½at²","back":"Integrate v = u + at: s = ut + ½at² + C. With s = 0 at t = 0, C = 0.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Derive v² = u² + 2as","back":"Write a = v dv/ds. Then v dv = a ds; integrate: ½v² − ½u² = as, so v² = u² + 2as.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"When do the constant-acceleration equations apply?","back":"Only when a is constant (in magnitude and direction). If a depends on t, integrate a(t) directly.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Slope of x–t graph","back":"Velocity. The slope of a v–t graph is acceleration; the area under a v–t graph is displacement.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Average velocity formula","back":"v_avg = Δx/Δt = (x₂ − x₁)/(t₂ − t₁). It is not the same as the instantaneous velocity dx/dt.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Related rates: sphere volume","back":"V = (4/3)πr³, so dV/dt = 4πr² dr/dt.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Related rates: circle area","back":"A = πr², so dA/dt = 2πr dr/dt.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Exponential decay rate","back":"If C = C₀e^(−kt), then dC/dt = −kC: the rate is proportional to the present value.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Particle momentarily at rest","back":"When v = 0. Solve dx/dt = 0 for t; the motion typically reverses there.","u":"Differentiation of Vectors"},{"front":"Mechanics map","back":"Mechanics splits into statics (equilibrium) and dynamics (motion). Dynamics splits into kinematics (geometry of motion) and kinetics (forces causing motion).","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Kinematics","back":"The study of how things move, ignoring the forces that cause the motion.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Kinetics","back":"Analysis of the forces that cause motion.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Statics","back":"The study of objects in equilibrium.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Distance","back":"Scalar: total length of path travelled. Unit: m.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Displacement","back":"Vector: straight-line change in position from start to end, with direction. Unit: m.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Speed vs velocity","back":"Speed is a scalar (how fast). Velocity is a vector (how fast and which way). Both in m s⁻¹.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Average velocity","back":"Total displacement divided by total time. Zero for a round trip.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Instantaneous velocity","back":"v = ds/dt, the slope of the s-t graph at one instant.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Acceleration","back":"Rate of change of velocity: a = dv/dt. Unit m s⁻².","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Retardation","back":"Negative acceleration, also called deceleration. It slows the body when velocity is positive.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"First suvat equation","back":"v = u + at (does not contain s).","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Second suvat equation","back":"s = ½(u + v)t (does not contain a).","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Third suvat equation","back":"s = ut + ½at² (does not contain v).","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Fourth suvat equation","back":"s = vt − ½at² (does not contain u).","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Fifth suvat equation","back":"v² = u² + 2as (does not contain t).","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Suvat validity","back":"Valid only for constant acceleration in a straight line.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Derive v² = u² + 2as","back":"Substitute t = (v − u)/a into s = ½(u + v)t to get 2as = v² − u².","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"g value","back":"Acceleration due to gravity ≈ 9.8 m s⁻² downward (often rounded to 10).","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Velocity at top of throw","back":"Zero. But the acceleration there is still g downward.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Max height from u","back":"H = u²/(2g) for a body thrown up at speed u.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Time to top and time of flight","back":"Time to top t = u/g; time of flight T = 2u/g (back to launch level).","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Free fall","back":"Fall under gravity alone from rest: v = gt, s = ½gt². Independent of mass in a vacuum.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Slope of v-t graph","back":"Acceleration.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Area under v-t graph","back":"Displacement.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Slope of s-t graph","back":"Velocity.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Area under a-t graph","back":"Change in velocity.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Calculus forms","back":"v = ds/dt, a = dv/dt, a = v dv/ds.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Why a = v dv/ds","back":"Chain rule: dv/dt = (dv/ds)(ds/dt) = v dv/ds.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Constant a by integration","back":"v = v₀ + at; s = s₀ + v₀t + ½at²; v² = v₀² + 2a(s − s₀).","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Projectile horizontal component","back":"Constant, unaffected by gravity (no air resistance): uₓ = u cos θ.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Projectile time of flight","back":"T = 2u sin θ / g (landing at launch height).","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Projectile max height","back":"H = u² sin²θ / (2g).","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Projectile range","back":"R = u² sin 2θ / g.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Angle of maximum range","back":"45°, giving R = u²/g. Complementary angles give equal ranges.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Velocity of A relative to B","back":"v_AB = v_A − v_B, both measured in the same frame.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Ball thrown up in a moving bus","back":"Ground displacement = bus speed × flight time = v₁ × 2v₀/g = 2v₀v₁/g.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Radial acceleration","back":"aᵣ = v²/r toward the centre; changes the direction of velocity.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Tangential acceleration","back":"aₜ = dv/dt along the tangent; changes the speed.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Total acceleration on a circle","back":"a = √(aₜ² + aᵣ²); angle with tangent: tan φ = aᵣ/aₜ.","u":"Kinematics"},{"front":"Define mechanics.","back":"The study of the motion of objects and the forces acting on them.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"What is Newtonian mechanics?","back":"The application of Newton's laws of motion (and gravitation) to bodies larger than atoms and much slower than light.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Define motion.","back":"A change in position or direction of an object with time.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Distance vs displacement?","back":"Distance is total path length (scalar). Displacement is the straight-line change in position, with direction (vector).","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Three types of motion in the module?","back":"Linear (rectilinear or curvilinear), rotary and oscillatory.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Rectilinear motion: meaning and example.","back":"Motion along a straight line, e.g. a car on a straight road or a freely falling stone.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Curvilinear motion: meaning and example.","back":"Motion along a curved path, e.g. a projectile or a car going round a bend.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Rotary motion: meaning and example.","back":"Rotation of a body about its own axis, e.g. the Earth spinning daily or a vehicle's wheels.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Oscillatory motion: meaning and examples.","back":"To-and-fro motion about a mean position, e.g. a pushed swing, a simple pendulum bob, guitar strings.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Rotation vs revolution?","back":"Rotation is spinning about an axis through the body; revolution is going round an external point or body.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"What is a macroscopic system?","back":"An object of ordinary size, containing a huge number of molecules.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"What is a system of particles?","back":"A collection of objects having mass, charge and other properties that may interact with each other.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Examples of systems of particles.","back":"Atoms and molecules, galaxies, the solar system.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Isolated system.","back":"No external interaction: no exchange of matter or energy with the surroundings.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Closed system.","back":"No exchange of matter with surroundings, but energy can be exchanged.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Open system.","back":"Exchange of both matter and energy with the surroundings.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Laws governing systems (module list).","back":"Newton's laws of motion; conservation of momentum; conservation of energy; conservation of angular momentum.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Internal vs external forces.","back":"Internal: exerted by other parts of the system, in equal opposite pairs. External: exerted by agents outside the system.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Define centre of mass.","back":"The point where the whole mass of a body or system can be taken as concentrated when analysing its translational motion.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Formula for r_cm.","back":"r_cm = (m₁r₁ + m₂r₂ + ... + mₙrₙ)/(m₁ + m₂ + ... + mₙ) = Σmᵢrᵢ/M.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"x_cm of two masses on a line.","back":"x_cm = (m₁x₁ + m₂x₂)/(m₁ + m₂).","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"COM in 3D: components.","back":"x_cm = Σmᵢxᵢ/M, y_cm = Σmᵢyᵢ/M, z_cm = Σmᵢzᵢ/M. Treat each coordinate separately.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"COM of equal masses?","back":"The simple midpoint (average) of their positions.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Where is the COM relative to heavier and lighter particles?","back":"Closer to the heavier one: m₁d₁ = m₂d₂ for two particles.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"COM of a uniform rod.","back":"At its midpoint.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"COM of a triangular lamina.","back":"The centroid, one third of the height above the base.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"COM of uniform sphere, disc or ring.","back":"At the geometric centre (symmetry). For a ring the centre is empty space.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Can the COM lie outside the body?","back":"Yes, e.g. for a ring, a boomerang, a horseshoe or an L-shape.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Symmetry rule for COM.","back":"A uniform body's COM lies on any axis or plane of symmetry.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Method for composite bodies.","back":"Replace each uniform part by a point mass at its own COM, then apply the weighted-average formula.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Newton's second law for a system.","back":"M a_cm = F_ext: the COM accelerates as if all mass were there and all external forces acted on it.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Effect of internal forces on COM.","back":"None. They cancel in pairs by Newton's third law.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"COM velocity formula.","back":"v_cm = Σmᵢvᵢ/M, so total momentum P = M v_cm.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"If F_ext = 0 and COM starts at rest?","back":"The COM stays at rest; total momentum remains zero.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"If F_ext = 0 and COM is moving?","back":"It continues with constant velocity; momentum is conserved.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Shell at rest explodes: what about COM?","back":"It stays at rest (momentum zero before and after). Fragments move apart with momenta that cancel.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Projectile explodes in flight: COM?","back":"The COM keeps following the original parabola (gravity is the only external force) until a fragment lands.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Gun recoil formula.","back":"m_gun v_gun = m_bullet v_bullet (opposite directions), so v_gun = m_b v_b/m_g.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"COM versus centre of gravity.","back":"COM depends only on mass distribution. CG uses weights; they coincide when g is uniform over the body.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"SI unit and dimension of COM position.","back":"Metre; dimension [L].","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Common mistake: COM of 3 kg and 1 kg at 0 and 4 m.","back":"1 m from the 3 kg mass (not 2 m). It is a mass-weighted, not a simple, average.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Who formulated the three laws of motion?","back":"Isaac Newton, in the Principia (1687).","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"Name two applications of the COM.","back":"High jump (Fosbury flop), vehicle stability, orbits about the barycentre, recoil analysis, posture and gait in biomechanics.","u":"Newtonian Mechanics and Centre of Mass"},{"front":"State Newton's first law.","back":"A body stays at rest or in uniform straight-line motion unless an external (impressed) force acts on it. Also called the law of inertia.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"State Newton's second law.","back":"The rate of change of momentum is proportional to the applied force and in the direction of the force: F = dp/dt. For constant mass, F = ma.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"State Newton's third law.","back":"For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction: F(AB) = − F(BA). The two forces act on different bodies.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Why don't action and reaction cancel?","back":"They act on two different bodies. Cancelling requires forces on the same body.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Define inertia.","back":"The tendency of a body to resist any change in its state of rest or uniform motion. Mass is the measure of inertia.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Define linear momentum.","back":"p = mv, a vector in the direction of velocity. Unit kg m s⁻¹ (= N s). Dimensions [M L T⁻¹].","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"SI unit and dimensions of force","back":"Newton: 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻². Dimensions [M L T⁻²].","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"CGS unit of force","back":"The dyne: 1 dyne = 1 g cm s⁻². 1 N = 10⁵ dyne.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Define impulse.","back":"J = FΔt for a constant force; J = ∫F dt in general. A vector, unit N s = kg m s⁻¹.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Impulse-momentum theorem","back":"Impulse of the net force equals change in momentum: J = FΔt = mv − mu = Δp.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Impulse from a force-time graph","back":"The area under the F-t graph. For a triangle, ½ × base × height.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Is force always an impulse?","back":"No. Force is [M L T⁻²], impulse is [M L T⁻¹]. Impulse is force multiplied by time of action (or integrated over it).","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Why do airbags reduce injury?","back":"Same change in momentum, but a longer collision time Δt gives a smaller average force F = Δp/Δt.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Mass vs weight","back":"Mass: amount of matter, scalar, kg, same everywhere. Weight: W = mg, a downward force in newtons, changes with g.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Formula for weight","back":"W = mg. Take g = 9.8 m/s² (or 10 m/s² if stated). Weight on the Moon (g ≈ 1.6) is about one sixth of that on Earth.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Mass from Newton's second law","back":"m = F/a, the ratio of net force to acceleration.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Equation: velocity from time","back":"v = u + at (constant acceleration only).","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Equation: displacement","back":"s = ut + ½at² (constant acceleration only).","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Equation without time","back":"v² = u² + 2as (constant acceleration only).","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"When do the equations of motion apply?","back":"Only when acceleration is constant, in a straight line.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"If s ∝ t², what is the motion?","back":"v = ds/dt = 2kt, so velocity increases linearly with time: constant acceleration 2k. 'Increasing velocity' is TRUE.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Normal reaction: direction","back":"Always perpendicular to the contact surface. Not always equal to mg.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Tension in a light string","back":"Pull along the string, away from the body. Same throughout a light string over a smooth pulley.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"What does a free-body diagram show?","back":"One body isolated, with only the forces acting ON it, as arrows; then resolve into components and apply ΣF = ma.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Frictionless incline acceleration","back":"a = g sinθ down the slope, independent of mass.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Normal reaction on a smooth incline","back":"N = mg cosθ, perpendicular to the plane.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Lift accelerating upward: reaction","back":"R = m(g + a). The person feels heavier.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Lift accelerating downward: reaction","back":"R = m(g − a). The person feels lighter.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Reaction in free fall","back":"R = 0 (a = g). Weightlessness: apparent weight is zero though gravity still acts.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Atwood machine: acceleration","back":"a = (m₁ − m₂)g/(m₁ + m₂) for a light string over a smooth pulley.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Atwood machine: tension","back":"T = 2m₁m₂g/(m₁ + m₂). It lies between m₂g and m₁g.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Force at angle θ above horizontal","back":"Horizontal part F cosθ, vertical part F sinθ. On a flat surface N = mg − F sinθ.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Passenger thrown forward as a bus stops: which law?","back":"First law (inertia): the body tends to keep moving forward.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Passengers pushed back when a bus starts: which law?","back":"First law (inertia): the body tends to stay at rest while the bus moves forward.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"When was Newton's Principia published?","back":"1687, containing the three laws of motion and universal gravitation.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Net force zero means what?","back":"Zero acceleration: the body is at rest or moving at constant velocity (equilibrium).","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Rebounding ball: change in momentum","back":"Δp = m(v − u) with opposite signs, so for equal speed v: |Δp| = 2mv.","u":"Newton's Laws, Equations of Motion and Impulse"},{"front":"Work done by a constant force","back":"W = F d cosθ, the dot product F · d, where θ is the angle between force and displacement.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Unit and dimensions of work","back":"Joule (J = N m = kg m² s⁻²); dimensions [M L² T⁻²]. Scalar, but can be negative.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Work done when force is perpendicular to displacement","back":"Zero, since cos 90° = 0. Examples: centripetal force, normal reaction on level ground.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Sign of work done by friction","back":"Negative: friction opposes motion, so W = −f d (θ = 180°).","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Kinetic energy formula","back":"KE = ½mv². Scalar, never negative. Doubling speed gives four times the KE.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Work-energy theorem","back":"The work done by the net external force equals the change in KE: W_net = ½mv² − ½mu².","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Common trap in work-energy calculations","back":"KE changes as v², so ΔKE = ½m(v² − u²), not ½m(v − u)².","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Gravitational potential energy","back":"U = mgh near the Earth's surface; depends only on vertical height, not on path.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Elastic potential energy of a spring","back":"U = ½kx², with k the spring constant (N/m) and x the extension or compression.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Work to stretch a spring from x₁ to x₂","back":"W = ½k(x₂² − x₁²), the area under the force-extension graph.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Mechanical energy","back":"The sum of kinetic and potential energy, E = KE + PE.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"When is mechanical energy conserved?","back":"When only conservative forces (gravity, spring) do work. Friction converts it to heat.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Speed after falling a height h from rest","back":"v = √(2gh), from mgh = ½mv². Independent of mass (no air resistance).","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Definition of power","back":"Rate of doing work: P = W/t = dW/dt.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Power in terms of force and velocity","back":"P = F v (cosθ in general). At constant power, force is inversely proportional to speed.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Unit and dimensions of power","back":"Watt, 1 W = 1 J/s; dimensions [M L² T⁻³].","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Kilowatt-hour in joules","back":"1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J. It is a unit of energy, not of power.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Types of friction","back":"Static friction (surfaces at rest relative to each other) and kinetic or dynamic friction (surfaces sliding).","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Limiting friction","back":"The maximum static friction, reached just before sliding starts. Static friction below the limit equals the applied force.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Coefficient of friction","back":"μ = F/R, limiting friction over normal reaction. No units. So F = μR.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Does friction depend on contact area?","back":"No. For a given normal reaction, friction is independent of the area of contact.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Does kinetic friction depend on speed?","back":"Approximately not. It is taken as independent of relative velocity. It does depend on the nature of the surfaces.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Relation between μk and μs","back":"Usually μk < μs: it is easier to keep a body sliding than to start it.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Angle of friction","back":"The angle λ between the total (resultant) reaction and the normal reaction. tan λ = μ.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Condition for a body to rest on a rough incline","back":"tanθ ≤ μ, equivalently θ ≤ λ. At limiting equilibrium tanθ = μ.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Acceleration down a rough incline","back":"a = g(sinθ − μk cosθ). The normal reaction is R = mg cosθ.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Moment of a force","back":"Force × perpendicular distance from the point to the line of action: M = F d⊥ = F r sinθ. Unit N m.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Sign convention for moments","back":"Anticlockwise positive, clockwise negative. Add moments algebraically with their signs.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"When is the moment of a force zero?","back":"When its line of action passes through the point (perpendicular distance zero).","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Torque as a vector","back":"τ = r × F, magnitude rF sinθ, direction perpendicular to both r and F (right-hand rule).","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"What is a couple?","back":"Two equal, opposite, parallel forces with different lines of action. ΣF = 0, moment = F × separation, pure rotation.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Principle of moments","back":"In rotational equilibrium, total clockwise moment = total anticlockwise moment about any point.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Conditions for equilibrium of a rigid body","back":"Translational: ΣF = 0 (ΣFx = ΣFy = 0). Rotational: Στ = 0 about any point.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Resultant of unequal anti-parallel forces","back":"R = F₁ − F₂, in the direction of the larger force, acting outside the pair on the larger-force side.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Net force on a couple","back":"Zero (equal anti-parallel forces), yet the body is not in equilibrium because the net torque is not zero.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Centre of gravity","back":"The point where the whole weight of a body can be taken to act. For point masses x = Σmx/Σm.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Stable equilibrium","back":"After a small displacement the body returns; CG rises, PE is a minimum (ball in a bowl).","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Unstable and neutral equilibrium","back":"Unstable: displaced body moves further away, CG falls (cone on tip). Neutral: it stays where placed (ball on a table).","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"What makes a body more stable?","back":"A low centre of gravity and a wide base; it topples when the CG line falls outside the base.","u":"Work, Energy, Friction and Equilibrium"},{"front":"Isolated system","back":"Exchanges neither matter nor energy with its surroundings and has no net external force or torque. Conservation laws apply to it.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Closed system (module wording)","back":"No matter crosses its boundary, but energy may be exchanged. Not the same as isolated.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Conservation law","back":"A measurable quantity of an isolated system does not change as the system evolves in time.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Linear momentum formula","back":"p = mv. A vector along the velocity. Unit kg m/s (= N s). Dimensions [M L T⁻¹].","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Law of conservation of linear momentum","back":"Total linear momentum of a closed system stays constant unless acted on by a net external force.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"When is momentum NOT conserved?","back":"When a net external force acts on the system, for example friction or gravity on only part of it.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Elastic collision","back":"Momentum and kinetic energy both conserved. Bodies bounce apart.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Inelastic collision","back":"Momentum conserved, kinetic energy partly lost (heat, sound, deformation). Bodies separate.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Perfectly inelastic collision","back":"Bodies stick together. Momentum conserved, maximum KE loss for that situation. v = (m₁u₁ + m₂u₂)/(m₁ + m₂).","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Is KE conserved in all collisions?","back":"No. Only in elastic collisions. Momentum is conserved in all collisions of an isolated system.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Equal masses, elastic head-on","back":"The two bodies exchange velocities.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Elastic collision speed rule","back":"Relative speed of approach equals relative speed of separation.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Gun recoil formula","back":"0 = mv + MV, so V = −(m/M)v. Equal and opposite momenta; the heavier body moves slower.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Explosion from rest","back":"Total momentum after is zero: fragments' momenta add vectorially to zero. KE is not conserved.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Rocket thrust","back":"F = vₑ (dm/dt): exhaust speed times mass of fuel burned per second.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Rocket speed gain","back":"Δv = vₑ ln(m₀/m), with m₀ the initial and m the final mass.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Why does a rocket work in space?","back":"It pushes exhaust backward, so by momentum conservation it gains forward momentum. No air needed.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Mechanical energy","back":"E = KE + PE. Constant when only conservative forces do work.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Speed of a body falling height h","back":"v = √(2gh) from mgh = ½mv², independent of mass (no air resistance).","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Pendulum energy","back":"Top: PE max, KE zero. Bottom: PE min, KE max. Total constant without drag.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Mass-energy relation","back":"E = mc², c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s. Mass and energy are interconvertible; total mass-energy is conserved.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Energy lost to friction","back":"Not destroyed: converted to thermal energy. Total energy is still conserved.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Electric charge: scalar or vector?","back":"Scalar. It has sign and size but no direction.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Charge is additive","back":"Total charge is the algebraic sum, with signs. +4 nC and −7 nC give −3 nC.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Three charge states","back":"Positive, negative and neutral. Neutral means equal positive and negative charge.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Charge conservation","back":"Charge cannot be created or destroyed, only transferred. Isolated system total is constant.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Angular momentum formula","back":"L = Iω for rotation about a fixed axis. Unit kg m²/s. A vector.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Law of conservation of angular momentum","back":"Total angular momentum of a system is constant if the net external torque is zero: I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Skater pulls arms in","back":"I decreases, so ω increases (L constant). KE increases because she does work.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Why do planets speed up near the Sun?","back":"Gravity gives no torque about the Sun, so L is constant. Smaller r means larger speed.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Quantity conserved with no external torque","back":"Angular momentum (not angular velocity, not moment of inertia).","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Conservative force: three tests","back":"Work independent of path; zero work around a closed loop; work is reversible.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Examples of conservative forces","back":"Gravity, elastic (spring) force, electrostatic force.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Examples of non-conservative forces","back":"Kinetic friction, air resistance, drag, viscous forces.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Work by a conservative force","back":"W = −ΔPE. Defining a potential energy is only possible for conservative forces.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Trap: net force zero but angular momentum changes?","back":"Yes. A couple has zero net force but nonzero torque, so L changes while linear momentum stays constant.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Trap: ball falling to Earth","back":"Momentum of the ball alone is not conserved (gravity is external). Take ball + Earth as the system.","u":"Conservation Principles"},{"front":"Newton's law of universal gravitation (statement)","back":"The force of attraction between two bodies is directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them: F = GM₁M₂/r².","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Value and unit of G","back":"G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻² (the lecture uses 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹).","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Dimensions of G","back":"[M⁻¹ L³ T⁻²], from G = Fr²/(M₁M₂).","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Dimensions of gravitational force","back":"[M L T⁻²], the same as any force (mass × acceleration).","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Inverse-square law: distance doubled","back":"The force becomes 1/4. Example: 16 units becomes 4 units. Tripling the distance gives 1/9.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Both masses doubled, distance fixed","back":"Force becomes 4 times larger, since F ∝ M₁M₂ (the product, not the sum).","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Is gravity attractive or repulsive?","back":"Always attractive, acting along the line joining the centres of the two masses; the two forces are equal and opposite.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Formula for g at a planet's surface","back":"g = GM/R². The mass of the falling object cancels.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"g at height h above the Earth's surface","back":"g' = GM/(R + h)² = g (R/(R + h))². At h = R, g' = g/4. Distance is from the centre.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Where is g largest on Earth's surface?","back":"At the poles (about 9.83 m/s²), smaller at the equator (about 9.78 m/s²) because of the equatorial bulge and rotation.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Mass vs weight in space or on the Moon","back":"Mass stays constant; weight W = mg changes with g. Moon g is about 1.62 m/s², one-sixth of Earth's.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Gravitational potential energy","back":"U = −GMm/r (zero at infinity). Negative because gravity is attractive. Unit: joule.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Gravitational potential V","back":"V = −GM/r, the potential energy per unit mass. Unit J/kg.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Dimensions of gravitational potential","back":"[L² T⁻²] = [M⁰ L² T⁻²], the same as velocity squared.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Potential at the Earth's surface","back":"V = −GM/R ≈ −6.25 × 10⁷ J/kg.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"What provides the centripetal force for a satellite?","back":"Gravity alone: GMm/r² = mv²/r.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Circular orbital speed","back":"v = √(GM/r), with r = R + h measured from the planet's centre. Independent of the satellite's mass.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Orbital period of a circular orbit","back":"T² = 4π²r³/(GM), i.e. T = 2π√(r³/GM). Also T = 2πr/v.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Effect of a larger orbit radius","back":"Orbital speed decreases (v ∝ 1/√r) and period increases (T ∝ r^{3/2}).","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Kepler's first law","back":"Law of ellipses: the orbit of a planet around the Sun is an ellipse with the Sun at one focus.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Kepler's second law","back":"Law of equal areas: a line joining planet and Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal times; planets move faster when closer to the Sun.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Kepler's third law","back":"Harmonic law: the square of the orbital period is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis, T² ∝ r³.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Natural vs artificial satellite","back":"Natural: moons that orbit planets. Artificial: man-made objects in orbit for communication, weather monitoring, navigation, imaging, etc.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Low Earth Orbit (LEO)","back":"About 160-2,000 km altitude; short period (about 90-120 min); imaging, communications and scientific research.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Medium Earth Orbit (MEO)","back":"About 2,000-35,786 km altitude; longer period than LEO; navigation such as GPS.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Geostationary orbit (GEO)","back":"35,786 km above the equator; period one sidereal day, so it stays over one point; weather forecasting and telecommunications.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Polar orbit and sun-synchronous orbit","back":"Polar: passes over both poles, so the whole Earth is covered as it rotates. Sun-synchronous: near-polar, with consistent solar lighting for imaging.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"GEO-synchronous transfer orbit","back":"An elliptical staging orbit whose far point is at geostationary altitude; used to move a satellite from low orbit up to GEO.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Escape velocity formula","back":"v_e = √(2GM/r), from ½mv² = GMm/r (total energy zero at infinity).","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Does escape velocity depend on the object's mass?","back":"No. The mass cancels in ½mv² = GMm/r.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Escape velocity vs orbital speed","back":"At the same radius v_e = √2 × v_orbit ≈ 1.414 v_orbit. At Earth's surface v_orbit is about 7.9 km/s.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Escape velocity and altitude","back":"It decreases with altitude, because r increases. At r = 2R it is v_e(surface)/√2.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Escape velocities: Earth, Moon, Mars, Jupiter","back":"Earth 11.2 km/s; Moon 2.4 km/s (2.38); Mars about 5 km/s; Jupiter about 60 km/s.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Why can the Moon not keep an atmosphere?","back":"Its escape velocity (2.4 km/s) is low, so gas molecules easily exceed it and drift away.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Why do astronauts feel weightless in orbit?","back":"They and the spacecraft are in free fall together, so the contact force is zero. Gravity is still about 90% of surface value at 400 km.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Earth-person force (70 kg)","back":"F = GMm/r² ≈ 686 N (G = 6.673×10⁻¹¹, M = 5.98×10²⁴ kg, r = 6.38×10⁶ m). It equals mg = 70 × 9.8.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Force between two cars 5 m apart (1000 kg, 1200 kg)","back":"F = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 1000 × 1200/25 = 3.2×10⁻⁶ N = 3.2 μN.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Common unit trap in gravity calculations","back":"Convert km to m before substituting (6378 km = 6.378×10⁶ m) and square the distance. Using km makes F about 10¹² times too large.","u":"Gravitation, Orbits and Escape Velocity"},{"front":"Radian definition","back":"Angle subtended at the centre by an arc equal in length to the radius. θ = s/r. Dimensionless.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Convert degrees to radians","back":"radians = degrees × π/180. So 180° = π rad and 90° = π/2 rad.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"1 revolution in radians","back":"2π rad = 360°.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Angular displacement","back":"The angle Δθ through which an object turns, measured in radians.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Definition of angular velocity","back":"ω = Δθ/Δt, rate of change of angular displacement. Unit rad/s.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Dimension of angular velocity","back":"[T⁻¹]. The radian is dimensionless.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Dimension of torque","back":"[ML²T⁻²], same as work and energy. Unit N m.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Types of angular velocity","back":"Average (Δθ/Δt over an interval) and instantaneous (limit as Δt → 0, dθ/dt). Both are types.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Is angular velocity scalar or vector?","back":"Vector. Its direction is along the rotation axis (right-hand rule).","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Relation between v and ω","back":"v = rω, with ω in rad/s. Points farther from the axis move faster.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Period and frequency link","back":"ω = 2π/T = 2πf. f = 1/T in hertz.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Convert rpm to rad/s","back":"ω = rpm × 2π/60.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Uniform circular motion","back":"Constant speed on a circle. Velocity still changes direction, so acceleration is non-zero and points to the centre.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Non-uniform circular motion","back":"Speed also changes. Acceleration has a centripetal part v²/r and a tangential part dv/dt.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Centripetal acceleration formulas","back":"a = v²/r = ω²r, directed towards the centre.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Centripetal force formula","back":"F = mv²/r = mω²r.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Is centripetal force a new force?","back":"No. It is the net inward force, supplied by tension, gravity, friction or normal reaction.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Direction of velocity in circular motion","back":"Along the tangent to the circle.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"What if the string breaks?","back":"The object moves off in a straight line along the tangent, by inertia (not radially outward).","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Doubling speed in a circle","back":"Centripetal force (and acceleration) increases four times at fixed r.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Banked curve, no friction","back":"tanθ = v²/(rg), so v = √(rg tanθ). Independent of mass.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Max speed on a flat bend","back":"v = √(μrg), from μmg = mv²/r.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Torque formula","back":"τ = rF sinθ = F × moment arm. Vector form τ = r × F.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Moment arm","back":"Perpendicular distance from the axis to the line of action of the force, r sinθ.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"False torque formula to avoid","back":"τ = F × r / sinθ is false. Sine multiplies: τ = rF sinθ.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"When is torque maximum or zero?","back":"Maximum at θ = 90°. Zero at θ = 0° or 180° or when r = 0.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Rotational equilibrium","back":"Net torque = 0, so angular acceleration is zero. Body at rest or turning at constant ω.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Moment of inertia definition","back":"I = Σmr². Resistance to angular acceleration. Unit kg m², dimension [ML²].","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"I of a solid disc and a ring","back":"Disc: ½MR². Ring: MR². Both about the central axis.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"I of a uniform rod","back":"Centre: (1/12)ML². End: (1/3)ML².","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"I of a solid sphere","back":"(2/5)MR² about a diameter.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Parallel-axis theorem","back":"I = I_cm + Md², d = distance between the parallel axes.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Newton's second law for rotation","back":"τ = Iα.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Angular momentum formulas","back":"L = Iω for a rigid body; L = r × p = mvr sinθ for a particle.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Unit and dimension of angular momentum","back":"kg m²/s (J s). [ML²T⁻¹], same as Planck's constant.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Conservation of angular momentum","back":"If net external torque = 0, L is constant: I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Skater pulls arms in","back":"I decreases, L is constant, so ω increases.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Rotational kinetic energy","back":"KE = ½Iω².","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Unit of angular acceleration","back":"rad/s², dimension [T⁻²]. α = Δω/Δt.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"Link between torque and angular momentum","back":"τ = dL/dt, analogous to F = dp/dt.","u":"Circular Motion, Torque and Angular Momentum"},{"front":"What is a gyroscope?","back":"A device with a fast-spinning rotor, mounted in a gimbal so it can turn freely about several axes. It resists changes to its spin axis.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"What does the rotor do?","back":"It is the spinning disc. It carries the angular momentum that gives the gyroscope its stability.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"What is the gimbal's role?","back":"A pivoted support (rings) letting the rotor turn freely about several axes, so base motion does not apply a torque to the spin axis.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Define the spin axis.","back":"The line through the rotor's centre about which it spins. Its direction gives the direction of the angular momentum vector.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Angular momentum of a rigid body spinning about a fixed axis?","back":"L = Iω, where I is the moment of inertia about the spin axis and ω is the angular velocity in rad/s.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"SI unit of angular momentum?","back":"kg m² s⁻¹ (equivalently N m s).","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Moment of inertia of a uniform disc about its central axis?","back":"I = ½MR², with M the mass and R the radius.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Relation between torque and angular momentum?","back":"τ = dL/dt. The net external torque equals the rate of change of angular momentum.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"What is rigidity in space?","back":"A spinning rotor keeps its spin axis fixed in direction when no external torque acts, because its angular momentum is conserved.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Which conservation law makes a gyroscope work?","back":"Conservation of angular momentum: with zero net external torque, L stays constant in magnitude and direction.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Define precession.","back":"The slow turning of a spin axis about another axis, caused by an external torque perpendicular to L.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Why does a gyroscope with gravity acting on it not fall over?","back":"Gravity's torque is perpendicular to L. It changes the direction of L, so the axis sweeps sideways instead of tipping down.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Precession angular velocity for a horizontal rotor?","back":"ω_P = rMg/(Iω) = τ/L, with r the pivot-to-centre-of-mass distance and M the mass.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Validity conditions for ω_P = rMg/(Iω)?","back":"Rigid rotor, spin ω much greater than ω_P, and the torque perpendicular to L (spin axis along the lever arm, horizontal).","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Torque of gravity when the pivot-to-centre line makes angle θ with the vertical?","back":"τ = rMg sinθ. The torque is largest when the line is horizontal (θ = 90°) and zero when it is vertical.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Direction of the precession vector ω_P?","back":"Along L × τ (right-hand rule). The tip of L moves in the direction of the torque.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Effect of a torque parallel to L?","back":"It changes only the magnitude of L, speeding up or slowing the spin. It produces no precession.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"How does ω_P depend on the spin rate?","back":"Inversely: ω_P ∝ 1/ω. Doubling the spin halves the precession rate; as the rotor slows, precession speeds up.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"How does ω_P depend on M and I?","back":"ω_P is proportional to M and inversely proportional to I, from ω_P = rMg/(Iω).","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"What is nutation?","back":"A small periodic wobble of the spin axis superimposed on steady precession, caused by an initial tilt or a disturbance.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"What path does the spin axis tip trace in precession?","back":"A cone around the precession axis when the spin axis is inclined.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Period of Earth's axial precession?","back":"About 26,000 years. It moves the celestial pole and causes the equinoxes to drift by about 50 arcseconds per year.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Cause of Earth's precession?","back":"Gravitational torques from the Sun and Moon acting on Earth's equatorial bulge.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Tilt of Earth's axis from the perpendicular to its orbit?","back":"About 23.5°. The axis traces a cone of this half-angle as it precesses.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"How do most smartphone gyroscopes work?","back":"They are MEMS devices: tiny vibrating elements that sense rotation through the Coriolis effect. They are not spinning rotors.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"How does a gyrocompass find true north?","back":"It uses a gyroscope's rigidity together with the Earth's rotation. It does not rely on the magnetic field.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Principle of inertial navigation?","back":"Gyroscopes keep a fixed reference direction in space and accelerometers track motion, so orientation is found from inside the vehicle without outside reference.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Define torque about a point.","back":"τ = r × F. Its magnitude is rF sinφ, where φ is the angle between r and F. Unit: N m.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"What is moment of inertia?","back":"The rotational analogue of mass: resistance to angular acceleration. I = Σmr², unit kg m².","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Is angular momentum a scalar or a vector?","back":"A vector. Its direction is along the spin axis, given by the right-hand rule.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Unit of the precession angular velocity?","back":"rad s⁻¹. Check: (N m)/(kg m² s⁻¹) gives s⁻¹, which is rad s⁻¹ in angular terms.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Common trap about precession?","back":"Precession does not mean the axis falls toward the ground. The torque changes the direction of L sideways, perpendicular to both L and the torque.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Common trap in the ω_P formula?","back":"Forgetting L or using ω in rpm. Use L = Iω with ω in rad/s, then ω_P = τ/L.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Convert 3000 rev/min to rad/s.","back":"ω = 2π × 3000/60 ≈ 314 rad/s. Always convert rev/min to rad/s before using L = Iω.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Why are gyroscopic effects seen in tops and bicycle wheels?","back":"Their spinning angular momentum resists changes to the axis direction. A torque on the axle produces precession perpendicular to the torque.","u":"Gyroscopes and Precession"},{"front":"Fluid (definition)","back":"A substance that flows and cannot resist a shear stress when at rest. It takes the shape of its container. Liquids and gases are both fluids.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Density formula and SI unit","back":"ρ = m/V, in kg/m³. Water at 4 °C has its maximum density, 1000 kg/m³.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Specific gravity","back":"SG = ρ/ρ_water. It is dimensionless. Mercury has SG 13.6.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Specific weight","back":"Weight per unit volume, w = ρg, in N/m³. Water is about 9.8 × 10³ N/m³ with g = 9.8.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Dynamic viscosity: unit","back":"η, in Pa s = N s m⁻². Caused by internal friction between fluid layers.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Kinematic viscosity","back":"ν = η/ρ, in m² s⁻¹. Not the same as dynamic viscosity.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Surface tension","back":"γ = F/L, a force per unit length acting along the surface, in N/m. Equal to energy per unit area (J/m²).","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Cohesion vs adhesion","back":"Cohesion: attraction between like molecules. Adhesion: attraction between unlike molecules. Water wets glass because adhesion beats cohesion.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Why mercury does not wet glass","back":"Its cohesion is stronger than its adhesion to glass, so it forms droplets instead of spreading.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Compressibility","back":"Fractional change in volume per unit pressure change, 1/B. Liquids have a very large bulk modulus.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Pressure definition","back":"P = F/A, the normal force per unit area. Unit: pascal, Pa = N/m².","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Pressure at depth","back":"P = ρgh. Depends on density, g and depth only, not on container shape or area.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Gauge vs absolute pressure","back":"P_absolute = P_gauge + P_atm. A gauge reads zero when open to the air.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Pascal's principle","back":"A pressure change applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished to every part of the fluid and to the container walls.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Hydraulic press","back":"F1/A1 = F2/A2. The output force is the input force multiplied by the ratio of the piston areas.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"U-tube manometer","back":"Gauge pressure = ρgΔh, where Δh is the difference in liquid levels. Mercury gives a large pressure for a small height.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Archimedes' principle","back":"Upthrust = weight of fluid displaced = ρ_fluid × V_submerged × g.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Upthrust vs weight of the body","back":"Upthrust equals the weight of the displaced fluid. It equals the body's weight only when the body floats or is in equilibrium.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Condition for flotation","back":"A body floats in equilibrium when upthrust equals its weight, so the net force is zero.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Fraction submerged in floating body","back":"V_submerged/V = ρ_body/ρ_fluid.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Apparent weight","back":"W_apparent = W − U, where U is the upthrust from the fluid.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Elasticity","back":"Ability of a body to return to its original shape when the deforming force is removed, provided the elastic limit is not exceeded.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Elastic limit","back":"The largest stress up to which the body fully recovers its shape. Beyond it a permanent set remains.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Hooke's law","back":"Within the elastic limit, stress is directly proportional to strain. For a wire, F = kx.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Strain","back":"ε = ΔL/L. Dimensionless: the ratio of two lengths. Unit-free.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Young's modulus","back":"Y = (F/A)/(ΔL/L) = FL/(AΔL), in Pa. Measures resistance to stretching. Independent of length and radius.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Bulk modulus","back":"B = −ΔP/(ΔV/V), in Pa. Measures resistance to a change of volume. Liquids have very large B.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Shear modulus","back":"G = shear stress/shear strain, in Pa. Resistance to change of shape without a change of volume.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Extension of a wire","back":"ΔL = FL/(AY). Extension is proportional to L and inversely proportional to the area, so ΔL ∝ 1/r².","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Force constant of a wire","back":"k = YA/L. It depends on the material, the area and the length.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Heat vs temperature","back":"Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference, in J. Temperature measures how hot a body is, in K or °C.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Heat capacity","back":"C = Q/ΔT, in J/K. Heat needed to raise the whole body by 1 K.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Specific heat capacity","back":"c = Q/(mΔT), in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. Heat needed per kilogram per kelvin.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Heating equation","back":"Q = mcΔT. Use ΔT in kelvin or °C (same size of change); m in kg.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Calorie to joule","back":"1 cal = 4.2 J in the module's convention (4.184 J precisely).","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Kelvin scale","back":"T(K) = θ(°C) + 273. Absolute zero is 0 K, about −273 °C, the lowest possible temperature.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Conduction","back":"Heat passes from particle to particle through a solid, with no bulk movement of the particles.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Convection","back":"Heat carried by the bulk movement of a liquid or gas. Land and sea breezes are examples.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Radiation","back":"Heat carried by electromagnetic waves. Needs no medium and works in a vacuum, like the Sun's heat.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Rate of conduction","back":"Q/t = kAΔT/d. k: thermal conductivity (W m⁻¹ K⁻¹); A: area (m²); d: thickness (m).","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Effect of doubling area or thickness","back":"Doubling the area doubles the rate. Doubling the thickness halves the rate. The area is not squared.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Perfect gas: controlling variables","back":"Pressure, volume and temperature (PV = nRT) set the state of a fixed amount of gas.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"},{"front":"Bulk modulus of an ideal gas at constant temperature","back":"B = P (equal to its pressure), because PV is constant in isothermal compression.","u":"Fluids, Elasticity and Heat (Tutorial Extras)"}]};
